const facts={
  "hildegardvonbingen":{"wiki":"Hildegard of Bingen","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Hildegard%20von%20Bingen.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The visionary abbess who sang her visions.","bio":"Hildegard von Bingen, born in the Rhineland in 1098, was a Benedictine abbess, mystic and polymath whose songs are the best known sacred monophony of the Middle Ages. Her music survives chiefly in the Symphonia armonie celestium revelationum, and in the Ordo Virtutum, the earliest morality play to come down to us with its music. For some forty years her secretary and provost Volmar of Disibodenberg wrote down her visions and, with them, the music; without him little of it would exist. Closer still was Richardis von Stade, the nun who worked with her on the Scivias, and whose transfer to another house Hildegard fought bitterly and never got over. She wrote on medicine and the natural world as well, and a number of scholars credit her with founding scientific natural history in the German lands. She died in 1179."},
  "peterabelard":{"wiki":"Peter Abelard","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Abelard.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The logician who wrote for Héloïse's convent.","bio":"Peter Abelard, born in France in 1079, was the leading logician of the twelfth century, a scholastic philosopher, theologian and teacher, and alongside all of that a poet and a composer. The music that survives under his name was written for the Paraclete, the convent led by Héloïse d'Argenteuil after the catastrophe that ended their life together: a body of hymns for her nuns to sing, and the planctus, his laments. He belongs in this constellation for that music rather than for the philosophy, though both came out of the same restless mind, and the songs gave one religious house a repertory that was entirely its own. He died in 1142. His monophonic music sits beside the visionary song of the cloister as the other great personal voice of the century."},
  "herradoflandsberg":{"wiki":"Herrad of Landsberg","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Herrade%20de%20Landsberg%20Hortus%20Deliciarum%20detail.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Abbess who bound song into an encyclopedia.","bio":"Herrad of Landsberg, born in Alsace about 1130, was a nun and later abbess of Hohenburg Abbey, up in the Vosges, and the author of the Hortus deliciarum. That book is a pictorial encyclopedia, illuminated throughout, and it carries notated songs among its pages. Those songs are why she belongs among the monastic monophonists: an abbess whose book gathered music alongside learning, serving the daily round of her own community rather than any court. She worked as artist and illuminator as well as writer and compiler, so the Hortus is both the record of a house's reading and an object made by hand inside it. She died in 1195, and the songs sit where she put them, bound into the encyclopedia itself rather than into any songbook."},
  "guillaumeixdukeofaquitaine":{"wiki":"William IX of Aquitaine","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/BnF%20ms.%2012473%20fol.%20128%20-%20Guillaume%20IX%20d'Aquitaine%20(1).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The first troubadour, and a duke.","bio":"Guillaume IX, born in 1071, was Duke of Aquitaine and Gascony, Count of Poitou from 1086 until his death, one of the great magnates of his age and a leader of the Crusade of 1101. He is also the first troubadour known by name: the earliest poet whose courtly songs survive with an author attached to them, and the point at which the whole tradition begins. The forms he established were the working material of the next generation, and Jaufre Rudel and his contemporaries composed inside them. His granddaughter Eleanor of Aquitaine carried the culture he began out of the south and into the French and English courts, where it took root. He died in 1126, a soldier and a ruler who happens to stand at the head of European song."},
  "bernartdeventadorn":{"wiki":"Bernart de Ventadorn","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/BnF%20ms.%2012473%20fol.%2015v%20-%20Bernart%20de%20Ventadour%20(1).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The most copied melodist of courtly love.","bio":"Bernart de Ventadorn, active in Occitania between about 1130 and 1190, is generally reckoned the most important of the troubadours in both poetry and music. Eighteen of his melodies survive from forty-five known poems, more than for any other twelfth-century troubadour, which is why the courtly canso can still be heard rather than only read. He worked in the trobar leu, the clear and singable manner, and his cansos did much to fix the classical shape of the courtly love song for the hundred and fifty years of troubadour writing that followed. His vida places him in the service of Eleanor of Aquitaine, the likeliest addressee of several songs. When the trouvères took the canso north and translated its conventions into French, Blondel de Nesle among them, it was largely his model they were working from."},
  "beatrizdedia":{"blurb":"The only trobairitz song with its music.","bio":"Beatriz de Dia was a trobairitz, one of the women troubadours of Occitania, active in the late twelfth century. A single song of hers survives with its melody attached, the only trobairitz composition to come down to us with its music, which makes hers the one woman's voice from the tradition that can actually be sung rather than only read."},
  "jaufrerudel":{"wiki":"Jaufre Rudel","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jaufre%20rudel.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The poet of love from afar.","bio":"Jaufre Rudel, prince of Blaye, was a troubadour working in Occitania around 1140, who probably died on the Second Crusade, in 1147 or later. His name is inseparable from amor de lonh, love from afar: a devotion fixed on someone distant and barely known, developed through his songs and taken up afterwards as one of the lasting themes of European lyric. He worked in the forms established by Guillaume IX, Duke of Aquitaine, the first troubadour known by name, and belongs to the generation that turned an aristocratic experiment into a tradition with rules. Rank was part of the picture: like Guillaume he was a lord before he was a singer, and in this world the two roles were not felt to be in any conflict at all."},
  "blondeldenesle":{"wiki":"Blondel de Nesle","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Pictures%20of%20English%20History%20Plate%20XXII%20-%20Blondel%20at%20Richard's%20Prison.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The trouvère of the Richard legend.","bio":"Blondel de Nesle, active in France between about 1155 and 1210, was a trouvère, a singer, poet and composer of the northern French courts. He can be identified either with Jean I of Nesle or with his son Jean II, which is as far as the documents will take us. He belongs to the generation that carried the Occitan canso north and translated its conventions into French, working from models such as Bernart de Ventadorn; the courtly love song survived the journey and acquired a second literature in the language of the north. His name later attached itself to the legend of Richard I, and that story has kept him better known than his surviving songs alone would explain, which is its own lesson in how medieval musicians reach us: sometimes through their music, sometimes through a tale told about them."},
  "adamdelahalle":{"wiki":"Adam de la Halle","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Adam%20de%20la%20halle.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Trouvère song's bridge into polyphony.","bio":"Adam de la Halle, born in Arras about 1240, was a poet-composer who stands at the hinge between trouvère song and polyphony. He wrote monophonic chansons in the old trouvère manner and polyphonic rondeaux in the new one, which has made him look conservative and progressive at once, and he was among the very few medieval composers to work convincingly in both. Le Jeu de Robin et de Marion, his sung play, is the best known thing he made. Robert II, Count of Artois, took him into his household and then to the Angevin court at Naples, which is how a musician from Arras came to be working in southern Italy. He represents the last generation of the trouvères, and has been counted ever since among the most important figures, musical and literary alike, of thirteenth-century Europe. He died in 1288."},
  "walthervondervogelweide":{"wiki":"Walther von der Vogelweide","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Codex%20Manesse%20Walther%20von%20der%20Vogelweide.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The summit of German courtly song.","bio":"Walther von der Vogelweide, active in Germany between about 1170 and 1230, was a Minnesänger: a composer and performer of love songs and political songs in Middle High German. Some hundred love songs survive under his name, widely held to be the high point of Minnesang, the German courtly love tradition, and his innovations gave fresh life to a genre whose conventions had begun to harden. He was also the first poet to write political verse in German, leaving a considerable body of praise, satire, invective and moralising, which was a genuinely new use for the courtly song. He has been called the greatest German lyric poet before Goethe. The two halves of the output, the amorous and the polemical, came out of a single working life as a singer, and it is that life the songs keep returning to."},
  "neidhartvonreuental":{"wiki":"Neidhart von Reuental","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Codex%20Manesse%20Neidhart.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Minnesang's largest surviving body of songs.","bio":"Neidhart von Reuental, active in Bavaria between about 1190 and 1240, was among the most famous of the Minnesänger and by a wide margin the best preserved. Around fifteen hundred strophes attributed to him survive, the largest corpus of lyrics of any Minnesänger, which is itself evidence of how popular the songs were: they were copied, recopied and passed along well beyond his own lifetime. More unusually, the music survives too. Manuscripts preserve almost seventy melodies for fifty-five of his songs, where most of his contemporaries reach us as words alone and have to be sung to borrowed or reconstructed tunes. That accident of transmission makes him the Minnesänger whose sound can actually be recovered, and puts him at the centre of any serious attempt to perform German courtly song today."},
  "alfonsoxelsabio":{"wiki":"Alfonso X of Castile","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Alfonso%20X%20el%20Sabio%20en%20El%20libro%20de%20los%20juegos.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The king who assembled the Cantigas.","bio":"Alfonso X, called el Sabio, the Learned, was born in 1221 and ruled Castile, León and Galicia from 1252 until his death in 1284. A dissident faction elected him king of Germany in 1257, a claim he renounced in 1275, as he had given up his claim on Gascony when he allied with England in 1254. His place in music is of a different kind from a composer's. He commissioned, compiled and had a hand in composing the Cantigas de Santa María, songs for the Virgin gathered into the largest single collection of medieval monophony that survives. He was a writer, a poet and an astronomer himself; the Cantigas exist because a king put the whole machinery of a learned court behind a body of song."},
  "leonin":{"wiki":"Léonin","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Codex%20montpellier%20P.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The first named composer of organum.","bio":"Léonin, active in Paris from the 1150s until about 1201, is the first significant composer of polyphonic organum known to us by name. He probably lived and worked at the cathedral of Notre-Dame, and he is credited with the Magnus liber organi, the great book of organum that gave the Paris repertory its foundation and its shape. Almost everything known about him comes from a single source, the theorist called Anonymous IV, who names him and describes the book; without that treatise he would be as anonymous as the rest of his century. Pérotin, the next figure of the Notre-Dame school, revised and abbreviated the Magnus liber, so the tradition begins with a book that was almost immediately rewritten, which is a fair picture of how medieval repertories actually worked."},
  "perotin":{"wiki":"Pérotin","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/P%C3%A9rotin%20photo.jpg?width=480","blurb":"He gave organum three and four voices.","bio":"Pérotin, working in Paris around 1200, is the composer who took organum from two voices to three and four. He belongs to the Notre-Dame school and to the wider ars antiqua, and his achievement was to develop the polyphonic practice of his predecessor Léonin into something far larger: pieces built over the long held notes of a chant, with several independent voices moving above them. According to the theorist known as Anonymous IV, he revised and abbreviated Léonin's Magnus liber organi, which makes him editor as well as successor to the Paris repertory. Very little else about his life is documented. What survives is enough to mark the point at which Western polyphony stops being ornament laid over plainsong and becomes architecture in its own right, with a scale that had not existed before."},
  "petrusdecruce":{"wiki":"Petrus de Cruce","blurb":"He split the breve open.","bio":"Petrus de Cruce, a cleric active around 1290 and associated with Amiens, was a composer and music theorist whose lasting work was done on the notational system rather than in song. He took the breve of Franco of Cologne and subdivided it further, splitting the unit of measured time finer than anyone had split it before. It sounds like a technicality and it changed what music could do: once time could be divided that finely on the page, the rhythmic complexity of the fourteenth century became possible. The line runs directly from him to Philippe de Vitry and the Ars nova treatise, which is why theorists rather than patrons are the load-bearing figures at this point in the story. He is remembered as a notator, and the notation is the legacy."},
  "francoofcologne":{"wiki":"Franco of Cologne","blurb":"Notation's first true measure of time.","bio":"Franco of Cologne, active around 1250, was among the most influential music theorists of the later Middle Ages, and possibly a composer as well. His idea was simple and transformative: a note's length should be readable from its shape on the page, rather than inferred from context by a singer who already knows the piece. He set it out in the Ars cantus mensurabilis, and the system that followed is still called Franconian notation. Everything downstream depends on it. Petrus de Cruce subdivided Franco's breve further, the Ars Nova followed from that, and the ability to write rhythm exactly is what allowed complex polyphony to travel across Europe instead of remaining a local oral practice. He is here as a theorist whose page changed what composers could imagine."},
  "anonymousiv":{"wiki":"Anonymous IV","blurb":"The treatise that saved two names.","bio":"Anonymous IV is not a name but a label, given by modern scholars to the writer of one of the most important surviving treatises on medieval music theory. Probably an English student at Notre-Dame in Paris during the 1270s or 1280s, he left nothing of his life behind him at all. Two partial copies of his writings survive, both from Bury St Edmunds, one made in the thirteenth century and one in the fourteenth. What makes him indispensable is that his treatise is the sole source for the names of Léonin and Pérotin: without those few sentences, the greatest polyphony of the twelfth and thirteenth centuries would be entirely anonymous. He is a standing reminder that the history of music depends as much on who wrote things down as on who composed them."},
  "philippedevitry":{"wiki":"Philippe de Vitry","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Philippe%20de%20Vitry%20-%20seal%2C%201349.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The man who named the Ars Nova.","bio":"Philippe de Vitry, born in 1291, was a composer and poet, a bishop and a music theorist, and the man who gave the Ars Nova its name. The treatise Ars nova notandi of 1322 is usually attributed to him; it set out the notation that made the new rhythmic freedom writable, building directly on Petrus de Cruce's subdivision of the breve. He worked alongside Jehan des Murs in Paris in the 1320s, the two of them co-architects of the new mensural theory. His standing among contemporaries was very high, and Petrarch wrote a glowing tribute to him as a philosopher and a seeker of truth. Guillaume de Machaut, his contemporary and correspondent, composed in the notation Vitry had codified and praised him in verse. He died in 1361."},
  "guillaumedemachaut":{"wiki":"Guillaume de Machaut","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Machaut.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The first Mass cycle by one hand.","bio":"Guillaume de Machaut, born about 1300 and long resident at Reims, stood at the centre of the Ars Nova and was, by common agreement, the leading French composer and poet of the fourteenth century. His Messe de Nostre Dame is the first complete Mass cycle by a single composer, a decision about scale and unity that later centuries built on. He learned his craft in the notation Philippe de Vitry codified, corresponded with him, and praised him in verse. From 1323 he served John of Luxembourg, King of Bohemia, as secretary and travelling companion, until the king was killed at Crécy in 1346; in later years he found royal patrons in Jean, Duc de Berry, and Charles II of Navarre. The Ars Subtilior generation, Solage and Baude Cordier among them, worked straight out of his ballades. He died in 1377."},
  "jehandesmurs":{"wiki":"Johannes de Muris","blurb":"Mathematician-architect of the new notation.","bio":"Jehan des Murs, born about 1290 and active in Paris, was a mathematician, astronomer and music theorist, a combination that was a good deal less unusual then than it sounds now. His treatise Ars nove musice is one of the founding documents of the Ars Nova style, and he and Philippe de Vitry were the co-architects of the new mensural theory worked out in Paris in the 1320s. Between them they gave the fourteenth century a way of writing rhythm that could be read the same way by musicians who had never met. The habits of mind are the same in his astronomy and in his music: measurement, proportion, and the conviction that time can be divided exactly. He died about 1355."},
  "anonymousromandefauvel":{"blurb":"The manuscript that carried the Ars Nova.","bio":"The music of the Roman de Fauvel reaches us with no composers' names attached to it. The manuscript was made in Paris in the 1310s, and it is the source through which much of the earliest Ars Nova survives at all: an anonymous body of work standing at the very beginning of the style."},
  "francescolandini":{"wiki":"Francesco Landini","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Francesco%20Landini%2C%20Squarcialupi%20Codex%2C%20page%20246.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Florence's blind organist and Trecento master.","bio":"Francesco Landini, born about 1325 and blind, was the central figure of the Italian Trecento: an organist, singer, poet, astrologer and instrument maker who worked in Florence and left over 150 surviving pieces. He was the son of Jacopo del Casentino, a Florentine painter in Giotto's circle, so the household was an artist's household before it was a musician's. At San Lorenzo he was a colleague of Lorenzo da Firenze. His ballate set the pattern that the generation after him followed: Paolo da Firenze carried the Florentine ballata on into the fifteenth century, and Johannes Ciconia absorbed the Trecento in Padua and married it to French practice. He died in 1397, the composer through whom Italian song of this period is still chiefly known."},
  "jacopodabologna":{"wiki":"Jacopo da Bologna","blurb":"Madrigalist of the northern Italian courts.","bio":"Jacopo da Bologna, documented between 1340 and 1386, was one of the earliest composers of the Italian Trecento, the period sometimes called the Italian ars nova. He concentrated on madrigals, canonic ones of the caccia-madrigal type as well as the ordinary kind, and left single examples of a caccia, a lauda-ballata and a motet. His career was a matter of northern courts: the Verona of Mastino II della Scala, and the Milan of Luchino Visconti in the 1340s. At both he crossed paths with Giovanni da Cascia, and the two set the same poems in direct competition, which tells us something about how court musicians were employed and how much their employers enjoyed watching them work against each other. He died in 1386."},
  "giovannidacascia":{"wiki":"Giovanni da Cascia","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Giovanni%20da%20cascia.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Jacopo's rival at the Verona court.","bio":"Giovanni da Cascia turns up in the sources under several names — Jovannes de Cascia, Maestro Giovanni da Firenze, Johannes de Florentia — all of them attached to a single Italian musician of the fourteenth century, who was at work in its middle decades between Florence and Verona. The spread of names is itself typical of the period, when a musician was identified by where he came from or where he happened to be serving. At Verona he was in the orbit of Mastino II della Scala, and it was there that he and Jacopo da Bologna set the same poems in direct competition, a form of court entertainment that also happens to preserve, for once, a clear picture of two composers measured against one another."},
  "lorenzodafirenze":{"wiki":"Lorenzo da Firenze","blurb":"Landini's colleague at San Lorenzo.","bio":"Lorenzo da Firenze, whose name in the documents is Lorenzo Masi, taught music and composed it in Trecento Florence, one of the generation working in what Italy called the ars nova. His close association was with Francesco Landini — the two were colleagues at San Lorenzo — which puts him at the centre of the city's musical life in the middle of the fourteenth century. Teaching was as much a part of the post as composing: a maestro at a Florentine church trained the singers who performed the music, and a style spread through those rooms as surely as through manuscripts. He died in 1372, and his name has travelled ever since in Landini's company, which is both how he is remembered and how he is overlooked."},
  "gherardellodafirenze":{"wiki":"Gherardello da Firenze","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gherardello%20da%20firenze.jpg?width=480","blurb":"One of the Trecento's first composers.","bio":"Gherardello da Firenze, born about 1320, was a Florentine composer and one of the first musicians of the Trecento — the Italian ars nova, as the period is sometimes called. He belongs to the founding generation of that style, the composers who in the decades around the middle of the fourteenth century gave Italy a secular polyphony with a character of its own. Florence was the centre of it, and Gherardello's working life sits at the point where the city's music begins to be documented at all. He died in 1362, before the generation that followed made the Trecento famous, and what we have of him is what the manuscripts happened to keep: a name among the first, and enough music to go with it."},
  "paolodafirenze":{"wiki":"Paolo da Firenze","blurb":"The last of the Trecento line.","bio":"Paolo da Firenze, born about 1355, was a Florentine composer and music theorist whose life straddles the end of the Middle Ages and the beginning of the Renaissance. More surviving Trecento music can be attributed to him than to any composer except Francesco Landini, whose Florentine ballata he took up and carried into the fifteenth century. That makes him the figure through whom the local tradition passes from one era into the next: he went on writing in an Italian idiom formed before he was born, at the moment when the international style of the north was becoming the common language of European music. He was a theorist as well as a composer, which was normal for a man of his training. He died in 1436, long after the world his music came from had gone."},
  "baudecordier":{"wiki":"Baude Cordier","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Cordier.jpg?width=480","blurb":"He notated a chanson as a heart.","bio":"Baude Cordier, born in Reims and active in the early fifteenth century, wrote the two best known pages in medieval notation: a chanson written out in the shape of a heart, and another laid out in a circle. They belong to the ars subtilior, the elaborate late-medieval style in which notation itself became a field for invention, and they extend Guillaume de Machaut's forms to their limit, the rhythmic and visual games pursued until the page is as much a puzzle as a score. Almost nothing else is known about him. One of his works carries an inscription recording his birth at Reims and a Master of Arts degree. Some scholars would identify him with Baude Fresnel, harpist and organist at the court of Philip the Bold; others reject it."},
  "solage":{"wiki":"Solage","blurb":"Ars Subtilior taken to its edge.","bio":"Solage — possibly Jean Soulage — was a French composer and very likely a poet too, active between about 1370 and 1400. He contributed more pieces than anyone else to the Chantilly Codex, the principal manuscript of the ars subtilior, the mannerist school that had its centre at Avignon in the closing decades of the fourteenth century. His Fumeux fume is the outer limit of that style, a piece that pushes the conventions of the age about as far as they will go. Like the rest of his generation he worked directly out of Guillaume de Machaut's ballades, taking their formal refinement and pressing it towards strangeness. Almost nothing is documented about his life. What survives is a body of songs of extraordinary complexity, made for singers and listeners who evidently relished the difficulty for its own sake."},
  "matteodaperugia":{"wiki":"Matteo da Perugia","blurb":"Milan Cathedral's first chapel master.","bio":"Matteo da Perugia, active between 1400 and 1416 and presumably from Perugia, was the first magister cappellae of Milan Cathedral, holding the post from 1402 to 1407. The job was practical as well as musical: he served as cantor and taught three boys chosen for him by the cathedral deputies, the kind of documentary detail that survives when the music itself has to be pieced together from manuscripts. He worked in the ars subtilior, the intricate late-medieval style of the decades around 1400, and stands among the last composers to write in it. Milan is his centre of gravity, and a cathedral post of this kind shows that rarefied manner in the hands of a salaried church musician with boys to train and services to sing."},
  "jacobsenleches":{"wiki":"Jacob Senleches","blurb":"Harpist-composer of the ars subtilior.","bio":"Jacob Senleches, documented in the 1380s in France and in Aragon, was a Franco-Flemish harpist and composer of the late Middle Ages. He wrote in the ars subtilior, the intricate style of the closing decades of the fourteenth century, in which rhythmic complexity and notational ingenuity were treated as accomplishments in themselves and a song could be a display of craft as much as of feeling. He played as well as composed, and the movement between France and Aragon in the surviving records is typical of how musicians of his kind made a living, following employment from one court to the next. Little else about him is documented, which is the usual condition for composers of this generation outside the major cathedral and chapel establishments."},
  "johannesciconia":{"wiki":"Johannes Ciconia","blurb":"Where Italian song met French craft.","bio":"Johannes Ciconia, born in Liège about 1370, spent his working life in Italy: first with the papal entourage in Rome, then, most importantly, at Padua Cathedral. He was a music theorist as well as a composer, and his significance is that he stands where two traditions meet: he absorbed the Italian Trecento, and Francesco Landini's music in particular, and married what he found there to French practice. At Padua he worked in the household of the cardinal Francesco Zabarella, for whom he wrote motets, ceremonial pieces for a powerful churchman and exactly the kind of commission that made a career possible. He died in 1412, and he stands in the histories as the transitional figure between the ars subtilior and the Renaissance that came after it."},
  "johndunstaple":{"wiki":"John Dunstaple","blurb":"The English sweetness Europe copied.","bio":"John Dunstaple, active in England from about 1390 until his death in 1453, is the composer Continental writers credited with a new sweetness of sound. That quality, the contenance angloise, was carried across the Channel by his music, and it helped turn medieval polyphony into Renaissance polyphony. Martin le Franc's poem Le Champion des Dames, written in 1442, says that Guillaume Du Fay and Gilles Binchois took on the English manner, a rare contemporary statement of who learned what from whom. His influence ran on through Ockeghem and Busnois as well, and he is often bracketed with Byrd and Purcell among England's most important early composers. At home he and Leonel Power were the two principal figures of the Old Hall Manuscript generation."},
  "leonelpower":{"wiki":"Leonel Power","blurb":"The Old Hall Manuscript's fullest voice.","bio":"Leonel Power, active in England from about 1370 until 1445, was with John Dunstaple the dominant figure of fifteenth-century English music. He wrote motets and sections of the Mass, and is the best represented contributor to the Old Hall Manuscript, the great collection of English polyphony of that generation. His importance in the wider story is structural: he was among the composers who began building Mass movements into unified cycles, binding several sections together with shared material instead of setting each one independently, the idea that would organise sacred music for the next hundred years. Scribes sometimes gave him his Christian name and nothing more, a sign of how familiar a figure he had become. He and Dunstaple stand together at the point where English music began to be exported."},
  "johnplummer":{"wiki":"John Plummer","blurb":"English polyphonist under Henry VI.","bio":"Almost the only fixed point in John Plummer's life is the reign he composed under, that of Henry VI of England. The dates attached to him, about 1410 to 1483, span a long life about which the documents say very little more. He is placed with the contenance angloise, the English manner that gives this group its name, and that placing rests on the music rather than on any recorded career. Beyond the reign and the pieces that carry his name, little survives, which is the ordinary condition of English composers of this period. He matters as evidence that the style had real depth to it — a school, rather than the achievement of two or three celebrated men — and that a generation of working musicians stood behind the few names the histories remember."},
  "walterfrye":{"wiki":"Walter Frye","blurb":"The Englishman the Continent kept copying.","bio":"Walter Frye, who died in 1475, was an Englishman writing at the opening of the Renaissance. What makes him matter is where his music turns up: his works were widely copied on the Continent, at exactly the time when the English manner, the contenance angloise, was being studied and absorbed by composers abroad. That circulation is the evidence. A composer whose pieces were worth copying into Continental manuscripts was a composer being learned from, and Frye belongs with the English figures whose influence ran well ahead of anything their documented careers would suggest. Very little is known of his life. He survives as a name in foreign sources and as a body of music that fifteenth-century Europe plainly valued, which is a different kind of fame from the kind his own country recorded."},
  "guillaumedufay":{"wiki":"Guillaume Du Fay","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/DufayBinchois.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The dominant composer of his century.","bio":"Guillaume Du Fay, born about 1397 and long associated with Cambrai, was the dominant composer of the fifteenth century, the leading European musician of his time, whose works were performed and reproduced everywhere. The sources describe him now as French, now as Franco-Flemish. Martin le Franc's Le Champion des Dames of 1442 says that he and Gilles Binchois took on the contenance angloise from John Dunstaple, and the two are pictured side by side in that book's famous miniature as the two poles of Burgundian style, though he never held a regular place in the Burgundian chapel. He was a music theorist as well as a composer. In 1464 the ageing Du Fay received Johannes Ockeghem at Cambrai, putting the two most eminent musicians of the century in one room. He died in 1474."},
  "gillesbinchois":{"wiki":"Gilles Binchois","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gilles%20Binchois.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Burgundian court's great chanson melodist.","bio":"Gilles de Bins, called Binchois, born about 1400, was a Franco-Flemish singer and composer, a figure at the centre of the Burgundian School and the great melodist of the court. He served Philip the Good, Duke of Burgundy, as court chaplain for some thirty years. He kept away from large-scale works, and his reputation rests on the shorter secular chansons, miniatures made with great refinement of detail. Le Champion des Dames names him with Guillaume Du Fay as one of the two who took on the English manner of John Dunstaple, and the pair are pictured together in that poem as the two poles of Burgundian style. Modern musicologists usually rank him below Du Fay and Dunstaple, but the three of them were the most celebrated musicians of the early Renaissance in Europe. He died in 1460."},
  "antoinebusnois":{"wiki":"Antoine Busnois","blurb":"L'homme armé's chief propagandist.","bio":"Antoine Busnois, born about 1430, was French, a composer, singer and poet, and with Johannes Ockeghem one of the two who led European music through the second half of the fifteenth century. The chansons are the core of his reputation, and he was probably the chief propagandist of the L'homme armé tune, doing more than anyone else to push that melody into circulation. His motet In hydraulis hails Ockeghem as a new Orpheus, an unusual document: a composer naming his elder publicly, in the music itself, rather than leaving the debt to be inferred. He belongs to the Burgundian world and to the early Franco-Flemish School that grew out of it, a generation whose singers were also its poets and its composers. He died in 1492."},
  "haynevanghizeghem":{"wiki":"Hayne van Ghizeghem","blurb":"A lutenist of the Burgundian School.","bio":"Hayne van Ghizeghem, born about 1445, was a Franco-Flemish composer and lutenist of the early Renaissance Burgundian School. He was a writer as well as a musician, and a performer as well as a composer, which was the ordinary shape of a court career at this date: the man who wrote the song was expected to play and sing it too. He belongs to the later Burgundian generation rather than to the founders of the style, inheriting a manner already formed and working inside it. He seems to have died about 1476, before he was much past thirty, so what survives is the record of a career cut short rather than one completed, which is why he is a name that turns up in the histories more often than on a concert programme."},
  "philipthegooddukeofburgundy":{"wiki":"Philip the Good","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Philipp%20the%20Good%20-%20Van%20der%20Weyden.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The duke who built the chapel.","bio":"Philip the Good, born in 1396, held the duchy of Burgundy from 1419 until he died in 1467; he came of a cadet Valois line, the house that supplied every fifteenth-century king of France. He is in this constellation as a patron. Under him the Burgundian state rose to its greatest wealth and standing and turned into one of Europe's leading homes for the arts, and the ducal chapel he maintained was the institution that made the Burgundian School possible: a standing body of singers and composers, paid, housed and given work worth doing. Gilles Binchois served him as court chaplain for some thirty years. What a ruler of this kind supplied was continuity, the assurance that a musician could spend a career in one place, writing for performers he knew."},
  "johannesockeghem":{"wiki":"Johannes Ockeghem","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Anonymous%20-%20Portrait%20of%20a%20Musician%2C%2064.54.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Canon-maker mourned by a whole generation.","bio":"Johannes Ockeghem, born about 1410 and for many years based at Tours, occupied the ground between Guillaume Du Fay before him and Josquin des Prez after as Europe's foremost composer, and did much to establish the early Franco-Flemish School. He is famous for canonic ingenuity, music in which voices are derived from one another by rule and the rule is part of the beauty. In 1464 he travelled to Cambrai to see the ageing Du Fay; in 1484 he went to Bergen op Zoom, apparently to meet Jacob Obrecht. His colleagues left the record of what he meant to them: Antoine Busnois hailed him as a new Orpheus in the motet In hydraulis, Loyset Compère prayed for him among the leading composers of the day, and Josquin's Déploration on his death is the tribute of a self-declared successor. He died in 1497."},
  "jacobobrecht":{"wiki":"Jacob Obrecht","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Obrecht2.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Builder of vast structural Masses.","bio":"Jacob Obrecht, born about 1457, wrote masses, motets and songs, and no one in late fifteenth-century Europe had a greater name as a composer of Masses; only after his death was that reputation overtaken, by Josquin des Prez. The Masses are vast structural things, organised with an evident appetite for scheme and proportion. Johannes Ockeghem travelled to Bergen op Zoom in 1484, apparently in order to meet him, which says something about how the older generation regarded him. He went twice to Ferrara, drawn there by the court of Ercole I d'Este, and it was in Ferrara that he died of plague in 1505, a long way from Bruges. He is the great architect of the generation between Ockeghem and Josquin."},
  "josquindesprez":{"wiki":"Josquin des Prez","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Josquin%20des%20Prez.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The first composer Europe knew by name.","bio":"Josquin des Prez — born about 1450, of Condé, dead in 1521 — was the first composer to hold a personal reputation across the whole of Europe. Building on Johannes Ockeghem, whose death he mourned in the Déploration to Molinet's text as a self-declared successor, he developed a polyphony of independent and often imitative voices, and pushed the relation of words to music to the front, preferring short repeated motifs to the long melismas of the earlier style. Ercole I d'Este hired him for Ferrara in 1503, after an agent's famous letter weighing him against Isaac. He taught Nicolas Gombert, who wrote a lament on his death; Jean Richafort built an entire Requiem out of quotations from him; Adrian Willaert's early motets work straight from his models; and Luther said the notes must do as Josquin wills."},
  "heinrichisaac":{"wiki":"Heinrich Isaac","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Isaac%2C%20gravure%20d%E2%80%99Hans%20Burkmair.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Florence and Vienna in one career.","bio":"Heinrich Isaac, born about 1450 in the southern Netherlands, had one of the most widely spread careers of his century. In Florence, Lorenzo de' Medici employed him to teach the Medici children and set Lorenzo's own verses to music; from 1497 he was court composer to Maximilian I, Holy Roman Emperor. His great project was the Choralis Constantinus, which his pupil and assistant Ludwig Senfl edited and completed after him. He wrote masses and motets, songs and instrumental music, and stood as a substantial contemporary of Josquin des Prez, with a lasting effect on the way music developed in Germany. His name appears in a great many forms across the sources, Arrigo il Tedesco among them. He died in 1517."},
  "pierredelarue":{"wiki":"Pierre de la Rue","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Pierre%20de%20la%20Rue.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Habsburg chapel's steadiest polyphonist.","bio":"Pierre de la Rue, born about 1452 and died in 1518, was a Franco-Flemish singer and composer of the Renaissance, and one of the long-serving musicians of the Habsburg-Burgundian court chapel. His name reaches us in a scatter of forms — Piersson, variants of Pierchon, and toponymics such as de Platea, de Robore and de Vico — a reminder of how loosely identity was recorded in his century. He was born into Josquin des Prez's generation, and later accounts place him beside Isaac, Obrecht, Weerbeke, Agricola, Brumel and Compère among the most admired practitioners of the Netherlandish polyphonic manner in the decades either side of 1500. That manner, dense and evenly woven for voices, was the common European language of sacred music, and the Habsburg chapel he served for so long was one of its great workshops."},
  "loysetcompere":{"wiki":"Loyset Compère","blurb":"He brought Italian lightness home to France.","bio":"Loyset Compère, born about 1445 and dead in 1518, was a Franco-Flemish composer, singer, cantor and Catholic priest attached to Cambrai. A contemporary of Josquin des Prez, he ranks among the most significant writers of motets and chansons of that generation, and among the first musicians to carry the lighter Italianate manner of the Renaissance into France — a loosening of the dense northern weave that later French song would build on. His debt to the older masters is written into the music itself: the motet Omnium bonorum plena is a prayer for the leading composers of the day, with Du Fay named first and Johannes Ockeghem among those he asks blessing for. That single piece is one of the clearest surviving statements of how a fifteenth-century composer understood the lineage he belonged to."},
  "alexanderagricola":{"wiki":"Alexander Agricola","blurb":"A widely copied voice of the Grande chapelle.","bio":"Alexander Agricola, born about 1456 and died in 1506, was a Netherlandish composer who wrote in the Franco-Flemish style and spent his most prominent years in the Grande chapelle, the musical establishment of the Habsburg court. He was one of the renowned names of the decades either side of 1500, and his music travelled: copies were distributed widely across Europe, which in an age when manuscripts still did most of the work was the real measure of a composer's reach. He worked across every significant sacred and secular genre then current, moving between the mass, the motet and the secular song rather than settling into one. His career shows how completely the Habsburg chapel had become a magnet for northern musicians, and how the polyphony of the Low Countries reached listeners far from the place where it was made."},
  "antoinebrumel":{"wiki":"Antoine Brumel","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Brunel%20from%20Celestial%20Concert.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Composer of the twelve-voice Earthquake Mass.","bio":"Antoine Brumel, born about 1460 and dead by around 1513, was among the first French composers to win international standing within the Franco-Flemish school, and after Josquin des Prez one of the most influential musicians of his generation. He worked in Paris and later in Ferrara, where Alfonso I d'Este engaged him following his father's death; Brumel took up the Ferrarese post in 1506. His boldest surviving work is the twelve-voice Earthquake Mass, a setting of extraordinary scale for its date, in which the sheer number of independent voices becomes the expressive point rather than a feat of arithmetic. That a Frenchman could stand at the front of a tradition dominated by singers from the Low Countries was itself new, and his reputation helped make French composers part of that story rather than an audience for it."},
  "jeanmouton":{"wiki":"Jean Mouton","blurb":"Refined motets, and Willaert's Paris teacher.","bio":"Jean Mouton, born about 1459 and died in 1522, was a composer of the French Renaissance who spent his career in the orbit of the royal chapel. He was celebrated in his own lifetime for his motets, counted then and since among the most refined of the period — smooth, unhurried, built from lines that seem to unfold without effort. His other great claim on the story of music is as a teacher: Adrian Willaert studied with him in Paris before leaving for Italy, where he became one of the men who founded the Venetian School. Through that one pupil, Mouton's polished French manner passed into Venice and from there into the whole later sixteenth century. He is a clear case of how influence in this period travels through teaching rooms as much as through printed books."},
  "jeanrichafort":{"wiki":"Jean Richafort","blurb":"He mourned Josquin in Josquin's own notes.","bio":"Jean Richafort, born about 1480 and living until around 1550, was a Franco-Flemish composer of the third generation of his school, working in Flanders. His name survives above all for one extraordinary act of homage: a Requiem written in memory of Josquin des Prez and built throughout on quotations from Josquin's own music, so that the older composer is not merely commemorated but literally sounded within the fabric of the piece. It is among the most direct evidence we have for how the generation after Josquin understood its inheritance — not as a style to be imitated from a distance, but as material to be handled, quoted and grieved over. The rest of Richafort's music sits comfortably within the mature northern manner of his day, patient in its imitation and unshowy in its expression."},
  "nicolasgombert":{"wiki":"Nicolas Gombert","blurb":"Polyphony at its densest, after Josquin.","bio":"Nicolas Gombert, born about 1495 and died in 1560, was a Franco-Flemish composer and chapelmaster attached to the Habsburg chapel, and the figure who best represents polyphony at its densest. Between Josquin des Prez and Palestrina he was the most famous and most influential composer in Europe, and his signature is a continuous imitation in which every voice takes up the same material and the texture almost never comes to rest — few clear seams, little room to breathe. Contemporary testimony names him as Josquin's pupil, and he wrote a musical lament on the older man's death, which is the kind of evidence historians trust for a teaching link at this distance. Later listeners have sometimes found his manner forbidding; his contemporaries heard mastery, and the complexity of mid-century sacred music is largely his doing."},
  "adrianwillaert":{"wiki":"Adrian Willaert","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Adrian%20Willaert.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Founder of the Venetian School.","bio":"Adrian Willaert, born about 1490 and died in 1562, was a Flemish composer who settled in Italy and founded the Venetian School, serving as maestro di cappella at St Mark's from 1527. He came south with a northern training: he had studied with Jean Mouton in Paris, and his early motets work directly from Josquin des Prez's models. In Venice he transplanted the Franco-Flemish polyphonic style and then made something local of it, and the roll of musicians who came through his establishment is remarkable. Cipriano de Rore succeeded him at St Mark's in 1563; Gioseffo Zarlino turned his practice into doctrine in the Istitutioni harmoniche; Andrea Gabrieli rose through the posts Willaert had built. Few composers have shaped a city's sound so completely, and everything Venetian that follows rests on his foundations."},
  "jacobusclemensnonpapa":{"wiki":"Jacobus Clemens non Papa","blurb":"He set the psalms in everyday Dutch.","bio":"Jacobus Clemens non Papa, born about 1510 and dead by 1555, was a Netherlandish composer who spent almost all his working life in Flanders. He was prolific and stylistically omnivorous, working in most of the current manners of his century, but he is remembered above all for the Souterliedekens — the psalms set polyphonically in Dutch rather than Latin. That project put composed polyphony into the everyday language of ordinary Netherlanders, at a moment when singing scripture in one's own tongue was a matter people cared about deeply, and it made his name in the Low Countries as his Latin church music did not. He belongs to the later Franco-Flemish generation, the years when the northern style had become an international idiom and its practitioners were finding local audiences for it."},
  "ciprianoderore":{"wiki":"Cipriano de Rore","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/CiprianoDeRore.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The madrigal turned expressive and chromatic.","bio":"Cipriano de Rore, 1515 to 1565, was a Franco-Flemish composer who made his career in Italy, at Ferrara and in Venice, and who did more than anyone of his generation to turn the madrigal into a vehicle for feeling. He had studied with Adrian Willaert, whom he succeeded as maestro di cappella at St Mark's in 1563. His madrigals are experimental and often chromatic, bending the harmony to follow the sense of a line of poetry rather than the demands of a smooth polyphonic surface, and that choice proved decisive for the Italian secular music that came after. Giaches de Wert inherited the manner at Mantua; Claudio Monteverdi went further still and named Rore as the founder of the seconda pratica, the practice in which the words command the music."},
  "orlandedelassus":{"wiki":"Orlando di Lasso","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Orlande%20de%20Lassus.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Two thousand works, and Europe's costliest chapel.","bio":"Orlande de Lassus, 1532 to 1594, known in Italy as Orlando di Lasso, was the chief representative of the mature Franco-Flemish style and, by common consent, among the foremost composers of the Renaissance's last decades. He spent his long maturity in Munich, where Albrecht V, Duke of Bavaria, maintained the most expensive chapel in Europe largely in order to keep him there. He was immensely prolific — over two thousand works, in every genre and in several languages — and the range is part of the point: music of great gravity and music of great levity sit in the same catalogue. His Munich chapel was also a school: Giovanni Gabrieli spent four years there from 1575, and Leonhard Lechner sang under him and later edited his music. Beyond it, Michael Praetorius's Syntagma musicum recorded the Franco-Flemish inheritance in Germany."},
  "philippedemonte":{"wiki":"Philippe de Monte","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Philippe%20de%20Monte%201.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The century's most prolific madrigalist.","bio":"Philippe de Monte, 1521 to 1603, was a Flemish composer of the Renaissance's later years who worked all over Europe and was based at Prague. He belonged to the third generation of madrigalists and left a larger madrigal output than anyone else then writing — a body of secular song on a scale that none of his contemporaries came near. Contemporaries rated him at the very top, naming him the best composer in the country and singling out his command of the new manner and of musica reservata; others set his collections beside those of Orlande de Lassus, which was the highest comparison then available. He remains one of the great unread figures of the period: hugely productive, admired in his own lifetime, and still waiting for the audience the sheer volume of his work deserves."},
  "giachesdewert":{"wiki":"Giaches de Wert","blurb":"The link from Rore to Monteverdi.","bio":"Giaches de Wert, 1535 to 1596, was a Franco-Flemish composer who spent his career in Italy, serving at Mantua and closely tied to the progressive musical circles of Ferrara. He was among the leaders who developed the late Renaissance madrigal, and he stands in the line of transmission running from Cipriano de Rore to Claudio Monteverdi: he inherited Rore's expressive chromaticism and carried it into his Mantuan years, and Monteverdi later took his post at Mantua and his manner with it. His later music was formative for the early Baroque, pushing the madrigal towards declamation and dramatic gesture — the direction opera would shortly take. Wert is a connective figure of the first importance, less a household name than the composer through whom a decisive idea passed from one generation to the next."},
  "giovannipierluigidapalestrina":{"wiki":"Giovanni Pierluigi da Palestrina","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Giovanni%20Pierluigi%20da%20Palestrina.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The model of the stile antico.","bio":"Giovanni Pierluigi da Palestrina, born about 1525 and died in 1594, was the central figure of the Roman School and, with Lassus and Victoria, one of the leading composers of late sixteenth-century Europe. Pope Julius III appointed him to the Cappella Giulia and then to the Sistine Chapel, and his sacred polyphony became the model of the stile antico: clear, balanced, evenly weighted, and endlessly teachable. He is among the handful of composers of his century whose music was never wholly forgotten. What carried furthest, though, was less any single piece than the Palestrinian style of counterpoint, codified as a teaching method by Johann Joseph Fux and drilled into composition students for centuries after. Tomás Luis de Victoria was in Rome throughout his years there and probably studied with him; Felice Anerio succeeded him as composer to the papal chapel."},
  "cristobaldemorales":{"wiki":"Cristóbal de Morales","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Retrato%20de%20Crist%C3%B3bal%20de%20Morales.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The first Spaniard of European standing.","bio":"Cristóbal de Morales, born about 1500 and died in 1553, was a Spanish composer who divided his career between Spain and Rome, and the first Spaniard to hold genuinely European standing in music. Among Spanish musicians of the generations before Tomás Luis de Victoria, none is reckoned more influential. At Seville he taught Francisco Guerrero, so a great deal of what Spanish cathedral polyphony sounded like across the sixteenth century can be traced back to that one teaching room. The style then crossed the Atlantic: Hernando Franco carried it, essentially whole, to Mexico City. Morales is therefore the source of two lineages at once — the cathedral tradition he shaped at home, and the colonial one that made Latin polyphony a New World practice with a long life of its own. He is where Spanish music stops being a local affair."},
  "tomasluisdevictoria":{"wiki":"Tomás Luis de Victoria","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Tom%C3%A1s%20Luis%20de%20Victoria.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Spain's great voice for the dead.","bio":"Tomás Luis de Victoria, 1548 to 1611, was the Renaissance's most famous Spanish composer, standing with Palestrina and Lassus among the principal composers of its later years. His career ran between Rome and Madrid. Unlike his colleagues he left almost nothing secular: the surviving output is sacred, polyphonic and set to Latin texts, and what drew the highest praise was the intensity of certain motets, and of his settings for Holy Week and the Office of the Dead. The Officium Defunctorum is the summit of that vein. He was a Catholic priest, and an accomplished organist and singer besides, but composing rather than performing was the life he wanted. He was in Rome through Palestrina's years there and probably studied with him, and behind his early writing lies the Sevillian model of Francisco Guerrero."},
  "gregorioallegri":{"wiki":"Gregorio Allegri","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gregorio%20Allegri%20Romano01.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Author of the celebrated double-choir Miserere.","bio":"Gregorio Allegri, 1582 to 1652, was an Italian Catholic priest, singer and composer of the Roman School, born and dead in the same city, Rome. He trained with Giovanni Maria Nanino, in the Roman teaching line that runs on from Palestrina, and his own music belongs firmly to that conservative tradition. One work has carried his name ever since: the Miserere, written for two choirs, the piece he is chiefly known for and the reason a working chapel musician's name survives at all. He was the brother of Domenico Allegri. Allegri's career, spent inside the Roman chapels as a singer as much as a composer, is a useful reminder that most of this repertory was made by church musicians on salary rather than by court celebrities, and that a whole reputation could come to rest on a single piece."},
  "feliceanerio":{"wiki":"Felice Anerio","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Felice%20Anerio.png?width=480","blurb":"Palestrina's successor at the papal chapel.","bio":"Felice Anerio, born about 1560 and died in 1614, was an Italian composer of the Roman School, working at the turn from the late Renaissance into the early Baroque. His central appointment tells the story: he succeeded Giovanni Pierluigi da Palestrina as composer to the papal chapel, inheriting the post and with it the care of the style Palestrina had made the Church's own. He wrote in that manner with real fluency, and the appointment placed him at the centre of Roman church music in his own day. His younger brother Giovanni Francesco Anerio was reckoned the more progressive composer of the pair. Felice's importance lies in continuity: he kept the Roman polyphonic tradition intact at exactly the moment when Italian music was rushing elsewhere, and that steadiness was itself a choice."},
  "giovannimariananino":{"wiki":"Giovanni Maria Nanino","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Giovanni%20Maria%20Nanino%20C.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The teacher behind Rome's next generation.","bio":"Giovanni Maria Nanino, born about 1543 and died in 1607, was an Italian composer and singer of the late Renaissance, and by common account the teacher who mattered most in Rome across the closing decades of the sixteenth century. That reputation is why his name recurs in so many other composers' biographies while his own music is heard far less often. Gregorio Allegri was among those he trained, in the teaching line that runs on from Palestrina. Nanino belonged to the Roman School himself, and was the older brother of another composer, Giovanni Bernardino Nanino. Teachers leave a thinner documentary trail than composers do, which makes them easy to underrate; a great deal of what Rome sounded like in the generation after him was settled in his lessons rather than on his own manuscript paper."},
  "andreagabrieli":{"wiki":"Andrea Gabrieli","blurb":"The St Mark's organist who exported Venice.","bio":"Andrea Gabrieli, born about 1533 and died in 1585, was an Italian composer and organist, and the first member of the Venetian School to win an international reputation. He came up through the musical establishment at St Mark's that Adrian Willaert had built, and held one of the organist's posts there. More than anyone of his generation he spread the Venetian style, in Italy and then in Germany, and he did some of that work directly as a teacher: Hans Leo Hassler travelled to Venice in 1584 expressly to study with him, and took what he learned back north. Andrea was also the uncle of the more famous Giovanni Gabrieli, who edited and published his uncle's music after his death — so a good deal of what survives reaches us through a nephew's editorial care."},
  "giovannigabrieli":{"wiki":"Giovanni Gabrieli","blurb":"Among the first to print dynamics.","bio":"Giovanni Gabrieli, born about 1557 and died in 1612, was an Italian composer and organist, the culminating figure of the Venetian School and among the most influential musicians of his generation, working exactly at the point where Renaissance idioms give way to Baroque ones. He spent four years from 1575 in Orlande de Lassus's chapel at Munich, and he was the nephew of Andrea Gabrieli, whose music he later edited and published after his uncle's death. His Sacrae symphoniae carries some of the earliest printed dynamic markings — instructions about loudness that composers had until then left to custom, a small notational step with large consequences for how music could be shaped. Heinrich Schütz came to Venice in 1609 to study with him and stayed until Gabrieli's death in 1612, when he was left his master's ring."},
  "claudiomerulo":{"wiki":"Claudio Merulo","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Claudius%20Merulus.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Innovator of the Venetian keyboard toccata.","bio":"Claudio Merulo, 1533 to 1604, was an Italian organist, composer and publisher of the late Renaissance, active in Venice and later in Parma, where he died. He was born Claudio Merlotti at Correggio and Latinised his surname once his reputation was made in Venetian cultural circles. His fame rests on two things. The first is his keyboard music, and above all his toccatas — the innovative keyboard writing for which he was best known in his own day. The second is his ensemble music in the Venetian polychoral style, built for the several choirs the city's churches could deploy at once. He also worked as a publisher, which in the sixteenth century meant deciding what music got printed and sold — and therefore, four centuries later, what still survives to be played."},
  "gioseffozarlino":{"wiki":"Gioseffo Zarlino","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gioseffo%20Zarlino.png?width=480","blurb":"The theorist who codified Willaert's practice.","bio":"Gioseffo Zarlino, 1517 to 1590, was an Italian music theorist, composer and organist at Venice, and the author of Le istitutioni harmoniche, the central theory text of the age. He had studied with Adrian Willaert, and his achievement was to take what Willaert and his circle actually did in practice and turn it into a coherent body of doctrine — rules of counterpoint that could be taught, argued with and handed on. He contributed as much to the theory of tuning, working out how the intervals that singers and instrument builders used might be described and justified. Theorists shape music indirectly but durably: for generations afterwards composers learned their craft through Zarlino's categories, and later quarrels about harmony were often quarrels with him. He was a practising musician too, composing and playing the organ in Venice."},
  "giovannicroce":{"wiki":"Giovanni Croce","blurb":"A Venetian who made his name in madrigals.","bio":"Giovanni Croce, born about 1557 and died in 1609, was an Italian composer of the Venetian School in its late Renaissance flowering. What sets him a little apart from his colleagues is where his reputation lies: he was prominent above all as a madrigalist, one of very few Venetians other than Claudio Monteverdi and Andrea Gabrieli to make a real mark in that secular form. Venetian musical prestige rested largely on grand sacred music for choirs, so a Venetian known for madrigals was an unusual figure. He worked through the decades when the madrigal was at its most sophisticated and Italian secular song stood on the point of turning into something else, and contemporaries noticed his part in it, even if his name has since been overshadowed by the Gabrielis."},
  "philippeverdelot":{"wiki":"Philippe Verdelot","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Verdelot1.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Father of the Italian madrigal.","bio":"Philippe Verdelot, born about 1480 and dead by around 1530, was a French composer who passed the greater part of his life in Italy, and is commonly called the father of the Italian madrigal. He was certainly among the form's earliest and most prolific composers, writing at the moment it was invented rather than inheriting it ready-made. His base was Florence, where he was prominent in the city's musical life in the period after the Medici recaptured it from the followers of Girolamo Savonarola — a political restoration that also restored a taste for elegant secular song. The madrigal he helped establish — serious Italian verse set for a handful of voices — is the form every madrigalist who follows in this story inherits, and he had a hand in shaping it before there were models to copy."},
  "jacquesarcadelt":{"wiki":"Jacques Arcadelt","blurb":"His first madrigal book outsold the century.","bio":"Jacques Arcadelt, born about 1507 and died in 1568, was a Franco-Flemish composer active in both Italy and France, known principally for secular vocal music. He worked in Florence and Rome, and was among the most famous of the early madrigalists: his first madrigal book, issued within ten years of the form's earliest examples, went through more printings than any other madrigal collection of the period. Il bianco e dolce cigno is the madrigal by which he is still known today. What distinguishes him from the other pioneers of the form, Philippe Verdelot and Costanzo Festa, is that he was equally prolific and equally adept at the French chanson, and wrote a great many of them, above all in his later years, once he had settled in Paris. He also left sacred music."},
  "lucamarenzio":{"wiki":"Luca Marenzio","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Luca%20conte%20Marenzio%2C%20Komponist%20des%2016.%20Jahrhunderts.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The most admired madrigalist of his day.","bio":"Luca Marenzio, 1553 to 1599, was an Italian singer and composer of the Renaissance's later decades, based in Rome. He was the most admired madrigalist of his day, which in the closing decades of the century meant standing at the head of the most sophisticated secular music in Europe: the Italian madrigal had become the form on which composers spent their finest invention, and contemporaries placed Marenzio's books at the front of it. Like most musicians of his generation he was a singer as well as a composer, the two careers belonging to the same world of courts and chapels, where a musician was expected to do both. His name has faded relative to the standing he enjoyed while alive, when it would have seemed odd to name a greater living madrigalist, and that gap is one of the period's sharper ironies."},
  "carlogesualdo":{"wiki":"Carlo Gesualdo","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gesualdo3.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Prince, murderer, and radical chromaticist.","bio":"Carlo Gesualdo, 1566 to 1613, was an Italian nobleman — Prince of Venosa and Count of Conza — and a composer of madrigals and sacred music whose harmonic language is unlike anything else in his century. Working at Naples, he wrote a chromaticism unmatched until the twentieth century: lines that slide between remote harmonies, dissonances left to ache, a manner that sounds less like experiment than obsession. He was a lutenist as well. His life is inseparable from his notoriety, since he murdered his first wife, and her noble lover with her, on finding them together. Later audiences have sometimes been too eager to read the murder into the music, but the music needs no biography to astonish; it remains the strangest sound anyone made in the Renaissance, and the least explicable by anything happening around it."},
  "maddalenacasulana":{"wiki":"Maddalena Casulana","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Maddalena%20Casulana.jpg?width=480","blurb":"First woman in print in Western music.","bio":"Maddalena Casulana, born about 1544 and dead by around 1590, was an Italian composer, singer and lutenist working in the late Renaissance, active in Venice, and the first woman in the history of Western music to have a whole book of her own music printed and published. The book was dedicated to her patron Isabella de' Medici, and that dedication matters as much as the notes: it records a woman with the standing to support another woman's work, in a culture that left female musicians little room to be anything but performers. Casulana composed in the madrigal, the most competitive secular form of her day, and she also sang and played the lute. Her name reaches us because she took the further step of getting the music into print, and her precedent begins a documented line."},
  "vittoriaaleotti":{"wiki":"Vittoria Aleotti","blurb":"An early published woman composer of Ferrara.","bio":"Vittoria Aleotti, born about 1575 and still living after 1620, was an Italian Augustinian nun, composer and organist at Ferrara, working across the turn from the late Renaissance into the early Baroque. Some scholars believe her to be the same person as Raffaella Aleotta — one of those identity puzzles that the sparse records of women's musical lives tend to produce, and which is still unresolved. She is recognised as one of the earliest women composers to have work published, and she contributed to both sacred and secular vocal music at a time when those forms were developing quickly. Convents were among the very few institutions in which a woman could be trained as a musician, direct other musicians and compose seriously, and Aleotti's career is a record of what that space made possible."},
  "claudiomonteverdi":{"wiki":"Claudio Monteverdi","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bernardo%20Strozzi%20-%20Claudio%20Monteverdi%20(c.1630).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The hinge between Renaissance and Baroque.","bio":"Claudio Monteverdi, 1567 to 1643, born at Cremona and formed at Mantua, was an Italian composer, choirmaster, string player and priest — the figure in whom the Renaissance turns into the Baroque. His madrigal books carry that old form over the divide; L'Orfeo, written for the Mantuan court, and L'incoronazione di Poppea stake out the new one, and the 1610 Vespers do comparable work in church. He took Giaches de Wert's post at Mantua and his manner with it, and he named Cipriano de Rore as founder of the seconda pratica, the practice in which words command the music — a position forced into the open by Giovanni Maria Artusi's attack of 1600 on modern music's imperfections. Vincenzo I Gonzaga kept him twenty-two years, then dismissed him. Francesco Cavalli, who sang under him at St Mark's from boyhood, carried the new style into the public opera houses."},
  "franciscoguerrero":{"wiki":"Francisco Guerrero (composer)","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Libro%20de...memorables%20varones%2C%201886%20%22Francisco%20Guerrero%22.%20(3816814223).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Seville's own, and Victoria's early model.","bio":"Francisco Guerrero, 1528 to 1599, was a Spanish Catholic priest, organist and composer, born and dead in Seville — a rarity in a century when musicians followed patronage across Europe. He studied with Cristóbal de Morales at Seville, taking the Spanish tradition of sacred polyphony directly from the older master's hands, and then spent his own career in that same city, in the tradition he had learned there. His importance runs forward as well as back: the Sevillian model that lies behind Tomás Luis de Victoria's early writing is Guerrero's. Between Morales, Guerrero and Victoria, Spanish sacred polyphony has an unbroken line of three generations, and Guerrero is its middle term — the pupil who became, in his turn, the example somebody else began from."},
  "antoniodecabezon":{"wiki":"Antonio de Cabezón","blurb":"Blind organist to Philip II.","bio":"Antonio de Cabezón, 1510 to 1566, was a Spanish organist and composer, blind from childhood, who rose quickly as a performer and entered the service of the royal family. Philip II made him court organist and took him along on his travels, so Cabezón played and listened in Italy, the Netherlands and England at a time when few Iberian musicians left the peninsula at all — a court appointment that turned into a career of unusual reach. He was the first major Iberian keyboard composer, and was reckoned in his own century one of the most important composers alive; later Spanish keyboard music begins from him. That a blind musician came to hold that place at the Spanish court, and to travel with his king, says as much about his playing as any surviving score can."},
  "juandelencina":{"wiki":"Juan del Encina","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Juan%20del%20Enzina%20Le%C3%B3n%20(cropped).JPG?width=480","blurb":"Poet-composer at the birth of Spanish drama.","bio":"Juan del Encina, 1468 to 1529, born Juan de Fermoselle and spelling himself Enzina, was a Spanish poet, playwright, priest and composer whose career ran from Salamanca to Rome. He is often credited, alongside Gil Vicente, as a joint father of Spanish drama: his plays put spoken and sung Spanish on stage at a moment when there was barely a stage to put it on. As a composer he is known for villancicos, the Spanish song form of his day, whose directness stands apart from the elaborate Latin polyphony his contemporaries were writing. Poet, dramatist and musician were not separate trades for him, and the strength of the songs is inseparable from the strength of the words. He is the point where Spanish theatre and Spanish song begin together, in the same hands."},
  "hernandofranco":{"wiki":"Hernando Franco","blurb":"He carried Spanish polyphony to Mexico City.","bio":"Hernando Franco, 1532 to 1585, was a Spanish Renaissance composer who spent his working life in the Americas, active in Guatemala and then as cathedral chapelmaster in Mexico City. What he took with him was the Spanish cathedral style at full strength — the manner of Cristóbal de Morales, transplanted essentially whole into New Spain — and what he built was an institution: a cathedral chapel where the imported European art was practised and taught on the far side of the Atlantic. Among those he taught was the Nahua chapelmaster of Valladolid in Michoacán who took his Spanish teacher's name, as converts commonly did, and is known as Don Hernando Franco. Franco is the pivot on which European polyphony becomes a New World tradition, continued by Indigenous and locally born musicians."},
  "vicentelusitano":{"wiki":"Vicente Lusitano","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Liber%20Primus%20Epigramatum%20(IA%20imslp-primus-epigramatum-lusitano-vicente).pdf?width=480","blurb":"First published composer of African descent.","bio":"Vicente Lusitano, born about 1520 and last recorded after 1561, was a Portuguese composer and music theorist, and the first published composer of African descent: he was described in his own lifetime as pardo. A small number of compositions survive along with some of his theoretical writing. He taught in several Italian cities and served for a period as a Catholic priest, before converting to Protestantism. His most famous hour came in Rome in June 1551, in a public debate with Nicola Vicentino over whether modern music made use of the ancient genera; the judges found for Lusitano. It was the sort of argument that mattered enormously then, setting the authority of antiquity against the practice of living musicians — and a Portuguese outsider carried the day in the city where such things were decided."},
  "gasparfernandes":{"wiki":"Gaspar Fernandes","blurb":"Villancicos in Nahuatl and Afro-Hispanic speech.","bio":"Gaspar Fernandes, born about 1566 and died in 1629, was a Portuguese-Mexican organist and composer who served the cathedrals of Santiago de Guatemala and then Puebla de los Ángeles in New Spain. His importance is not only that he brought European polyphony across the Atlantic but what he did once he was there: he wrote villancicos in Nahuatl and in the Afro-Hispanic dialect of the enslaved population, setting the languages actually spoken around him to composed music for the church. That makes his manuscripts a rare written trace of those voices inside the written art music of their own time. Juan Gutiérrez de Padilla came to Puebla as his assistant in 1622 and succeeded him as chapelmaster in 1629, so the tradition Fernandes established there passed directly into the next generation's hands."},
  "juandelienas":{"wiki":"Juan de Lienas","blurb":"A Mexico City composer known from marginalia.","bio":"Juan de Lienas, active in Mexico City in the 1620s to 1640s, was a composer, chapel master and conductor in the colonial church, probably of Indigenous descent. Almost everything known about him comes from notes written into the only two manuscripts that carry his music — the Códice del Convento del Carmen and, separately, the Newberry Choirbooks — and some of those notes are insults, aimed at his appearance and his character. They also identify him as a cacique, as an Indian and as a married man, any of which would have blocked his advance within the Church. Scholars disagree on the rest: most place him in the employ of a convent connected to the Mexico City cathedral, one suggests he was born in Spain, another that he also worked in Havana. He wrote polyphony for the convent of Santa Inés."},
  "donhernandofranco":{"wiki":"Hernando Franco","blurb":"A Nahua chapelmaster who entered the record.","bio":"Don Hernando Franco, active around 1550 to 1585 at Valladolid in Michoacán, was a Nahua chapelmaster who took the name of his Spanish teacher, Hernando Franco, as converts commonly did — which is why the two men are so easily confused, and why the record of the second is so much thinner than the record of the first. The music attributed to him in the Valladolid Codex is contested, and scholars have not settled whether the pieces are his own. What the codex records beyond argument is the practice itself: an Indigenous musician trained in the imported European art and put in charge of a chapel that performed it in New Spain. That fact survives the quarrel over attribution, and it belongs to the story of this music as much as any securely signed manuscript does."},
  "martinluther":{"wiki":"Martin Luther","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Lucas%20Cranach%20d.%C3%84.%20-%20Martin%20Luther%2C%201528%20(Veste%20Coburg).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Reformation's musical policy-maker.","bio":"Martin Luther, 1483 to 1546, was a German priest, theologian, professor and hymnwriter, the figure at the centre of the Protestant Reformation — and, for the history of music, the movement's policy-maker. He determined that the reformed church at Wittenberg would sing, in German, in tunes a congregation could actually hold, and he supplied hymn texts and melodies himself. That decision created the chorale, which became the raw material of German church music for centuries afterwards. He was no professional composer but a serious and well-informed listener: he admired Josquin des Prez above all others, praising him as the master of the notes, who made them do as he wished. For the practical musical work he turned to Johann Walter, who supplied the music for his liturgical reforms and for the 1524 Geystliche gesangk Buchleyn."},
  "johannwalter":{"wiki":"Johann Walter","blurb":"He gave Luther's reforms their music.","bio":"Johann Walter, 1496 to 1570, was a German Lutheran composer and poet of the Reformation period, based at Torgau. His role in the story is to have given Martin Luther's reforms their music. Luther decided that congregations should sing in German; Walter did the practical work, supplying settings for the reformed liturgy and assembling the 1524 Geystliche gesangk Buchleyn. Collaborations of this kind usually leave little trace, and this one is unusually well documented: a theologian with strong musical convictions working with a trained musician who could realise them. The chorale repertory that resulted became the shared inheritance of German Protestant music, sung by congregations and returned to by composers long afterwards. Walter is one of those figures whose influence is enormous and whose own name has stayed quiet."},
  "ludwigsenfl":{"wiki":"Ludwig Senfl","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ludwig%20senfl.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Isaac's pupil, who Germanised Flemish polyphony.","bio":"Ludwig Senfl, born about 1486 and died in 1543, was a Swiss composer active in Germany and the most celebrated pupil of Heinrich Isaac. He directed the music at the court of the Holy Roman Emperor Maximilian I, and worked at Munich. His relationship with Isaac went well beyond study: he was his assistant and then his editor, completing the great cycle Isaac left unfinished, the Choralis Constantinus — one of the clearest cases in the period of a pupil finishing a teacher's life work. Between them the two did much to establish the Franco-Flemish polyphonic style in Germany and, just as consequentially, they treated German folksong as material worth composing on, using existing popular melodies as models for polyphony. That habit points straight towards the German chorale tradition."},
  "hansleohassler":{"wiki":"Hans Leo Hassler","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Hans%20Leo%20Ha%C3%9Fler.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Composer of the future Passion Chorale tune.","bio":"Hans Leo Hassler, 1564 to 1612, was a German organist and composer working across the end of the Renaissance and the start of the Baroque, born in Nuremberg and dead in Frankfurt. Like a number of ambitious German musicians of his generation he went south to learn: he travelled to Venice in 1584 specifically to study with Andrea Gabrieli, and brought the Venetian manner back with him into German music. His most familiar legacy is a single melody — the tune later taken up as the Passion Chorale, which has been sung by far more people than have ever heard his name. His younger brother Jakob Hassler was a composer too, less known than he. Hassler's career is a clear illustration of how Italy taught Germany in these decades: one traveller, one apprenticeship and one borrowed tune at a time."},
  "michaelpraetorius":{"wiki":"Michael Praetorius","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Michael%20Praetorius.jpg?width=480","blurb":"He wrote down how music was actually made.","bio":"Michael Praetorius, 1571 to 1621, was a German organist, composer and theorist of music, based at Wolfenbüttel, and one of the most versatile musicians of his age. He was particularly significant in developing musical forms built on Protestant hymns, taking the chorale melodies of the reformed church and making large composed structures from them. He is valued equally for a book: Syntagma musicum, a survey of music as it was actually practised in his own time, and one of the most useful documents any period of music history has left behind. Theory books of that era usually preserve rules; his preserves practice, which is rarer and more helpful. Among the things it sets down for German readers is the Franco-Flemish inheritance in Germany, the tradition of Orlande de Lassus and the generation around him."},
  "leonhardlechner":{"wiki":"Leonhard Lechner","blurb":"Lassus's pupil, and a leading German choral voice.","bio":"Leonhard Lechner, born about 1553 and died in 1606, was a composer, kapellmeister, tenor and music editor, taught by Orlande de Lassus — he sang under Lassus at Munich and later edited his music, which is about as close to a master as a pupil comes. He signed himself Athesinus, after his origins in what is now South Tyrol, and ended his career in the service of the Stuttgart court. He is reckoned among the foremost German choral composers of the century's later decades, and though a good deal of his output is lost, a Passion, a song cycle and a quantity of strikingly expressive songs survive; the complete edition published by Bärenreiter runs to fourteen volumes. His career traces the path this generation took: a Franco-Flemish master's chapel first, then a German court of his own."},
  "johntaverner":{"wiki":"John Taverner","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/John%20Taverner%20d%201545.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The mass that spawned the In Nomine.","bio":"John Taverner, born around 1490 and active in Oxford until his death in 1545, was an English composer and organist counted among the most important musicians of his era. His reputation rests on large-scale settings of the mass: Missa Gloria tibi Trinitas above all, with The Western Wynde Mass and Missa Corona Spinea also regarded as masterworks. A single passage from Gloria tibi Trinitas, the In Nomine section, was lifted out by later English musicians and turned into a form of its own. Generations wrote consort pieces on that fragment, beginning with Thomas Tallis, until the In Nomine became the central form of the English consort tradition. That one borrowed phrase from a Latin mass should seed a whole instrumental repertory is among the stranger routes to lasting influence in English music, and it makes Taverner the founder of a tradition that grew up after him."},
  "thomastallis":{"wiki":"Thomas Tallis","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Thomas%20Tallis.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Forty parts at once, and Byrd's master.","bio":"Thomas Tallis, born about 1505 and serving in the Chapel Royal until his death in 1585, was an English composer of the High Renaissance whose music is overwhelmingly vocal and who sits at the centre of every anthology of English choral writing. His best-known work, Spem in alium, is laid out in forty independent parts. He took from John Taverner the In Nomine, the fragment of mass that became the English consort tradition's central form, and he passed on considerably more than he received: William Byrd was his pupil, then his colleague, and finally his partner when Elizabeth I granted the two men a twenty-one-year monopoly on printed music in 1575. That patent did as much as any single score to secure English polyphony in print. Tallis worked through the whole span of the Tudor century and is honoured for an original voice that anthologies have never let go."},
  "williambyrd":{"wiki":"William Byrd","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/William%20Byrd.jpg?width=480","blurb":"A recusant Catholic at Elizabeth's chapel.","bio":"William Byrd, born around 1540 and a member of the Chapel Royal until his death in 1623, is reckoned one of the greatest composers the Renaissance produced and, with John Dunstaple and Henry Purcell, one of the most important England has given music. His influence reached composers at home and on the Continent. He was Thomas Tallis's pupil, then his colleague and joint holder of the printing monopoly the two were granted together. He was also a recusant Catholic who went on writing for both rites, and he kept his place at the heart of Elizabeth I's Chapel Royal because the queen protected him there, valuing the musician above the confession. As a teacher he shaped the generation after him: Thomas Morley called him 'my loving master' in print, and Thomas Tomkins dedicated a madrigal to 'my ancient and much reverenced master'."},
  "thomasmorley":{"wiki":"Thomas Morley","blurb":"Brought the Italian madrigal into English music.","bio":"Thomas Morley, born in 1557 and working in London until his death in 1602, was an English composer, organist, singer and theorist, and the foremost member of the English Madrigal School. The New Grove Dictionary gives him the chief credit for importing the Italian manner and touching off the short, dazzling madrigal boom that is one of the brightest passages in English music. He learned his craft from William Byrd, whom he called 'my loving master' in print, and he taught the rest of the country through A Plaine and Easie Introduction to Practicall Musicke, an accessible treatise that carried the practice of his generation to amateur singers and composers. A parish neighbour of William Shakespeare in Bishopsgate, Morley set It was a lover and his lass for As You Like It, a song by a composer the playwright could actually have met."},
  "johndowland":{"wiki":"John Dowland","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/John%20Dowland.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Melancholy made into the lute song.","bio":"John Dowland, born in 1563 and dividing his career between London and Denmark before his death in 1626, was an English composer, lutenist and singer who brought the lute song to its height. He is remembered above all for songs of studied melancholy: 'Flow my tears', 'Come again', 'I saw my Lady weepe', 'Come, heavy sleep', 'Now o now I needs must part', 'In darkness let me dwell'. In these a singer and a lute hold a conversation of unusual intimacy, and the sadness is worked out in detail rather than merely struck as a pose. The instrumental music has had a major revival, and since the twentieth-century rediscovery of early music it has been continuous repertoire for lutenists and guitarists. No master or pupil ties him into this map; his line of descent runs instead through four centuries of players who keep returning to the songs."},
  "thomasweelkes":{"wiki":"Thomas Weelkes","blurb":"Chichester's organist of madrigals and anthems.","bio":"Thomas Weelkes, born in 1576 and dying in 1623, was an English composer and organist whose working life was spent in college and cathedral choirs. In 1598 he took the organist's post at Winchester College, moving on to Chichester Cathedral. His output is chiefly vocal, and it falls into the two halves a Jacobean church musician's work normally took: madrigals for the secular printed market, and anthems and services for the liturgy he played for every day. No teacher or pupil is recorded for him in this map, and no patron either; his influence travelled through the printed madrigal collections of his generation rather than through a classroom, and his name has outlasted almost every other musician of the English cathedrals and college chapels he served."},
  "orlandogibbons":{"wiki":"Orlando Gibbons","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Orlando%20Gibbons.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The last master of the virginalists.","bio":"Orlando Gibbons, born in 1583 and serving in the Chapel Royal until his death in 1625, was an English keyboard player and composer, one of the last masters of both the English Virginalist School and the English Madrigal School. He was the outstanding figure in a musical family dynasty and, by the 1610s, England's leading composer and organist. His untimely death in 1625 cut that career short, so his output is smaller than that of contemporaries such as the elder William Byrd; even so he contributed substantially to most of the genres open to him, keyboard music and madrigals above all. Musicologists regularly place him at the hinge between the Renaissance and the Baroque, which makes him less a late flowering of the old style than the point where English music turns."},
  "johnbull":{"wiki":"John Bull (composer)","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/John%20Bull%20(composer).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Keyboard virtuosity, and exile in Antwerp.","bio":"John Bull, born about 1562 and working in London before he ended his life in Antwerp in 1628, was an English composer, organist and virginalist who also built organs. He ranked among the most renowned keyboard performers the virginalist school produced, and nearly everything he wrote was for that medium: virtuoso writing that shows how far English players had got. He taught as well, an unusual breadth of competence for one musician. In 1613 he left England for the Low Countries, and there he found in Jan Pieterszoon Sweelinck a counterpart rather than a rival. The two exchanged themes and each wrote on the other's, one of the clearest documented meetings between the English and Dutch keyboard traditions. Bull's final years were spent abroad, carrying the virginalists' manner into a Continental setting where it had not been heard at first hand."},
  "thomascampion":{"wiki":"Thomas Campion","blurb":"Poet and composer of his own songs.","bio":"Thomas Campion, born in 1567 and dying in 1620, was an English poet, physician and composer, and one of the very few men of his age to supply both halves of a song. London-born and Cambridge-educated, he read law at Gray's Inn; the verse and the music ran alongside all of it. He wrote over a hundred songs for voice and lute, masques to be danced, and a technical treatise on music of real authority, so that he stands in three histories at once: of English poetry, of the lute song, and of theory. There Is a Garden in Her Face is his best-remembered poem. Because he set his own texts, he could match word to note at the moment of invention rather than afterwards, which is why his songs sit so comfortably in the voice."},
  "giovannidebardi":{"wiki":"Giovanni de' Bardi","blurb":"The house where opera was argued into being.","bio":"Giovanni de' Bardi, Count of Vernio, born in 1534 and dying in Florence in 1612, was an Italian literary critic, poet, soldier and composer, and the nobleman in whose house the Florentine Camerata met. Through the 1570s and 1580s his rooms were the setting for the arguments out of which opera came: Vincenzo Galilei, Giulio Caccini and Jacopo Peri were all among the company that gathered there. Bardi's contribution was not a score but a room, a standard of debate and the humanist learning to set the terms of it. He gave a loose group of theorists, singers and poets somewhere to become a movement, and the ideas that changed European music were formed in his house rather than in any one man's study, which makes him among the most consequential hosts in musical history."},
  "vincenzogalilei":{"wiki":"Vincenzo Galilei","blurb":"Attacked counterpoint, and fathered Galileo.","bio":"Vincenzo Galilei, born about 1520 and dying in Florence in 1591, was an Italian lutenist, music theorist and composer, and one of the most consequential arguers in the history of music. Active in Florentine musical life in the late Renaissance, he met with the Camerata at Giovanni de' Bardi's house through the 1570s and 80s, and his Dialogo mounted the attack on counterpoint that Giulio Caccini's Le nuove musiche would then put into practice: the case against many-voiced polyphony and for a single voice carrying the words. Among his children were Galileo Galilei, the astronomer and physicist, and Michelagnolo, a lute virtuoso who composed as well; and his own experiments on string tension count among the first quantitative acoustics anyone attempted, a family habit of measurement one generation early. His part in the upheaval that opens the Baroque was substantial."},
  "jacopoperi":{"wiki":"Jacopo Peri","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jacopo%20Peri%201.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Wrote the first opera.","bio":"Jacopo Peri, born in 1561 and dying in Florence in 1633, was an Italian composer, singer and instrumentalist who stands at the exact join between Renaissance and Baroque. He wrote Dafne in 1598, considered the first opera and now mostly lost, and Euridice in 1600, the earliest opera to survive. Both had librettos by the poet Ottavio Rinuccini, which makes their partnership the first of its kind. Peri belonged to the Camerata that met in Giovanni de' Bardi's house, and Euridice was produced for the Medici wedding festivities of 1600 under Ferdinando I de' Medici, patronage without which the experiment would have had no stage. The same occasion made him enemies: Giulio Caccini had his own settings substituted into the performance and rushed a rival version into print first, and Emilio de' Cavalieri left Florence in fury over the whole affair."},
  "giuliocaccini":{"wiki":"Giulio Caccini","blurb":"The manifesto of the new singing.","bio":"Giulio Caccini, born in 1551 and dying in Florence in 1618, was an Italian composer, singer, teacher, instrumentalist and writer, and one of the founders of opera and of the new Baroque style. His collection Le nuove musiche is the manifesto of the new singing: it takes Vincenzo Galilei's attack on counterpoint, argued in the Camerata that met at Giovanni de' Bardi's house, and turns it into a practical method for the solo singer. He was also a formidable competitor. At the Medici festivities of 1600 he had his own settings substituted into Jacopo Peri's Euridice and rushed his version into print ahead of Peri's, a manoeuvre that has shadowed both reputations since. He taught his daughter Francesca himself, and she became the highest-paid musician at the Medici court; another daughter, Settimia, sang."},
  "emiliodecavalieri":{"wiki":"Emilio de' Cavalieri","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Roma%2C%20Aracoeli%2C%20Cappella%20de'%20Cavalieri%20-%20marzo%202005%20-%20Foto%20Giovanni%20Dall'Orto.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Wrote what is called the first oratorio.","bio":"Emilio de' Cavalieri, born about 1550 and dying in 1602, worked in Rome and Florence as a composer, organist, producer, choreographer, dancer and diplomat, a range that says something about how music was made at a princely court, where mounting a spectacle and carrying a negotiation could fall to the same man. A member of the Roman School, he was among the important early composers of monody, and his Rappresentatione di Anima et di Corpo is usually counted the first oratorio. With other musicians at work in Rome, Florence and Venice, he helped define the start of the musical Baroque. In Florence he and Jacopo Peri were rivals for precedence in the new dramatic music, and Cavalieri left the city in fury over the 1600 festivities: a quarrel about who had got there first, at the moment when being first mattered enormously."},
  "francescacaccini":{"wiki":"Francesca Caccini","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Francesca%20Caccini.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The first opera by a woman.","bio":"Francesca Caccini, born in 1587 and last recorded after 1641, was an Italian singer, lutenist, poet and composer, and a teacher of music in the early Baroque, known to Florentines as La Cecchina. She was the daughter of Giulio Caccini and was trained by him, and she went on to become the highest-paid musician at the Medici court, a fact worth stating plainly because it measures her standing rather than her novelty. La liberazione di Ruggiero, the one stage work of hers that survives, is generally reckoned the earliest opera written by a woman. She served a female patron whose cultural and political programme her music helped to advance, which is how court music generally worked: the composer's art and the patron's argument moved together. Much of her output has not survived, and her reputation rests on La liberazione and on the position she held."},
  "ottaviorinuccini":{"wiki":"Ottavio Rinuccini","blurb":"The first opera librettist.","bio":"Ottavio Rinuccini, born in 1562 and dying in Florence in 1621, was an Italian poet and courtier who became the first opera librettist. Collaborating with Jacopo Peri, he wrote the text of Dafne, the first opera, and then of Euridice, the second, so that between them the two men invented not only a genre but the division of labour that has governed it since: poet and composer answerable to one another. Rinuccini's part of the problem was to find a verse that could be sung continuously and still be heard as speech, which no poet before him had been asked to do. He was a courtier first, and these works were made for Florentine court occasions at the turn of the seventeenth century. Every librettist since has worked in a role he was the first to occupy."},
  "giovannimariaartusi":{"wiki":"Giovanni Artusi","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Giovanni%20Maria%20Artusi.png?width=480","blurb":"The attack that provoked the seconda pratica.","bio":"Giovanni Maria Artusi, born about 1540 and dying in Bologna in 1613, was an Italian music theorist, composer, writer and Catholic priest, and he owes his place in this map to an argument he lost. In 1600 he published an attack on the imperfections of modern music, taking as his target the manner in which Claudio Monteverdi was writing. Monteverdi answered by naming a second practice, a seconda pratica in which the words command the harmony rather than the reverse, and that reply became the manifesto of a whole era. Artusi's conservatism was not stupidity: he was defending a coherent and finely worked system of counterpoint against music that appeared to break it deliberately. Without a critic so clear about what was being broken, the break itself might never have been stated so clearly either."},
  "francescocavalli":{"wiki":"Francesco Cavalli","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Francesco%20Cavalli.png?width=480","blurb":"Made opera a paying public art.","bio":"Francesco Cavalli, born in 1602 and dying in Venice in 1676, was a Venetian composer, organist and singer who sang under Claudio Monteverdi at St Mark's from boyhood and succeeded his teacher as the leading opera composer of the mid-seventeenth century. What he did with that inheritance mattered more than the succession itself: Monteverdi's new style had belonged to courts and chapels, and Cavalli took it into the public theatres, writing more than thirty operas, almost all of them premiered in Venice, for audiences who paid at the door. Ormindo of 1644, Giasone of 1649 and La Calisto of 1651 are the best known. He was a central figure in Venetian musical life and a teacher of consequence: Barbara Strozzi studied with him, and Antonio Cesti, who sang in Giasone, carried Venetian opera into its second generation."},
  "antoniocesti":{"wiki":"Antonio Cesti","blurb":"Venetian opera's second generation, in Vienna.","bio":"Antonio Cesti, born in 1623 and dying in 1669, was an Italian composer, tenor and organist who worked between Venice and Vienna and was rated in his own day the outstanding Italian musician of his generation. He came up as a singer inside the tradition he then extended: he sang in Francesco Cavalli's Giasone, learning the Venetian manner from within it, and took that manner into its second generation. Il pomo d'oro is the work his name is most often attached to, a court spectacle on the largest scale, of a kind that Venetian commercial opera had made imaginable but that only an imperial establishment could stage. His career shows the two paths open to an opera composer of his time, the paying public theatre and the princely court, and Cesti took both. He died in 1669 with his reputation at its height."},
  "barbarastrozzi":{"wiki":"Barbara Strozzi","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bernardo%20Strozzi%20001.jpg?width=480","blurb":"More secular music in print than her contemporaries.","bio":"Barbara Strozzi, born in 1619 and dying in Venice in 1677, was an Italian singer and composer of the middle Baroque. In her lifetime eight volumes of her music went to press, and no composer of the century put as much secular vocal writing into print. She managed it without either of the supports her contemporaries relied on: no church appointment and no consistent patronage from the nobility. The printed record is therefore her career, deliberately built. She studied with Francesco Cavalli in Venice, which put her inside the line of the new dramatic manner rather than at its edge. Her father, Giulio Strozzi, probably her natural rather than adoptive parent, wrote texts for her to set and founded an academy largely to give her somewhere to be heard. A teacher at the top of Venetian opera and a father who built her an audience: she used both."},
  "stefanolandi":{"wiki":"Stefano Landi","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Stefano%20Landi.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The first opera on a historical subject.","bio":"Stefano Landi, born in 1587 and dying in Rome in 1639, was an Italian musician of the early Baroque Roman School, a composer and teacher and a singer and organist besides. He was an influential figure in opera's first decades, and Il Sant'Alessio of 1632 is the earliest opera to take a historical subject, a departure worth noticing: the genre had begun with gods and myths, and Landi turned it towards a life that had actually been lived. His career belongs to Rome rather than to the commercial opera houses, in the orbit of the church and the great households. No teacher or pupil of his is recorded here, though teaching was part of the work, as were singing and the organ loft; his was a whole musician's career in one city."},
  "luigirossi":{"wiki":"Luigi Rossi","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Luigi-Rossi%201597-1653.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Italian song, from Rome to Paris.","bio":"Luigi Rossi, born about 1597 at Torremaggiore, a village near Foggia in the old kingdom of Naples, and dying in 1653, was an Italian Baroque composer, singer and organist who worked in Rome and then Paris. He went to Naples young and studied music with Jean de Macque, the Franco-Flemish organist at the Santa Casa dell'Annunziata, who also served the Spanish viceroy as maestro di cappella: a Netherlander teaching an Italian in a Spanish-run city, which is a fair picture of how style travelled in the period. Rossi passed afterwards into the household of the Caetani, dukes of Traetta. His move to Paris put Italian vocal music in front of a French court at the moment when France was working out what its own opera would be, and the traffic he was part of would run in both directions for the rest of the century."},
  "giacomocarissimi":{"wiki":"Giacomo Carissimi","blurb":"Invented the Latin oratorio; taught Charpentier.","bio":"Giacomo Carissimi, born in 1605 and dying in Rome in 1674, was an Italian composer and teacher of music, and among the Roman School's most celebrated masters. He fixed the features that define the Latin oratorio, sacred drama sung without staging, and wrote masses, motets and cantatas in great quantity. His influence travelled by two routes at once. His music circulated very widely; and his pupils carried the manner out of Italy, Kerll into Germany and Marc-Antoine Charpentier into France, the latter spending three years in Rome studying with him. Kerll and Charpentier are the visible proof of how far one Roman teacher reached into northern European music, and the reason Carissimi belongs in a map of influence rather than only in a catalogue of works."},
  "girolamofrescobaldi":{"wiki":"Girolamo Frescobaldi","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Girolamo%20Frescobaldi%20(1583-1643)%2C%20engraving%20by%20Claude%20Mellan%20(1619).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The toccata, and St Peter's organ loft.","bio":"Girolamo Alessandro Frescobaldi, born in the Duchy of Ferrara in 1583 and dying in 1643, was an Italian composer and a virtuoso of the keyboard, and the most important writer of keyboard music between the late Renaissance and the early Baroque. A child prodigy, he studied with Luzzasco Luzzaschi in Ferrara and took a good deal from others as well, among them Ascanio Mayone, Giovanni Maria Trabaci and Claudio Merulo. He was appointed organist of St Peter's Basilica in July 1608, held the post until 1628, and returned to it from 1634 until his death, so the central organ of Catholic Europe was under his hands for most of a working life. The toccata as he shaped it became the model for the century that followed. Johann Jacob Froberger was sent from Vienna to Rome to study with him between 1637 and 1641, and carried that manner north."},
  "arcangelocorelli":{"wiki":"Arcangelo Corelli","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Arcangelo%20Corelli%2C%20portrait%20by%20Hugh%20Howard%20(1697)%20-%202.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Europe's model for sonata and concerto.","bio":"Arcangelo Corelli, born in 1653 and dying in Rome in 1713, was an Italian violinist and composer whose published sets gave Europe its models for the trio sonata and the concerto grosso. His music was decisive in three ways at once: it established the pre-eminence of the violin, it fixed the shapes of sonata and concerto, and it is where modern tonality and functional harmony first cohere. He lived in the Cancelleria under Cardinal Pietro Ottoboni's patronage and led the cardinal's Monday concerts for two decades, a stable base few musicians of the century enjoyed. From there the influence radiated outward: Francesco Geminiani took his manner to London and wrote the treatise that transmitted it, Pietro Locatelli studied with him in Rome, Antonio Vivaldi inherited the concerto grosso and broke it open, and in 1707 Corelli led the orchestra for Handel's Roman oratorios."},
  "giuseppetorelli":{"wiki":"Giuseppe Torelli","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Giuseppe%20Torelli.png?width=480","blurb":"Bologna's early form of the solo concerto.","bio":"Giuseppe Torelli, born in 1658 and dying in Bologna in 1709, was an Italian violinist, violist, composer and teacher of the middle Baroque. His importance is structural rather than famous. Working in Bologna, one of the great instrumental centres of the period, he did much of the early work on the solo concerto, setting a single violin against the band in the alternation of tutti and solo that would organise the form for a century. Antonio Vivaldi then took the Bolognese solo concerto and standardised it, which is the kind of debt that leaves a composer remembered as a precursor rather than a master. Torelli taught as well as played, and the sheer volume of concerto writing coming out of Bologna in his years there is part of why Italy, rather than anywhere else, settled the shape of orchestral music."},
  "tomasoalbinoni":{"wiki":"Tomaso Albinoni","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Tomaso%20Albinoni.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Famous for an Adagio he didn't write.","bio":"Tomaso Giovanni Albinoni, born in Venice in 1671 and dying in 1751, was an Italian composer of the Baroque whose output ran across operas, concertos, sinfonias, solo cantatas and sonatas for anything from one to six instruments. In his own day he was famous as a man of the theatre; today it is the instrumental music, and especially the concertos, that keeps his name in circulation. He also carries one of the odder posthumous fates in music. The Adagio in G minor universally attributed to him was for the most part written by Remo Giazotto, a twentieth-century musicologist who catalogued Albinoni's works, so that Giazotto's most-played composition appears under another man's name. The real Albinoni, a Venetian writing for the concerto and opera market of his own city, is a good deal more interesting than the piece he is remembered for."},
  "antoniovivaldi":{"wiki":"Antonio Vivaldi","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Onbekend%20-%20Antonio%20Vivaldi%20-%20Bologna%20Museo%20Internationale%20e%20biblioteca%20della%20musica%20di%20bologna%2028-04-2012%209-21-050.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Five hundred concertos for the Pietà girls.","bio":"Antonio Lucio Vivaldi, born in Venice in 1678 and dying in 1741, was an Italian composer, virtuoso violinist, impresario and Catholic priest, regarded as one of the greatest figures of the Baroque. He wrote some five hundred concertos, most of them for the girls of the Pietà, the Venetian orphanage whose musicians he trained: an institution, not a court, is what this enormous body of work was made for. He took the concerto grosso from Arcangelo Corelli and broke it open, took the Bolognese solo concerto from Giuseppe Torelli and standardised it, and so consolidated the emerging form into an idiom the whole of Europe adopted. What he did with orchestration, violin technique and programmatic music produced imitators and admirers across the Continent in his lifetime. Johann Sebastian Bach transcribed at least nine of his concertos for keyboard and rebuilt his own concerto writing around them."},
  "alessandroscarlatti":{"wiki":"Alessandro Scarlatti","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Alessandro%20Scarlatti.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Naples' opera seria, and the symphony's ancestor.","bio":"Alessandro Scarlatti, born in 1660 and dying in Naples in 1725, was an Italian Baroque composer whose operas and chamber cantatas made his name, and the figure usually named as the Neapolitan School of opera's leading representative. The opera seria he shaped there became the dominant model for serious opera, and one of its by-products outlived the operas themselves: the sinfonia avanti l'opera, the instrumental piece played before the curtain went up, is the ancestor of the concert symphony. He worked in Rome as well, under the patronage of Cardinal Pietro Ottoboni, and he taught, though not always easily; Domenico Zipoli's period of study with him in Naples was brief and reportedly stormy. His son Domenico he placed in post after post, until the son escaped to Iberia and became something the father had not arranged for him."},
  "domenicoscarlatti":{"wiki":"Domenico Scarlatti","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Retrato%20de%20Domenico%20Scarlatti.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The 555 sonatas, written for a queen.","bio":"Giuseppe Domenico Scarlatti, born in 1685 and dying in Madrid in 1757, was an Italian composer, classified chronologically with the Baroque though his music did much to open the way to the Classical style. Like his father Alessandro he composed in many forms, yet he is known today for the 555 keyboard sonatas. Those are inseparable from a single relationship: Maria Barbara of Portugal was his pupil from her childhood in Lisbon and then, as Queen of Spain, his employer, and the sonatas were written for her, an unusually exact case of a repertory shaped by the hands it was meant for. Much of his life was passed serving the royal families of Portugal and Spain, and the Iberian move was also an escape. His father had placed him in post after post; in Madrid he was out of reach and free to write as he liked."},
  "francescogeminiani":{"wiki":"Francesco Geminiani","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Francesco%20Geminiani.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Carried Corelli's manner to London.","bio":"Francesco Xaverio Geminiani, born in 1687 and dying in 1762, was an Italian violinist, composer and theorist of music who made his career in London. He had studied with Arcangelo Corelli, and his life's work was transmission: he took his master's manner to England and wrote the treatise that codified it, so that a way of playing the violin learned at first hand became teachable anywhere. His contemporaries ranked him almost as a deity of music, level with Handel and with Corelli; posterity has mostly let him drop. That is a common fate for musicians whose achievement lies in carrying a practice rather than inventing one. The playing style he documented has outlived the pieces he wrote in it, and his treatise remains where players go to find out how Corelli's music was meant to sound."},
  "pietrolocatelli":{"wiki":"Pietro Locatelli","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Portret%20van%20Pietro%20Antonio%20Locatelli%2C%20RP-P-1910-1512.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Pushed the violin to its limits.","bio":"Pietro Antonio Locatelli, born in 1695 and dying in Amsterdam in 1764, was an Italian violinist and composer of the Baroque who studied with Arcangelo Corelli in Rome and then went about as far in the opposite direction as the instrument allowed. Where Corelli's writing is measured and singable, Locatelli's reaches for the violin's outer technical limits, until the difficulty itself becomes the subject of the music. He settled in the Dutch Republic, and spent his later decades in Amsterdam, a long way from the Rome where he had trained. The habit he established, of a composer-performer writing music at the very edge of what a player can manage, connects the Roman school he came from to the virtuoso tradition of the century after him."},
  "giuseppetartini":{"wiki":"Giuseppe Tartini","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Giuseppe%20Tartini%20(Tartini%20House).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Devil's Trill, and a violin school.","bio":"Giuseppe Tartini, born at Pirano in the Venetian Republic in 1692 and dying in Padua in 1770, was an Italian violinist and composer of the Baroque, and one of the period's great teachers. He was prolific, with over a hundred works for the violin and the majority of them concertos, and he is best remembered for the Violin Sonata in G minor, the Devil's Trill, whose difficulty and whose legend have together kept it in the repertoire. From Padua he ran a famous violin school, which is why his name recurs in so many players' pedigrees. He was a music theorist as well, and that combination of player, composer, teacher and writer is precisely what a leading Italian violinist of the eighteenth century was expected to be."},
  "heinrichignazfranzbiber":{"wiki":"Heinrich Ignaz Franz Biber","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/H.I.F.Biber.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Scordatura, and the Rosary Sonatas.","bio":"Heinrich Ignaz Franz von Biber, born in 1644 and dying in Salzburg in 1704, was a Czech-Austrian violinist and composer of the middle Baroque, and the author of the most advanced violin writing of his century. The Rosary Sonatas are his monument: a cycle on the rosary in which the instrument is retuned between movements, the scordatura altering sound and fingering together so that the player's hands do one thing while the strings sound another. He worked in Graz and at Kroměříž before leaving his employer, the Prince-Bishop Karl Liechtenstein-Kastelkorn, without permission, and settled in Salzburg, where he stayed until he died. He published much of his music but seems seldom, if ever, to have given concert tours. What he asked of a violinist went beyond anything else being demanded in his lifetime."},
  "antoniocaldara":{"wiki":"Antonio Caldara","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Antonio%20Caldara%20(I-Bc%2C%20B%2011881%2C%20B%2039202).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Venetian cellist who served imperial Vienna.","bio":"Antonio Caldara, born about 1670 in the Republic of Venice and dying in 1736, was an Italian Baroque composer who made his career in Vienna. He was a cellist and a singer as well as a composer, and he worked as a chapelmaster, which was the standard route for an Italian musician of his generation: practical skill on an instrument and in a choir led to charge of an entire musical establishment. Vienna in his years there drew Italians in numbers, and their arrival is much of the reason Austrian court music of the early eighteenth century sounds Italian. Caldara was one of those who took the style north, and his working life belongs as much to Vienna as to the Venice he came from."},
  "jeanbaptistelully":{"wiki":"Jean-Baptiste Lully","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jean-Baptiste%20Lully.jpeg?width=480","blurb":"Louis XIV's monopoly on French opera.","bio":"Jean-Baptiste Lully, born in Florence in 1632 and dying in 1687, was a composer, dancer and instrumentalist, Italian by birth and French by adoption, who took French nationality in 1661 and became a master of the French Baroque style. Almost his whole career was spent at Louis XIV's court. He danced in the king's ballets before he governed the king's music, and was then granted a legal monopoly over opera in France, patronage so complete that it defined French music partly by what it shut out, keeping Marc-Antoine Charpentier off the operatic stage for most of his life. With Molière, a close friend, he made ten comédies-ballets, Le Bourgeois gentilhomme among them, before a bitter break over Psyché. The librettist Philippe Quinault supplied the texts of eleven tragédies en musique. Marin Marais studied composition with him, and Charles II sent Pelham Humfrey to France to learn the style."},
  "marcantoinecharpentier":{"wiki":"Marc-Antoine Charpentier","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/MA%20Charpentier%20II.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Sacred riches, kept off the stage.","bio":"Marc-Antoine Charpentier, born in 1643 and dying in Paris in 1704, was a French Baroque composer of the reign of Louis XIV whose career was shaped as much by what he was prevented from writing as by what he wrote. He spent three years in Rome studying with Giacomo Carissimi, and brought the Italian way of setting sacred Latin dramatically back into French music. What he could not do was write opera: Jean-Baptiste Lully's royal monopoly kept him off the operatic stage for most of his career, and his dramatic instincts went into sacred music of extraordinary richness instead. The best-known fragment of it is the prelude to his Te Deum H.146, the Marche en rondeau, which since 1954 has served as the fanfare before European Broadcasting Union transmissions and is now among the most-heard music of the Baroque."},
  "marinmarais":{"wiki":"Marin Marais","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Marin-marais.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Gave the viol its central repertory.","bio":"Marin Marais, born in 1656 and dying in 1728, was a French viol player and composer of the middle Baroque who gave the instrument its central repertory. He learned composition from Jean-Baptiste Lully, often conducted his operas and played in his opera orchestra, and he took the bass viol from Monsieur de Sainte-Colombe in six months of lessons that, by legend and by Titon du Tillet's account, Sainte-Colombe ended when the pupil overtook the master. He joined the royal musicians at Versailles in 1676 and was named ordinaire de la chambre du roy pour la viole in 1679, holding that office until 1725. Nearly fifty years in one post is its own kind of statement: the whole French flowering of the viol happened inside a single man's tenure at court."},
  "elisabethjacquetdelaguerre":{"wiki":"Élisabeth Jacquet de La Guerre","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Elisabeth%20Jacquet%20de%20La%20Guerre-full.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Versailles prodigy; first French woman opera composer.","bio":"Élisabeth Claude Jacquet de La Guerre, born in 1665 and dying in Paris in 1729, was a French harpsichordist and composer, and one of the significant figures of the French Baroque. Louis XIV took her into royal protection as a child prodigy and kept her there, so her career was shaped throughout by royal patronage and by the musical culture of Versailles. She was the first French woman to write an opera, and among the first French composers of either sex to write cantatas, a genre she helped plant in France; she was also central to the development of the French keyboard tradition. Her music blends French stylistic habits with Italian elements, particularly in the vocal and instrumental works. She was among the first women in France to win recognition as a composer, with her works widely performed and printed in her lifetime."},
  "francoiscouperin":{"wiki":"François Couperin","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fran%C3%A7ois%20Couperin.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Couperin le Grand, reconciler of tastes.","bio":"François Couperin, born in 1668 and dying in Paris in 1733, was a French Baroque composer, organist and harpsichordist, called Couperin le Grand to set him apart from the rest of a musically crowded family. His harpsichord music is gathered into ordres, and Les Goûts-réunis announces his central project in its title: the reuniting of French and Italian taste at a time when the two were treated as opposed camps. He was also exact about ornament in a way few of his contemporaries were, writing down what other composers left to the player. That precision is one reason he mattered abroad. Johann Sebastian Bach copied out his music and adopted the French ornament signs wholesale, so the ornaments in Bach's own scores are still read through the system Couperin had settled in Paris."},
  "jeanphilipperameau":{"wiki":"Jean-Philippe Rameau","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Attribu%C3%A9%20%C3%A0%20Joseph%20Aved%2C%20Portrait%20de%20Jean-Philippe%20Rameau%20(vers%201728)%20-%20001.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Wrote the theory, then fought the Querelle.","bio":"Jean-Philippe Rameau, born in 1683 and dying in Paris in 1764, was a French composer and theorist of music in the late Baroque, and the rare figure who first explained how harmony worked and then wrote the music to demonstrate it. His Traité de l'harmonie is the ancestor of the way chords are still taught. He went on to displace Jean-Baptiste Lully at the head of French opera, and ranks with François Couperin as the leading French composer for the harpsichord of his time. Alexandre Le Riche de La Pouplinière housed him for over twenty years and kept a private orchestra for him, patronage that let him work at scale outside the court. He wrote several stage works with Voltaire in a long, quarrelsome working friendship, and fought the Querelle des Bouffons against Jean-Jacques Rousseau, who held French unsingable and Rameau's theory a fraud."},
  "jeanferyrebel":{"wiki":"Jean-Féry Rebel","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jean%20Ferry%20Rebel.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Opened a work with notated chaos.","bio":"Jean-Féry Rebel, born in 1666 and dying in 1747, was an innovative French Baroque composer, violinist and harpsichordist working in Paris. His claim on attention is one startling gesture: Les Élémens opens with a representation of chaos, written out as a sustained dissonance rather than left to a player's improvisation. That it is notated matters as much as that it is harsh; the disorder is fixed on the page, exactly as he wanted it, at a moment when nothing in French music sounded like that. Beyond that one stroke the record leaves him with his instruments, the violin and the harpsichord, and a single word: innovative. But the opening of Les Élémens is why his name survives, and a reminder that the Baroque had room for experiments as radical as any attempted since."},
  "louisxiv":{"wiki":"Louis XIV","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Louis%20XIV%20of%20France.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Made music an instrument of the state.","bio":"Louis XIV, King of France from 1643 until his death in 1715, was the patron who turned music into an instrument of the state. Styling himself the Sun King, he presided over a patronage of the arts at Versailles that defined the French Baroque, and a reign of seventy-two years, longer than any other sovereign monarch's, gave that project time to harden from a fashion into an institution. As a young man he danced in Jean-Baptiste Lully's ballets; he then handed Lully a legal monopoly over opera in France, a single decision that shaped a century of French music by settling who was permitted to write it. He also took Élisabeth Jacquet de La Guerre into royal protection as a child prodigy and kept her there. He was no composer. What he did was determine, personally and absolutely, whose music was heard."},
  "heinrichschutz":{"wiki":"Heinrich Schütz","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Schutz.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Brought Venice into German music.","bio":"Heinrich Schütz, born in 1585 and dying in Dresden in 1672, was a German composer and organist, counted the foremost German musician of the seventeenth century and the greatest before Johann Sebastian Bach. His achievement was a transplant. He studied with Giovanni Gabrieli in Venice from 1609 until Gabrieli's death in 1612 and was left his teacher's ring, then returned to Venice in 1628 specifically to study Claudio Monteverdi's new dramatic manner at its source. What he brought home was the Italian style, which he then carried onward in German: the bulk of what survives is Lutheran church music, made above all for the Electoral Chapel in Dresden. Dafne, performed at Torgau in 1627, is traditionally counted the first German opera; its music is lost, along with nearly all his ceremonial and theatrical scores. Over five hundred works survive nonetheless."},
  "johannjacobfroberger":{"wiki":"Johann Jakob Froberger","blurb":"Frescobaldi's pupil, and the keyboard suite.","bio":"Johann Jakob Froberger, born in the Duchy of Württemberg in 1616 and dying in 1667, was a German composer of the Baroque, an organist and a keyboard virtuoso, and among the most widely travelled musicians of his century. Based in Vienna, he was sent to Rome to study with Girolamo Frescobaldi between 1637 and 1641, and the Italian keyboard manner he learned there he carried back north and outward. He did much to develop the suite of dances as a keyboard form, and his harpsichord writing is idiomatic and openly programmatic, music about events written for an instrument he understood completely. His visits to Paris are the origin of the French unmeasured prelude that Louis Couperin and the harpsichordists after him made their own. Few composers sit so exactly at a crossroads: Frescobaldi's Rome behind him, the German suite and the French prelude ahead."},
  "dieterichbuxtehude":{"wiki":"Dieterich Buxtehude","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dieterich%20Buxtehude.png?width=480","blurb":"The organ master Bach walked to hear.","bio":"Dieterich Buxtehude, born around 1637 and organist at Lübeck until his death in 1707, was a Danish-German church musician whose work is the model of the north German organ school. He composed in a wide range of vocal and instrumental idioms, and his Abendmusiken, the evening concerts he mounted at Lübeck, made the city a place younger musicians travelled to reach. In 1703 Handel and Mattheson came to look over his post and both declined it, since it came with his daughter. Two years later the young Johann Sebastian Bach walked some 250 miles north to hear him and overstayed his leave by three months. That single fact says most of what needs saying about his standing among the players of the next generation, and he ranks now among the century's most important composers."},
  "johannpachelbel":{"wiki":"Johann Pachelbel","blurb":"The south German organ school's peak.","bio":"Johann Pachelbel, born in 1653 and active in Nuremberg until his death in 1706, was a German organist, composer and teacher who carried the south German organ tradition to its highest point. He left a large body of music, sacred and secular alike, and his work on the chorale prelude and the fugue puts him among the central figures of the middle Baroque. His importance to what followed runs partly through his teaching. Among his pupils was Johann Christoph Bach, elder brother of Johann Sebastian, and Johann Christoph in turn taught the younger Bach, so Pachelbel's manner reached him at second hand, through family. As organist and pedagogue he made the chorale-based keyboard piece into a form other composers could build on, and it was through that repertory, as much as through his pupils, that his manner travelled."},
  "johannhermannschein":{"wiki":"Johann Hermann Schein","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Johann%20Hermann%20Schein%201620.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Leipzig's Thomaskantor who imported Italy.","bio":"Johann Hermann Schein, born in 1586 and Thomaskantor in Leipzig from 1615 until his death in 1630, was one of the founding composers of the early German Baroque. With Schütz and Scheidt he makes up the trio conventionally named as its beginning, and he was among the first to carry the new Italian innovations into German music. A choir director and conductor who also wrote poetry, he worked across sacred and secular forms, and contemporaries reckoned him among the most polished composers then writing. The Leipzig cantorate, which he held for fifteen years, put him at the head of the city's music and gave his example a long reach in the church music of Saxony. The Italianate manner he introduced became the ordinary language of the German church music that came after him."},
  "georgbohm":{"wiki":"Georg Böhm","blurb":"The young Bach's model at Lüneburg.","bio":"Georg Böhm, born in 1661 and organist at Lüneburg until his death in 1733, was a German Baroque composer remembered for two things: the chorale partita, which he developed, and his effect on the young Johann Sebastian Bach. Böhm taught Bach at Lüneburg, and he was the route by which French style reached him, having absorbed that manner himself and set it beside the German and northern tradition he worked in, so that the pupil received both at once and early. Less of Böhm's own life is documented than of the men he stands between, but his position on the map of influence is secure: a good deal of what Bach later did with chorale melodies at the keyboard begins in what Böhm showed him. The partita is what he added to the form; the French manner is what he handed on."},
  "johannkuhnau":{"wiki":"Johann Kuhnau","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Johann%20Kuhnau.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Bach's polymath predecessor at the Thomaskirche.","bio":"Johann Kuhnau, born in 1660 and Thomaskantor in Leipzig for twenty-one years until his death in 1722, was a polymath now known mainly as a composer. He was also a lawyer, a translator, a novelist and a theorist of music, and he combined all of it with his official duties in Leipzig. A great deal of what he wrote is lost, operas and masses and other large vocal works among it, so his reputation rests on the Biblical Sonatas of 1700, keyboard pieces built as programme music, each one narrating a single episode from scripture in close detail. His other hold on posterity is positional. The office he had filled for two decades passed at his death to Johann Sebastian Bach, who took it up in 1723 and inherited with it the pattern of work that went with the Thomaskirche."},
  "georgphilipptelemann":{"wiki":"Georg Philipp Telemann","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Telemann.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The most published composer in Europe.","bio":"Georg Philipp Telemann, born in 1681 and long the presiding musician of Hamburg, where he died in 1767, left more surviving music than almost any composer who ever worked, and was the most published composer in Europe of his day. His contemporaries ranked him among the leading Germans of the age. He met George Frideric Handel when both were in their twenties and the friendship lasted fifty years, kept up by correspondence and by an exchange of exotic plants for their gardens. Johann Sebastian Bach made him godfather to his son Carl Philipp Emanuel, who took Telemann's name and in time succeeded him at Hamburg, and when Leipzig looked for a Thomaskantor, Telemann was its first choice and Bach only its third. A multi-instrumentalist, organist and chapelmaster, he wrote fluently in every genre then going."},
  "janpieterszoonsweelinck":{"wiki":"Jan Pieterszoon Sweelinck","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jan%20Pieterszoon%20Sweelinck.png?width=480","blurb":"The maker of German organists.","bio":"Jan Pieterszoon Sweelinck, born in 1562 and organist in Amsterdam until his death in 1621, worked across the join between the Renaissance and the Baroque and was one of the first great keyboard composers Europe produced. He was also, and above all, a teacher: he was called the maker of German organists, and the whole north German organ tradition begins in his Amsterdam teaching room. Samuel Scheidt came to study with him around 1608, and Heinrich Scheidemann was one of the Hamburg organists he trained, so his method took root in Germany through the men he sent home again. When John Bull fled to the Low Countries in 1613, the two exchanged themes and wrote pieces on each other's, a rare documented meeting between the English and Dutch keyboard schools. His pupils carried his contrapuntal method across northern Germany."},
  "samuelscheidt":{"wiki":"Samuel Scheidt","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Samuel%20Scheidt.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Sweelinck's pupil, Halle's organ master.","bio":"Samuel Scheidt, born in 1587 and based in Halle, where he died in 1654, was an early-Baroque German organist and composer who also taught. Around 1608 he travelled to Amsterdam to study with Jan Pieterszoon Sweelinck, and he brought that Dutch keyboard discipline home with him. His Tabulatura nova set the new manner down in print, and it became a foundation for later German organ writing precisely because it was printed: a method others could take up without having made the journey themselves. He worked as a university teacher and as a chapelmaster besides playing the organ, so his influence ran through instruction as much as through publication. Much of his importance lies in transmission, which makes him a link rather than an endpoint in the northern line of organists."},
  "johannadamreincken":{"wiki":"Johann Adam Reincken","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Reincken%20cropped.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Hamburg organist the young Bach sought out.","bio":"Johann Adam Reincken, born in 1643 and organist at the Katharinenkirche in Hamburg until his death in 1722, ranked among the most important composers his century produced, although very little of his music survives. He studied with Heinrich Scheidemann and then succeeded his teacher at the Katharinenkirche. He was a friend of Dieterich Buxtehude, and he also played the viol and worked as a publisher. His largest effect was on Johann Sebastian Bach, who walked to Hamburg as a boy to hear him and came back in 1720 to play for the old man, who is reported to have said afterwards that the art was not dead after all. Standing between his own teacher Scheidemann and Bach, Reincken is a link in the chain by which the north German organ manner was handed down."},
  "johannjosephfux":{"wiki":"Johann Joseph Fux","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Portrait%20of%20Johann%20Joseph%20Fux%2C%20by%20Jacob%20van%20Schuppen.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Wrote the counterpoint textbook everyone used.","bio":"Johann Joseph Fux, born in 1660 and long in Vienna, where he died in 1741, was an Austrian composer, theorist and teacher of the late Baroque who also worked as an organist and a conductor. The work of his that has lasted is not a composition but a book. Gradus ad Parnassum, his treatise on counterpoint, became the most influential account ever written of Palestrina's Renaissance polyphony and the standard training in strict writing for generations of musicians. Joseph Haydn taught himself counterpoint from it and then used it on his own pupils for fifty years. Johann Georg Albrechtsberger took it as the basis for teaching Beethoven, so Fux's exercises stand behind the Viennese classical style at one remove. He taught in person too: Georg Christoph Wagenseil was his pupil, and Fux's recommendation carried him into the imperial court."},
  "johannsebastianbach":{"wiki":"Johann Sebastian Bach","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Johann%20Sebastian%20Bach.png?width=480","blurb":"The summation of the contrapuntal tradition.","bio":"Johann Sebastian Bach, born in 1685 and Thomaskantor in Leipzig from 1723 until his death in 1750, gathered the contrapuntal tradition into one body of work: the Brandenburg Concertos, the Cello Suites and the solo violin Sonatas and Partitas, the Well-Tempered Clavier and the Goldberg Variations, the organ chorales and the Toccata and Fugue in D minor, the St Matthew Passion and the Mass in B minor, and finally The Musical Offering and The Art of Fugue. Almost all of it was learned from others. His elder brother Johann Christoph took him in at ten and taught him the keyboard, Georg Böhm taught him at Lüneburg and brought French style with him, he walked to Hamburg for Reincken and 250 miles to Lübeck for Buxtehude, and he transcribed Vivaldi and copied Couperin. His pupil Kirnberger carried that practice into the next century; Mendelssohn's 1829 St Matthew Passion began the revival."},
  "georgefriderichandel":{"wiki":"George Frideric Handel","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/George%20Frideric%20Handel%20by%20Balthasar%20Denner.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Italian opera turned English oratorio.","bio":"George Frideric Handel, born in 1685 and settled in London, where he died in 1759, was a German-British composer of anthems, oratorios, operas, organ concerti and concerti grossi: an Italian opera career that turned, in England, into the English oratorio. In 1703 he and Mattheson called on Buxtehude to look over his post, and both declined it, since it came with the organist's daughter. His Italian years, from 1706 to 1710, were carried by the Roman patronage network around Cardinal Pietro Ottoboni, and Arcangelo Corelli led the orchestra for his Roman oratorios in 1707. In England, Queen Anne granted him an annual pension that survived the change of dynasty, and the Duke of Chandos kept him at Cannons in 1717 and 1718, where he wrote the Chandos Anthems and Acis and Galatea. Telemann remained a friend for fifty years."},
  "johannadolphhasse":{"wiki":"Johann Adolph Hasse","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Johann%20Adolf%20Hasse.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Opera seria's most performed composer.","bio":"Johann Adolph Hasse, born in 1699 and long associated with Dresden, was a German composer, singer and teacher whose operas were performed more widely than anyone else's in his century, and who lived until 1783. He counted as a decisive figure in the shaping of opera seria, and he wrote a great deal of church music besides. He married the soprano Faustina Bordoni, and he was a friend of the librettist Pietro Metastasio, whose texts he set again and again, the pairing of Metastasio's verse with Hasse's setting becoming the model of serious opera for a generation. A singer himself before he was a chapelmaster, and a teacher of music all his life, he wrote for voices from inside the craft. Popularity of that order was rare, and in his own lifetime it was unmatched."},
  "jandismaszelenka":{"wiki":"Jan Dismas Zelenka","blurb":"Dresden's contrapuntist, copied out by Bach.","bio":"Jan Dismas Zelenka, born in 1679 and for most of his career at Dresden, where he died in 1745, was a Czech composer, conductor and double-bass player of the Baroque who also worked as an arranger. Bohemian by birth and Saxon by employment, he belonged entirely to neither. Admired for harmonic inventiveness and for a mastery of counterpoint, his music has stayed prized among musicians who go looking for difficulty. No meeting with Johann Sebastian Bach is recorded, but Bach and his sons copied out Zelenka's music, which almost nobody else did, and that is the clearest measure of how a working contrapuntist regarded him. It is also the shape of his reputation ever since: a composer's composer, valued first by the people who had to write the same kind of music themselves."},
  "johannjoachimquantz":{"wiki":"Johann Joachim Quantz","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Quantz%20by%20Gerhard.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Frederick the Great's flute teacher.","bio":"Johann Joachim Quantz, born in 1697 and resident in Berlin until his death in 1773, was a German flautist, flute maker, composer and music theorist of the late Baroque. Most of his professional life was spent at the court of Frederick the Great as the king's flute teacher, a position that kept him close to the monarch for decades and made him the best paid musician at Potsdam. He wrote hundreds of sonatas and concertos for his own instrument, built flutes himself, and set out his method in On Playing the Flute, a treatise later musicians have read as a guide to how the music of his time was actually played. Bach, Haydn and Mozart all knew his work and thought well of it. Few musicians have so completely shaped the taste of one court, or left so full a record of it."},
  "juangutierrezdepadilla":{"wiki":"Juan Gutiérrez de Padilla","blurb":"The greatest New World chapelmaster.","bio":"Juan Gutiérrez de Padilla, born in Spain around 1590 and from 1622 in Puebla, where he died in 1664, was the greatest of the chapelmasters of the New World. He arrived at the cathedral as assistant to Gaspar Fernandes and succeeded him as chapelmaster in 1629, holding the post for thirty-five years. Trained in the Spanish Renaissance polyphonic tradition, he wrote polychoral Masses of considerable scale for the cathedral's divided choirs, and villancicos, songs in the vernacular, in which Black and Indigenous voices enter the repertory of a colonial cathedral. That double output, high Latin polyphony on one side and the languages of Puebla's streets on the other, is why his music matters well beyond its region: it is a record of who was actually singing in seventeenth-century Mexico, and of what they were allowed to sing."},
  "manueldezumaya":{"wiki":"Manuel de Sumaya","blurb":"Composed the first North American opera.","bio":"Manuel de Zumaya, born around 1678 and active in Mexico City and later Oaxaca, where he died in 1755, was the outstanding composer of New Spain. He sang as a boy in the choir of Mexico City cathedral under Antonio de Salazar, studied with him, and succeeded him as chapelmaster in 1715. An ordained priest, he wrote music that joins traditional Spanish practice to the Italian style then becoming influential across the Spanish empire. In 1711 he composed La Parténope, to an Italian libretto, the first opera written in North America and possibly the first opera in Italian by any composer in the New World. His name appears in the record as both Sumaya and Zumaya, the former more common in his own manuscripts. With Ignacio de Jerusalem, who followed him at Mexico City, he marks the two poles of the cathedral's taste, Iberian and Neapolitan."},
  "juandearaujo":{"wiki":"Juan de Araujo","blurb":"Left 150 villancicos from Upper Peru.","bio":"Juan de Araujo, born in Spain in 1646 and chapelmaster at La Plata, now Sucre in Upper Peru, where he died in 1712, was a composer and working musician of the early to middle Baroque whose life was spent in South and Central America. Some 150 of his villancicos survive, vernacular sacred songs written for an Andean cathedral, and they are enough to show what such a place actually sang. Little else about him is well documented, and the archive is effectively the biography: a Spanish-born musician who made his whole career in the Americas and left, in place of a life story, 150 pieces of the music his congregations heard. That repertory, rather than any documented life, is what his name now stands on, and it is why he is heard again at all."},
  "domenicozipoli":{"wiki":"Domenico Zipoli","blurb":"Left Europe for the Paraguay missions.","bio":"Domenico Zipoli, born in Italy in 1688 and dead at Córdoba in 1726, was an organist, harpsichordist and composer who gave up a European career for the Jesuit missions of South America. He studied in Rome with Bernardo Pasquini and, briefly and by report stormily, in Naples with Alessandro Scarlatti. He entered the Jesuit order so as to serve in the Reductions of Paraguay, left for them in 1717, and spent his remaining years in the Viceroyalty of Peru, teaching music among the Guaraní. He is remembered as the ablest musician the Jesuit missions produced. His afterlife is the remarkable part: his works were copied and performed by Guaraní and Chiquitano musicians for a century after his death, which means the career he gave up in Europe was outlasted, several times over, by the one he chose instead."},
  "ignaciodejerusalem":{"wiki":"Ignacio de Jerusalem","blurb":"Brought Naples to Mexico City's cathedral.","bio":"Ignacio de Jerusalem, born in 1707 and chapelmaster at Mexico City cathedral until his death in 1769, was an Italian-born violinist, organist, composer and teacher of the Novohispanic Baroque. What he brought with him to New Spain was the Neapolitan manner, the idiom of contemporary Italian opera, and he wrote it into the music of the cathedral. He followed Manuel de Zumaya in the post, and the two are habitually set against one another as the two poles of Mexico City's taste, Iberian and Neapolitan, the older Spanish polyphonic style giving way to a sound imported from Naples. Little of his life before New Spain is documented. What represents him now is the repertory he left the cathedral and the change of direction it records: an Italian idiom taking hold in a Mexican choir loft, and an older tradition giving ground to it."},
  "tomasdetorrejonyvelasco":{"wiki":"Tomás de Torrejón y Velasco","blurb":"Composed the first opera in the Americas.","bio":"Tomás de Torrejón y Velasco, born in Spain in 1644 and based in Lima until his death in 1728, was a composer, musician and organist of the American Baroque, and the author of the first opera composed in the Americas. In 1701 he set La púrpura de la rosa, a Calderón text that Juan Hidalgo had already set in Madrid; taking up the same libretto in Peru moved the Spanish court genre across the Atlantic and gave the viceregal capital a stage work of its own. The rest of his long working life went in the ordinary service of music at Lima, where he was based for decades. Roque Ceruti, who succeeded him there, turned the cathedral's style Italianate, which leaves Torrejón as the last major representative of the older Iberian manner in Peru before the Italian taste arrived."},
  "antoniodesalazar":{"wiki":"Antonio de Salazar (composer)","blurb":"Mexico City's chapelmaster, Zumaya's teacher.","bio":"Antonio de Salazar, born around 1650 and chapelmaster at Mexico City cathedral until his death in 1715, was a composer of the Novohispano Baroque. His clearest mark on the map is a pupil. Manuel de Zumaya sang in his choir as a boy, learned composition from him, and succeeded him in the post in 1715, so that the succession from one to the other kept the cathedral's music in a single line. Beyond the cathedral's own records, little about Salazar is documented: his birth year is approximate, his nationality is given as Spanish and Mexican both, and what survives of the man is largely the office he held and the pupil he formed in it. That is the ordinary condition of the cathedral musicians of New Spain, whose music outlasted almost every other trace of them."},
  "roqueceruti":{"wiki":"Roque Ceruti","blurb":"Turned Lima's cathedral music Italianate.","bio":"Roque Ceruti, born in Italy around 1683 and resident in Lima until his death in 1760, was recruited to conduct the private orchestra of the Viceroy of Peru and later took charge of the music at the cathedral. He succeeded Tomás de Torrejón y Velasco there, and the succession marks a change of taste: Ceruti was the dominant Italianizing influence of his period, setting the idiom of contemporary Italy in place of the older Iberian manner. Not everyone welcomed it: among the Spanish musicians of the older school there was resentment. He is the clearest case in the viceroyalty of a musician imported with a style attached, brought in by a viceroy's household and then installed at the centre of the church's music, where what began as court fashion became the cathedral's ordinary sound."},
  "henrypurcell":{"wiki":"Henry Purcell","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Henry%20Purcell%20by%20John%20Closterman.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The last great English voice before Elgar.","bio":"Henry Purcell, born in London in 1659 and dead there in 1695 at thirty-six, was an English organist and composer of the middle Baroque and, on the usual reckoning, the last great native voice before Elgar. He was extremely prolific for so short a life: more than a hundred songs, the tragic opera Dido and Aeneas, and the incidental music for The Fairy Queen, made out of Shakespeare's A Midsummer Night's Dream. His style took in Italian and French elements but stayed uniquely English, rooted in the consort and theatre traditions he inherited from Matthew Locke. His teacher John Blow stood down as organist of Westminster Abbey in 1679 to make room for him, and took the post back when Purcell died, which is a plain enough measure of what the older man believed he had in his pupil."},
  "johnblow":{"wiki":"John Blow","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/John%20Blow%201687.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Stepped aside for his pupil Purcell.","bio":"John Blow, born in 1649 and organist of Westminster Abbey from late 1668, was an English composer, organist and educator who trained a generation of Restoration church musicians, Henry Purcell among them, with William Croft and Jeremiah Clarke. In 1679 he stood down from the Abbey post to make room for Purcell, and took it back when his pupil died, which may be the plainest act of teacherly generosity in English music. James II took him on as a private musician in 1685; in 1687 he moved to St Paul's Cathedral as choirmaster, where much of his music was performed; and in 1699 a new office, Composer to the Chapel Royal, was created and given to him. Venus and Adonis, his one work for the stage, is credited with shaping Purcell's Dido and Aeneas. He died in 1708."},
  "matthewlocke":{"wiki":"Matthew Locke (composer)","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Matthew%20Locke.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The theatre tradition Purcell inherited.","bio":"Matthew Locke, born around 1621 and working in London until his death in 1677, was an English Baroque composer, singer and music theorist. He wrote for the consort and for the theatre in the years around the Restoration, and it is that double inheritance, the older English consort writing on one side and dramatic music on the other, that passed to Henry Purcell, who took up both and is hard to imagine without them. Locke worked as a singer and wrote as a theorist as well as composing, and he belonged to the generation before the men he shaped, old enough to have known English music before the Restoration brought continental fashions in. He is remembered less for particular pieces than for a position: the thread from Locke to Purcell is the clearest single strand in English music of the seventeenth century."},
  "pelhamhumfrey":{"wiki":"Pelham Humfrey","blurb":"Brought Lully's French style to England.","bio":"Pelham Humfrey, born in 1647 and dead in London in 1674 at twenty-seven, was the first musician of the new English generation to rise to prominence after the Restoration. Charles II sent him to France and Italy at royal expense, specifically to learn the current style and bring it home, and he returned full of Jean-Baptiste Lully and the French court manner, which he then fitted to English words. His working life was very short and his output small, but what he set going held: a continental technique learned abroad on a king's money and naturalised at home within a few years of his return. He is among the clearest cases anywhere on this map of a monarch deliberately importing a style through a single young musician, and of how quickly that import can take root."},
  "giovannibattistapergolesi":{"wiki":"Giovanni Battista Pergolesi","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Giovanni%20Battista%20Pergolesi.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Wrote the manifesto of the new taste.","bio":"Giovanni Battista Pergolesi, born in 1710 and dead at twenty-six in 1736, was an Italian composer, violinist and organist, a leading exponent of the Neapolitan school and reckoned among the finest Italian musicians of his half-century. He was born Giovanni Battista Draghi. His La serva padrona became the manifesto of the new galant taste, and its effects long outlived him: revived in Paris in 1752, it was the spark for the Querelle des Bouffons, and Jean-Jacques Rousseau was among the writers it worked on. Few composers with so short a working life have had so direct an effect on what came next, and fewer still through a single comic piece that was still being argued over in Paris sixteen years after its composer was dead."},
  "baldassaregaluppi":{"wiki":"Baldassare Galuppi","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Galuppi.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Venice's opera buffa, with Goldoni.","bio":"Baldassare Galuppi, born on the Venetian island of Burano in 1706 and dead in Venice in 1785, was a composer, organist and conductor who held a succession of leading musical appointments in his own city. The centre of his reputation is comic opera, written in partnership with Goldoni, whose librettos gave the Venetian form its shape at mid-century. His career reached well beyond the lagoon, with periods of international success in Saint Petersburg, London and Vienna, but Venice remained his base throughout. He is grouped with Giovanni Battista Sammartini, Johann Adolph Hasse and C. P. E. Bach as a representative of the galant music that spread across Europe through the eighteenth century: tuneful, elegantly proportioned, and turned away from the density of the Baroque he was born into."},
  "carlphilippemanuelbach":{"wiki":"Carl Philipp Emanuel Bach","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Carl%20Philipp%20Emanuel%20Bach.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The keyboard's great voice of feeling.","bio":"Carl Philipp Emanuel Bach, born in 1714, was the fifth child of Johann Sebastian Bach and Maria Barbara Bach and the second of their sons to live to grow up, a musician who worked across the Baroque and the Classical eras both. He spent nearly thirty underpaid years as court harpsichordist to Frederick the Great in Berlin before moving to Hamburg, where he succeeded his godfather Georg Philipp Telemann and where he died in 1788. His Essay on the True Art of Playing Keyboard Instruments is the central document of eighteenth-century keyboard practice, and his own music is the clearest instance of the empfindsamer Stil, with its abrupt turns of feeling and its refusal to settle. Joseph Haydn was direct about the debt: whoever knew him thoroughly, he said, must realise how much he owed to Emanuel Bach."},
  "wilhelmfriedemannbach":{"wiki":"Wilhelm Friedemann Bach","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Wilhelm%20Friedeman%20Bach.png?width=480","blurb":"Bach's eldest son, gifted and unlucky.","bio":"Wilhelm Friedemann Bach, born in 1710, was the second child of Johann Sebastian Bach and Maria Barbara Bach, and the eldest of their sons. His father wrote the Clavier-Büchlein for him, the notebook in which the boy's keyboard lessons were set down, and the pieces in it are where his own playing began. He grew up to be an organist, harpsichordist and composer, and his gift as an improviser was acknowledged in his own lifetime. His working life nonetheless went badly: employment and income were unstable, and he died in poverty in 1784. His music sits between his father's counterpoint and the galant taste that was replacing it, belonging fully to neither, and that awkward position is part of why it took so long to find listeners of its own."},
  "johannchristianbach":{"wiki":"Johann Christian Bach","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Johann%20Christian%20Bach%20by%20Thomas%20Gainsborough.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The English Bach; Mozart's boyhood model.","bio":"Johann Christian Bach, born in 1735, was the youngest son of Johann Sebastian Bach. After his father's death he was taken to Berlin and raised by his brother Emanuel, and he eventually settled in London, where he became known as the English Bach and where he died in 1782. His music belongs to the Classical era rather than his father's Baroque: singing melody, clear phrase lengths, an easy orchestral manner. In 1764 and 1765 the eight-year-old Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart was in London, sat on his knee at the keyboard, and took from him the singing Allegro that stayed with him for the rest of his life. Of all the Bach sons, Johann Christian is the one whose style points most directly forward, into the music that boy would go on to write."},
  "johannschobert":{"wiki":"Johann Schobert","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Johann%20schobert.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Paris's galant keyboard composer.","bio":"Johann Schobert, who worked in Paris and died there in 1767, was a German composer, harpsichordist and keyboard player of the galant generation who also published his own music. Almost nothing about his origins is settled. His birth is dated anywhere between about 1720 and about 1740, and his birthplace given as Silesia, Alsace or Nuremberg, so even the region he came from depends on which record you follow. Paris is the fixed point: there he composed, played the harpsichord, and issued his own editions rather than leaving that to a publisher. His death is the detail everyone repeats. He ate mushrooms that he had insisted were edible; they were not, and they killed him. A career that is essentially one certain city, one certain year, and one very certain dinner."},
  "georgchristophwagenseil":{"wiki":"Georg Christoph Wagenseil","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Georg%20Christoph%20Wagenseil%20-%20silhouette%201776.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Fux's pupil at the imperial court.","bio":"Georg Christoph Wagenseil, born in 1715 and active in Vienna, where he died in 1777, was an Austrian composer, organist and keyboard player whose career was made in the service of the imperial court. He was the pupil of Johann Joseph Fux, and it was Fux's recommendation that carried him into that court, so the strictest teaching of the older Viennese school stands directly behind his training. What he wrote, though, was the new galant music: keyboard concertos and other pieces in a light, tuneful manner. Those concertos were among the works the six-year-old Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart played to the Empress, and the child asked for the composer himself to come and turn his pages. Wagenseil is the hinge in Vienna between Fux's Baroque discipline and the idiom Mozart's generation would take as its starting point."},
  "nicolaporpora":{"wiki":"Nicola Porpora","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nicola%20Antonio%20Porpora.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The century's greatest teacher of singers.","bio":"Nicola Porpora, born in 1686 and working in Naples, Vienna and London before his death in 1768, was an Italian composer and the most celebrated teacher of singing of his century. The castrati Farinelli and Caffarelli were his students, and so were composers: Johann Adolph Hasse, Matteo Capranica, and the young Joseph Haydn, who worked as his valet and accompanist in exchange for lessons and said afterwards that he had learned from him the true fundamentals of composition. He also taught singing and composition to Marianna Martines, in the same Vienna building where Haydn was teaching her the keyboard. Porpora wrote music of his own, but his largest claim is as a maker of musicians: the most famous voices of the age, and at least one pupil who changed what music could do."},
  "johannphilippkirnberger":{"wiki":"Johann Kirnberger","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Johann%20Philipp%20Kirnberger.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Bach's pupil, and Bach's conduit forward.","bio":"Johann Philipp Kirnberger, born in 1721 and long resident in Berlin, where he died in 1783, was a musician, composer and theorist whose importance is chiefly as a transmitter. His organ teachers were Johann Peter Kellner and Heinrich Nicolaus Gerber; from 1738 he took lessons at Sondershausen from the violinist Meil; and from 1739 to 1741 he studied performance and composition under Johann Sebastian Bach, the decisive experience of his life. His treatises set out Bach's practice as rules that could be taught, and they are the main channel by which that practice reached the nineteenth century. In Berlin he taught Anna Amalia of Prussia, the King's sister, whose library preserved a great deal of Bach. Much of what later ages knew as Bach's method they had at second hand from Kirnberger, who had troubled to write it down."},
  "giovannipaisiello":{"wiki":"Giovanni Paisiello","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/PaiselloVigeeLeBrun.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The late century's most popular opera composer.","bio":"Giovanni Paisiello, born in 1740 and dead in 1816, was the most popular writer of opera of the late 1700s and the most performed composer of opera buffa of his generation, an Italian working in the Classical era. His career took him from Naples to Saint Petersburg and back, and his fluent, singer-friendly stage manner influenced both Mozart and Rossini, the two composers whose reputations later overshadowed his. He belongs to the Neapolitan tradition that carried Italian comic opera across Europe, and Saint Petersburg was simply the furthest point that reach extended. The shape of comic opera at the end of the eighteenth century, its pacing and its writing for voices, is in large part his doing, and Mozart and Rossini both took it from him rather than around him."},
  "domenicocimarosa":{"wiki":"Domenico Cimarosa","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Domenico%20Cimarosa.png?width=480","blurb":"Il matrimonio segreto's comic master.","bio":"Domenico Cimarosa, born in 1749 and dead in 1801, was an Italian of the Classical period, a composer of the Neapolitan school, and he wrote more than eighty operas, most of them comedies. The best known is Il matrimonio segreto of 1792. He also wrote instrumental music and music for the church, though it is the stage works his name rests on. His career ran between Naples and Vienna, and his comic writing, brisk and generous to singers, represents the form at its late eighteenth-century height. More than eighty operas is a working rate that tells you what the trade was like: a composer of that generation supplied theatres the way a printer supplied books, on demand and at speed, and the best of what he made has outlasted the system that demanded it."},
  "domenicoalberti":{"wiki":"Domenico Alberti","blurb":"The left hand that bears his name.","bio":"Domenico Alberti, born around 1710 and dead in 1746, was a Venetian singer, harpsichordist and composer of the early galant generation. He is remembered less for his music than for a habit within it: the broken-chord keyboard accompaniment that carries his name. The Alberti bass takes the notes of a chord and lays them out in a rocking pattern beneath the melody instead of striking them together, which gives a keyboard player a way of holding harmony under a tune on an instrument that cannot hold anything for long. It became the standard left hand of the mid-eighteenth-century sonata, and it is still among the first things a student learns to recognise in music of that period. Alberti sang, played the harpsichord and wrote his own pieces, all of them comprehensively outlived by one figure of accompaniment."},
  "johannstamitz":{"wiki":"Johann Stamitz","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jan%20V%C3%A1clav%20Stamic%20(1717-1757).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Built the Mannheim orchestra and its style.","bio":"Johann Stamitz, born in 1717 and dead at Mannheim in 1757, is regarded as the man who founded the Mannheim school. A Czech violinist, conductor and composer whose style stands between the Baroque and the Classical, he is credited with a long list of innovations, but the essential one was the orchestra itself: a body of players drilled to a common discipline, so that effects depending on an ensemble moving as one became possible for the first time. Carl Theodor, Elector Palatine, paid for it, and without that patronage the style could not have existed. Christian Cannabich inherited the orchestra and its discipline, his surviving sons Carl and Anton Stamitz carried on the manner of composition, and the Mannheim orchestral sound reached Vienna before Haydn's symphonies did."},
  "carlstamitz":{"wiki":"Carl Stamitz","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Karl%20Stamitz.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Johann's son, Mannheim's second generation.","bio":"Carl Stamitz, born in 1745 and dead in 1801, was the son of Johann Stamitz and the leading figure of the Mannheim School's second generation. A German composer of partly Czech descent, he worked as a violinist, conductor and composer of the Classical era, growing up inside the tradition his father had founded and carrying its manner outward through his own playing and writing. What the school stood for was orchestral discipline rather than any single composer's signature, which is why it could survive being handed on: what Carl inherited was a way of playing as much as a way of writing. He took it into the concert life of his own generation, and the Mannheim sound outlasted its inventor by decades, travelling well beyond the court where it had been made."},
  "franzxaverrichter":{"wiki":"Franz Xaver Richter","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Franz%20Xaver%20Richter%20by%20Heinrich%20Eduard%20von%20Wintter.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Mannheim veteran, then Strasbourg's cathedral.","bio":"Franz Xaver Richter, born in 1709 and dead in 1789, was a singer, violinist, conductor and music theoretician as well as a composer, Austro-Moravian by origin and known in France as François Xavier Richter. He spent the first part of his life in Austria, then joined the court at Mannheim, whose orchestra and manner made the Mannheim School, and later moved to Strasbourg, where he directed the music at the cathedral. He was a teacher and theorist as much as a composer, and from 1783 his deputy at Strasbourg was Ignaz Pleyel, Haydn's favourite pupil, which places Richter exactly where the Mannheim inheritance meets Haydn's. His long career is a reminder that the Mannheim School was less a single generation than a relay, and that its members ended up scattered across Europe."},
  "christiancannabich":{"wiki":"Christian Cannabich","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Egid%20Verhelst%20-%20Portrait%20of%20Christian%20Cannabich.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Drilled Europe's best-disciplined orchestra.","bio":"Christian Cannabich, born in 1731 and dead in 1798, was a German violinist and composer, and in time Kapellmeister at Mannheim, who inherited the orchestra and its discipline from his teacher Johann Stamitz and turned it into an ensemble that Charles Burney judged Europe's most complete and best drilled. He composed around two hundred works, but his real instrument was the ensemble: the graduated crescendos and diminuendos, drilled with great care, that identify the Mannheim school were his players' doing, and the orchestral texture he and Stamitz developed is what the First Viennese School took up. In 1777 Mozart spent his Mannheim days with the Cannabich household and wrote the sonata K. 309 for the daughter of the house. Cannabich's discipline, more than any single composition of his own, is what he left behind."},
  "ignazholzbauer":{"wiki":"Ignaz Holzbauer","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Holzbauer.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Mannheim's Sturm und Drang composer.","bio":"Ignaz Holzbauer, born in 1711 and dead in 1783, was an Austrian composer, conductor and chapelmaster who belonged to the Mannheim school. He wrote operas, symphonies, concertos and chamber music, and his aesthetic ran with the Sturm und Drang movement then unsettling German art and literature, which puts him on the darker and more agitated side of the Mannheim manner rather than its polished one. As a conductor and chapelmaster as well as a composer, he was one of the working musicians of the court, not merely a name attached to its style. He also stands as evidence that the school was not only a matter of orchestral effect: it had room for a composer whose taste ran to storm and unrest, and who turned the ensemble's discipline towards drama in operas and symphonies rather than towards display."},
  "carltheodorelectorpalatine":{"wiki":"Charles Theodore, Elector of Bavaria","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Anton%20Hickel%20(workshop)%20Karl%20Theodor.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Elector who paid for Mannheim.","bio":"Charles Theodore, Carl Theodor, Elector Palatine, born in 1724, was a German nobleman of the House of Wittelsbach, from its Sulzbach branch, and he came into his titles young. He was Count Palatine of Sulzbach at six, after his father Johann Christian died in 1733; at eighteen, in 1742, when his cousin Charles III Philip died, the Rhine electorate and the Palatine county came to him; and in his fifties, in 1777, another cousin's death made him Elector of Bavaria, which he remained until he died in 1799. He was not a composer. He was the reason there was something to compose for: a dignitary and art collector who paid for the Mannheim court orchestra that Johann Stamitz built, so that every innovation credited to the Mannheim School rested in the end on his household budget."},
  "christophwillibaldgluck":{"wiki":"Christoph Willibald Gluck","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Joseph%20Siffred%20Duplessis%20-%20Christoph%20Willibald%20Gluck%20-%20Google%20Art%20Project.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Opera reformed from inside the court.","bio":"Christoph Willibald Gluck, 1714–1787, was an opera composer who worked between Vienna and Paris and changed what opera was for. Raised in Bohemia and prominent at the Habsburg court, he turned in the 1760s to a series of deliberately radical works — Orfeo ed Euridice, then Alceste, whose printed preface set out the reform as a programme. Written with the poet Ranieri de' Calzabigi, they traded the usually long da capo aria for continuous drama, orchestral recitative and far shorter spans; the target was the opera seria convention Pietro Metastasio had perfected. In Paris his cause was carried by Marie Antoinette, his former Viennese pupil, who as queen pushed his operas onto the stage. He mentored Antonio Salieri generously, even letting Les Danaïdes appear under his own name so that Paris would hear it."},
  "ranieridecalzabigi":{"wiki":"Ranieri de' Calzabigi","blurb":"The poet behind Gluck's reform.","bio":"Ranieri de' Calzabigi, 1714–1795, was an Italian poet who worked in Vienna as a librettist, and whose name is inseparable from Christoph Willibald Gluck's. He wrote the texts of the reform operas — Orfeo ed Euridice, Alceste and Paride ed Elena — and the two men together drafted the preface to Alceste that set out what the reform was for. That preface is one of the rare moments in opera's history when a poet and a composer stated their intentions in print before the argument was settled, and it made the case against the opera seria conventions the age had inherited. Calzabigi supplied what Gluck's music needed: direct language, and scenes that move continuously rather than stopping for display. The partnership is the standard example of a librettist shaping a composer rather than merely serving him, and the reform carries both their names."},
  "niccolojommelli":{"wiki":"Niccolò Jommelli","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Niccol%C3%B2%20Jommelli.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Reform arrived at from Stuttgart's pit.","bio":"Niccolò Jommelli, 1714–1774, was an Italian composer trained in the Neapolitan school who spent his most productive years as court chapel master at Stuttgart. Working for a German court rather than an Italian opera house gave him a theatre and an orchestra he could shape over years, and he used them to pull opera away from the star singer. Along with composers elsewhere in the Holy Roman Empire and in France, he is credited with a set of reforms that reduced the ornateness of the received style and curbed the singer's licence to decorate at will. That places him in the same current as the reform going on elsewhere, reached independently and from another direction: the practical corrections of a working chapel master rather than a printed manifesto. His name has faded beside those who published manifestos, but the change they were all making was the same one."},
  "tommasotraetta":{"wiki":"Tommaso Traetta","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Tommaso%20traetta.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Neapolitan polish turned toward drama.","bio":"Tommaso Traetta, 1727–1779, was an Italian composer trained in the Neapolitan school, who worked at Parma and later in St Petersburg. He is counted among the composers, active mainly in the Holy Roman Empire and in France, who carried through the operatic reforms of the middle of the century: reducing the ornateness of the inherited style and loosening the hold of the star singer on the shape of a work. That places him inside a reform underway at several courts at once, arrived at through practical theatre work rather than announced as a programme. His career, moving from an Italian court to the far north of Europe, shows how far the Neapolitan training travelled and how readily it bent to the tastes it met. The credit has gone largely to more famous names; the current was wider than any one of them."},
  "antoniosalieri":{"wiki":"Antonio Salieri","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Antonio%20Salieri%20painted%20by%20Joseph%20Willibrord%20M%C3%A4hler.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Vienna's gatekeeper and greatest teacher.","bio":"Antonio Salieri, 1750–1825, was an Italian composer and teacher who spent his whole adult career as a Habsburg subject in Vienna. Florian Leopold Gassmann brought him from Venice in 1766, aged sixteen, and trained him; Christoph Willibald Gluck then took him up as protégé and advocate, going so far as to let Les Danaïdes go out under his own name so that Paris would stage it. As court Kapellmeister he became the musical authority of the imperial capital. His deepest mark, though, was made in the teaching room. Ludwig van Beethoven studied Italian vocal setting with him and was still signing himself pupil of Salieri years afterwards; Franz Schubert worked with him from 1812 to 1817; the eleven-year-old Franz Liszt was taught composition free of charge. Few musicians have laid hands on so much of the next generation."},
  "pietrometastasio":{"wiki":"Pietro Metastasio","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Metastasio%20by%20Batoni.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Opera seria's supreme poet.","bio":"Pietro Metastasio — born Pietro Trapassi, 1698–1782 — was an Italian poet, librettist and priest, and by common judgement the most important writer of opera seria texts. From Vienna his libretti travelled everywhere; composers set the same dramas over and over for decades, so that his verse effectively supplied the century with its operatic plots and its idea of what an aria was for. That success made him the thing the next generation had to argue with: Gluck's reform was aimed squarely at the conventions Metastasio had perfected. He was also a quietly decisive patron, and a generous one. In Vienna he paid for the whole education of the young Marianna Martines, who lived in the same house as he did, and arranged her lessons with Porpora and with Haydn. It is the kind of intervention that leaves no trace in a work list but changes a life outright."},
  "josephhaydn":{"wiki":"Joseph Haydn","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Joseph%20Haydn%2C%20m%C3%A5lning%20av%20Thomas%20Hardy%20fr%C3%A5n%201792.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Father of the symphony and quartet.","bio":"Joseph Haydn, 1732–1809, made the symphony and the string quartet into serious forms: 106 symphonies of his own, and a body of quartets that set the terms for everyone after him. He learned the craft the hard way, serving as Nicola Porpora's valet and accompanist in exchange for lessons in which, he said, he learned the true fundamentals of composition, and teaching himself counterpoint from Fux's Gradus ad Parnassum, which he then used on his own pupils for fifty years. He acknowledged a large debt to Carl Philipp Emanuel Bach, and the Mannheim manner of Johann Stamitz reached Vienna ahead of him. Thirty years in Prince Nikolaus Esterházy's service left him, as he put it, cut off from the world and so forced to become original. He taught Marianna Martines and the young Beethoven, and recommended Hummel as his own successor at Eszterháza."},
  "michaelhaydn":{"wiki":"Michael Haydn","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Michaelhaydn1.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Haydn who stayed in Salzburg.","bio":"Michael Haydn, 1737–1806, was an Austrian composer and organist who spent his working life in Salzburg while his elder brother Joseph became famous elsewhere. The Classical style is usually told through the careers that left home, but Michael's is the other half of the story: a church musician of real quality holding a provincial post for decades and shaping the musicians who passed through it. The most consequential of those was Carl Maria von Weber, who studied with him in Salzburg in 1797–98 and took what he learned away with him. Two brothers who took opposite roads — one out into the wider musical world, one into a single town's church and court music — between them cover most of what a musician's life could be in the eighteenth century. Michael's reputation has always rested with musicians rather than with the public."},
  "wolfgangamadeusmozart":{"wiki":"Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Wolfgang-amadeus-mozart%201.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The complete musician of his century.","bio":"Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart, 1756–1791, moved from Salzburg to Vienna and completed more than 800 works — symphonies, concertos, chamber music, opera and church music, outstanding examples of nearly every genre available to him. His father Leopold was his first teacher and his impresario, and it was Leopold who compiled the Nannerl Notenbuch of 1759 for his sister. In London in 1764 the eight-year-old sat at the keyboard on Johann Christian Bach's knee, and the singing Allegro he learned there stayed with him for life; earlier still, aged six, he played Georg Christoph Wagenseil's concertos to the Empress and asked the composer to turn his pages. Baron Gottfried van Swieten's Sunday mornings of Bach and Handel permanently altered his counterpoint, and Emperor Joseph II commissioned Die Entführung. Johann Nepomuk Hummel, taken in at eight, lived in the Mozart household for two years."},
  "leopoldmozart":{"wiki":"Leopold Mozart","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Leopold%20Mozart.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The teacher who made two prodigies.","bio":"Leopold Mozart, 1719–1787, was a German violinist, composer and theorist of music, based in Salzburg, and the author of the Versuch einer gründlichen Violinschule of 1756 — a violin method so thorough that it remains the standard account of how eighteenth-century string playing worked. The treatise is his own claim on posterity; everything else about him is bound up with his children. He taught Maria Anna, called Nannerl, first, and toured her across Europe as an equal prodigy beside her younger brother, and then stopped. For Wolfgang he was teacher, manager and impresario, planning the journeys that turned a Salzburg child into a European name. The result was the most closely documented musical education in history, and one of the most difficult relationships — a father who built a genius and could not then let go of him."},
  "ludwigvanbeethoven":{"wiki":"Ludwig van Beethoven","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Beethoven.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The bridge from Classical to Romantic.","bio":"Ludwig van Beethoven, 1770–1827, came from Bonn to Vienna and drove the transition from the Classical style to the Romantic, expanding the symphony, the string quartet and the piano sonata past what they had been built to carry. He arrived to study with Joseph Haydn in 1792, complained that he learned nothing, and dedicated his first three sonatas to him anyway; Johann Georg Albrechtsberger took over the counterpoint when those lessons broke down, and Antonio Salieri taught him Italian vocal setting, a debt he acknowledged for years. He thought Luigi Cherubini the greatest living composer and built Fidelio on the French rescue opera Cherubini had perfected. Vienna's aristocracy carried him: Prince Karl Lichnowsky housed him and paid an annuity, Count Razumovsky commissioned the Op. 59 quartets, and Archduke Rudolph was pupil, patron and dedicatee. Carl Czerny, his pupil from ten, preserved how he played."},
  "johannbaptistvanhal":{"wiki":"Johann Baptist Wanhal","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jan%20K%C5%99titel%20Va%C5%88hal.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Bohemian his famous contemporaries respected.","bio":"Johann Baptist Vanhal, 1739–1813, was a Czech composer of the Classical period, born at Nechanice in Bohemia and settled for most of his life in Vienna. He belongs to the large Bohemian contingent whose move to the imperial capital gave Viennese Classicism much of its working population, and his standing among his colleagues was unusually high: Mozart, Haydn, Beethoven and Schubert all thought well of what he wrote, which is about as good a set of references as an eighteenth-century composer could hold. He was a serious instrumentalist too — an accomplished player of organ, violin and cello — and so moved through the city's music-making as a player as much as a writer. His name is now largely a specialist's, but for his contemporaries he was simply one of the composers worth hearing, and they said so."},
  "carldittersvondittersdorf":{"wiki":"Carl Ditters von Dittersdorf","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Carl%20Ditters%20von%20Dittersdorf%20-%20Heinrich%20Eduard%20Wintter.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Haydn and Mozart's genial Viennese colleague.","bio":"Carl Ditters von Dittersdorf, 1739–1799, was an Austrian violinist and composer who moved through the same Viennese circles as Haydn and Mozart and counted both as friends. That friendship is part of why his name survives, but the music earns its own place: the German singspiel Doktor und Apotheker was his most popular stage work, and he wrote a series of programmatic symphonies on subjects drawn from Ovid's Metamorphoses — orchestral storytelling of a kind the Classical symphony is not usually credited with. He was a conductor and bandleader as well, at home in the practical business of keeping an orchestra together. Dittersdorf is a useful corrective: he shows how broad and populous the Viennese style was in its own day, and how far posterity has narrowed it to three or four names."},
  "muzioclementi":{"wiki":"Muzio Clementi","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Muzio%20Clementi.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The piano's first true composer.","bio":"Muzio Clementi, 1752–1832, was born in Italy and made his life in England, where he became the first composer to write for the piano as a piano rather than as a loud harpsichord. He was a virtuoso, a teacher, a conductor, an editor, a publisher and a piano manufacturer at once, which meant he shaped the instrument's repertoire, the instrument itself and the trade that sold both. In 1781 he was set against Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart in a keyboard contest before the Emperor; Mozart's letters about him afterwards were savage, and that verdict clung to his reputation for a long time. The sonatas and studies stayed on every pianist's desk regardless. Through his pupils, his editions and his firm, Clementi did as much as anyone alive to make the piano the central instrument of the coming century."},
  "luigiboccherini":{"wiki":"Luigi Boccherini","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/LBoccherini.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Courtly grace, and the cello quintet.","bio":"Luigi Boccherini, 1743–1805, was an Italian composer and cellist who spent his career in Madrid, at a distance from the main centres of the Classical style. That distance shows: he kept a courtly, galante manner while the major centres of the style pushed on toward drama and weight, and as a cellist he gave his own instrument a leading voice, building his chamber music around the cello quintet. The minuet from the String Quintet in E, Op. 11 No. 5, has outlived nearly everything else he wrote, and the Cello Concerto in B flat was for generations known only through Friedrich Grützmacher's heavily altered arrangement before the original was restored. Musica notturna delle strade di Madrid catches the sound of his adopted city after dark. His elegance is not slightness; it is a considered alternative to the road the symphonists took."},
  "johannnepomukhummel":{"wiki":"Johann Nepomuk Hummel","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/JNHummel%202.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Mozart's pupil, Haydn's chosen successor.","bio":"Johann Nepomuk Hummel, 1778–1837, is the living link between the Classical style and what came after it. At eight he moved into the Mozart household and lived there for two years as Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart's pupil; he went on to study with Antonio Salieri and with Joseph Haydn, who thought enough of him to recommend him as his own successor at Eszterháza. Few musicians have carried that combination of endorsements. As pianist and composer he stood at the pivot between two eras, and his way of writing for the instrument — fluent, ornamented, singing — passed directly into the piano music of Frédéric Chopin, Franz Liszt and Felix Mendelssohn, who all absorbed the manner before finding their own. His own works have never quite held the repertory, but the style he refined is audible right across the century that followed."},
  "carlczerny":{"wiki":"Carl Czerny","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Carl%20Czerny.jpeg?width=480","blurb":"The hinge between Beethoven and Liszt.","bio":"Carl Czerny, 1791–1857, was an Austrian composer, pianist and teacher of Czech origin whose whole working life ran through Vienna, and whose importance is best measured by what passed through him. From the age of ten he studied with Ludwig van Beethoven, and he became the guardian of Beethoven's performing tradition — how the music was actually played, at what speed and with what touch, knowledge that would otherwise have simply vanished. In 1822–23 he taught the young Franz Liszt, without payment; Liszt later dedicated the Transcendental Études to him. Between those two names sits the whole nineteenth-century idea of piano playing. Czerny left more than a thousand compositions, and the study books he wrote are still on piano stands today, which makes him one of the very few composers whose music nearly every pianist alive has played."},
  "johanngeorgalbrechtsberger":{"wiki":"Johann Georg Albrechtsberger","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Johann%20Georg%20Albrechtsberger.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The counterpoint teacher of the age.","bio":"Johann Georg Albrechtsberger, 1736–1809, was an Austrian organist, composer and music theorist, and the counterpoint teacher of his age. He wrote a great deal of church music, orchestral and keyboard music, but his authority rested on theory and on the patience to drill it: he taught out of Johann Joseph Fux's Gradus ad Parnassum, the manual that carried strict species counterpoint into the Classical period. His most famous pupil came to him second-hand. When Ludwig van Beethoven's lessons with Joseph Haydn broke down, Albrechtsberger took over the counterpoint and finished the training his pupil had come to Vienna to get. A teacher of this kind leaves no monument under his own name; the evidence sits in other people's scores, which is the most durable form of influence a musician can have."},
  "josephbolognechevalierdesaintgeorges":{"wiki":"Chevalier de Saint-Georges","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mather%20Brown%2C%20William%20Ward%20-%20Monsieur%20de%20St%20George%2C%201788%2C%20NPG%20D4132%2C%20London.png?width=480","blurb":"Violinist, fencer, and Haydn's Paris commissioner.","bio":"Joseph Bologne, Chevalier de Saint-Georges, 1745–1799, was born in Guadeloupe and made his career in Paris as a violinist, composer and conductor — and, famously, as a fencer, athlete and dancer. A biracial free man of colour, he was the first composer of African descent to win widespread acclaim in European concert life. François-Joseph Gossec taught him composition and handed him the Concert des Amateurs; Philippe d'Orléans, Duc de Chartres, made him director of his private theatre at the Palais-Royal. As conductor of the Concert de la Loge Olympique he commissioned Joseph Haydn's six Paris symphonies and led their premieres in 1787, which alone would secure his place here. His own output runs to violin concertos, string quartets, sinfonie concertanti, duets, sonatas, two symphonies and stage works. In 1778 he and Mozart moved through the same Parisian salons."},
  "josemauricionunesgarcia":{"wiki":"José Maurício Nunes Garcia","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jos%C3%A9%20Mauricio%20Nunes%20Garcia.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Classical Rio's priest and chapel master.","bio":"José Maurício Nunes Garcia, 1767–1830, was a Brazilian priest and composer, the grandson of enslaved Africans, and the outstanding exponent of the Classical style in the Americas. Working in Rio de Janeiro, he wrote extensively for the church and served as mestre de capela to the Portuguese court once it had transplanted itself in exile to Brazil. Dom João VI kept him in the post and then, by degrees, displaced him: the arrival of the court composer Marcos Portugal in 1811 pushed Nunes Garcia out of the principal appointments he had held. He went on composing, conducting and teaching nonetheless, and his music is the strongest evidence that the Classical style had a serious life outside Europe — written by a Black Brazilian priest for a colonial capital that had unexpectedly become the seat of an empire."},
  "georgebridgetower":{"wiki":"George Bridgetower","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bridgetower2.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The violinist the Kreutzer was written for.","bio":"George Bridgetower, c. 1778–1860, was a British violinist of African and Polish descent who spent much of his life in England and made his name as a virtuoso in London and Vienna. His father was in service at Eszterháza, so the boy grew up around Joseph Haydn's orchestra; the Prince of Wales, later George IV, then paid for his education and placed him in the Brighton orchestra. In Vienna his playing impressed Ludwig van Beethoven, and on 24 May 1803 the two gave the first performance of the sonata now known as the Kreutzer, Bridgetower sight-reading over the composer's shoulder. The dedication was his. Then a remark he made about a woman ended the friendship, and Beethoven transferred it to Rodolphe Kreutzer, who never played the piece. One of music's most famous titles commemorates the wrong violinist."},
  "ignatiussancho":{"wiki":"Ignatius Sancho","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Portr%C3%A4t%20Ignatius%20Sancho%20(1729%E2%80%931780).png?width=480","blurb":"First Black Briton known to have voted.","bio":"Charles Ignatius Sancho, 1729–1780, was born on a slave ship in the Atlantic and became a writer, composer and abolitionist in London. Sold into slavery in New Granada and orphaned as an infant, he was brought to Britain and given to three sisters in Greenwich, where he spent eighteen years before running away to Montagu House in Blackheath. John Montagu, 2nd Duke of Montagu, taught him to read and encouraged the literary appetite that made him; the household later set him up as a shopkeeper in Westminster. From there he wrote essays, plays and books, and the correspondence on slavery he exchanged with Laurence Sterne was published and became an abolitionist text. Thomas Gainsborough painted him at Bath in 1768, in a sitting reported to have lasted an hour and forty minutes. He published music too, the first Black Briton to do so in Britain."},
  "chevalierdemeudemonpas":{"wiki":"Chevalier de Meude-Monpas","blurb":"Violin concertos from the king's household.","bio":"The Chevalier de Meude-Monpas, active in Paris in the 1780s, was a French violinist, composer and author, known also as J.J.O. de Meude-Monpas, and a royalist by conviction. He held the position of gentilhomme-servant du roi, which put him on the king's payroll, and he belonged to several academies. What survives of his reputation rests on a set of violin concertos. He is one of several Black musicians working in the Paris orchestras of the years before the Revolution — a presence usually reduced now to a single celebrated name, but never confined to one man. How much else Meude-Monpas wrote, and what became of him once the world he served was swept away, is not securely known. He is a reminder of how much of that generation's musical life has survived only in fragments."},
  "mariannamartines":{"wiki":"Marianna Martines","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Marianna%20Martines%2C%20Pupil%20of%20P.%20Metastasio%3B%20born%20in%20Vienna%2C%204th%20day%20of%20May%201744%2C%20Member%20Academia%20Filarmonica.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Michaelerhaus prodigy Metastasio paid for.","bio":"Marianna Martines, 1744–1812, was an Austrian singer, pianist and composer, and the most conspicuous product of a single Viennese address. The poet Pietro Metastasio lived in the same house, the Michaelerhaus, and paid for her education, arranging lessons in singing and composition with Nicola Porpora and keyboard lessons with the young Joseph Haydn, who lived in the same building. Few children have ever been so well placed. She grew into a composer in her own right as well as a performer, good enough as a singer to appear with Mozart. Her career shows what a woman of the period could reach when the ordinary obstacles of money and access were removed by a patron who happened to live upstairs — and how quickly the reputation faded afterwards, in the way that eighteenth-century women composers' reputations generally did."},
  "mariatheresiavonparadis":{"wiki":"Maria Theresia von Paradis","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Maria%20Theresia%20Paradis%20Wachsstatue.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The blind pianist Mozart wrote for.","bio":"Maria Theresia von Paradis, 1759–1824, was an Austrian pianist, singer and composer who lost her sight in early childhood and built a career at the centre of Viennese musical life regardless. She was in contact with Salieri, Haydn and Gluck, and Mozart — a close friend — almost certainly wrote his Piano Concerto No. 18 in B flat, K. 456, for her: a major work composed for a musician who could never read it from the page. That fact alone indicates the regard she commanded among the best judges in the city. Blindness shaped her method rather than limiting her ambition; in an age of sight-readers, everything she played had to be carried in memory. She is remembered now mostly through the concerto, but the company she kept tells the fuller story of a musician treated by Vienna's leading composers as one of their own."},
  "mariaannanannerlmozart":{"wiki":"Maria Anna Mozart","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nannerl%20Mozart.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The prodigy sister whose touring stopped.","bio":"Maria Anna Mozart, known as Nannerl, 1751–1829, was an outstanding keyboard player, trained by her father Leopold and toured across much of Europe as a celebrated child prodigy alongside her younger brother Wolfgang. The Nannerl Notenbuch, compiled for her by Leopold in 1759, is the book in which the family's musical education begins. At seventeen her life as a travelling performer was stopped; she stayed in Salzburg, teaching piano and performing when the chance came. At thirty-three she married and moved to a village a six-hour carriage ride from Salzburg, raising her husband's children along with her own. Widowed in 1801, she came back to Salzburg and took up teaching and performance again. She is known to have composed, though nothing survives in manuscript. Late in her life she fed what she remembered into the biographical study of her brother."},
  "annaamaliaofprussia":{"wiki":"Anna Amalia, Abbess of Quedlinburg","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Antoine%20Pesne%20hofdame%20%3B%20Prinzessin%20Amalia%20von%20Preussen%20als%20Amazone.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The princess whose library saved Bach.","bio":"Princess Anna Amalia of Prussia, 1723–1787, was a composer and music curator in Berlin, daughter of Frederick William I, sister of Frederick the Great, and princess-abbess of Quedlinburg. She studied with Johann Philipp Kirnberger, a pupil of Johann Sebastian Bach, so the Bach discipline reached her at first hand, and she worked as a serious contrapuntist at a time when royal amateurs rarely bothered with anything so demanding. The consequence outlives her own compositions. The library she assembled preserved a great deal of Bach's music, and the survival of that repertoire through the decades when it was unfashionable owes much to her collecting. She belongs on this map twice over: as a woman composing rigorous music under her own name, and as the curator without whom part of the tradition would not have reached the nineteenth century intact."},
  "annaamaliaofbrunswickwolfenbuttel":{"wiki":"Duchess Anna Amalia of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Heinsius%20Anna%20Amalia%40Goethe-Museum%20Frankfurt%20a.M.20170819.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The patron who brought Goethe to Weimar.","bio":"Anna Amalia of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel, 1739–1807, a German princess who composed and edited, made her court at Weimar the centre of German literary life. She composed rather than merely commissioned: her setting of Johann Wolfgang von Goethe's Erwin und Elmire is the clearest evidence of a princess working as a musician among writers rather than above them. Her decisive act, though, was bringing Goethe to Weimar — a piece of patronage whose consequences run through the whole of German letters, and the sort of thing that never appears in a work list. As a salonnière and editor she shaped what was written, read and performed around her. She is remembered for what she made possible rather than for what she wrote, which is the proper measure of a patron, and by that measure her record is extraordinary."},
  "marieemmanuellebayonlouis":{"wiki":"Marie Emmanuelle Bayon Louis","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jean-Baptiste%20ROBIN%2C%20Portrait%20de%20Madame%20Louis%20faisant%20de%20la%20musique%2C%20circa%201777%2C%20Paris%2C%20collection%20particuli%C3%A8re.jpg?width=480","blurb":"She made the fortepiano French.","bio":"Marie-Emmanuelle Bayon Louis, 1746–1825, was a French pianist, salonnière and composer whose contemporaries credited her with making the fortepiano popular in France — a claim about influence rather than about sales, and a considerable one at a moment when the new instrument was still displacing the harpsichord. Her surviving music is small but pointed: six keyboard sonatas issued as her opus 1 in 1769, three of them with violin accompaniment, and the two-act opéra comique Fleur d'épine, staged in 1776. Further pieces her contemporaries talked about — more chamber music and opéras comiques, plus the score for La fête de Saint Pierre in 1771 — have not come down to us. She married the architect Victor Louis in 1770 and published thereafter as Louis. The change of name has helped obscure a musician whose taste altered a whole country's instrument."},
  "coronaschroter":{"wiki":"Corona Schröter","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Korona%20Schr%C3%B6ter.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Weimar's singer who set Goethe.","bio":"Corona Schröter, 1751–1802, was a German musician and actress, best known in her own lifetime as a singer, who spent her career at Weimar in the years when that small town was the meeting point of German literature and music. She worked on the stage as an actress as well as a singer, and she composed: songs setting texts by Johann Wolfgang von Goethe and Friedrich Schiller, the two poets whose words would define the German Lied for the century after her. That combination — a performer who set the poetry she sang — is characteristic of how the art song actually came into being, in rooms and theatres rather than in publishers' catalogues. Little of her music has stayed in circulation, but her name sits at the point where Weimar's poetry and its music met."},
  "andregretry":{"wiki":"André Grétry","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Andre%20Gretry%20by%20Elisabeth%20Vigee%20Le%20Brun.png?width=480","blurb":"Opéra comique's most quoted melodist.","bio":"André Grétry, 1741–1813, came from the Prince-Bishopric of Liège, settled in France from 1767 and took French nationality; his career and his fame belong to Paris. He was the great melodist of opéra comique, the genre that mixed sung numbers with spoken dialogue, and his stage works carried his name across Europe. The measure of his standing is who borrowed from him: both Mozart and Beethoven wrote variations on his tunes, which is the eighteenth century's way of recording that a melody had entered common property. Grétry's gift was for the singable, character-defining phrase rather than for symphonic development, and that has cost him with later audiences trained to prize the second over the first. He was also a writer on music. In his own time his melodies travelled further than almost anyone's."},
  "francoisjosephgossec":{"wiki":"François-Joseph Gossec","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fran%C3%A7ois-Joseph%20Gossec%20by%20Antoine%20Vestier.png?width=480","blurb":"Music for the Revolution's festivals.","bio":"François-Joseph Gossec, 1734–1829, lived through the ancien régime, the Revolution and the Empire, and his most distinctive music belongs to the middle of the three. For the revolutionary festivals he wrote for massed public forces, music addressed to crowds rather than to court rooms — one of the moments when European music changed who it was speaking to. He was also a composer of operas, symphonies, string quartets and choral works in the ordinary way, and a founding inspector of the Paris Conservatoire, where he worked alongside Luigi Cherubini in building the institution that would train the next century of French musicians. He taught Joseph Bologne composition and handed him the Concert des Amateurs, an act of professional trust whose consequences reached well beyond one orchestra."},
  "etiennemehul":{"wiki":"Étienne Méhul","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/M%C3%A9hul.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Revolution's foremost opera composer.","bio":"Étienne Méhul, 1763–1817, has been called the most important opera composer working in France during the Revolution, and he was the first composer anyone thought to describe as a Romantic. Trained as an organist, he wrote for the Paris stage in the years when the audience, the subject matter and the politics of opera were all being remade at once. His operas keep faith with the reforms Gluck and Mozart had introduced — drama first, ornament subordinate — carried into a French idiom and a revolutionary decade. That combination of classical discipline with an unmistakable new intensity of tone is why the Romantic label attached to him before it attached to anybody else. He stands between the reform generation and the Romantics who followed, in a stretch of French music usually skipped over, and the neglect says more about the period than about him."},
  "luigicherubini":{"wiki":"Luigi Cherubini","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jean-Auguste-Dominique%20Ingres%20-%20Luigi%20Cherubini%20-%20Google%20Art%20Project.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Médée, and the Conservatoire's iron rule.","bio":"Luigi Cherubini, 1760–1842, was an Italian composer who made his career in Paris and dominated French musical institutions for a generation. His operas and sacred music were the most admired of their kind: Médée is the work that keeps his name alive, and the French rescue opera he perfected is the direct model for Beethoven's Fidelio. Beethoven called him the greatest living composer; Rossini praised and performed his operas. For twenty years he ran the Conservatoire, an institution he had helped shape alongside François-Joseph Gossec, and there his second reputation was made — as an administrator of legendary severity. He once tried to have the young Hector Berlioz thrown out of the library, and Berlioz's Memoirs never forgave him for it. Both reputations are deserved, and the second has done more harm to the first than the music ever warranted."},
  "gasparespontini":{"wiki":"Gaspare Spontini","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Aubry-Lecomte%20-%20Spontini%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Empire grandeur, staged in La Vestale.","bio":"Gaspare Spontini, 1774–1851, was an Italian who became one of the central figures of French opera during the first two decades of the nineteenth century, a composer and conductor who later worked in Berlin. La Vestale is the work that carries his name, mounted with the scale and ceremony that imperial Paris wanted from its theatre, and it remains the clearest surviving example in music of the Empire manner. He composed over twenty works in all. The grandeur he specialised in — massed forces, processional pacing, monumental design — pointed directly toward the French grand opera that followed, even as his own works slipped out of the repertory. Moving between Paris and Berlin, he carried that manner north with him. Spontini is one of those composers whose influence is easier to hear in other people's music than in his own."},
  "jeanfrancoislesueur":{"wiki":"Jean-François Le Sueur","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/J.%20F.%20Le%20Sueur%20cropped.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Berlioz's first and kindest teacher.","bio":"Jean-François Le Sueur, 1760–1837, was a French composer whose reputation rests on his oratorios and his stage works — and, for the purposes of a map like this one, on the pupil who came to him at the Conservatoire. Hector Berlioz found in Le Sueur his first and most sympathetic teacher, in an institution where the young man's instincts were more often treated as faults to be corrected. Le Sueur's own taste ran to the large religious canvas and the dramatic tableau, which was precisely the encouragement that particular pupil needed. He was a conductor and a music educator of standing in his own right, a Conservatoire man through and through, though his music has now fallen away almost entirely. A teacher's monument is a pupil, and few teachers have been left a better one."},
  "franzschubert":{"wiki":"Franz Schubert","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Franz%20Schubert%20by%20Wilhelm%20August%20Rieder%201875%20larger%20version.png?width=480","blurb":"Six hundred songs in thirty-one years.","bio":"Franz Schubert, 1797–1828, wrote more than a thousand works in thirty-one years, among them over six hundred songs, and in doing so made the Lied a serious art rather than a domestic pastime. Gretchen am Spinnrade and Erlkönig show the method complete and early: a piano part that is the drama, a voice that is the character. He studied with Antonio Salieri from 1812 to 1817. The presence of Beethoven in the same city hung over everything he wrote — he carried a torch at Beethoven's funeral and asked to be buried beside him. The baritone Johann Michael Vogl premiered much of the song repertory and toured it with Schubert at the piano, the partnership that got the music heard at all. Die schöne Müllerin and Winterreise, the last three piano sonatas and the String Quintet in C are all his."},
  "carlmariavonweber":{"wiki":"Carl Maria von Weber","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Caroline%20Bardua%20-%20Bildnis%20des%20Komponisten%20Carl%20Maria%20von%20Weber.jpg?width=480","blurb":"German Romantic opera begins with him.","bio":"Carl Maria von Weber, 1786–1826, was a German composer and conductor, a virtuoso on both piano and guitar, a critic — and the crucial figure in the making of German Romantic opera. Der Freischütz is the work that did it: a German subject, German speech and an orchestral colour that told audiences a national opera was possible on its own terms. He had the training for it. As a boy he studied with Michael Haydn at Salzburg in 1797–98, and he worked at Dresden, in the theatre and the pit, where opera is learned from the inside. German opera after him is unimaginable without him. Weber died at forty, having redirected an entire national tradition in a handful of stage works and a career shorter than most composers' apprenticeships."},
  "felixmendelssohn":{"wiki":"Felix Mendelssohn","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Felix%20Mendelssohn%20Bartholdy%20by%20Eduard%20Magnus%20(1833).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The prodigy who gave Bach back.","bio":"Felix Mendelssohn, 1809–1847, was a composer, pianist, organist and conductor whose prodigy years produced the String Octet and the Midsummer Night's Dream overture, and whose maturity produced the Scottish and Italian symphonies, the Violin Concerto, Elijah and the Songs Without Words. Carl Friedrich Zelter trained him in strict counterpoint and introduced him to Goethe, who became a friend across sixty years of age difference — the boy played Bach to the old poet for a fortnight in 1821. Ignaz Moscheles taught him in Berlin and later followed him to run the piano department at the Leipzig Conservatory, which Mendelssohn founded. In 1829, aged twenty, he conducted the revival of Bach's St Matthew Passion that began the Bach revival outright. He championed the young Dane Niels Gade at Leipzig and made him his assistant, and worked alongside Robert Schumann there."},
  "fannymendelssohnhensel":{"wiki":"Fanny Hensel","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fannymendelssohn-improved.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Over 450 works, kept from print.","bio":"Fanny Mendelssohn Hensel, 1805–1847, composed more than 450 works — over 250 songs, more than 125 pieces for solo piano, a string quartet, a piano trio and quartet, an orchestral overture and four cantatas — and most of them went unpublished in her lifetime. Carl Friedrich Zelter taught her and her brother Felix together and rated her as highly as him. Felix published some of her songs under his own name and opposed her going into print at all. Praised for her piano technique, she rarely performed in public outside the family circle. In 1829 she married the painter Wilhelm Hensel, who sketched the Sunday concerts she ran in Berlin for twenty years — a concert series that was, in effect, her own institution. Her music is not a footnote to her brother's; it is a parallel body of work."},
  "robertschumann":{"wiki":"Robert Schumann","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Robert-schumann.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The critic-composer who discovered Brahms.","bio":"Robert Schumann, 1810–1856, was a German composer, pianist and critic, and the most literary musician of his generation. The piano cycles and the songs are his centre — music that behaves like poetry, in short movements with recurring characters and private codes — and he wrote across every genre of his time. He founded the Neue Zeitschrift für Musik and used a magazine as no composer had before: the article Neue Bahnen, in 1853, announced an unknown twenty-year-old named Johannes Brahms to the whole of Europe. His teacher Friedrich Wieck became his landlord and then his implacable opponent at law, fighting to prevent his marriage to Wieck's daughter Clara; they married in 1840, and she premiered and edited most of his music. He was close to Felix Mendelssohn in Leipzig, campaigned for him in print, and named a son after him."},
  "claraschumann":{"wiki":"Clara Schumann","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Franz%20Hanfstaengl%20-%20Clara%20Schumann%20(1857).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The century's pianist and Schumann's editor.","bio":"Clara Schumann, 1819–1896, ranked among the most distinguished pianists the Romantic era produced, with a concert career of sixty-one years, and she changed what a piano recital was — lessening the weight given to purely virtuosic display and building programmes around music worth returning to. She was a composer too — piano solos, a piano concerto, chamber and choral music, songs — and an editor of consequence. Her father Friedrich Wieck trained her, then fought her marriage to Robert Schumann through the courts and lost; from 1840 she premiered and edited most of her husband's music, and after his collapse she carried it into the repertory herself. Her friendship with Johannes Brahms ran forty-three years, sustained by constant correspondence and mutual editing. Few musicians have shaped a century's listening so directly, from the platform and the desk at once."},
  "fredericchopin":{"wiki":"Frédéric Chopin","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Chopin%2C%20by%20Wodzinska.JPG?width=480","blurb":"Poland's poet of the piano.","bio":"Frédéric Chopin, 1810–1849, was a Polish composer and virtuoso pianist who left Warsaw for Paris and wrote almost entirely for solo piano — a concentration with few parallels in music. His standing has never slipped: the judgement handed down is of a poetic genius resting on a professional technique with no equal in his generation, and the two halves are inseparable. The poetry is not vagueness; it is a matter of exact voicing, pedalling and timing, which is why his music is so much harder to play well than to play. The long singing line he took from Vincenzo Bellini's operas — bel canto lifted off the voice and set on the keyboard — runs through his melodies, and explains why pianists talk about breathing in music written for an instrument that cannot breathe. He is the composer against whom pianists have measured themselves ever since."},
  "hectorberlioz":{"wiki":"Hector Berlioz","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Berlioz%20Petit%20BNF%20Gallica-crop.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The orchestra enlarged and set loose.","bio":"Hector Berlioz, 1803–1869, was a French composer, conductor and critic who wrote for an orchestra larger and stranger than the one he inherited, and then wrote the book on it — the Treatise on Instrumentation, which taught the rest of the century how to score. The Symphonie fantastique is the work everyone knows, but the output runs from Harold in Italy through the Requiem and L'Enfance du Christ to the operas Benvenuto Cellini, Béatrice et Bénédict and Les Troyens, and to hybrids of his own devising — Roméo et Juliette, a dramatic symphony, and La Damnation de Faust, a dramatic legend. Jean-François Le Sueur, his first teacher, encouraged him; Luigi Cherubini, running the Conservatoire, tried to have him thrown out of the library, and the Memoirs never forgave it. Niccolò Paganini commissioned Harold and sent him 20,000 francs."},
  "niccolopaganini":{"wiki":"Niccolò Paganini","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/NiccoloPaganini.jpeg?width=480","blurb":"The original demonic virtuoso.","bio":"Niccolò Paganini, 1782–1840, was an Italian violinist, guitarist and composer, the most celebrated violin virtuoso of his day and one of the pillars on which modern violin technique rests. What he did to the instrument survives in the 24 Caprices for solo violin, Op. 1, still the measure of what one player can do alone and endlessly borrowed by later composers as raw material. What he did to musical culture matters just as much: he created the figure of the demonic virtuoso, the performer as a creature set apart, and every later star's mystique descends from it. Hearing him in Paris in 1832 sent Franz Liszt into two years of technical seclusion. He commissioned Harold en Italie from Hector Berlioz and, after hearing the piece, sent the composer 20,000 francs — the grand gesture that was also entirely in character."},
  "louisefarrenc":{"wiki":"Louise Farrenc","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Louise%20Farrenc%20painting.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Conservatoire's only woman professor.","bio":"Louise Farrenc, born in Paris in 1804, was a French composer, a virtuoso at the piano and a teacher through the Romantic decades, and the only woman to hold a professorship at the Paris Conservatoire in the whole of the nineteenth century. Her catalogue leans unusually hard toward instrumental music for a French composer of her generation: three symphonies, a handful of choral works, a large body of chamber pieces and piano music of every kind. Her husband Aristide Farrenc, a publisher, issued her work and brought out Le Trésor des pianistes jointly with her, the one undertaking that carried both their names. Teaching the piano was the steady centre of her working life, and the professorship gave her a standing in French institutional music that no woman of her century matched. She died in Paris in 1875."},
  "johnfield":{"wiki":"John Field (composer)","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/John%20field.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The inventor of the nocturne.","bio":"John Field, born in Dublin in 1782, was an Irish pianist, composer and teacher who made most of his career in St Petersburg. He is widely credited with inventing the nocturne. Several of his contemporaries were writing in a comparable manner, but Field was the first to attach the name to a short character piece built on a singing, cantabile melody floating above a spread, arpeggiated accompaniment. That texture, with the right hand as voice and the left as harp, turned out to be one of the most productive ideas in nineteenth-century piano music, and later composers took it a great deal further than he did. Field was also a celebrated virtuoso and a much-sought teacher, and his long Russian years were given over to performing and to passing the manner on. He died in 1837."},
  "carlfriedrichzelter":{"wiki":"Carl Friedrich Zelter","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Carl-Friedrich-Zelter.jpeg?width=480","blurb":"Mendelssohn's teacher, Goethe's musical adviser.","bio":"Carl Friedrich Zelter, born in Berlin in 1758, was a German composer, conductor, music educator and publisher who came to music sideways: he worked in his father's bricklaying business and qualified as a master in that trade, teaching himself composition on his own account. What he had learned he then passed on with unusual rigour. As Felix Mendelssohn's teacher he drilled the boy in strict counterpoint, the discipline that underpins everything Mendelssohn went on to write, and he taught Fanny Mendelssohn Hensel alongside her brother, rating her gifts just as highly, a judgement his age was not much inclined to make. He was also Goethe's musical adviser, and the channel through which the young Mendelssohn was brought to the poet, one of the formative encounters of the boy's life. Zelter died in Berlin in 1832."},
  "ignazmoscheles":{"wiki":"Ignaz Moscheles","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ignaz%20Moscheles.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Beethoven's associate, Mendelssohn's piano teacher.","bio":"Ignaz Moscheles, born in 1794, was a Bohemian piano virtuoso, conductor, composer and pedagogue, based first in London and later in Leipzig. He belonged to two generations at once. As a young man he worked close to Ludwig van Beethoven, preparing the piano score of Fidelio under the composer's own supervision, which is about as direct a line to that music as anyone could claim. Decades later he was Felix Mendelssohn's teacher in Berlin, and then his friend and colleague: he followed Mendelssohn to Leipzig as professor of piano at the Conservatory, where he ran the piano department. Between the two lay a long career of performing, conducting and composing for his own instrument. The seriousness he had absorbed beside Beethoven is what his pupils carried into the age of the travelling virtuoso. He died in 1870."},
  "josephinelang":{"wiki":"Josephine Lang","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Josephine%20Caroline%20Lang.jpg?width=480","blurb":"A song composer Mendelssohn took trouble over.","bio":"Josephine Caroline Lang, born in Munich in 1815, was a German composer, pianist, singer and teacher who grew up inside music. Her father Theobald Lang was a violinist and her mother Regina Hitzelberger an opera singer, and it was her mother who taught her the piano. Her gift for composing was apparent by the age of five, and she was giving piano lessons herself at eleven. Through her godfather Joseph Stieler she met many of the leading artists of her day, and two of them took real trouble over her: Felix Mendelssohn and Ferdinand Hiller both went to lengths to see that she was properly grounded in the theory of song-writing, and used their own connections to get her music published. Robert Schumann printed one of her songs in the Neue Zeitschrift für Musik in 1838. She died in 1880."},
  "franzliszt":{"wiki":"Franz Liszt","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/FLisztFXD.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Symphonic poet, and the century's most generous teacher.","bio":"Franz Liszt, born in 1811, was a Hungarian composer, a virtuoso pianist, a conductor and a teacher, and at Weimar the presiding figure of a whole musical party, where he invented the symphonic poem. His own training came from the top and came free: Antonio Salieri taught the eleven-year-old composition in Vienna without charge, and Carl Czerny took him for piano in 1822 and 1823 without payment, a debt Liszt repaid by dedicating the Transcendental Études to him. Hearing Niccolò Paganini in Paris in 1832 sent him into two years of technical seclusion. He then gave away what he had been given: Hans von Bülow, his most important pupil and later his son-in-law; Carl Tausig, the formidable technician of the Weimar circle, dead at twenty-nine. He played Grieg's concerto at sight in Rome in 1870 and told him to carry on. He died in 1886."},
  "richardwagner":{"wiki":"Richard Wagner","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/RichardWagner.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Architect of the total artwork.","bio":"Richard Wagner, born in 1813, was a German composer, theatre director, conductor and essayist who, unlike almost anyone else, wrote both words and music for every stage work he made. He began in the Romantic tradition of Weber and Meyerbeer, then rebuilt the genre around the Gesamtkunstwerk, in which poetry, music, staging and drama are one continuously sung argument rather than a chain of arias; the essays of 1849 to 1852 set the theory out and the first half of Der Ring des Nibelungen realised it. Ludwig II of Bavaria paid his debts, staged the operas and underwrote Bayreuth. Otto Wesendonck housed him in Zurich until the affair with Mathilde ended the arrangement; Mathilde wrote the texts of the Wesendonck Lieder, the studies for Tristan. Hans von Bülow conducted the Tristan and Meistersinger premieres, and kept doing so after Wagner took his wife Cosima. He died in 1883."},
  "hansvonbulow":{"wiki":"Hans von Bülow","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Hans%20von%20buelow.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The first modern conductor.","bio":"Hans Guido von Bülow, born in 1830, was a German conductor, pianist and composer, and by common consent the first modern conductor, the man who made interpretation itself a public art. Alongside Carl Tausig he was the most prominent of Franz Liszt's early pupils; he played the first public performance of Liszt's B minor Sonata in 1857, and he fell in love with and married Cosima, Liszt's daughter, who later left him for Richard Wagner. His career was largely spent in the service of other men's music. He conducted the premieres of Tristan und Isolde and Die Meistersinger, and went on conducting Wagner even after Wagner took his wife, and he was just as decisive for Johannes Brahms in an age that treated the two composers as opposites. Noted for his Beethoven, he was among the first European musicians to tour America. He died in 1894."},
  "petercornelius":{"wiki":"Peter Cornelius","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Peter%20cornelius.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Weimar circle's poet-composer.","bio":"Carl August Peter Cornelius, born in the Grand Duchy of Hesse in 1824, was a German composer, poet, translator and writer about music, and one of the more literary members of the circle around Franz Liszt at Weimar. His opera The Barber of Baghdad had its premiere there under Liszt, who had staked his own authority on the piece; the audience booed it, and Liszt resigned his post. It was a small scandal with lasting consequences for both men. In a movement dominated by two enormous personalities, Cornelius was the one who could write the verse as readily as the notes, and he worked across all of it, composing, translating, writing about music and teaching at university level. He died in 1874."},
  "joachimraff":{"wiki":"Joachim Raff","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jjraffportrait.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Liszt's assistant and orchestrator at Weimar.","bio":"Joseph Joachim Raff, born in Switzerland in 1822, was a composer, pianist and pedagogue whose most consequential years were spent as Franz Liszt's assistant at Weimar. The work was largely invisible: he served as Liszt's orchestrator as well as his aide, and in later life he made no secret of his resentment about how much of it he had done. Whatever the justice of the grievance, it places him exactly, inside the New German School's workshop rather than at its podium. Nothing about the position was glamorous, but a movement that produced orchestral music on that scale depended on musicians who could actually score at speed, and Raff was one of them. He composed steadily on his own account throughout, and taught, and Wiesbaden was where he settled. He died in 1882."},
  "carltausig":{"wiki":"Karl Tausig","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Carl%20Tausig%20-%20Project%20Gutenberg%20eText%2016431.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Liszt's most formidable pupil, dead at twenty-nine.","bio":"Karl Tausig, born in Congress Poland in 1841, was a piano virtuoso, arranger, composer and teacher, and by wide agreement the greatest of Franz Liszt's pupils, the most formidable technician of a Weimar circle that was not short of them. He settled in Berlin. What survives of him is mostly other people's music remade for the keyboard: his transcriptions, above all of J. S. Bach, and his sets of piano exercises, which generations of students have worked through without necessarily knowing whose hand shaped them. He died in 1871 at twenty-nine, before he had had time to become anything else, and he is still regularly named among the greatest pianists who ever lived. What he might have grown into is one of the open questions of the century's piano playing."},
  "ludwigiiofbavaria":{"wiki":"Ludwig II of Bavaria","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Albert%2C%20Joseph%20-%20K%C3%B6nig%20Ludwig%20II.%20im%20Ornat%20des%20Gro%C3%9Fmeisters%20vom%20Georgi-Ritterorden%20(Zeno%20Fotografie).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The king who paid for Bayreuth.","bio":"Ludwig II, born in 1845, reigned as King of Bavaria from 1864 until his death in 1886, and the most consequential musical patron of the nineteenth century. He was not a composer; he was an art collector by temperament, remembered as the Swan King and the Fairy Tale King. What he did for Richard Wagner was entirely concrete. He paid Wagner's debts at a point when they threatened to end his career, put the operas on stage, and underwrote the building of the festival theatre at Bayreuth. That last is the part that outlasted everyone involved: a theatre built to one composer's specification, and the institution that carried his ideas long past his death. Bayreuth exists because a young king decided to spend a kingdom's money on it. Ludwig died in 1886."},
  "franzbrendel":{"wiki":"Franz Brendel","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/FranzBrendel.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The critic who named the New German School.","bio":"Karl Franz Brendel, born in 1811 at Stolberg to a successful mining engineer, was a German music critic, journalist, musicologist and teacher, and the man who gave a movement its name. Working in Leipzig, he coined the term New German School in 1859, deliberately gathering the very different causes of Franz Liszt and Richard Wagner under a single banner so that they could be argued for, and against, as one programme. It was an act of criticism that shaped how the century understood itself: partisans and opponents alike accepted the label, and the long war between the New Germans and their conservative opponents was fought over ground Brendel had mapped. He wrote from the centre of that argument until his death in 1868."},
  "johannesbrahms":{"wiki":"Johannes Brahms","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/JohannesBrahms.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Vienna's great traditionalist, and Dvořák's champion.","bio":"Johannes Brahms, born in Hamburg in 1833, was a German composer, pianist and conductor who settled in Vienna and became the standard by which the century's conservatives measured everything else. Robert Schumann announced him to Europe in the 1853 article Neue Bahnen, when he was an unknown twenty-year-old; Joseph Joachim had made the introduction, and later premiered the Violin Concerto he had helped Brahms write. After Schumann's collapse came forty-three years of friendship, correspondence and mutual editing with Clara Schumann. Eduard Hanslick made him the yardstick against which Wagner was found wanting. Brahms then did for others what had been done for him, putting Antonín Dvořák forward for the Austrian state stipend, taking him to his own publisher and correcting his proofs unasked. He sent almost everything in manuscript to Elisabeth von Herzogenberg, whose criticism he trusted above anyone's. He died in 1897."},
  "josephjoachim":{"wiki":"Joseph Joachim","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Joseph%20Joachim.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The century's violinist, and Brahms's closest ally.","bio":"Joseph Joachim, born in Hungary in 1831, was a violinist, conductor, composer and teacher whose international career was based in Hanover and Berlin, and who is reckoned among the most distinguished violinists of the nineteenth century. He began inside the opposing camp: he was Franz Liszt's concertmaster at Weimar, then broke with the New German School and in 1860 co-signed the manifesto against it, one of the sharpest defections in musical politics. His lasting alliance was with Johannes Brahms, whom he introduced to the Schumanns and whose Violin Concerto he helped shape and then premiered; the piece is as much a record of that friendship as anything either man wrote. In Berlin he taught and conducted, and the standard of playing he set became the tradition that followed him. He died in 1907."},
  "eduardhanslick":{"wiki":"Eduard Hanslick","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Hanslick.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Vienna's leading critic, and Wagner's antagonist.","bio":"Eduard Hanslick, born in 1825, was an Austrian critic, aesthetician and historian of music, and from 1864 until the end of his life the chief music critic of Vienna's Neue Freie Presse, which put him among the leading and most contested critical voices in European music. His 1854 treatise Vom Musikalisch-Schönen set out his philosophical and artistic position on what music is, and became a landmark in musical aesthetics. In practice he used the argument as a weapon: he made Johannes Brahms the standard against which Richard Wagner was to be found wanting. Wagner repaid him in kind, naming the pedant of Die Meistersinger 'Veit Hanslich' in an early draft and reading the libretto to his face in 1862. He was also a professor and a historian of the art he judged. He died in Vienna in 1904."},
  "heinrichvonherzogenberg":{"wiki":"Heinrich von Herzogenberg","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Herzogenberg.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Taught Ethel Smyth and Alberto Nepomuceno.","bio":"Heinrich Picot de Peccaduc, Freiherr von Herzogenberg, born in 1843, was an Austrian composer, conductor and composition teacher descended from a French aristocratic family. He worked in Leipzig, a stronghold of the conservative German musical world, and composed in the inherited forms that circle defended while the New German School argued that the forms themselves had to go. As a teacher he sent that very German training in unexpected directions: Ethel Smyth studied with him in Leipzig from 1877, and Alberto Nepomuceno with him in Berlin, both of them carrying the technique away into music of their own. He conducted too, and wrote about music, which is the part of the work the old label musicographer was meant to cover. He died in 1900."},
  "elisabethvonherzogenberg":{"wiki":"Elisabeth von Herzogenberg","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Herzogenberg-E%26H.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The critic Brahms trusted above anyone.","bio":"Elisabeth von Herzogenberg, born Elisabeth von Stockhausen in 1847, was a German pianist, singer, composer and philanthropist, and the person whose judgement Johannes Brahms trusted more than anyone else's. He sent her almost everything he wrote in manuscript, and took her criticism seriously in a way he took almost nobody's, which makes her, on the strength of judgement alone, one of the shaping presences inside his music. Leipzig, where she lived, was one of the strongholds of that conservative circle, and she stood at its centre as a pianist and singer as well as a judge of other people's manuscripts. She was also the consuming attachment of Ethel Smyth's Leipzig years, and the reason Smyth was eventually expelled from the circle, so her influence runs through two very different bodies of work. She died in 1892."},
  "theodorkirchner":{"wiki":"Theodor Kirchner","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Theodor%20Kirchner%20-%20Pastellgem%C3%A4lde%20von%20Julie%20de%20Boor%201898.JPG?width=480","blurb":"A Romantic composer-pianist working in Zurich.","bio":"Fürchtegott Theodor Kirchner, born in the Kingdom of Saxony in 1823, was a German composer, pianist and conductor of the Romantic era, active in Zurich. He belongs on this map with the Brahms circle and the musical conservatives, the composers who went on working in inherited forms while the New German School argued that the forms themselves had to go. He performed and conducted as well as composed, and his eighty years span the whole arc of that quarrel, from its opening shots to a point at which both sides had been overtaken. Zurich kept him at a slight remove from where that quarrel was loudest. He is one of many capable musicians of that generation whose names now survive chiefly through the circle they belonged to. He died in 1903."},
  "robertfuchs":{"wiki":"Robert Fuchs (composer)","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Robert%20Fuchs%2C%201926.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Vienna's teacher of Mahler and Sibelius.","bio":"Robert Fuchs, born in Austria in 1847, was a composer, conductor, arranger and, above all, a teacher. Teaching music theory as a professor at the Vienna Conservatory, he trained a remarkable share of the next generation: Gustav Mahler, Hugo Wolf and Alexander Zemlinsky all passed through his classes there, and Jean Sibelius studied with him in Vienna in 1890 and 1891. They are four musicians who went on to sound nothing like each other, and nothing much like Fuchs, which is a fair measure of what a good theory teacher actually does. He was a highly regarded composer in his own lifetime, working in the conservative Viennese idiom of the Brahms circle, though his music is now heard far less often than his pupils' names are spoken. He died in 1927."},
  "gioachinorossini":{"wiki":"Gioachino Rossini","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Composer%20Rossini%20G%201865%20by%20Carjat.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Thirty-nine operas, then thirty-nine silent years.","bio":"Gioachino Antonio Rossini, born in 1792, was an Italian composer and conductor working across the late Classical and early Romantic eras, working chiefly in Naples and then Paris. He wrote thirty-nine operas, raising the standard of comic and serious opera alike, and then, at the height of his popularity and still in his thirties, he retired from large-scale composition. Nearly four decades of near-silence followed; the chamber music, piano pieces and sacred works sit around the edges of that vast operatic achievement. His authority survived the retirement intact. When José White Lafitte reached Paris, Rossini's public praise was enough on its own to open the concert circuit to him. Rossini died in 1868, having spent more of his life as a legend than as a working composer for the stage."},
  "vincenzobellini":{"wiki":"Vincenzo Bellini","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Lucchini-Bellini.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The long bel canto melody.","bio":"Vincenzo Bellini, born in the Kingdom of Sicily in 1801, was an Italian opera composer, an early Romantic and a central figure of bel canto, whose working life ran through Milan and was over before he was thirty-five. He trained under Nicola Zingarelli at the Naples Conservatory. What he is loved for is the long melody: graceful, unhurried lines that balance florid embellishment against a deceptively simple way of setting a lyric, in settings whose atmosphere does as much dramatic work as the words. Norma is the summit of it. Other composers admired him quite as much as the public did, and one in particular carried the lesson away: the long bel canto line is what Frédéric Chopin transferred from the voice to the piano. Bellini died in 1835."},
  "gaetanodonizetti":{"wiki":"Gaetano Donizetti","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fotoreproductie%20van%20Gaetano%20Donizetti.png?width=480","blurb":"Seventy operas of the bel canto age.","bio":"Gaetano Donizetti, born in Bergamo in 1797, was an early Romantic Italian composer who wrote more than seventy operas and, with Rossini and Bellini, led the bel canto style through the first half of the nineteenth century. He owed his start entirely to Simon Mayr, who took him up as a boy, enrolled him with a full scholarship at the school he had set up in Bergamo, gave him a detailed musical training, then obtained him a place at the Bologna Academy and found him his first commissions. At nineteen came a first one-act opera, the comedy Il Pigmalione, which may well have gone unstaged while he lived. His later career was divided between Naples and Paris, and he was a probable influence on Giuseppe Verdi. He died in 1848."},
  "giuseppeverdi":{"wiki":"Giuseppe Verdi","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Giuseppe%20Verdi%20xilografia.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Italian opera from Nabucco to Falstaff.","bio":"Giuseppe Verdi, born near Busseto in 1813 to a family of moderate means, was the composer who carried Italian opera from Nabucco to Falstaff. A local patron, Antonio Barezzi, helped pay for his musical education; when the Milan Conservatory turned him down he took private lessons from Vincenzo Lavigna instead. His break came from the La Scala impresario Bartolomeo Merelli, who put the libretto of Nabucco into his hands. The soprano Giuseppina Strepponi sang in that opera, then lived with him for a decade before they married, to local scandal. His publisher Giulio Ricordi negotiated for him and brokered the reconciliation with Arrigo Boito after a twenty-year estrangement, which produced Otello and Falstaff. He could be generous to younger men: of Antônio Carlos Gomes's Il Guarany he is reported to have said it was the work of a truly musical genius. He died in 1901."},
  "arrigoboito":{"wiki":"Arrigo Boito","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Arrigo%20Boito.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The librettist of Otello and Falstaff.","bio":"Arrigo Boito, born in 1842, was an Italian librettist, composer, poet, critic and journalist, working in Milan. His own music amounts to one completed opera, Mefistofele, and it is as a writer of words for other men's music that he altered the course of Italian opera. He wrote the libretto of Amilcare Ponchielli's La Gioconda, and then, after a twenty-year estrangement from Giuseppe Verdi that was finally patched up, he gave Verdi the texts of Otello and Falstaff, the two operas of Verdi's old age. Working with a poet of that calibre is a large part of why those last works sound like nothing else in the repertoire. Boito also wrote criticism and journalism, and remained one of the most literate men in Italian music until his death in 1918."},
  "saveriomercadante":{"wiki":"Saverio Mercadante","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Saverio%20Mercadante%20by%20Andrea%20Cefaly.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The bridge from bel canto to Verdi.","bio":"Giuseppe Saverio Raffaele Mercadante, born in 1795, was an Italian composer, particularly of operas, who spent much of his career in Naples as a chapelmaster and director as well as a composer and musicologist. His fame did not outlive him as Bellini's, Donizetti's and Rossini's did, yet he was quite as prolific as any of them, and his importance lies in what he made possible: the operatic structures he developed, his way with melody and his handling of the orchestra are among the foundations Giuseppe Verdi built his dramatic technique upon. He was a working professional of Italian musical life rather than a celebrity. If bel canto is usually told through three names, Mercadante is the fourth, and the one standing nearest to what came next. He died in 1870."},
  "amilcareponchielli":{"wiki":"Amilcare Ponchielli","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Amilcare%20Ponchielli%20(before%201886)%20-%20Archivio%20Storico%20Ricordi%20FOTO000794%20-%20Restoration.jpg?width=480","blurb":"La Gioconda, and Puccini's teacher.","bio":"Amilcare Ponchielli, born in 1834, was an Italian opera composer, conductor and music educator based in Milan, and by a long way the best known of his operas is La Gioconda. His wife was the soprano Teresina Brambilla. His larger claim on this map, though, is what he handed on. At the Milan Conservatory he taught both Giacomo Puccini and Pietro Mascagni, who shared lodgings while they were his students, and who between them went on to define the next age of Italian opera. A good deal of the craft they carried into their own theatre came out of Ponchielli's classroom, which is the kind of influence that never shows up in a list of works. He also conducted and worked as a musicologist. He died in 1886."},
  "giacomomeyerbeer":{"wiki":"Giacomo Meyerbeer","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Meyerbeer%20d'apr%C3%A8s%20P.%20Petit%20b%201865.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Grand opéra at its most lavish.","bio":"Giacomo Meyerbeer, born in the Kingdom of Prussia in 1791, was a German opera composer who made Paris his stage and became the most frequently performed opera composer of the nineteenth century, the figure who links Mozart and Wagner. With Robert le diable in 1831 and the works that followed he gave grand opéra its decisive character: German orchestral writing married to the Italian vocal tradition, poured into the sensational and melodramatic libretti of Eugène Scribe and mounted with all the up-to-date machinery the Paris Opéra could supply. The result set a standard that helped keep Paris the opera capital of the century, and no composer working in France afterwards could ignore it. He also conducted, directed music and left an autobiographical record. He died in 1864."},
  "charlesgounod":{"wiki":"Charles Gounod","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Charles%20Gounod%20(1890)%20by%20Nadar.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The composer of Faust.","bio":"Charles-François Gounod, born in Paris in 1818, was a French composer, organist and conductor of the Romantic era. Twelve operas came from him. Faust, of 1859, has been the popular favourite from the first, while Roméo et Juliette of 1867 also holds its place in the international repertoire. Alongside the stage works he produced a great deal of church music, many songs and short pieces that travelled everywhere, his Ave Maria and the Funeral March of a Marionette among them. He was a generous mentor as well: from Georges Bizet's student years onward Gounod was his advocate and his model, and the lyric French manner Bizet took into his own operas is hard to imagine without him. Gounod also taught, wrote on music and served as an organist. He died in 1893."},
  "georgesbizet":{"wiki":"Georges Bizet","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Georges%20bizet.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The composer of Carmen.","bio":"Georges Bizet, born in Paris in 1838, was a French composer of the Romantic decades whose career was cut short by his early death. He trained at the Paris Conservatoire under Fromental Halévy, whose daughter Geneviève he later married, so that his teacher became his father-in-law; Charles Gounod was his advocate and mentor from his student years onward. Success mostly eluded him. He achieved few real triumphs until his final work, Carmen, which went on to become one of the best-loved and most often staged operas in the whole repertoire, a piece whose directness and refusal of decorum changed what opera was permitted to be about. He also conducted and was a formidable pianist. Bizet died in 1875, without seeing what Carmen would become."},
  "julesmassenet":{"wiki":"Jules Massenet","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jules%20Massenet%20by%20Eug%C3%A8ne%20Pirou%2C%20edit%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Manon, and a generation of Conservatoire pupils.","bio":"Jules Émile Frédéric Massenet, born in 1842, was a French composer of the Romantic decades who wrote more than thirty operas, of which Manon of 1884 and Werther of 1892 are the two most frequently staged. Around them sit ballets and oratorios, orchestral and incidental music, piano pieces and songs, the output of a working theatre composer with an unfailing ear for the singing line. He trained at the Paris Conservatoire with Ambroise Thomas, and returned there to teach a whole generation of French composers. That teaching makes him one of the most consequential French musicians of his century, a fact his reputation as a purveyor of charm has tended to obscure. He also worked as a musicologist and a university teacher. He died in Paris in 1912."},
  "jacquesoffenbach":{"wiki":"Jacques Offenbach","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jacques%20Offenbach%20by%20F%C3%A9lix%20Nadar%20(restored).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Operetta turned into social satire.","bio":"Jacques Offenbach, born in Germany in 1819, was a composer, cellist, conductor and theatre entrepreneur who became thoroughly French and turned the operetta into an instrument of social satire. Between the 1850s and the 1870s he wrote nearly a hundred of them, quick, tuneful and formally exact pieces whose absurd plots carried a good deal of comment about the society watching, and which he mounted himself in theatres he ran. His influence on the genre was decisive: Franz von Suppé, Johann Strauss II and Arthur Sullivan all worked on ground he had cleared. He died in 1880 leaving The Tales of Hoffmann unfinished; it remains his one work in the standard opera repertory, while the operettas were revived throughout the twentieth century and are staged still."},
  "camillesaintsaens":{"wiki":"Camille Saint-Saëns","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Camille%20Saint-Saens%20b%20Meurisse%201921.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Co-founder of the Société Nationale de Musique.","bio":"Charles-Camille Saint-Saëns, born in Paris in 1835, was a French composer, organist, conductor and pianist whose long working life reached from the Romantic era well into the twentieth century. The list of what stayed in the repertoire is unusually long: the Introduction and Rondo Capriccioso of 1863, the Second Piano Concerto, the First Cello Concerto, Danse macabre, the opera Samson and Delilah, the Third Violin Concerto, and both the Organ Symphony and The Carnival of the Animals from 1886. In 1871 he co-founded the Société Nationale de Musique with César Franck to give French composers a platform of their own, though the two later found themselves on opposite sides of it. He brought the symphonic poem to France and dedicated the Third Symphony to Liszt's memory. He taught Gabriel Fauré, beginning a friendship of sixty years. He died in 1921."},
  "cesarfranck":{"wiki":"César Franck","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/C%C3%A9sar%20Franck%20by%20Pierre%20Petit.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The organ loft and its devoted disciples.","bio":"César Franck, born in 1822 in what is now Belgium, was a French Romantic composer, organist, pianist and teacher whose base was the organ loft at Sainte-Clotilde in Paris. His deepest influence ran through his pupils; la bande à Franck was the most devoted circle in nineteenth-century French music. Vincent d'Indy, the most doctrinaire of them, went on to co-found the Schola Cantorum in 1894 to restore chant and Palestrina to the centre of training. Ernest Chausson studied with him at the Conservatoire, and Henri Duparc became the mélodie's other great practitioner. Augusta Holmès took private lessons because, as a woman, she could never be admitted to a Conservatoire composition class. In 1871 he co-founded the Société Nationale de Musique with Camille Saint-Saëns, and later opposed him within it. He died in 1890."},
  "gabrielfaure":{"wiki":"Gabriel Fauré","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Faur%C3%A9Pirou1905.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Master of the French mélodie.","bio":"Gabriel Urbain Fauré, born in 1845, was a French composer, organist, pianist and teacher, and the central figure of the French mélodie. He studied with Camille Saint-Saëns at the École Niedermeyer, which began a friendship of sixty years. His most familiar pieces, the Pavane, the Requiem, the Sicilienne, the piano nocturnes and songs such as Après un rêve and Clair de lune, are mostly early or middle-period, yet he composed many of his most highly regarded works late, in a harmonically and melodically far more elusive style. Running the Paris Conservatoire made him decisive as a teacher: he kept Maurice Ravel on after the Prix de Rome scandal, taught Nadia Boulanger, and taught Charles Koechlin, who orchestrated Pelléas et Mélisande for him. The Dolly Suite was written for the daughter of Emma Bardac. He died in 1924."},
  "edouardlalo":{"wiki":"Édouard Lalo","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/%C3%89douard%20Lalo2.jpeg?width=480","blurb":"The Symphonie espagnole's composer.","bio":"Édouard-Victoire-Antoine Lalo, born in 1823, was a French composer, violinist and violist who also taught, and who came to composition by way of a string player's training. He worked in the Paris of the salon and the Société Nationale, among a generation arguing about what French music ought to be and setting out to build an instrumental repertoire that could stand beside the opera house. His most celebrated piece belongs squarely to that project: the Symphonie espagnole, a violin concerto in five movements that has never left the standard repertoire. Writing from inside the instrument gave his string music a practical brilliance, which is a large part of why the concerto outlasted so much else that Paris applauded in those years. He died in 1892, at sixty-nine."},
  "vincentdindy":{"wiki":"Vincent d'Indy","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Vincent%20d'Indy%201914.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Founded the Schola Cantorum de Paris.","bio":"Paul Marie Théodore Vincent d'Indy, born in 1851, was a French composer whose influence as a teacher was greater still. He was César Franck's pupil and the most doctrinaire member of la bande à Franck, and in 1894 he co-founded the Schola Cantorum de Paris with the explicit aim of restoring chant and Palestrina to the centre of musical training, a conservative revolution mounted against the Conservatoire, where he also taught. His pupils were startlingly various and not at all doctrinaire in their results: Albéric Magnard, Albert Roussel, Arthur Honegger, Darius Milhaud, Yvonne Rokseth, Cole Porter, and Erik Satie, who came to the Schola from 1905 to 1912. He also taught Xian Xinghai there, and Ahmed Adnan Saygun from 1928 to 1931. He conducted and wrote about music. He died in 1931."},
  "ernestchausson":{"wiki":"Ernest Chausson","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/A-Ernest%20Chausson.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Franck's pupil, and a Romantic to the end.","bio":"Amédée-Ernest Chausson, born in Paris in 1855, was a French Romantic composer who also worked as a conductor and a pianist. He studied with César Franck at the Paris Conservatoire and belonged to the circle that formed around him, the group that carried Franck's habits of mind into the next French generation. What those pupils shared was less a style than a seriousness about instrumental music, in a city that had long measured its composers by their operas; the Société Nationale existed to give exactly that music a hearing. Chausson worked at the centre of it, in the Paris of the salon as much as the concert hall. He died in 1899, at forty-four, with the best of his generation's work still ahead of it."},
  "cecilechaminade":{"wiki":"Cécile Chaminade","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Cecile%20chaminade.jpg?width=480","blurb":"First woman composer given the Légion d'Honneur.","bio":"Cécile Louise Stéphanie Chaminade, born in Paris in 1857, was a French composer, pianist and flautist, and by a wide margin the most commercially successful woman composer of her time. She worked as a performing pianist as well as a composer, and her music belongs to the world of the salon and the recital that ran alongside the French opera house and the Société Nationale. The Légion d'Honneur came to her in 1913, the first time that decoration had gone to a woman composer. That mattered beyond her own career: it registered officially, and in the most conventional language available, that such a person existed and had a body of work behind her. She lived until 1944, long outlasting the musical culture that had made her famous."},
  "augustaholmes":{"wiki":"Augusta Holmès","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Augusta%20Holmes.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Franck's pupil who wrote her own words.","bio":"Augusta Mary Anne Holmès, born in 1847, was a composer of British and Irish origin who became French, and who sometimes published under the pseudonym Hermann Zenta. She studied privately with César Franck, private lessons being the only route open to her, since as a woman she could not be admitted to a Conservatoire composition class. She wrote the words to almost all of her own vocal music — the songs, the oratorios, the text of her opera La Montagne noire, and the verse programmes behind symphonic poems such as Irlande and Andromède. That double authorship is the point of her, a composer who controlled the subject as well as its setting, in a Paris that preferred its women at the piano. She was a poet and writer as well. She died in 1903."},
  "mikhailglinka":{"wiki":"Mikhail Glinka","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Michail-Glinka-(Repin).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The fountainhead of Russian classical music.","bio":"Mikhail Ivanovich Glinka, born in 1804, was the first Russian composer to win wide recognition inside his own country, and is generally treated as the fountainhead of Russian classical music. A Life for the Tsar gave Russia an opera on a Russian subject that could stand in any European theatre. What makes him a beginning rather than an isolated case is the transmission: he met the teenaged Mily Balakirev and handed him the nationalist programme directly, and Balakirev built The Five around it. Every Russian composer of the following generation worked in some relation to Glinka's example, whether extending his fusion of folk material with Western practice or arguing against it. He worked in St Petersburg and died in 1857."},
  "alexanderdargomyzhsky":{"wiki":"Alexander Dargomyzhsky","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Makovskiy%20Dargomyzhsky.jpg?width=480","blurb":"He set Russian speech to music.","bio":"Alexander Sergeyevich Dargomyzhsky, born in 1813, was a Russian composer and pianist who worked in St Petersburg and stands, in the history of Russian opera, between Mikhail Glinka and the generation that followed him — The Five, and Tchaikovsky. His most radical work is The Stone Guest, in which the vocal line follows the rhythms and inflections of Russian speech rather than the shapes of song, an idea that sounds like theory and turned out to be entirely practical. Modest Mussorgsky took it as the direct model for Boris Godunov, which means that the most unmistakably Russian music of the century runs back through Dargomyzhsky's experiment. He died in 1869, having pointed Russian opera toward a manner it would go on to make its own."},
  "milybalakirev":{"wiki":"Mily Balakirev","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/%D0%9C%D0%B8%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%B9%20%D0%91%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%BA%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B2.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The organiser and teacher of The Five.","bio":"Mily Alexeyevich Balakirev, born in 1837, was a Russian composer, pianist and conductor whose importance lies less in his own scores than in what he organised. Mikhail Glinka met him as a teenager and handed him the nationalist programme directly; Balakirev extended Glinka's fusion of folk material and experimental practice, and developed musical patterns capable of carrying overt national feeling. Then he assembled The Five and taught them, their only formal training being Balakirev going through scores with them. He persuaded Alexander Borodin, a working chemist, to write symphonies, made the naval officer Nikolai Rimsky-Korsakov finish a symphony at sea, and drilled Modest Mussorgsky and César Cui. For twenty years he led the self-taught nationalists against Anton Rubinstein's Conservatory professionals. A breakdown and long sabbatical ended that influence. He died in 1910."},
  "modestmussorgsky":{"wiki":"Modest Mussorgsky","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Modest%20Musorgskiy%201876%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Boris Godunov's uncompromising creator.","bio":"Modest Petrovich Mussorgsky, born in 1839, was a Russian composer, one of The Five, and the most uncompromising of them: he wanted a specifically Russian musical identity and was willing to defy the established conventions of Western music to get it. His only formal training was Mily Balakirev going through scores with him. From Alexander Dargomyzhsky's The Stone Guest he took the practice of setting the rhythms of speech directly, and made it the foundation of Boris Godunov; Pictures at an Exhibition did something comparable at the piano, character before polish. In 1871 he shared a room with Nikolai Rimsky-Korsakov, the two working at the same piano on alternate days. Rimsky-Korsakov later edited and rewrote much of his friend's music, which is how most of the world first heard it. He died in 1881."},
  "alexanderborodin":{"wiki":"Alexander Borodin","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Borodin.jpg?width=480","blurb":"A professor of chemistry who composed at weekends.","bio":"Alexander Porfiryevich Borodin, born in 1833 of Georgian and Russian parentage, was a Russian Romantic composer and a chemist, and the chemistry was not the hobby: he was a serious scientist who fitted composition into his weekends. He belonged to The Five, the group that set out to make a uniquely Russian kind of classical music, and he got there because Mily Balakirev persuaded a working chemist that he ought to be writing symphonies. What came of that persuasion is a small and unusually durable body of work: the symphonies, two string quartets, the symphonic poem In the Steppes of Central Asia and the opera Prince Igor. He was also a doctor by training, and played the piano and the flute. He died in 1887, remembered by two professions at once."},
  "nikolairimskykorsakov":{"wiki":"Nikolai Rimsky-Korsakov","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nikolai%20A.%20Rimsky-Korsakov.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The great orchestrator, and everyone's teacher.","bio":"Nikolai Rimsky-Korsakov, born in 1844, was a Russian composer, conductor and writer, and the great orchestrator among the composers of The Five. Mily Balakirev found him a serving naval officer and made him finish a symphony at sea; the orchestral colour he later commanded made Scheherazade, Capriccio Espagnol and the Russian Easter Festival Overture, along with suites from his fifteen operas, permanent repertoire. In 1871 he shared a room with Modest Mussorgsky, the two working at the same piano on alternate days, and he later edited and rewrote much of his friend's music. As a teacher he shaped nearly everyone who followed: Alexander Glazunov privately from 1879, Ottorino Respighi, who came to St Petersburg in 1900 for orchestration, Maximilian Steinberg, who married his daughter Nadezhda, and Igor Stravinsky, whose lessons of 1905 to 1908 were the only training he ever had."},
  "cesarcui":{"wiki":"César Cui","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Cui%20CuiIP%20Frontispiece%20600.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Critic-in-chief of the Five, and army general.","bio":"César Cui, born in 1835, was a Russian composer and music critic who ran two careers at once and let neither interrupt the other. In the Imperial Russian Army he rose all the way to engineer-general, lectured on fortification at the Russian military academies and put out a series of monographs on the subject. In St Petersburg's musical life he was the man with the pen. Mily Balakirev drew him into the group of composers known as The Five, gathered around the idea of creating a specifically Russian kind of music, and Cui became its critic-in-chief, making the group's case in print and defending its members. He belonged later to the Belyayev circle as well, and went on composing steadily alongside the fortifications and the journalism until his death in 1918."},
  "pyotrilyichtchaikovsky":{"wiki":"Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Portr%C3%A4t%20des%20Komponisten%20Pjotr%20I.%20Tschaikowski%20(1840-1893).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Russia's first internationally beloved composer.","bio":"Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky, born in 1840, was the first Russian composer to leave a lasting impression on audiences beyond Russia. He trained under Anton Rubinstein at the St Petersburg Conservatory from 1862 to 1865, and it was Rubinstein's brother Nikolai who gave him his Moscow post, savaged the First Piano Concerto on first hearing and then played it everywhere. For thirteen years Nadezhda von Meck supported him financially through a correspondence of some 1,200 letters, conducted on the agreed condition that the two of them never meet. Out of that working life came the symphonies, the Violin Concerto, the 1812 Overture and the Romeo and Juliet Overture-Fantasy, along with Eugene Onegin for the opera stage and the ballets Swan Lake, The Nutcracker and The Sleeping Beauty. His pupil Sergei Taneyev became his most exacting critic, and the editor of the work he left unfinished when he died in 1893."},
  "antonrubinstein":{"wiki":"Anton Rubinstein","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Anton%20Rubinstein%20by%20J%20Ganz.png?width=480","blurb":"Founded the Conservatory the nationalists despised.","bio":"Anton Rubinstein, born in 1829, was a Russian pianist, composer and conductor, and the founder of the Saint Petersburg Conservatory. That institution is his largest legacy: it brought systematic professional training to Russian music, and it made him the standing enemy of the self-taught nationalists around Mily Balakirev, with whom he was at odds for some twenty years — conservatory professionals on one side, self-taught nationalists on the other. The work ran in the family as well: his younger brother Nikolai founded the Moscow Conservatory, so that between them the two Rubinsteins built the training system of an entire country. Among his own pupils was Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky, who studied with him in St Petersburg from 1862 to 1865. Rubinstein died in 1894, a celebrated pianist and a still more consequential administrator."},
  "nikolairubinstein":{"wiki":"Nikolai Rubinstein","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nikolai%20Rubinstein%201872.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Moscow's conservatory founder, Tchaikovsky's champion.","bio":"Nikolai Rubinstein, born in 1835, was a Russian pianist, conductor, composer and teacher, and the founder of the Moscow Conservatory. Anton Rubinstein was his elder brother, and his own founding gave Moscow the institution through which professional musical training reached the city. His closest musical friendship was with Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky, whom he brought to Moscow and gave a post there, and whose career he set in motion. Their most famous exchange concerned the First Piano Concerto: Rubinstein savaged it on first hearing, then changed his mind and played it everywhere he went, so that the piece owed its early circulation to the man who had condemned it. He died in 1881, his conservatory and his advocacy outlasting his own playing."},
  "sergeitaneyev":{"wiki":"Sergei Taneyev","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Taneev.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Tchaikovsky's pupil; Rachmaninoff and Scriabin's teacher.","bio":"Sergei Taneyev, born in 1856, was a Russian composer, pianist, music theorist, author and teacher, and one of the great transmitting figures of Moscow musical life. He came up as the pupil of Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky and then reversed the relationship, becoming his teacher's most exacting critic and, after Tchaikovsky's death, the editor of the work he had left unfinished. His own reputation rests as much on rigour as on repertoire: a theorist of counterpoint who wrote about music as seriously as he wrote it. At the Moscow Conservatory he taught counterpoint to Sergei Rachmaninoff and had Alexander Scriabin in his classes too, so that two composers who went on to sound nothing like each other, or like him, were both grounded in his exactness. He died in 1915."},
  "nikolaizverev":{"wiki":"Nikolai Zverev","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nikolai-Zverev.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The boarding teacher who trained them all.","bio":"Nikolai Zverev, born in 1832, was a Russian pianist, teacher and one-time civil servant who became the most important trainer of young pianists in Moscow. He is remembered less for his playing than for his pupils — Sergei Rachmaninoff, Alexander Scriabin, Alexander Siloti, Konstantin Igumnov and Alexander Goldenweiser among them. His method was total. He boarded his best pupils in his own flat, where their practice, their days and their manners were all his to arrange, and the results were formidable. Rachmaninoff grew up in that household; Scriabin came to the same rooms as a day pupil. Two of the most individual Russian pianist-composers of the following generation were therefore formed side by side, under one roof and one disciplinarian, before either had a style of his own. Zverev died in 1893."},
  "nadezhdavonmeck":{"wiki":"Nadezhda von Meck","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nadezhda%20von%20Meck.jpeg?width=480","blurb":"Bought Tchaikovsky thirteen years of freedom.","bio":"Nadezhda von Meck, born in 1831, was a Russian businesswoman and one of the most consequential patrons in nineteenth-century music. She was not a composer; what she supplied was money and sustained attention, and the results appear on other people's title pages. For thirteen years she supported Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky financially so that he could give up other work and devote himself to composition, corresponding with him across some 1,200 letters while stipulating that the two of them were never to meet — a condition both of them kept. His Symphony No. 4 in F minor carries a dedication to her. Her patronage reached well beyond him: among the musicians she helped was the eighteen-year-old Claude Debussy, whom she employed as her household pianist and took with her to Russia, Italy and Vienna. She died in 1894."},
  "bedrichsmetana":{"wiki":"Bedřich Smetana","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/FriedrichSmetana.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The father of Czech music.","bio":"Bedřich Smetana, born in 1824, was a Czech composer, conductor and teacher who pioneered a musical style bound up with his people's hopes for a cultural and political revival, and who has been regarded at home ever since as the father of Czech music. Abroad he is known for two things: the comic opera The Bartered Bride of 1866, and the symphonic cycle Má vlast, which sets out the history, legends and landscape of his native Bohemia and contains the river piece Vltava, still widely known by its German name Die Moldau. He was also a working man of the theatre, conducting at Prague's Provisional Theatre, where the young Antonín Dvořák sat in the viola section from 1862 and played under his baton from 1866 — the older man's example passing directly to the younger. He died in 1884."},
  "antonindvorak":{"wiki":"Antonín Dvořák","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dvorak.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Bohemian folk music given symphonic form.","bio":"Antonín Dvořák, born in 1841, was a Czech composer, organist, conductor and professor who took the rhythms and inflections of Bohemian and Moravian folk music into the symphonic tradition, following the nationalist example of Bedřich Smetana, in whose Provisional Theatre orchestra he had played viola from 1862 and under whose baton he played from 1866. Johannes Brahms put him forward for the Austrian state stipend, sent him to his own publisher Fritz Simrock and corrected his proofs unasked; the Slavonic Dances made fortunes for composer and publisher alike. Jeanette Thurber then paid him twenty-five times his Prague salary to run the National Conservatory in New York, where his student Harry T. Burleigh sang him spirituals for hours and the New World Symphony followed. He taught Josef Suk, who married his daughter Otilie, and walked the countryside with his younger friend Leoš Janáček."},
  "leosjanacek":{"wiki":"Leoš Janáček","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Leo%C5%A1%20Jan%C3%A1%C4%8Dek.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Speech-melody, notated from life.","bio":"Leoš Janáček, born in 1854, was a Czech composer, teacher, folklorist and theorist of music who worked from Brno rather than Prague. He collected and studied Moravian and other Slavic folk music, including the folk music of eastern Europe, and built from it a language that was both deeply rooted and unmistakably modern. His most distinctive habit was notating speech-melody from life: the rise and fall of ordinary talk, written down as he heard it and carried into his music, so that his vocal lines follow the shape of the spoken language. He was a friend of Antonín Dvořák, thirteen years his senior, and the two took walking holidays together across a long friendship. Born a subject of the Austrian Empire, he died in 1928 a citizen of Czechoslovakia, claimed by folk modernism as much as by national romanticism."},
  "zdenekfibich":{"wiki":"Zdeněk Fibich","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Zdenek%20Fibich.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Czech romantic behind the Poème.","bio":"Zdeněk Fibich, born in 1850, was a Czech composer, conductor, choirmaster and teacher, and one of the busiest figures of Prague musical life in the second half of the nineteenth century. His output was unusually wide: symphonic poems, three symphonies, chamber works, at least seven operas, liturgical music including a missa brevis, and a substantial body of melodrama, among it the large Hippodamia trilogy. Most personal of all is the piano cycle Moods, Impressions and Reminiscences, a long sequence that served him as a sort of diary of his love for a piano pupil. One piece from that cycle became the short instrumental Poème, and it is by the Poème — a fragment of a private record, detached from the diary it belonged to — that he is chiefly remembered today. He died in 1900."},
  "josefsuk":{"wiki":"Josef Suk (composer)","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Josef%20Suk(1).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Dvořák's pupil, and then his son-in-law.","bio":"Josef Suk, born in 1874, was a Czech composer and violinist, and the closest thing Antonín Dvořák had to an heir. He studied with Dvořák at the Prague Conservatory and then married his teacher's daughter Otilie, so that the professional relationship became a family one. The family losses that followed came close together and took everything at once: Otilie and Dvořák both died within fourteen months, so that Suk lost his wife and his teacher in little more than a year. He remained a performing violinist as well as a composer, and taught in his turn, carrying the Dvořák line into the next generation of Czech musicians. He was also, improbably, a silver medallist at the 1932 Summer Olympics. He died in 1935, a Czechoslovak citizen born under Austrian rule."},
  "stanisawmoniuszko":{"wiki":"Stanisław Moniuszko","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Stanis%C5%82a%C5%AD%20Maniu%C5%A1ka.%20%D0%A1%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%96%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%B0%D1%9E%20%D0%9C%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%8E%D1%88%D0%BA%D0%B0%20(T.%20Maleszewski%2C%201865).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The father of Polish national opera.","bio":"Stanisław Moniuszko, born in 1819, was a Polish composer, conductor, organist and teacher, generally called the father of Polish national opera. He worked in Warsaw at a time when Poland had no state of its own, and his music is packed with patriotic folk material drawn from the peoples of the old Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth — a repertoire of shared song standing in for a country that did not exist on the map. He wrote many popular art songs alongside the stage works, and his operas Halka and The Haunted Manor became the founding pieces of the Polish operatic repertoire. Because the Commonwealth's inheritance was never only Polish, he has since the 1990s also been recognised in Belarus as an important figure in Belarusian culture. He died in 1872."},
  "ferencerkel":{"wiki":"Ferenc Erkel","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Erkel%20Ferenc%20Gy%C3%B6rgyi%20Alajos.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The father of Hungarian grand opera.","bio":"Ferenc Erkel, born in 1810, was a Hungarian composer, conductor, pianist and teacher, and the father of Hungarian grand opera. His stage works, written mainly on historical subjects, gave Hungary a national operatic repertoire where none had existed, and they are still regularly performed there. He also wrote the music of Himnusz, adopted in 1844 as the national anthem of Hungary — so that a nation acquired both its operas and its anthem from the same hand. He worked as a conductor and a pianist as well as a composer, and taught alongside all of it. Away from music he was a serious chess player, a second career of a kind. A subject of the Austrian Empire who spent his working life making Hungarian music Hungarian, he died in Budapest in 1893."},
  "edvardgrieg":{"wiki":"Edvard Grieg","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/(Edvard%20Grieg%20portrait)%20(3470673354).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Norwegian folk music made world repertoire.","bio":"Edvard Grieg, born in Bergen in 1843, was a Norwegian composer and pianist whose use of his country's folk music brought Norwegian music to international attention and helped shape a national identity, much as Smetana's did in Bohemia and Sibelius's in Finland. In Copenhagen, Niels Gade told the young man to go home and write a symphony. In Rome in 1870 Franz Liszt read through the Piano Concerto at sight and told him to carry on, which settled the matter of whether he should. He married his first cousin Nina Hagerup, a singer, and it was she who gave his songs their first voice. His teaching carried further than he travelled: his Norwegian pupil Walborg Bang married the composer Alberto Nepomuceno, who translated Grieg into Portuguese. He died in 1907."},
  "carlnielsen":{"wiki":"Carl Nielsen","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Carl%20Nielsen%20c.%201908%20-%20Restoration.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Denmark's foremost composer and conductor.","bio":"Carl Nielsen, born in 1865, was a Danish composer, conductor and violinist, and is widely recognised as his country's most prominent composer. He worked from Copenhagen, and his career runs from the late Romantic decades into the twentieth century, ending with his death in 1931. He came to composition from inside the orchestra, as a playing violinist, and conducting remained part of his working life alongside writing — a practical musician's training that stayed audible in what he wrote. He also set down his own story, leaving an autobiographical account of his life to go with the music. Denmark places no composer above him, and among the musicians of the Nordic countries in his generation few are so completely identified with a single country's music."},
  "jeansibelius":{"wiki":"Jean Sibelius","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jean%20Sibelius%20in%201890%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Seven symphonies, then thirty years of silence.","bio":"Jean Sibelius, born in 1865, was a Finnish composer working across the late Romantic and early modern periods, and is generally held to be his country's greatest. He studied in Vienna with Robert Fuchs in 1890 and 1891, and returned to a Finland then under repeated attempts at Russification; his music is credited with having helped the country develop a stronger sense of its own identity at exactly that moment, which made him a national figure long before he was an international one. He was a conductor and a university teacher as well as a composer. The shape of his career is as famous as its content: seven symphonies, and then some thirty years of silence, lived out in the independent Finland that the Grand Duchy of his birth had become. He died in 1957, long outliving his own last work."},
  "nielsgade":{"wiki":"Niels Gade","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Niels%20Gade%20by%20Georg%20Weinhold.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Mendelssohn's Danish deputy at Leipzig.","bio":"Niels Gade, born in 1817, was a Danish composer, conductor, organist, violinist and teacher, and with Johan Peter Emilius Hartmann the leading Danish musician of the Danish Golden Age. His career turned on a piece of foreign advocacy: Felix Mendelssohn performed the young Dane's music at Leipzig and then made him his assistant, giving him the training and the standing that a post at Leipzig conferred. Gade brought that authority home to Copenhagen, where he spent the rest of his working life at the centre of Danish concert and teaching institutions. His pupils included Edvard Grieg, whom he sent away with an instruction that shaped a career — go home and write a symphony. He died in 1890, having passed on what Leipzig had given him."},
  "franzberwald":{"wiki":"Franz Berwald","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Franz%20Berwald%20Painting.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Sweden's symphonist, appreciated far too late.","bio":"Franz Berwald, born in 1796, was a Swedish Romantic composer and violinist whose reputation arrived after he did. Music did not pay him: he made his living first as an orthopaedist and then by managing a saw mill and, after it, a glass factory, and composed around the working week. The result is an output of real ambition — several operas, a large body of chamber music and four symphonies — written by a man whose contemporaries mostly did not know what to do with it. He was also active as a teacher and a writer on music. Appreciation came only after his death in 1868, when Sweden and then the wider concert world caught up with the symphonies, and the reputation he never had in his lifetime has belonged to him ever since."},
  "johansvendsen":{"wiki":"Johan Svendsen","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Portrett%20av%20komponist%20og%20dirigent%20Johan%20Svendsen%20(1840-1911)%20(34133117716)%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Norway's symphonist, Copenhagen's conductor.","bio":"Johan Severin Svendsen, born in Christiania in 1840, was a Norwegian composer, conductor and violinist. He belongs to the generation that gave the Nordic countries a concert life of their own, and his working energy was divided between writing music and standing in front of orchestras, with conducting claiming a large share of it. Although Norwegian by birth and formation, he spent most of his life in Copenhagen, so that Danish musical life had as much of his career as Norwegian did and his biography runs between the two cities. He was a trained violinist before he was anything else, and the orchestra remained the medium he understood from the inside. He died in 1911, his name attached to both of the capitals he worked in."},
  "agathebackergrndahl":{"wiki":"Agathe Backer Grøndahl","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Agathe%20Backer%20Gr%C3%B8ndahl.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Norway's pianist-composer, championed by her son.","bio":"Agathe Backer Grøndahl, born in 1847, was a Norwegian pianist and composer who worked in Oslo, and one of the relatively few women of her generation in the Nordic countries to sustain a public career as performer, teacher and composer at once. The three occupations fed one another: she wrote for the instrument she played, and taught what she knew of both. Her music belongs to the Romantic decades in which Norwegian concert life was building its own institutions and its own audience, and it was written by someone who was also playing and teaching inside them. Her son Fridtjof Backer-Grøndahl, born in 1885 and himself a pianist and composer, took up her cause after her, programming his mother's compositions in his own concerts and keeping them in circulation. She died in 1907."},
  "felipepedrell":{"wiki":"Felip Pedrell","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Felipe%20Pedrell%2001.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The theorist behind Spanish musical nationalism.","bio":"Felipe Pedrell, born in 1841, was a Catalan composer, guitarist, musicologist, critic and teacher, and the theorist who supplied Spanish musical nationalism with its argument. His own compositions matter less than the case he made and the pupils he made it to: that a Spanish art music could be built from the country's own inherited material rather than imported ready-made from abroad. He taught in Barcelona, where Isaac Albéniz and Enrique Granados both came to him, and in Madrid, where Manuel de Falla became his pupil from 1902. Between them, those three pupils wrote much of the Spanish music that the rest of Europe came to know. He worked as a music critic as well, arguing the same case in print. He died in 1922."},
  "isaacalbeniz":{"wiki":"Isaac Albéniz","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Isaac%20Alb%C3%A9niz%2C%20de%20Napoleon.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Iberia: Spain written for the piano.","bio":"Isaac Albéniz, born in 1860, was a Spanish virtuoso pianist, composer and conductor whose working life ran between Barcelona and Paris. He studied with Felipe Pedrell in Barcelona, and what he made of that training was pianistic: a body of keyboard work that absorbs the idioms and elements of Spanish folk music into writing of real difficulty and real atmosphere. The suite Iberia, written between 1905 and 1908, is the summit of it, and is counted among the masterpieces of the post-Romantic era; it influenced both European classical music and the Spanish nationalist current that followed. He moved in the orbit of the Generation of '98, and exerted a marked influence on his contemporaries and on the younger composers who came after him. He died in 1909."},
  "enriquegranados":{"wiki":"Enrique Granados","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Enric%20Granados%20-%20Ilustraci%C3%B3%20Catalana.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Goyescas, and the Catalan piano school.","bio":"Enrique Granados, born Pantaleón Enrique Joaquín Granados Campiña in 1867, was a Spanish and Catalan pianist and composer, and one of the defining figures of the modern Catalan school of piano — the tradition begun by Pere Tintorer, to which Isaac Albéniz, Joaquín Malats and other pianists also belonged. He studied in Barcelona with Felipe Pedrell, and like his fellow pupils there he turned what he took from those lessons into keyboard music rather than argument. His best-known work is Goyescas, in which a national inheritance is filtered through a pianist's hands. Barcelona was his base throughout, and the two halves of his working life fed one another: he wrote for the instrument he played in public, and he played what he wrote. Much of his reputation was still ahead of him when he died in 1916."},
  "edwardelgar":{"wiki":"Edward Elgar","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Edward%20Elgar%201857%20-%201934.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Ended England's land-without-music reputation.","bio":"Edward Elgar, born in 1857, was an English composer and conductor whose Enigma Variations put an end to England's reputation as a land without music. From a provincial base around Worcester he built a body of work that entered the international repertoire: the Pomp and Circumstance Marches, concertos for cello and for violin, two symphonies, the choral Dream of Gerontius, and a quantity of chamber music and song. In 1924 he became Master of the King's Musick. Two people made the career possible. His pupil Alice, who became his wife, organised a career out of a provincial violin teacher; and August Jaeger, Novello's editor and the Nimrod of the Enigma Variations, talked him out of giving up. He passed the favour on, recommending Samuel Coleridge-Taylor to the Three Choirs Festival in 1898 as \"far and away the cleverest fellow going amongst the young men\". He died in 1934."},
  "arthursullivan":{"wiki":"Arthur Sullivan","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Arthur%20Sullivan%201898cr.jpg?width=480","blurb":"One half of Gilbert and Sullivan.","bio":"Arthur Sullivan, born in 1842, was an English composer, organist, conductor and teacher, best known for the fourteen comic operas he wrote with the dramatist W. S. Gilbert, among them The Mikado, The Pirates of Penzance and H.M.S. Pinafore — a partnership so complete that it has never quite let him be remembered for anything else. The rest of the catalogue is large and various: twenty-four operas in all, eleven major orchestral works, two ballets, ten choral works and oratorios, incidental music for several plays, and a long list of church pieces, songs, and piano and chamber music. Two of his shorter pieces, the hymn Onward, Christian Soldiers and the song The Lost Chord, were sung far beyond the theatres. He worked in London throughout, and died in 1900."},
  "hubertparry":{"wiki":"Hubert Parry","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Hubert%20Parry%20c1893cr.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Jerusalem, and the Royal College of Music.","bio":"Hubert Parry, born at Richmond Hill, Bournemouth in 1848, was an English composer, teacher and music historian whose first major works appeared in 1880. He is best known now for the choral song Jerusalem, for the coronation anthem I was glad of 1902, for the ode Blest Pair of Sirens, and for the hymn tune Repton, set to Dear Lord and Father of Mankind. The orchestral output is larger than the reputation suggests: five symphonies and the Symphonic Variations. He also wrote the music of Ode to Newfoundland, the national anthem of the Dominion of Newfoundland. His teaching at the Royal College of Music mattered as much as his composing — Ralph Vaughan Williams was among his pupils — and his historical writing gave English music a sense of its own past. He died in 1918."},
  "charlesvilliersstanford":{"wiki":"Charles Villiers Stanford","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Charles%20Villiers%20Stanford%20c1894bw.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Taught a whole English generation, argumentatively.","bio":"Charles Villiers Stanford, born in Dublin in 1852 into a prosperous and highly musical family, was an Anglo-Irish composer, conductor and teacher of the late Romantic era. He was educated at Cambridge and then studied in Leipzig and Berlin, and on his return was instrumental in raising the Cambridge University Musical Society to a body that could attract international stars to perform with it. His largest legacy is a teaching one. At the Royal College of Music he taught Samuel Coleridge-Taylor from 1890 to 1897, Gustav Holst, Frank Bridge, and Ralph Vaughan Williams, whose lessons with him were mostly arguments. George Butterworth, killed on the Somme at thirty-one, belonged to the same Royal College generation. Very nearly the whole first wave of the English musical revival passed through his room. He died in 1924."},
  "ethelsmyth":{"wiki":"Ethel Smyth","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ethel%20Smyth.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Wreckers, and prison for suffrage.","bio":"Ethel Smyth, born in 1858, was an English composer, librettist, conductor and autobiographer, and a campaigner in the women's suffrage movement who went to prison for it. Her catalogue covers songs, piano works, chamber music, orchestral and choral works and a series of operas, of which The Wreckers is the best known. Her training was German: she went to Leipzig from 1877 to study with Heinrich von Herzogenberg, and entered the circle around him. The defining attachment of those years was to Elisabeth von Herzogenberg — a consuming relationship, and ultimately the reason Smyth was expelled from the circle that had formed her. She wrote about her own life at length and with force, and the memoirs are part of the achievement. She died in 1944."},
  "frankbridge":{"wiki":"Frank Bridge","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Frank%20Bridge-Edwin%20Marcus.png?width=480","blurb":"Britten's teacher and earliest advocate.","bio":"Frank Bridge, born in 1879, was an English composer, violist and conductor who worked in London. He came up through the Royal College of Music as a pupil of Charles Villiers Stanford, and made his living as a player and a conductor as well as a writer of music — a working professional's career rather than an academic one. His largest single act of influence was taking on a boy of thirteen named Benjamin Britten and teaching him properly. Britten later said that Bridge was the only English composer he thought had taught him anything, and the relationship remained the young man's most important early support at a time when he had few other advocates in English music. Bridge went on composing, playing and conducting to the end of his life, and died in 1941, better remembered for that one pupil than for his own catalogue."},
  "frederickdelius":{"wiki":"Frederick Delius","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Frederich%20Delius%20portrait%20(5427720404)%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Bradford merchant's son turned composer.","bio":"Frederick Delius, born in Bradford in 1862 to a prosperous mercantile family, was an English composer who spent much of his working life away from England. He resisted every attempt to recruit him into commerce, and in 1884 was sent to Florida to manage an orange plantation — a posting whose managerial duties he soon neglected. By 1886 he was back in Europe, and the settled part of his career was made at Grez-sur-Loing in France, where he lived and worked. His music sits at an angle to the English tradition he was born into: it is grouped as often with Impressionism and Symbolism as with the English composers of his own generation, and the life behind it — Bradford, Florida, France — explains some of the distance. He died in 1934."},
  "antoniocarlosgomes":{"wiki":"Antônio Carlos Gomes","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Carlos%20Gomes%20portrait%2C%201886.tif?width=480","blurb":"The New World's first Italian-opera success.","bio":"Antônio Carlos Gomes, born in 1836, was a Brazilian composer and pianist, and the first composer from the New World whose work was accepted in Europe. Dom Pedro II of Brazil paid for the Milan studies that produced Il Guarany, and the opera reached La Scala in 1870. It made him the only non-European to build a successful career in Italian opera through its golden age, working as a contemporary of Verdi and Puccini — Verdi is reported to have called Il Guarany the work of a truly musical genius. Of mixed race, he was also the first musician of non-European descent admitted to the classical tradition on those terms, an achievement that had no precedent to follow. His life ran between Rio de Janeiro and Milan, and he died in 1896."},
  "samuelcoleridgetaylor":{"wiki":"Samuel Coleridge-Taylor","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Samuel%20Coleridge-Taylor.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Hiawatha made him Britain's most-performed composer.","bio":"Samuel Coleridge-Taylor, born in London in 1875 to a Sierra Leonean father and an English mother, was a British composer and conductor. He studied with Charles Villiers Stanford at the Royal College of Music from 1890 to 1897, and Edward Elgar recommended him to the Three Choirs Festival in 1898 as the cleverest of the young men then coming up. That year, at twenty-three, he premiered the first part of his three cantatas on Longfellow's The Song of Hiawatha, and the success made him the most performed British composer of his generation. Harry T. Burleigh brought him spirituals in London that became the Twenty-Four Negro Melodies; Booker T. Washington wrote its preface and arranged the three American tours on which New York musicians called him the African Mahler. He gave joint recitals with the poet Paul Laurence Dunbar, and set his verse. He died in 1912."},
  "josewhitelafitte":{"wiki":"José White Lafitte","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jos%C3%A9%20White%201856.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Havana violinist who topped the Conservatoire.","bio":"José Silvestre White Lafitte, born in 1836 and of Afro-Cuban descent, was a Cuban-French violinist and composer — Joseph White to his Paris audiences — whose career ran between Havana and Paris. He studied at the Paris Conservatoire with Jean-Delphin Alard, and took the premier prix in 1856, a year after enrolling and ahead of the entire class. The result was a virtuoso career built on technical authority that could not be argued with, at a moment when a Black soloist in Europe was required to prove it twice over. Gioachino Rossini's public praise in Paris opened the concert circuit to him, and he worked it for decades as a performer and as a composer. He remained a French musician as much as a Cuban one, and died in 1918."},
  "edmonddede":{"wiki":"Edmond Dédé","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Edmond%20Dede%20-%20Musiker%20und%20Komponist.jpg?width=480","blurb":"New Orleans Creole, conductor in Bordeaux.","bio":"Edmond Dédé, born in New Orleans in 1827, was an American composer, violinist and conductor, and a free-born Creole of colour. In 1855 he left Louisiana for Europe, and for good. In Paris he studied at the Conservatoire with Fromental Halévy, and then settled in Bordeaux, where he spent the rest of his working life — first as the Grand Théâtre's assistant conductor and afterwards leading orchestras at other theatres in the city, a podium career of some twenty-five years within a French residency that lasted more than forty. It is an emigration story as much as a musical one: a musician who found in provincial France the professional standing that his own country would not give a free man of colour. He died in 1901."},
  "ignaciocervantes":{"wiki":"Ignacio Cervantes","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ignacio%20Cervantes.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Cuban dance material at the piano.","bio":"Ignacio Cervantes, born in Havana in 1847, was a Cuban pianist and composer, and one of the influential figures in the creolization of Cuban music — the process by which the island's mixed inheritance became a written art music of its own. His training was French: he studied at the Paris Conservatoire with Charles-Valentin Alkan, alongside Marmontel, and came home with a European keyboard schooling behind him. He turned it homeward. His Danzas cubanas take Cuban dance material and treat it with the concentration of concert writing, and it is on them that his reputation has rested since — the island's own rhythm, written for the piano by a pianist who had been trained in Paris to take it seriously. He worked from Havana for the rest of his life, and died in 1905."},
  "manuelsaumell":{"wiki":"Manuel Saumell","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Saumell.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Turned the contradanza into concert music.","bio":"Manuel Saumell Robredo, born in Havana in 1817, was a Cuban composer, pianist and conductor, and the man credited with inventing genuinely creolized forms of Cuban music. He was the first to cultivate a Cuban musical nationalism — to treat the material of the island not as local colour but as the basis of an art music — and he did it above all through the contradanza, which he was the first to shape into a concert form. The comparison usually made is with Glinka, who set Russian musical nationalism going with A Life for the Tsar at almost exactly the same moment on the other side of the world, and Saumell is held in Cuba to have a similar standing. He worked in Havana all his life as a composer, pianist and conductor, and died in 1870."},
  "chiquinhagonzaga":{"wiki":"Chiquinha Gonzaga","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Chiquinha%206a.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Brazil's first woman to conduct an orchestra.","bio":"Francisca Edwiges Neves Gonzaga, known everywhere as Chiquinha Gonzaga, was born in Rio de Janeiro in 1847. A composer, pianist and conductor of mixed race, she became the first woman to conduct an orchestra in Brazil — a fact that says as much about what she had to push through as about what she achieved. Composing, playing and conducting were all part of one working life, and she was an activist as well as a musician, putting a public profile to work for causes as readily as for concerts. Rio was her city from beginning to end, and hers was a long working life rather than a single celebrated occasion. She died in 1935, by then a national figure in Brazilian music."},
  "albertonepomuceno":{"wiki":"Alberto Nepomuceno","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Alberto%20Nepomuceno.png?width=480","blurb":"Insisted opera could be sung in Portuguese.","bio":"Alberto Nepomuceno, born in 1864, was a Brazilian composer, conductor, musicologist and teacher who worked from Rio de Janeiro. His training was German — he studied in Berlin with Heinrich von Herzogenberg — and he brought that schooling home to a musical culture in which opera was not expected to be sung in the country's own language. His central argument, pressed against real opposition, was that it could be: that a Brazilian audience was entitled to hear Portuguese in its own opera house, and that Brazilian music could be written in it. He also looked north, marrying Walborg Bang, Edvard Grieg's Norwegian pupil, and translating Grieg into Portuguese, importing one small national tradition to help make another. He died in 1920."},
  "blindtomwiggins":{"wiki":"Blind Tom Wiggins","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blind%20Tom%20LCCN2002712734%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"A celebrated pianist who was never free.","bio":"Thomas Wiggins, known as Blind Tom, was born enslaved in Georgia in 1849, and became an American pianist and composer with one of the longest and most successful performing careers of the nineteenth century. He toured throughout the United States, had numerous original compositions published, and was among the best-known concert pianists in the country and among the best-known Black musicians of the era. The success was real; the control of it was not his. His concert career was conducted from beginning to end under the direction of the people who had owned him, and their hold on it outlasted emancipation entirely. He stands in the record as two things at once — a widely admired musician of the American circuit, and a man whose professional life was never his own property. He died in 1908."},
  "teresacarreno":{"wiki":"Teresa Carreño","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Teresa%20Carreno%202.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Valkyrie of the Piano.","bio":"Teresa Carreño, born in Caracas in 1853, was a Venezuelan pianist, composer, conductor and soprano whose concert career lasted fifty-four years and made her one of the most renowned virtuosos in the world, known as the Valkyrie of the Piano. Louis Moreau Gottschalk gave her her first lessons in New York at eight; at ten she played for Lincoln. She taught the boy Edward MacDowell, was an early adopter of his music and premiered his concerto around the world twenty years later, and she performed Grieg constantly. She composed roughly seventy-five works — for solo piano, for voice and piano, for choir and orchestra and for instrumental ensemble — and other composers wrote for her in return, among them Amy Beach and MacDowell himself. Her life ran between Caracas and Berlin, and she died in 1917."},
  "justinelie":{"wiki":"Justin Elie","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Justin%20Elie.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Haitian méringue written for the concert hall.","bio":"Justin Elie, born in Port-au-Prince in 1883, was a Haitian composer and pianist, and one of the best-known Haitian composers outside his own country. His working life ran between Port-au-Prince and Paris, the two poles of a Haitian musician's career in his generation, and the Paris years sit behind music whose material is emphatically local. His central project was to take the Haitian méringue — the island's own dance — and give it concert form, writing it out for the recital platform rather than the dance floor. That move, from popular inheritance to written art music, is the same one being made across the Americas in his lifetime, and Elie made it for Haiti. He remains among the most widely known of Haitian composers, and died in 1931."},
  "ludoviclamothe":{"wiki":"Ludovic Lamothe","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ludovic%20Lamothe.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Haiti's Black Chopin at the keyboard.","bio":"Ludovic Lamothe, born in Port-au-Prince in 1882, was a Haitian composer and virtuoso pianist, and is considered one of the most important classical composers his country has produced. The two descriptions belong together in his case: he was a performing virtuoso first, and the music he wrote came from the hands of a man who played it himself. He was known as the Black Chopin of Haiti — a label that names both the instrument he lived at and the Romantic keyboard tradition his writing came out of, and that measures him, as such comparisons always do, against a European yardstick. He worked from Port-au-Prince rather than abroad, so that his career belongs directly to Haitian concert life rather than to the emigrations of some of his contemporaries. He died in 1953, after a long working life at the centre of music in the capital."},
  "amybeach":{"wiki":"Amy Beach","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Amy%20March%20(Cheney)%20Beach%2C%201867-1944%2C%20head%20and%20shoulders%2C%20facing%20slightly%20left%20LCCN2006690272.jpg?width=480","blurb":"America's first successful woman composer.","bio":"Amy Marcy Cheney Beach, born in 1867, was an American composer and pianist, and the first American woman to succeed as a composer of large-scale art music. She worked from Boston, and made her name in a double career as performer and composer at a time when a woman was expected to keep to the first of those. The Gaelic Symphony of 1896 is the work that established the claim: a full-scale symphony written by an American woman and performed as such, at a moment when neither the nationality nor the sex was expected on a symphonic title page. She belongs with the composers of the Americas who set about building a concert repertoire of their own rather than importing one wholesale. She died in 1944."},
  "antonbruckner":{"wiki":"Anton Bruckner","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Joseph%20Anton%20Bruckner%20(1868).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The organist who built cathedral symphonies.","bio":"Anton Bruckner, born in 1824 and working in Vienna until his death in 1896, was an Austrian composer, organist, theorist and teacher whose nine symphonies he revised almost without end. They are usually taken as the last stage of Austro-German Romanticism: enormous in span, densely polyphonic, harmonically rich, and full of dissonances, unprepared modulations and roving keys that struck his contemporaries as radical. Alongside them stands a body of sacred music, including Masses, a Te Deum and motets. He dedicated his Third Symphony to Richard Wagner and was, to his cost, adopted by the Wagner party in Vienna, a banner he had not asked to march under. Gustav Mahler attended his lectures in Vienna and made the four-hand arrangement of that same Third Symphony, one of the threads by which Bruckner's scale and patience reached the next generation."},
  "gustavmahler":{"wiki":"Gustav Mahler","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gustav-Mahler-Kohut.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The symphony as a whole world.","bio":"Gustav Mahler, born in 1860 and dead in 1911, divided his working life between Vienna and New York, and his identity between two callings. As a conductor he was reckoned among the finest of his generation, his authority never questioned in his lifetime. As a composer he was the bridge from the nineteenth-century tradition into early modernism, holding to the idea that a symphony must be like the world and contain everything. He studied at the Vienna Conservatory with Robert Fuchs, attended Bruckner's lectures and arranged his Third Symphony for four hands. He defended Arnold Schoenberg in public, bought his paintings, and left instructions that the younger man be looked after. In 1902 he married Alma Mahler on condition that she stop composing, relenting only in 1910, too late. His own music was neglected, banned in much of Nazi-era Europe, and rediscovered after 1945."},
  "hugowolf":{"wiki":"Hugo Wolf","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Postcard-1910%20Hugo%20Wolf.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Lied rebuilt on Wagnerian speech.","bio":"Hugo Wolf, born in 1860 and dead in Vienna in 1903, was an Austrian composer and music critic remembered above all for his songs. He met Richard Wagner as a boy, and built the whole of his song writing on Wagnerian declamation, letting the voice follow the shape and stress of the words rather than a tune laid over them. What he brought to the Lied was a concentrated expressive intensity with no real parallel in late Romantic music, close to the concision the Second Viennese School would later prize even though his technique went another way. He had studied at the Vienna Conservatory with Robert Fuchs, and he earned his living for years by his pen, working in Vienna as a music critic. A short and difficult life left the German song wholly transformed."},
  "richardstrauss":{"wiki":"Richard Strauss","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Postcard-1910%20Strauss%20Richard.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Tone poems, then Salome and Elektra.","bio":"Richard Strauss, born in 1864 and working between Munich and Garmisch, was a German composer and conductor, and the central figure in music's passage out of late Romanticism into the early modern era. His reputation was made first by the tone poems, the orchestral works in which he set stories and characters going in sound, and then by the operas, above all 'Salome' and 'Elektra', which carried that harmonic daring into the theatre. He conducted and directed orchestras throughout his life, and worked on occasion as a librettist himself. He lived until 1949, long enough to see the harmonic language he had helped to loosen taken far past him by younger composers, and long enough for his own name to stand for a whole era of German music that ended with him."},
  "maxreger":{"wiki":"Max Reger","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Reger.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Bach filtered through chromatic saturation.","bio":"Max Reger, born in 1873 in the Kingdom of Bavaria and dead in 1916 at forty-three, was an organist and pianist by trade, a conductor and an academic teacher, and a composer whose music is often described as Bach filtered through chromatic saturation: old contrapuntal habits pushed to the edge of tonal legibility. His working life was that of a busy professional musician rather than a bohemian. He toured as a concert pianist, served as musical director at the Leipzig University Church, held a professorship at the Royal Conservatory in Leipzig, and took charge of the music at the Saxe-Meiningen court of Duke George II. He wrote about music as well as making it. His death in 1916 cut short a career of extraordinary industry, and he left a body of work in which German counterpoint survived the chromatic pressure of his own time."},
  "alexanderzemlinsky":{"wiki":"Alexander von Zemlinsky","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Zemlinsky.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Schoenberg's only teacher, and his brother-in-law.","bio":"Alexander Zemlinsky, born in Vienna in 1871 and dead in 1942, was an Austrian Jewish composer, conductor, teacher, writer and art collector, and one of the great connecting figures of Viennese musical life. Trained at the Vienna Conservatory under Robert Fuchs, he gave Arnold Schoenberg the only formal composition teaching he ever had, and the tie became a family one when Schoenberg married his sister Mathilde in 1901. He also taught the young Alma Mahler, whose lover he briefly was before Mahler, and in Vienna he taught Erich Wolfgang Korngold. His own music, late Romantic in its warmth and steadily more modern in its harmony, stood in the shadow of his pupils and of his own reputation as a conductor. He belongs to both worlds this map divides: the fin de siècle he came from, and the Second Viennese School he made possible."},
  "sergeirachmaninoff":{"wiki":"Sergei Rachmaninoff","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sergei%20Rachmaninoff%2C%20pianist%20(SAYRE%201608)%20(restored).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Romanticism's last great pianist-composer.","bio":"Sergei Rachmaninoff, born in 1873 and dead in 1943, made his life first in Moscow and then in New York. His contemporaries counted him among the very best pianists then alive, and he stands as one of the last great Romantics in Russian music. He grew up in the flat of Nikolai Zverev, who boarded his pupils under his own roof, and Alexander Scriabin grew up in it beside him; the two were companions and rivals from boyhood. At the Moscow Conservatory he studied counterpoint with Sergei Taneyev. Early debts to Tchaikovsky and Rimsky-Korsakov hardened into an idiom wholly his own: melody that sings, counterpoint laid on thick, orchestral colour used richly, and the piano at the centre of it, its expressive and technical reach explored by a man who could play anything he wrote. After Scriabin's death he toured his old rival's music."},
  "alexanderscriabin":{"wiki":"Alexander Scriabin","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Skrjabin%20Alexander.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The mystic chord and a colour organ.","bio":"Alexander Scriabin, born in 1872 and dead in 1915, spent his career in Moscow and travelled further from common practice than any Russian of his generation. He began close to Chopin, in a tonal late-Romantic idiom, and ended, independently of Schoenberg, with a highly dissonant language that left traditional tonality behind without being strictly atonal, organised around the harmony known as the mystic chord. His metaphysics went with it: a colour-coded circle of fifths drawn from theosophy, a belief in synaesthesia and in the total artwork, and 'Prometheus', scored with a colour organ. He studied at the Moscow Conservatory with Sergei Taneyev, and came to Nikolai Zverev as a day pupil while Rachmaninoff boarded in the teacher's flat, beginning a rivalry that lasted their lives. He is the symbolist composer of Russian music above all others, and a major presence in the Silver Age of Russian poetry."},
  "ferrucciobusoni":{"wiki":"Ferruccio Busoni","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/FerruccioBusoni1913.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The pianist who sketched music's future.","bio":"Ferruccio Busoni, born in Italy in 1866 and based for his major years in Berlin, where he died in 1924, was a composer, pianist, conductor, editor, writer and teacher whose international career put him alongside most of the leading musicians, artists and writers of his time. He was sought after as a keyboard instructor and as a teacher of composition, and it is partly through his pupils that he matters here. Edgard Varèse studied with him in Berlin and took from his 'Sketch of a New Aesthetic of Music' the idea that sound might be liberated from fixed pitch altogether, a thought Varèse then spent a lifetime realising. Kurt Weill studied with him in Berlin from 1921 until his death in 1924. Busoni's writing, more than any single score of his, opened a door that younger composers walked through."},
  "almamahler":{"wiki":"Alma Mahler","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/YoungAlmaMahler.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The songs a marriage silenced.","bio":"Alma Mahler-Werfel, born in Vienna in 1879 and dead in 1964, was a composer, author, editor and painter, and a fixture of the city's fin de siècle culture. Active in music from childhood, she studied with Alexander Zemlinsky, her teacher and, briefly, her lover. In 1902 she married Gustav Mahler on the condition that she give up composing; he relented in 1910, too late, and the songs that survive are the ones written before the marriage stopped her. She kept friendships and relationships with many of the notable artists and composers of her time, and later wrote and edited, becoming a biographer of the world she had lived in. Her presence on this map is double: a composer curtailed, and one of the great connecting figures of Viennese modernism, in whose rooms much of it was argued out."},
  "pietromascagni":{"wiki":"Pietro Mascagni","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Pietro%20Mascagni%202.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Cavalleria rusticana launched verismo overnight.","bio":"Pietro Mascagni, born in 1863 and dead in 1945, was an Italian composer and conductor whose 'Cavalleria rusticana' of 1890 made a sensation that almost nothing in the operatic century has matched, and by itself carried verismo into Italian dramatic music: ordinary people, present-day violence, and a directness of feeling that swept the older manner aside. He had studied at the Milan Conservatory with Amilcare Ponchielli, sharing lodgings there with Giacomo Puccini, and the two then competed for the same audience for thirty years. He was written off, as Leoncavallo was, as a man with a single opera in him who could never match his first success, though 'L'amico Fritz' and 'Iris' never left the European stage after their first nights. He went on conducting as well as composing, and his long career, anchored in Livorno, ran on to 1945."},
  "ruggeroleoncavallo":{"wiki":"Ruggero Leoncavallo","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Zonder%20titel%20PK-F-MM.5862%20-%20recto.tiff?width=480","blurb":"Pagliacci, and a lifelong shadow.","bio":"Ruggero Leoncavallo, born in 1857 and dead in 1919, was an Italian opera composer and librettist who worked out of Naples and wrote his own texts as well as his own music, a rarer combination than it sounds in the Italian opera of the period. He produced numerous operas and songs across his career, but it is 'Pagliacci' of 1892 that remained his lasting contribution, and the single work by which he is still remembered. Everything after it was measured against it, and he spent years trying to get out from under his own greatest success, without ever managing it. He belongs to the verismo generation that turned Italian opera towards present-day life and immediate emotion, and his one enduring work is among the handful that defined what verismo sounded like."},
  "giacomopuccini":{"wiki":"Giacomo Puccini","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/GiacomoPuccini.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Italian opera's greatest voice after Verdi.","bio":"Giacomo Puccini, born in Lucca in 1858 and trained in Milan, came from a long line of composers stretching back to the late Baroque, and is reckoned the most successful writer of Italian opera in the generation after Verdi, and by common consent the finest. He studied at the Milan Conservatory with Amilcare Ponchielli, sharing a room with Pietro Mascagni, who then competed with him for the same audience for thirty years. His early work sat firmly inside late nineteenth-century Romantic Italian opera before developing into verismo, of which he became a leading exponent. The publisher Giulio Ricordi carried him through the failure of 'Edgar' and paid him a monthly stipend for years, a wager that produced 'La bohème', 'Tosca' and 'Madama Butterfly', all with librettos by Giuseppe Giacosa and Luigi Illica. He died in 1924, leaving 'Turandot' the last of the four operas by which he is known."},
  "umbertogiordano":{"wiki":"Umberto Giordano","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Umberto%20Giordano%20by%20Gaetano%20Esposito%20(color).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Andrea Chénier, his one lasting hit.","bio":"Umberto Giordano, born in 1867 and dead in 1948, was an Italian composer who worked chiefly in opera and made his career in Milan. He belongs to the generation that came of age just as verismo opened Italian opera to present-day subjects and unguarded emotion, and he took his chance in that new manner with 'Andrea Chénier' of 1896, the work by which he is still known and the one on which the whole of his reputation now rests. His later stage works never repeated its success, and his name has come to stand, as several of his contemporaries' names do, for a single unforgettable score rather than a body of work. He lived on until 1948, long after the movement he had joined as a young man had passed."},
  "francescocilea":{"wiki":"Francesco Cilea","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Guigoni%20%26%20Bossi.%20Francesco%20Cilea.%201910.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Remembered now for Adriana Lecouvreur.","bio":"Francesco Cilea, born in 1866 and dead in 1950, was an Italian composer and pianist based in Naples, and one of the verismo generation, the group of Italians who turned the stage towards present-day feeling. Two of his operas have kept their place: 'L'arlesiana' and, above all, 'Adriana Lecouvreur', the work he is chiefly known by today. His output was small beside that of the busiest of his contemporaries, and his reputation rests on that pair rather than on a large catalogue. He outlived the movement he belonged to by decades, dying in 1950, when the operatic Italy of his youth, with its publishers, its rival premieres and its national appetite for new work, had already become history. The lyrical warmth of his best writing is what has kept him in the repertoire."},
  "giulioricordi":{"wiki":"Giulio Ricordi","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Giulio%20Ricordi%20(before%201912)%20-%20Archivio%20Storico%20Ricordi%20FOTO000715%20detail.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The publisher who ran Italian opera.","bio":"Giulio Ricordi, born in 1840 and dead in 1912, was an Italian music publisher, editor and businessman who composed as well, though he is remembered as the man who made a great deal of the era's composing possible. He joined the family firm, Casa Ricordi, in 1863, when it was run by his father Tito, son of the founder Giovanni; on Tito's death in 1888 he took charge and held it until his own. From Milan he ran Italian opera as a business, controlling catalogues, contracts and premieres. He was Verdi's publisher and negotiator, and the man who brokered the reconciliation with Boito that led to the late masterpieces. He then backed the young Giacomo Puccini through the failure of 'Edgar' and paid him a monthly stipend for years, a piece of sustained private faith that bought Italian opera its next thirty years."},
  "claudedebussy":{"wiki":"Claude Debussy","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Claude%20Debussy%20by%20Atelier%20Nadar.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Rejected the label he came to define.","bio":"Claude Debussy, born at Saint-Germain-en-Laye in 1862 and dead in Paris in 1918, is sometimes called the first Impressionist composer, a term he rejected vigorously and a description his music outgrew. At eighteen he was employed as household pianist by Nadezhda von Meck, who took him with her to Russia, Italy and Vienna. At the Paris Conservatoire he studied with Ernest Guiraud, whose recorded conversations with him about parallel fifths remain the best evidence of how he thought. The 'Prélude à l'après-midi d'un faune' was written for a staged reading of Mallarmé's poem, and the poet told him it went far beyond the verse; Maeterlinck granted permission for 'Pelléas et Mélisande' and then denounced it in print when his mistress was not cast. Ravel dated his understanding of music to hearing the 'Prélude'; a quarrel in 1907 ended that friendship."},
  "mauriceravel":{"wiki":"Maurice Ravel","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Maurice%20Ravel%201925.jpg?width=480","blurb":"A craftsman's precision in every bar.","bio":"Maurice Ravel, born in 1875 and dead in 1937, was a French composer, pianist and conductor, associated with Impressionism alongside his elder contemporary Debussy though both men refused the word. Through the 1920s and 1930s the world outside France took him for the greatest living French composer, admired for a craftsman's precision, displayed at its most expansive in 'Daphnis et Chloé'. At the Paris Conservatoire he studied with Gabriel Fauré, who kept him on after the Prix de Rome scandal. Hearing Debussy's 'Prélude à l'après-midi d'un faune', he said, was the moment he understood what music was; their 1907 quarrel over priority in the Habanera, inflamed by partisans on both sides, ended the friendship. Ralph Vaughan Williams spent three months with him in Paris in 1908 and learned to orchestrate in points of colour rather than lines. He refused to teach Gershwin at all."},
  "eriksatie":{"wiki":"Erik Satie","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Satie-erik-4ff9d0bde1749.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The anti-Romantic gadfly a generation adopted.","bio":"Erik Satie, born in 1866 to a French father and a British mother and dead in Paris in 1925, studied at the Paris Conservatoire without distinction and took no diploma. In the 1880s he played piano in the café-cabarets of Montmartre and began writing, mostly for solo piano: the 'Gymnopédies', the 'Gnossiennes', and music for the Rosicrucian sect he attached himself to for a while. At thirty-nine he enrolled at the Schola Cantorum under Albert Roussel and Vincent d'Indy, from 1905 to 1912, to learn counterpoint properly. Diaghilev commissioned 'Parade' in 1917, Satie's score joined to Picasso's cubist costumes, Cocteau's scenario and Massine's choreography; Cocteau then built the manifesto 'Le Coq et l'Arlequin' around him. The next generation adopted him as their anti-Romantic ancestor, and Les Six took him as their chosen forebear, Poulenc most devotedly of all."},
  "pauldukas":{"wiki":"Paul Dukas","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Paul%20Dukas%2001.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Destroyed his scores; taught Messiaen instead.","bio":"Paul Dukas, born in Paris in 1865 and dead there in 1935, worked as composer, critic, scholar and teacher. Retiring by nature and merciless with himself, he abandoned and destroyed a great deal of his own music. What survives includes the opera 'Ariane et Barbe-bleue', a Symphony in C, a Piano Sonata, the 'Variations, Interlude and Finale on a Theme by Rameau' and the ballet 'La Péri', all of it overshadowed by the orchestral 'Sorcerer's Apprentice', whose fame was multiplied by Disney's 'Fantasia' in 1940. His teaching outlasted his self-doubt. At the Paris Conservatoire he taught Olivier Messiaen, whom he sent out with the instruction to listen to the birds because they are great masters, as well as Duruflé and Xian Xinghai, who studied with him at the Conservatoire until his death in 1935."},
  "albertroussel":{"wiki":"Albert Roussel","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Roussel%20Albert%201913.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The midshipman who became a composer.","bio":"Albert Roussel, born in 1869 and dead in 1937, came to music late and by an unusual route: seven years as a midshipman first, and composition only as an adult. He became one of the leading French composers of the years between the wars. Debussy and Ravel stand behind the early works, whose Impressionism he exchanged in later years for neoclassicism, trading atmosphere for firmer line, clearer form and a plainer sort of vigour. He taught at the Schola Cantorum in Paris, where his most famous pupil arrived as a middle-aged man: Erik Satie, who enrolled at thirty-nine determined to learn counterpoint properly. That a former naval officer should be the one to drill a famous eccentric in strict counterpoint is among the quieter ironies of French musical history."},
  "charleskoechlin":{"wiki":"Charles Koechlin","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Charles%20Koechlin%201.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Taught Poulenc; wrote a film-star symphony.","bio":"Charles Koechlin, born in 1867 and dead in 1950, was a French composer, teacher and musicologist who spent his career in Paris and wrote a great deal that the concert world has still not caught up with. Among his better-known works are 'Les Heures persanes', piano pieces drawn from Pierre Loti's novel 'Vers Ispahan', and 'The Seven Stars Symphony' of 1933, each of its seven movements themed on a film star popular at the time. He studied with Gabriel Fauré, for whom he orchestrated 'Pelléas et Mélisande'. As a teacher he gave Francis Poulenc his only sustained study of composition, and in Paris in 1938 he taught the composer Camargo Guarnieri. His writing about music, technical and encyclopaedic, was as influential among professionals as any of his scores."},
  "liliboulanger":{"wiki":"Lili Boulanger","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Lili%20Boulanger%201.jpg?width=480","blurb":"First woman to win the Prix de Rome.","bio":"Lili Boulanger, born in Paris in 1893, was a French composer associated with the Symbolist and Impressionist movements, and the first woman ever to win the Grand Prix de Rome for composition, which she did at nineteen. Music was the family trade: her father Ernest Boulanger was a composer, and her elder sister Nadia became the century's most consequential composition teacher. Her own life was very short: she died in 1918 at twenty-four, leaving a small body of work whose intensity has kept it alive. Nadia then spent sixty years promoting the music of the sister she had lost, programming it, teaching it and refusing to let it disappear, so that the shortest career in this constellation was preserved by one of the longest. What survives suggests one of the great unfinished bodies of French music."},
  "nadiaboulanger":{"wiki":"Nadia Boulanger","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nadia%20Boulanger%201925%20colorized.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Taught half the twentieth century.","bio":"Nadia Boulanger, born in Paris in 1887 and dead in 1979 at ninety-two, was a French teacher, conductor and composer who taught more twentieth-century composers than anyone else alive, and who performed occasionally as pianist and organist. She studied with Gabriel Fauré at the Paris Conservatoire. Marion Bauer became her first American pupil in 1906, in exchange for English lessons; Aaron Copland was the first American to study composition with her at Fontainebleau, in 1921, after which she taught most of the rest, Virgil Thomson and Roy Harris among them, and Elliott Carter from 1932 to 1935. Others came from further afield: Alejandro García Caturla in 1928, Ulvi Cemal Erkin, and Grażyna Bacewicz on a Paderewski scholarship. The roll of names is the real achievement: no other teacher of the century has one to match it, and much of twentieth-century music was formed in her studio."},
  "karolszymanowski":{"wiki":"Karol Szymanowski","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Karol%20Szymanowski.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Poland's bridge to modern music.","bio":"Karol Szymanowski, born in 1882 and dead in 1937, was a Polish composer, pianist and writer, and belonged to the modernist current known as Young Poland, which flourished around the turn of the century. His work falls into distinct phases: a middle period of French-facing exoticism, sensuous in harmony and colour, and a late turn to the Górale mountain music of the Polish highlanders, which gave his writing a harder and more angular rhythmic profile. Working from Warsaw in the years when Poland regained its statehood, he became the country's central musical figure, and for the generation that followed he was the only recent Polish model available. Witold Lutosławski inherited that position, and inherited it from him. Szymanowski is the reason Polish music of the twentieth century had a modern starting point of its own."},
  "arnoldschoenberg":{"wiki":"Arnold Schoenberg","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Arnold%20sch%C3%B6nberg%20man%20ray.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Emancipated the dissonance, then ordered twelve tones.","bio":"Arnold Schoenberg, born in Vienna in 1874 and dead in Los Angeles in 1951, was a composer, theorist, teacher and painter, the author of free atonality from 1908 and of the twelve-tone method from 1923, and the great exponent of developing variation and the emancipation of the dissonance. Alexander Zemlinsky gave him the only formal composition teaching he ever had and became his brother-in-law when he married Mathilde Zemlinsky in 1901. Mahler defended him publicly, bought his paintings, and left instructions that he be looked after. From 1904 he taught Alban Berg, who arrived unable to orchestrate, and Anton Webern; Egon Wellesz was his first private pupil and later his first biographer; Hanns Eisler and Viktor Ullmann also studied with him. He taught at the Prussian Academy of Arts until 1933, reaffirmed his Judaism, emigrated, and taught at UCLA from 1936."},
  "albanberg":{"wiki":"Alban Berg","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Alban%20Berg%20(1885%E2%80%931935)%20~1930%20%C2%A9%20Max%20Fenichel%20(1885%E2%80%931942).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Twelve-tone music with a Romantic heart.","bio":"Alban Berg, born in Vienna in 1885 and dead there in 1935 at fifty, was the member of the Second Viennese School who made its methods sing. He joined Romantic lyricism to twelve-tone technique, and though he left a relatively small body of work he is remembered as one of the century's essential composers for an expressive range that takes in whole worlds of emotion and structure at once. The operas 'Wozzeck' and 'Lulu', and the Violin Concerto, are its centre. He came to Arnold Schoenberg in 1904 unable to orchestrate, and remained a disciple for life, the pupil who never rebelled. With Anton Webern, who studied alongside him, he ran the Society for Private Musical Performances and sustained thirty years of three-way correspondence with their teacher, a document of modernism arguing with itself."},
  "antonwebern":{"wiki":"Anton Webern","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Anton%20Webern%20(1883%E2%80%931945)%201927%20%C2%A9%20Georg%20Fayer%20(1891%E2%80%931950)%20OeNB%2010450290.png?width=480","blurb":"Whole worlds compressed into minutes.","bio":"Anton Webern, born in Vienna in 1883 and dead in 1945, wrote music as radical as any of its milieu and shorter than almost all of it, lyrical and poetically concise, built from then-new atonal and twelve-tone techniques. His rigour came from two directions: his scholarly work on the Franco-Flemish polyphonists, undertaken with Guido Adler, and Arnold Schoenberg's habit of teaching structure from Bach, the First Viennese School and Brahms. He began with Schoenberg in 1904 alongside Alban Berg, and the three of them formed the core of what became the Second Viennese School. After 1945 his aphoristic manner mattered more than his teacher's: Pierre Boulez's 'Schoenberg est mort' of 1952 raised Webern above Schoenberg as the only usable starting point for the postwar generation, and Stockhausen took the pointillist Webern of the Op. 27 Variations as the model for 'Kreuzspiel'."},
  "hannseisler":{"wiki":"Hanns Eisler","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Eisler1940.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Schoenberg's pupil who chose Brecht.","bio":"Hanns Eisler, born in 1898 and dead in 1962, was an Austrian and German composer whose life ran from Vienna through Berlin to East Berlin, and whose music was made to be useful. He studied with Arnold Schoenberg in Vienna, absorbing the most advanced technique available, and then turned it towards political song and the theatre; the politics eventually broke the relationship, and Schoenberg never forgave him. From 1930 until Brecht's death he worked with Bertolt Brecht, a partnership longer and less celebrated than Weill's but central to both men's work. He wrote a large number of film scores, and he composed the national anthem of East Germany, where he settled after the war. The Hochschule für Musik Hanns Eisler Berlin carries his name, an institutional afterlife for a composer who wanted music to do a job."},
  "egonwellesz":{"wiki":"Egon Wellesz","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Egon%20Wellesz%20(1885%E2%80%931974)%201927%20%C2%A9%20Georg%20Fayer%20(1892%E2%80%931950).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Schoenberg's first pupil, and first biographer.","bio":"Egon Wellesz, born in 1885 and dead in 1974, lived a life split between Vienna and Oxford and between two vocations, composer and scholar, and was distinguished in both. He was Arnold Schoenberg's first private pupil, and later Schoenberg's first biographer, which makes him the earliest witness to how the Second Viennese School taught and thought. As a musicologist he was notable above all in the field of Byzantine music, a study he pursued for decades alongside his composing. Austrian by birth and later British, he was made CBE and elected a Fellow of the British Academy, and he went on composing into old age in England. His presence on this map is that of the participant who also wrote the record, a man who both made the new music and gave scholarship the tools to read a far older one."},
  "luigidallapiccola":{"wiki":"Luigi Dallapiccola","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Luigi-Dallapiccola.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Brought the twelve-tone method to Italy.","bio":"Luigi Dallapiccola, born in 1904 and dead in 1975, was an Italian composer and pianist based in Florence, and the man who brought the twelve-tone method into Italian music. What he made of it was distinctive: his serial writing is lyrical rather than austere, singable in a way that connects it to the long Italian tradition of the voice, which is part of why it took root in a country whose musical life had been built around opera. At Tanglewood in 1952 he taught Luciano Berio, who carried the technique into the postwar avant-garde and far beyond it. Through that single link the Second Viennese School's method reached the generation of Italian composers who dominated European new music after the war. Dallapiccola is the hinge between Vienna's invention and Italy's modernism."},
  "viktorullmann":{"wiki":"Viktor Ullmann","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Viktor%20Ullmann%202.jpg?width=480","blurb":"From Prague to Theresienstadt to Auschwitz.","bio":"Viktor Ullmann, born in Silesia in 1898, was an Austrian composer, conductor, pianist and choir director whose working life was centred on Prague. During those Prague years he studied with Arnold Schoenberg, and his music belongs to the orbit of the Second Viennese School while keeping its own Central European inflection. He was later held in the Theresienstadt ghetto, and from there he was sent to Auschwitz, where he was murdered in 1944, at forty-six. The record of his life names two places: the city where he worked and the camp where he was held. His name stands in this constellation for a whole generation of Central European composers whose work was cut off by the Nazi regime, and whose scores had to be recovered decades afterwards by people who went looking for them."},
  "ernstkrenek":{"wiki":"Ernst Krenek","blurb":"Jonny spielt auf, then a long American life.","bio":"Ernst Krenek, born in 1900 and dead in 1991, was an Austrian and later American composer whose ninety-one years took him from Vienna to Los Angeles and through most of the century's musical arguments. The opera 'Jonny spielt auf' is the work that carried his name furthest, and he went on to explore atonality and a succession of other modern styles rather than settling into any one of them. He was a considerable writer too, author of 'Music Here and Now' in 1939, a study of the Renaissance composer Johannes Ockeghem in 1953, and the reflections 'Horizons Circled' in 1974; two pieces appeared under the pseudonym Thornton Winsloe. In Minneapolis he taught the Panamanian composer Roque Cordero, who took the twelve-tone method he learned there and turned it onto the rhythms of his own country."},
  "igorstravinsky":{"wiki":"Igor Stravinsky","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Igor%20Stravinsky%20LOC%2032392u.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Three revolutions in one long career.","bio":"Igor Stravinsky, born in 1882 and dead in 1971, worked in St Petersburg, Paris and Los Angeles and changed direction twice without ever losing his authority. Private lessons with Nikolai Rimsky-Korsakov from 1905 to 1908 were the only teaching he had. Sergei Diaghilev heard a student piece in 1909, commissioned 'The Firebird', and began a partnership that ran twenty years; out of it came 'The Rite of Spring', with Nijinsky's choreography and the riot that came with it, and 'Pulcinella' in 1920, made with Picasso. He worked with Jean Cocteau on 'Oedipus Rex' in 1927. Forty years of neoclassicism followed, then serialism, into which Robert Craft, his assistant and amanuensis, talked him at seventy. Paul Sacher commissioned from him across five decades and collected the manuscripts. Schoenberg, who had mocked 'der kleine Modernsky' in 1925, was his Los Angeles neighbour for eleven years; they never met."},
  "sergeidiaghilev":{"wiki":"Sergei Diaghilev","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sergei%20Diaghilev%2001.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The impresario who commissioned modern music.","bio":"Sergei Diaghilev, born in 1872 and dead in 1929, was a Russian art critic, patron and ballet impresario, and the founder of the Ballets Russes, the company from which many of the century's great dancers and choreographers emerged. He wrote no music; he caused it to exist. Hearing a student piece by Igor Stravinsky in 1909, he commissioned 'The Firebird' and began a partnership that lasted twenty years and produced the scores that broke modern music open. He commissioned Sergei Prokofiev from 1915, brought Manuel de Falla's 'The Three-Cornered Hat' to the stage in 1919 with designs by Picasso, and gave Erik Satie 'Parade' in 1917. His method was to put composers, painters, choreographers and dancers into one room and pay for the result. A great deal of what this map calls modernism was ordered by him."},
  "manueldefalla":{"wiki":"Manuel de Falla","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Manuel%20de%20Falla%20con%20bast%C3%B3n%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Andalusian material with a modernist finish.","bio":"Manuel de Falla, born in 1876 and dead in 1946, spent his central years in Granada and his last in Argentina, and stands with Albéniz, Tárrega and Granados among the most important Spanish musicians of the first half of the twentieth century. His catalogue is relatively modest in size and has a claim nonetheless to make him Spain's greatest composer of the century, because almost nothing in it is slack. From 1902 he studied in Madrid with Felipe Pedrell. Sergei Diaghilev commissioned 'The Three-Cornered Hat' for the Ballets Russes in 1919, with designs by Picasso, and that production carried Andalusian idiom, sharpened by modernist rhythm and orchestration, onto the stages of Europe. He is the composer through whom Spanish material entered the mainstream of European modernism instead of remaining local colour. He died in Argentina in 1946."},
  "sergeiprokofiev":{"wiki":"Sergei Prokofiev","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sergei%20Prokofiev%20circa%201918%20over%20Chair%20Bain.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Went home to Stalin's Soviet Union.","bio":"Sergei Prokofiev, born in 1891, wrote acknowledged masterpieces in nearly every genre he touched: seven symphonies and seven completed operas, eight ballets, nine piano sonatas, five concertos for piano and two for violin, along with the pieces everyone knows, the March from 'The Love for Three Oranges', the 'Lieutenant Kijé' suite, 'Romeo and Juliet' and 'Peter and the Wolf'. Reinhold Glière gave him private lessons at the family estate when he was eleven. Diaghilev commissioned him from 1915. He met Nikolai Myaskovsky as a student in 1906 and corresponded with him for forty-four years. After years in Paris he returned to the Soviet Union in 1936, and in 1948 was denounced alongside Dmitri Shostakovich, his wary colleague, in the decree issued under Andrei Zhdanov. He died in Moscow in 1953, on the same day as Stalin."},
  "ottorinorespighi":{"wiki":"Ottorino Respighi","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ottorino%20Respighi%2C%201927%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Rome painted in orchestral colour.","bio":"Ottorino Respighi, born in 1879 and dead in 1936, was an Italian violinist, teacher and musicologist as well as a composer, and among the leading Italian musicians of the early twentieth century, working chiefly in Rome. His output ranges across operas, ballets, orchestral suites, choral songs and chamber music, and includes many transcriptions of Italian compositions of the sixteenth to eighteenth centuries, a scholar's habit that fed straight back into his own writing. International fame came from three orchestral tone poems on Roman subjects: 'Fountains of Rome' in 1916, 'Pines of Rome' in 1924 and 'Roman Festivals' in 1928. The brilliance of their scoring has a traceable source. In 1900 he went to St Petersburg to study orchestration with Nikolai Rimsky-Korsakov, and brought the Russian school's command of instrumental colour back to a country whose orchestral tradition had long been secondary to opera."},
  "bohuslavmartinu":{"wiki":"Bohuslav Martinů","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Martinu%201945.jpg?width=480","blurb":"A Czech in Paris, endlessly productive.","bio":"Bohuslav Martinů, born in 1890 and dead in 1959, left an output few of his contemporaries could match: fifteen operas, fourteen ballet scores, six symphonies, and a great deal besides for orchestra, for chamber forces, for voices and for solo instruments. He began as a practical musician, playing violin in the Czech Philharmonic, and studied briefly with the Czech composer Josef Suk. Leaving Czechoslovakia for Paris in 1923 was the decisive act of his life; there he deliberately turned his back on the Romantic style he had been trained in and spent the 1920s experimenting with modern French developments, as in the orchestral 'Half-time' and 'La Bagarre', and with jazz idioms, as in his 'Kitchen Revue'. He later worked in the United States. The patron Paul Sacher commissioned the Double Concerto and much else from him, sustaining a composer who spent his career away from home."},
  "alfredocasella":{"wiki":"Alfredo Casella","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Alfredo%20Casella.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Rome's composer, pianist and conductor.","bio":"Alfredo Casella, born in 1883 and dead in 1947, was an Italian composer, pianist, conductor and musicologist who worked out of Rome. He belongs to the generation of Italian musicians who came of age when their country's musical life was almost entirely given over to opera, and who are grouped instead with the neoclassical current running through Stravinsky and the Ballets Russes years, a turn away from late-Romantic expression towards clarity of line and the recovery of older forms. His career was threefold and the parts fed each other: a composer who also played and conducted could programme the music he believed in and put it in front of audiences. He wrote as a scholar too. He died in 1947, having lived through both wars and the whole first phase of Italian musical modernism."},
  "dariusmilhaud":{"wiki":"Darius Milhaud","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Darius%20Milhaud%20b%20Meurisse%201923.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Polytonality, jazz, and famous pupils.","bio":"Darius Milhaud, born in 1892 and dead in 1974, divided his life between Aix-en-Provence and Oakland, California, and was among the most prolific composers the twentieth century produced. A member of Les Six, the group assembled by the critic Henri Collet in 1920 and given its programme by Jean Cocteau, he wrote music shaped by jazz and by Brazilian song and made extensive use of polytonality, several keys sounding at once. His teaching may have spread even further than his music. At Mills College he taught Dave Brubeck, who named a son after him, and Steve Reich from 1961 to 1963; at Aspen in 1960 he taught Philip Glass; his pupils also included Burt Bacharach, György Kurtág, Karlheinz Stockhausen and Iannis Xenakis. Few teachers can claim to stand behind minimalism, cool jazz and European serialism at once."},
  "francispoulenc":{"wiki":"Francis Poulenc","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Francis%20Poulenc%20and%20Wanda%20Landowska%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Half monk, half delinquent.","bio":"Francis Poulenc, born in Paris in 1899 and dead there in 1963, was described as half monk and half delinquent, and the description holds: the same man wrote the irreverent ballet 'Les Biches' of 1923 and the 'Dialogues des Carmélites' of 1957. His catalogue runs from songs and solo piano through chamber and choral music to opera, ballet and the concert hall, and includes the piano suite 'Mouvements perpétuels' of 1919, the 'Concert champêtre' for harpsichord of 1928, the Organ Concerto of 1938 and the 'Gloria' of 1959. Charles Koechlin gave him his only sustained study of composition. Erik Satie was the chosen ancestor of Les Six, and Poulenc the member of the group who took that inheritance most to heart. Jean Cocteau, in whose circle Les Six formed, later wrote him the libretto of 'La Voix humaine'."},
  "arthurhonegger":{"wiki":"Arthur Honegger","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Arthur%20Honegger%201921.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Les Six's least frivolous member.","bio":"Arthur Honegger, a Swiss composer born in France in 1892 and resident for most of his life in Paris, where he died in 1955, was the member of Les Six least interested in lightness. 'Pacific 231' is the orchestral piece his name is most often attached to, though for the scholar Halbreich it is 'Jeanne d'Arc au bûcher', rather than that work or 'Le Roi David', that counts as his most popular of all, and the large dramatic scores are where his weight really lies. He wrote extensively for the cinema, which gave a composer of his generation both a living and an audience the concert hall could not, and he taught as well. Grouped with Les Six around Jean Cocteau, he kept a seriousness of tone that set him apart from the group's reputation for wit."},
  "germainetailleferre":{"wiki":"Germaine Tailleferre","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Tailleferre%20Harcourt%201937%202.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The only woman among Les Six.","bio":"Germaine Tailleferre, born in 1892 and dead in 1983, was a French composer and the only woman among Les Six, the Paris group associated with Jean Cocteau that set the tone of French music between the wars. Her ninety-one years covered an extraordinary stretch of history, from the world before the First World War through to the 1980s, and she went on composing across almost all of it, working also as a pianist and writing about music. The group label has cut both ways for her: it kept her name in the histories while encouraging the assumption that her contribution was the decorative one. The catalogue says otherwise, and much of it is still being recovered and recorded. She remains the clearest reminder that the interwar Parisian avant-garde was not made by men alone."},
  "georgesauric":{"wiki":"Georges Auric","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Georges%20Auric%201940.jpg?width=480","blurb":"From Les Six to film scores.","bio":"Georges Auric, born in Lodève in the Hérault in 1899 and dead in 1983, was one of Les Six, the group informally gathered around Jean Cocteau and Erik Satie. He was precocious to an unusual degree: before he turned twenty he had already orchestrated and supplied incidental music for a number of ballets and stage productions, work that would have made a career on its own. What followed was a long and distinguished second life as a film composer, at a time when the cinema was drawing serious concert musicians into its orbit and paying them properly for it. He worked as a choreographer as well, and appeared as an actor. Of the Six, he is the one whose music the largest number of people have heard without knowing whose it was, which is the ordinary fate of the screen composer."},
  "louisdurey":{"wiki":"Louis Durey","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/DureyLouis1930.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Les Six's first defector.","bio":"Louis Edmond Durey, born in Paris in 1888, was a French composer who also worked as a conductor and as a music critic. He is remembered chiefly as one of Les Six, the loose group of young Paris composers whose public identity ran through Jean Cocteau, and whose members were forever being spoken for by other people. Durey was the least willing of them to be spoken for, and he was the first to go, stepping away from the group and its publicity while the others made their names. He kept composing well out of the spotlight, and he lived a very long time: he died in 1979, decades after the label was fixed to him, having never quite escaped it and never much wanted it."},
  "jeancocteau":{"wiki":"Jean Cocteau","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jean%20Cocteau%20b%20Meurisse%201923.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The manifesto behind Les Six.","bio":"Jean Cocteau, who lived from 1889 to 1963 and worked in Paris, was a poet, playwright, novelist, designer, film director and critic, and one of the busiest impresarios of the avant-garde. He was not a composer, yet few people did more to shape what French composers of his generation were understood to be doing. When the critic Henri Collet grouped six young musicians together in 1920, it was Cocteau who supplied them with a programme, in 'Le Coq et l'Arlequin', a manifesto built around Erik Satie, with whom he had already made 'Parade'. Milhaud, Poulenc, Honegger, Tailleferre, Auric and Durey were bound less by a shared style than by his advocacy. He wrote 'Oedipus Rex' with Stravinsky in 1927 and later gave Poulenc the text of 'La Voix humaine'. Across theatre, film and print, one contemporary verdict had him the nearest thing to a Renaissance man."},
  "belabartok":{"wiki":"Béla Bartók","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/B%C3%A9la%20Bart%C3%B3k%201943.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Folk song made into modernism.","bio":"Béla Bartók, born in 1881, was a Hungarian composer, pianist and ethnomusicologist who divided his life between Budapest and, at the end, New York, where he died in 1945. He studied with Hans Koessler at the Budapest Academy, and his course changed when Zoltán Kodály brought Debussy's scores back from Paris; they showed him, he said, what could be done with modal folk material. With Kodály he then spent twenty years collecting in the villages, thousands of field recordings across the Balkans and North Africa, and the two published Hungarian folk song together. Ahmed Adnan Saygun accompanied and transcribed with him on the 1936 expedition through southern Anatolia. Paul Sacher commissioned the 'Music for Strings, Percussion and Celesta' and the 'Divertimento'; Serge Koussevitzky commissioned the 'Concerto for Orchestra' in 1943, when Bartók was ill and destitute. His second wife, Ditta Pásztory-Bartók, had first been his piano pupil."},
  "zoltankodaly":{"wiki":"Zoltán Kodály","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Zolt%C3%A1n%20Kod%C3%A1ly%20(1928%2C%20P%C3%A1l%20M.%20Vajda).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Hungary's folk collector and teacher.","bio":"Zoltán Kodály, born in Budapest in 1882, was a Hungarian composer, ethnomusicologist, teacher and linguist, and one of the two men who put Hungarian folk song at the centre of twentieth-century music. He studied at the Budapest Academy with Hans Koessler, and with Béla Bartók he spent some twenty years walking into villages to record what was sung there, work that produced their joint publication of Hungarian folk song. Trained as a linguist as well as a musician, he brought a scholar's ear to what he heard. Internationally he is known less for any single piece than for the Kodály method, the system of music education built on singing and on the songs of a child's own culture, which spread from Hungary into classrooms around the world. He conducted, wrote and taught until his death in 1967."},
  "ralphvaughanwilliams":{"wiki":"Ralph Vaughan Williams","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ralph%20Vaughan%20Williams%201913.jpeg?width=480","blurb":"English music's turn toward home.","bio":"Ralph Vaughan Williams, born in 1872 and based in London, wrote nine symphonies across sixty years, along with operas, ballets, chamber music and a great deal of sacred and secular vocal music. He studied at the Royal College of Music with Hubert Parry and with Charles Villiers Stanford, whose lessons were mostly arguments, and in 1908 spent three months in Paris with Maurice Ravel, who he said taught him to orchestrate in points of colour rather than in lines. From 1903 he was out collecting English folk song, often over the same Somerset and Essex ground as Cecil Sharp, a fellow worker for the Folk-Song Society. That material, with Tudor polyphony, gave British music a decisive way out of its nineteenth-century German habits. For forty years he and Gustav Holst held 'field days', showing each other unfinished scores and tearing them apart. He died in 1958."},
  "gustavholst":{"wiki":"Gustav Holst","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gustav%20Holst.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Planets, and much else besides.","bio":"Gustav Holst, born in 1874, was an English composer, arranger and teacher, remembered above all for the orchestral suite 'The Planets' and for a large body of work in other genres that never matched its success. He studied at the Royal College of Music with Charles Villiers Stanford. Wagner and Richard Strauss were the decisive early influences on his style; what changed it was the English folk-song revival of the early twentieth century and the example of rising modern composers such as Ravel, out of which he refined something recognisably his own. His closest professional friendship was with Ralph Vaughan Williams: for forty years the two held 'field days' in which each showed the other unfinished scores and pulled them apart. He wrote his own librettos and spent much of his working life teaching. He died in London in 1934."},
  "percygrainger":{"wiki":"Percy Grainger","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Percy%20Grainger%20portrait%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Folk collector and restless sound experimenter.","bio":"Percy Grainger, born in Australia in 1882, composed, arranged and played the piano; he settled in the United States in 1914 and took American citizenship in 1918. He played a prominent part in the early twentieth-century revival of British folk music, and did it with unusual method: he carried a phonograph into Lincolnshire and recorded singers rather than tidying their tunes onto paper. Much of his own work was experimental and unusual, and late in life he was building his Free Music machines, the instruments his last experiments called for. He is nevertheless best known for a single piano arrangement, of the folk dance 'Country Gardens'. He and Henry Cowell were two experimenters working at the margins of the American academy. Grainger also played saxophone and conducted, and he died in 1961."},
  "georgebutterworth":{"wiki":"George Butterworth","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/George%20Sainton%20Kaye%20Butterworth%20(1885%E2%80%931916).png?width=480","blurb":"Housman's songwriter, killed on the Somme.","bio":"George Butterworth, born in 1885, belonged to the English generation that came through the Royal College of Music in Charles Villiers Stanford's orbit and turned to folk song for its material. The orchestral idyll 'The Banks of Green Willow' is the piece that carries his name, together with his settings of A. E. Housman's poems from 'A Shropshire Lad', songs of great plainness and restraint. He was a musicologist as well as a composer, but the catalogue is small for an unhappy reason: he served in the First World War, was awarded the Military Cross for gallantry in the fighting at Pozières, and was killed during the Battle of the Somme in 1916, at thirty-one. What survives is admired partly for itself and partly for what it implies about the music he did not live to write."},
  "heitorvillalobos":{"wiki":"Heitor Villa-Lobos","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Heitor%20Villa-Lobos%20(c.%201922).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Brazil in two thousand pieces.","bio":"Heitor Villa-Lobos, born in Rio de Janeiro in 1887, was a Brazilian composer, conductor, cellist and guitarist, and by common reckoning the central creative figure of Brazilian art music in the twentieth century. He was enormously prolific: by his death in 1959 he had written more than two thousand pieces, orchestral, chamber, instrumental and vocal. His language fused Brazilian folk material with elements of the European tradition, nowhere more openly than in the 'Bachianas brasileiras', which read Bach through Brazil, and in the 'Chôros'. His guitar music matters especially: the Études of 1929, dedicated to Andrés Segovia, and the five Preludes of 1940, dedicated to his companion Arminda Neves d'Almeida, are cornerstones of that instrument's repertoire. Artur Rubinstein heard him play in Rio in 1918 and spent the next thirty years introducing his music to the rest of the world."},
  "carloschavez":{"wiki":"Carlos Chávez","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Carlos%20Chavez.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Mexico's composer and orchestra builder.","bio":"Carlos Chávez, born in Mexico City in 1899, was a composer, conductor, theorist, teacher and journalist, and the man who founded and directed the Mexican Symphonic Orchestra, an institution as much a part of his legacy as anything he wrote. His music drew on the native cultures of Mexico; of his six symphonies the most popular is the 'Sinfonía india', which brings Yaqui percussion instruments into the orchestra. As conductor and administrator he was the organising force of Mexican concert life for decades, and he used that position for other people: he brought Silvestre Revueltas back from Texas to be his assistant conductor in Mexico City. His friendship with Aaron Copland ran forty years, each of them conducting and championing the other's music, and it is a large part of why Mexican and American composers of that generation knew each other's work. Chávez died in 1978."},
  "silvestrerevueltas":{"wiki":"Silvestre Revueltas","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Silvestre-revueltas.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Chávez's assistant, and his counterweight.","bio":"Silvestre Revueltas, born in 1899, was a Mexican composer, violinist and conductor, and with Carlos Chávez one of the two figures at the centre of Mexican concert music between the wars. He was working in Texas when Chávez brought him home to be his assistant conductor in Mexico City, an appointment that placed him at the heart of the country's musical life. He conducted, studied folk material as a musicologist, and wrote for the cinema, and of the two he was the more volatile and instinctive, next to Chávez the patient institution-builder. His life was short, ending in Mexico City in 1940, and his output is correspondingly small in proportion to a reputation that has grown steadily in the decades since, in Mexico and well beyond it."},
  "vitezslavakapralova":{"wiki":"Vítězslava Kaprálová","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kapralova1935.jpg?width=480","blurb":"A Czech voice lost at twenty-five.","bio":"Vítězslava Kaprálová, born in Brno in 1915, was a Czech composer and conductor who studied with Novák and with Martinů. Her working life ran from Brno to Paris and lasted only a handful of years: she died in 1940, at twenty-five, having been active as a conductor as well as a composer at an age when most of her contemporaries were still students. She belongs to the folk-inflected Czech modernism of her teachers' orbit, and what survives of her music has kept her name alive on its own terms rather than merely as a footnote to theirs. Almost everything about her is shadowed by the same question, which no amount of study can answer: what the work of the next fifty years would have been."},
  "paulhindemith":{"wiki":"Paul Hindemith","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Paul%20Hindemith%201923.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The craftsman-theorist of German modernism.","bio":"Paul Hindemith, born in Germany in 1895, was a composer, violist, conductor, theorist and teacher, and the most systematic musical craftsman of his generation. He founded the Amar Quartet in 1921 and toured Europe with it, and in the 1920s became the leading advocate of Neue Sachlichkeit, the sober, useful manner of the 'Kammermusik' series, several written for viola and viola d'amore in a neo-Bachian spirit. The song cycle 'Das Marienleben' (1923), 'Der Schwanendreher' (1935), the 'Symphonic Metamorphosis' on Weber themes (1943) and the opera and symphony 'Mathis der Maler' are the works most often heard, alongside oratorios from 'Das Unaufhörliche' to his Whitman requiem of 1946. He set out his thinking in 'The Craft of Musical Composition'. He and his wife left for Switzerland and then the United States ahead of the Second World War, and at Yale he taught Lukas Foss and Ulysses Kay. He died in 1963."},
  "kurtweill":{"wiki":"Kurt Weill","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bundesarchiv%20Bild%20146-2005-0119%2C%20Kurt%20Weill.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Weimar's stage composer, then Broadway's.","bio":"Kurt Weill, born in Germany in 1900, was a composer for the stage who held that music should do a job in the world — Gebrauchsmusik, music for use. He studied with Ferruccio Busoni in Berlin between 1921 and 1924, and then found the collaborator who set the terms: with Bertolt Brecht he wrote 'The Threepenny Opera', with its ballad 'Mack the Knife', then 'Mahagonny' and 'The Seven Deadly Sins', until politics pulled the two of them apart. The singer Lotte Lenya, whom he married twice, sang almost every major role he composed. He fled Nazi Germany in 1933, arrived in America two years after that, and from New York made a substantial contribution to the country's musical theatre with 'Street Scene' and 'Lady in the Dark' among the results. He also wrote concert music and a number of pieces on Jewish themes. He died in 1950."},
  "carlorff":{"wiki":"Carl Orff","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Carl%20Orff%20um%201970.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Carmina Burana, and a children's method.","bio":"Carl Orff, born in Munich in 1895, was a German composer and music educator whose two legacies are usually kept in separate rooms. From 1930 he was developing the Schulwerk, the body of material and ideas for teaching children that became one of the most influential approaches to music education anywhere. Carmina Burana followed in 1937 and made him famous far beyond the classroom: a cantata of blunt repeated rhythms and bare harmony that has never left the repertoire. He was a musicologist as well as a composer, and he taught at university level for much of his working life. He spent almost the whole of that life in and around Munich, and died in 1982, better known to schoolchildren and to concert audiences than either group tends to realise."},
  "bertoltbrecht":{"wiki":"Bertolt Brecht","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bundesarchiv%20Bild%20183-W0409-300%2C%20Bertolt%20Brecht.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The playwright who set the terms.","bio":"Bertolt Brecht, born in 1898, was a German playwright, poet and theatre director and, for the purposes of a map of music, the collaborator who set the terms. His first successes came in Munich; in 1924 he moved to Berlin, and there, with Elisabeth Hauptmann and Kurt Weill, wrote 'The Threepenny Opera'. Immersed in Marxist thought in those years, he produced the didactic Lehrstücke and became the leading theoretician of epic theatre and of the Verfremdungseffekt, the alienation effect meant to keep an audience thinking rather than swooning — which asked of his composers a music that argued rather than soothed. 'Mahagonny' and 'The Seven Deadly Sins' followed with Weill, before politics separated them. His partnership with Hanns Eisler was longer and less famous, running from 1930 until Brecht's death in 1956."},
  "lottelenya":{"wiki":"Lotte Lenya","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Lotte%20Lenya.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The voice Weill wrote for.","bio":"Lotte Lenya, born in Austria in 1898, was a singer and actress whose voice defined a repertoire. She married Kurt Weill twice — the same man, two marriages — and sang almost every major role he wrote, from Berlin to New York. In the German-speaking world and in classical music generally she is remembered first as his interpreter: a diseuse as much as a singer, nearer speech than song, and the voice his songs were built to fit. In English-language cinema she had a second career, with an Academy Award nomination as the jaded aristocrat in 'The Roman Spring of Mrs. Stone' in 1961 and, two years later, the murderous Rosa Klebb in 'From Russia with Love'. She spent her later decades based in the United States, and died in 1981."},
  "dmitrishostakovich":{"wiki":"Dmitri Shostakovich","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dmitri%20Shostakovich%20credit%20Deutsche%20Fotothek%20adjusted.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Fifteen symphonies under official scrutiny.","bio":"Dmitri Shostakovich, born in 1906, became internationally known when his First Symphony was premiered in 1926, and spent the rest of his life as the Soviet Union's most conspicuous composer: fifteen symphonies, fifteen string quartets, two official denunciations. Alexander Glazunov, as Conservatory director, found the boy extra rations and a stipend; Maximilian Steinberg taught him in Petrograd, conservatively, to a pupil who was not. Andrei Zhdanov's decree of 1948 attacked him for formalism and cost him his teaching posts, and named Sergei Prokofiev, a wary colleague, in the same breath. He taught Galina Ustvolskaya from 1939, proposed marriage and was refused, then quoted her music in two of his own works; he told the young Sofia Gubaidulina to continue on her own incorrect path, a line she repeated for the rest of her life. Both cello concertos were written for Mstislav Rostropovich. He died in 1975."},
  "aramkhachaturian":{"wiki":"Aram Khachaturian","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Aram%20Khachaturian%2C%20Pic%2C%2017.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Soviet Armenia's leading composer.","bio":"Aram Khachaturian, born in 1903, was a Soviet Armenian composer and conductor, counted among the leading composers of the Soviet era. He studied in Moscow with Reinhold Glière and built his whole career in the capital, working as composer, conductor and music educator. Popularity did not protect him: Andrei Zhdanov's decree of 1948 named him among the composers condemned for formalism, and the charge stuck to a musician whose scores were among the most widely played in the country. He remained a central figure in Soviet musical institutions and in the training of younger musicians for three decades afterwards, conducting as steadily as he wrote, and carrying his Armenian inheritance into the mainstream of Soviet concert life. He died in 1978, thirty years after the decree, and the music went on being played."},
  "nikolaimyaskovsky":{"wiki":"Nikolai Myaskovsky","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nikolai%20Myaskovsky.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Father of the Soviet symphony.","bio":"Nikolai Myaskovsky, born in 1881, has been called the father of the Soviet symphony, and the count alone supports it: twenty-seven symphonies, spanning the whole of the Soviet Union's first decades. He studied with Reinhold Glière and became one of the central teachers of his generation in Moscow, a composer other composers measured themselves against rather than a public celebrity. He met Sergei Prokofiev as a fellow student in 1906, and the two corresponded for forty-four years, until Myaskovsky's death. He was awarded the Stalin Prize five times over, a mark of official standing that has complicated his reputation ever since, and he died in Moscow in 1950 with his symphonies far less played than his influence on other musicians would suggest."},
  "alexandermosolov":{"wiki":"Alexander Mosolov","blurb":"The Iron Foundry, then the camps.","bio":"Alexander Mosolov, born in 1900, was a Soviet composer whose early work belonged to the machine-struck futurism of the 1920s. A graduate of the Moscow Conservatory, he wrote piano sonatas and vocal music, but international fame came from a single orchestral piece, now known as 'The Iron Foundry' (1926), which was heard everywhere as the sound of industry itself. What followed was the camps. He survived them and lived until 1973, but the early futurist career did not survive with him, and he remains a composer whose international reputation rests almost entirely on one short piece written in his twenties. Few careers illustrate so compactly what the Soviet century did to the experimenters it had briefly encouraged: the noise of the new factories was a permitted subject only for as long as the authorities said it was."},
  "galinaustvolskaya":{"wiki":"Galina Ustvolskaya","blurb":"Shostakovich's pupil, and like nobody else.","bio":"Galina Ustvolskaya, born in 1919, was a Russian composer who spent her working life in Leningrad and wrote music that sounds like nobody else's. She became Dmitri Shostakovich's pupil in 1939, and the relationship ran in both directions: he proposed marriage and was refused, and he thought well enough of her work to quote it in two of his own pieces. She kept her distance from the Soviet musical mainstream and from her teacher's shadow alike, and taught as well as composed. Her severe, spiritually charged later music is usually placed beside the holy minimalism of the late twentieth century, though she arrived at it on her own and by another route entirely. She died in 2006, by then quietly regarded as one of the most singular composers her country produced."},
  "reinholdgliere":{"wiki":"Reinhold Glière","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Reinhold%20Glier.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The teacher of three Soviet giants.","bio":"Reinhold Glière, born in 1875, came of German and Polish stock and made his career in Russia and then the Soviet Union; his largest contribution to the century was arguably made inside other people's music. When Sergei Prokofiev was eleven, Glière came to the family estate to give him private lessons; in Moscow he later taught Nikolai Myaskovsky and Aram Khachaturian, which means three of the defining figures of Soviet music passed through his hands. He composed steadily on his own account and was honoured accordingly, named People's Artist of the RSFSR in 1935 and of the USSR in 1938. He remained a fixture of Moscow musical life, teaching and conducting, until his death in 1956, and is now met more often as a name in other composers' biographies than as a voice of his own."},
  "andreizhdanov":{"wiki":"Andrei Zhdanov","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/%D0%90%D0%BD%D0%B4%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B9%20%D0%96%D0%B4%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B2%20(1941)%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The official who denounced the composers.","bio":"Andrei Zhdanov, born in 1896, was a Soviet politician rather than a musician: after the Second World War he served as the party's propagandist-in-chief and built the cultural policy that carries his name. The Zhdanov Doctrine set the terms on which Soviet artists worked and remained in force until Stalin's death. His place on a map of music comes from the decree of 1948, which denounced Dmitri Shostakovich, Sergei Prokofiev and Aram Khachaturian for formalism; Shostakovich lost his teaching posts, and all three were marked by it for years. Zhdanov was widely considered Stalin's likeliest successor, but died in 1948, the year of the decree itself. He is included here because in those postwar years he did more than most composers to determine what Soviet music could be."},
  "charlesives":{"wiki":"Charles Ives","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Charles%20Edward%20Ives%20-%20National%20Portrait%20Gallery%2C%20Smithsonian%20Institution%20(NPG.82.185).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Actuary, businessman, American original.","bio":"Charles Ives, born in Danbury in 1874, worked in New York as an actuary and businessman and composed in his own time, arriving decades early at quarter-tones, polytonality and collage while almost nobody was listening. The training came from two directions. His father, George Ives, a bandmaster, made him sing in one key while being accompanied in another; Horatio Parker taught him at Yale, and Ives kept the training and hid the experiments. For years his music went unperformed. Henry Cowell published him in 'New Music Quarterly' when hardly anyone else would, and it was through Cowell and Lou Harrison that the work was at last recognised, and Ives with it, as an American original. He returned such favours where he could: he wrote Elliott Carter's letter of recommendation to Harvard. He died in 1954."},
  "henrycowell":{"wiki":"Henry Cowell","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Henry%20Cowell%20portrait%20NYPL%204002097%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Tone clusters, and everyone else's publisher.","bio":"Henry Cowell, born in California in 1897, was a composer, pianist, writer, teacher and publisher, and the great connector of American musical experiment. As a performer he was notorious for his tone clusters; as a thinker he set out possibilities in 'New Musical Resources' that others spent decades realising — it was that book that gave Conlon Nancarrow the idea of composing for player piano. Through 'New Music Quarterly' he published Charles Ives when almost nobody else would, and Amadeo Roldán's 'Rítmicas', which is why they survive. He taught John Cage in California and New York before sending him on to Schoenberg, and his writing and music marked Lou Harrison and George Antheil as well. With Percy Grainger he shared the position of experimenter at the margins of the academy. He died in 1965."},
  "carlruggles":{"wiki":"Carl Ruggles","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ruggles%201911.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Dissonant counterpoint, painstakingly few works.","bio":"Carl Ruggles, born in 1876, was an American composer, painter and teacher who worked in Vermont with no formal musical education and at an almost impossibly slow rate. His method was a rigorous atonal counterpoint — Charles Seeger coined the term 'dissonant counterpoint' for it — in which a pitch was not repeated until some eight other pitch classes had intervened, a discipline as strict as serialism while never being serial. He is counted a founder of the American ultramodernist group that also included Henry Cowell and Ruth Crawford Seeger. Perfectionism kept the catalogue tiny: a handful of scores, revised for years, which is why a composer of his standing is heard so seldom. He painted, taught, and lived to be ninety-five, dying in 1971."},
  "ruthcrawfordseeger":{"wiki":"Ruth Crawford Seeger","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ruth%20Crawford%20Seeger.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Ultramodernist, then folk song's transcriber.","bio":"Ruth Crawford Seeger, born in 1901, was an American composer and musicologist and a central member of the group of Americans known as the ultramodernists, whose music announced the emerging modernist aesthetic. She composed chiefly in the 1920s and 1930s, and the String Quartet of 1931 is the work that carries her reputation; Elliott Carter was among the composers who learned from her. Charles Seeger was her teacher first, then her husband — they married in 1932 — and her collaborator on the folk-song transcriptions that took over her later working life, as her attention turned from modernist construction to the songs themselves from the late 1930s onward. She worked in Chicago and then Washington, and died in 1953. The modernist output is small and has grown steadily in stature since."},
  "edgardvarese":{"wiki":"Edgard Varèse","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Edgar%20Var%C3%A8se%20-%20John%20French%20Sloan.jpg?width=480","blurb":"He liberated sound from pitch.","bio":"Edgard Varèse, born in 1883, was a French-American composer who thought of music as organised sound and of sound as living matter, conceived in masses that behave like crystals rather than in melody and accompaniment. Ferruccio Busoni's 'Sketch of a New Aesthetic' gave him, in Berlin, the idea of liberating sound from pitch, and Luigi Russolo's 'L'arte dei rumori' of 1913 supplied the argument that noise is compositional material; for Varèse, music was organised noise before it was anything else. He wrote 'Ionisation', later 'Déserts', and in 1958 'Poème électronique' for Le Corbusier's Philips Pavilion at the Brussels Expo. He taught William Grant Still from 1923 in New York, and Chou Wen-chung, who became his assistant and, after his death in 1965, his editor and executor. Musical space, he insisted, had no walls to it at all."},
  "aaroncopland":{"wiki":"Aaron Copland","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Aaron%20Copland%20in%201962.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The dean of American music.","bio":"Aaron Copland, born in New York in 1900, was the composer his peers called the dean of American music. In 1921 he became the first American to study composition with Nadia Boulanger at Fontainebleau, and she went on to teach most of the rest of his generation. From the 1930s he wrote in the deliberately accessible vernacular style that fixed an idea of how American music sounds — the ballets 'Appalachian Spring', 'Billy the Kid' and 'Rodeo', the 'Fanfare for the Common Man', the Third Symphony. Serge Koussevitzky premiered nearly everything and built Tanglewood partly around him, and Copland used that platform for others: Alberto Ginastera in 1946, Halim El-Dabh in 1950, and Mario Davidovsky, brought from Buenos Aires in 1958. Leonard Bernstein, who always named him as the decisive influence, conducted and recorded almost all of it. He died in 1990."},
  "virgilthomson":{"wiki":"Virgil Thomson","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Virgil%20Thomson%20by%20Carl%20Van%20Vechten.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Composer-critic of the American sound.","bio":"Virgil Thomson, born in 1896, was an American composer and critic who divided his life between Paris and New York and was instrumental in the making of the American sound in concert music. He studied with Nadia Boulanger, and found in Gertrude Stein the collaborator who mattered most: together they made 'Four Saints in Three Acts' and 'The Mother of Us All'. He has been called a modernist, a neoromantic and a neoclassicist by turns, and described as combining humanity with an Olympian detachment, his expressive voice always carefully muted — until the late opera 'Lord Byron', which, unlike everything before it, lets real passion through. He conducted, wrote film scores, and spent years as one of the most quotable critics in America. He died in 1989."},
  "royharris":{"wiki":"Roy Harris","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Roy%20Harris%20(ca%201965).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The symphonist of American subjects.","bio":"Roy Harris, born in 1898, was an American composer who wrote on American subjects and is remembered above all for one work, his Third Symphony, which carried his name further than anything else he produced. Like so many of his generation he went to Paris to study with Nadia Boulanger, and brought that discipline home to a music consciously rooted in his own country rather than in Europe. He worked for much of his life as a music educator, based for long stretches in California, and wrote prolifically in a manner that never again caught the public as the Third had. He died in 1979, a composer whose reputation narrowed to a single score while the ambition behind it went largely unheard."},
  "georgegershwin":{"wiki":"George Gershwin","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/George%20Gershwin%201937.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Popular song meets the concert hall.","bio":"George Gershwin, born in New York in 1898, wrote across jazz, popular song and classical music without ever quite accepting the borders between them. From the song trade came 'Swanee' (1919), 'Fascinating Rhythm' (1924), 'Embraceable You' (1928) and 'I Got Rhythm' (1930), which became part of the basic vocabulary of jazz; from the same hands came 'Rhapsody in Blue' (1924) and 'An American in Paris' (1928), and then the opera 'Porgy and Bess' (1935), with 'Summertime' inside it. 'Of Thee I Sing' (1931) took the Pulitzer Prize for Drama, the first musical ever to do so, though as its composer he had no share in the award. He asked both Maurice Ravel and Nadia Boulanger to teach him and both refused on much the same grounds — 'why be a second-rate Ravel when you are a first-rate Gershwin?' He died in 1937."},
  "samuelbarber":{"wiki":"Samuel Barber","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Samuel%20Barber.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Lyricism held against the century.","bio":"Samuel Barber, born in 1910, studied composition for nine years with Rosario Scalero at the Curtis Institute in Philadelphia and for more than twenty-five with his uncle, the composer Sidney Homer. The result was a musician who mostly declined his century's experimental options in favour of nineteenth-century harmony and form, lyric line and open emotional expression. Arturo Toscanini broadcast the 'Adagio for Strings' in 1938 and made it, and him, instantly famous. After 1940 he absorbed more of modernism than that reputation suggests: the dissonance and chromaticism of the Cello Concerto (1945) and 'Medea's Dance of Vengeance' (1955), the tonal ambiguity and narrow serialism of the Piano Sonata (1949), 'Prayers of Kierkegaard' (1954) and 'Nocturne' (1959). Gian Carlo Menotti was his partner for more than forty years and wrote the libretto of his opera 'Vanessa'. He died in 1981."},
  "giancarlomenotti":{"wiki":"Gian Carlo Menotti","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gcmenotti.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Verismo carried past the war.","bio":"Gian Carlo Menotti, born in Italy in 1911, was a composer, librettist, director and playwright who wrote his own texts and staged his own works, producing twenty-five operas in the course of it. He called himself an American composer while keeping his Italian citizenship all his life, and worked between Philadelphia and Spoleto. Deeply marked by Puccini and Mussorgsky, he carried the verismo tradition into the years after the Second World War, turning his back on atonality and on everything the Second Viennese School stood for, in favour of an expressive lyricism that sets language to natural speech rhythms so as to sharpen its meaning. His most successful works came in the 1940s and 1950s, and he stood among the century's most frequently performed composers of opera. Samuel Barber was his partner for more than forty years; Menotti wrote the libretto of Barber's 'Vanessa'. He died in 2007."},
  "williamgrantstill":{"wiki":"William Grant Still","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/William%20Grant%20Still%20by%20Carl%20Van%20Vechten.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Afro-American Symphony, and doors opened.","bio":"William Grant Still, born in Mississippi in 1895 and raised in Little Rock, composed nearly two hundred works — five symphonies, four ballets, nine operas, more than thirty choral pieces, art songs and chamber music — and settled in Los Angeles, where he also wrote for film. He attended Wilberforce University and Oberlin Conservatory, studied with George Whitefield Chadwick at the New England Conservatory, and from 1923 worked in New York with Edgard Varèse, which is to say he took instruction from the most conservative and the most radical teachers available. The 'Afro-American Symphony' of 1931 made his name, and his close association with Black literary and cultural figures places him within the Harlem Renaissance. He encouraged Ulysses Kay from the younger composer's student years and pressed his music on conductors. He died in 1978."},
  "florenceprice":{"wiki":"Florence Price","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Composer%20Florence%20Price%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The first Black woman symphonist.","bio":"Florence Price, born in Little Rock in 1887, trained at the New England Conservatory and settled in Chicago in 1927, working there for the rest of her life. No African-American woman before her had been recognised as a symphonic composer, and none had had a work played by a major orchestra — a distinction that says as much about American concert life as about her. She wrote more than three hundred pieces: four symphonies, four concertos, choral settings, art songs, chamber works and pieces for solo instruments, and she taught, played piano and worked as an organist. Margaret Bonds was her pupil in Chicago and gave the premiere of Price's 'Fantasie Nègre' in 1930. Much of it went unheard for decades, until a large cache of her scores and papers surfaced in 2009 in the summer house she had abandoned, beginning the reappraisal now under way. She died in 1953."},
  "margaretbonds":{"wiki":"Margaret Bonds","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Margaret%20Bonds.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Langston Hughes's composer, Price's pupil.","bio":"Margaret Bonds, born in Chicago in 1913, was a composer, pianist, arranger and teacher who studied with Florence Price and gave the premiere of Price's 'Fantasie Nègre' in 1930. Her thirty-year collaboration with Langston Hughes, in songs, settings and stage works, sits at the centre of her output, alongside the arrangements of African American spirituals for which she is still best known. Hers was a career of firsts: the first Black woman to appear with the all-white, all-male Chicago Symphony Orchestra, among the first whose music was broadcast on European radio, the first to have it played widely in Africa, only the second Black woman in classical music elected to full membership of ASCAP, and the first woman of any background to win three ASCAP awards. She later worked in New York, and died in 1972."},
  "dukeellington":{"wiki":"Duke Ellington","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Duke%20Ellington%20-%20publicity.JPG?width=480","blurb":"The orchestra as his instrument.","bio":"Duke Ellington, born in 1899, led his own jazz orchestra in New York from 1924 until the end of his life, and used it the way other composers use a score, writing for the particular voices of the players in front of him. He belongs on a map of concert music because of the extended works: the long-form suites and concert pieces in which he pushed past the three-minute record and the dance floor into forms of his own devising. Ralph J. Gleason, the critic, judged him the most important composer America had produced, and the claim has been argued seriously ever since. He was also a pianist of real originality and, late in life, the author of his own memoir. He died in 1974, having spent fifty years demonstrating that a band could be a composer's instrument."},
  "marionbauer":{"wiki":"Marion Bauer","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/MarionBauer1922.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Boulanger's first American pupil.","bio":"Marion Bauer, born in 1882, was an American composer, teacher, writer, critic and music theorist who helped shape the way American music thought of itself in the first half of the twentieth century. In 1906, long before her countrymen went to Nadia Boulanger in numbers, Bauer became the very first American student that teacher had, in an arrangement paid for in English lessons rather than money. Back in New York she taught, wrote and worked as a critic, and became one of the country's most persistent advocates for new music, which is a large part of why her name recurs in other composers' stories. She composed throughout, and died in 1955, remembered as much for what she argued for as for what she wrote."},
  "luigirussolo":{"wiki":"Luigi Russolo","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Luigi%20Russolo%20ca.%201916.gif?width=480","blurb":"The man who composed noise.","bio":"Luigi Russolo, born in 1885, was a Futurist painter first and a composer second, and the author in 1913 of 'L'arte dei rumori', the manifesto arguing that noise itself is compositional material. He finished his schooling at the seminary of Portogruaro in 1901 and moved to Milan, where the Futurists were gathering; Francesco Balilla Pratella's musical manifestos of 1910 and 1911 prompted his reply, and F. T. Marinetti published it. Then he built the instruments to prove the case: the intonarumori, a family of noise-generating devices that he presented in concerts in 1913 and 1914, and again after the First World War, notably in Paris in 1921. Edgard Varèse took up the argument directly, and the intonarumori are the acknowledged ancestor of Pierre Schaeffer's musique concrète. Russolo died in 1947, usually counted among the first composers of noise music."},
  "francescobalillapratella":{"wiki":"Francesco Balilla Pratella","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Francesco%20Balilla%20Pratella.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Futurism's first musical manifestos.","bio":"Francesco Balilla Pratella, born in 1880 and working from Ravenna, was an Italian composer, musicologist and essayist, and the first person to try to set out what a Futurist music would be. His manifestos of 1910 and 1911 made him the leading advocate of Futurism in Italian music, and their most consequential effect was the reply they provoked from Luigi Russolo, who went a great deal further than Pratella ever had. The irony sits plainly in the record: little of his own music sounds much like the doctrine he argued so loudly in print. He went on composing and writing about music until his death in 1955, remembered less for his own scores than for the argument he set in motion and then could not control."},
  "ftmarinetti":{"wiki":"Filippo Tommaso Marinetti","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/FilippoTommasoMarinetti.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Futurism's founder and loudest megaphone.","bio":"F. T. Marinetti, born in 1876, was an Italian poet, editor and art theorist, and the founder and impresario of Futurism. His 'Manifesto of Futurism', written and published in 1909, launched the movement; before that he had been associated with the utopian, Symbolist community at the Abbaye de Créteil in 1907 and 1908. He was not primarily a musician, but he mattered to music as a publisher and organiser: it was Marinetti who put Luigi Russolo's musical manifesto into print, and it was the Futurist circle, where Russolo began as a painter, that gave noise its first theory. The other half of his legacy is harder to carry: in 1919 he was co-author of the Fascist Manifesto. He worked in Milan and died in 1944."},
  "amadeoroldan":{"wiki":"Amadeo Roldán","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Amadeo%20Roldan.JPG?width=480","blurb":"Percussion alone, Havana, 1930.","bio":"Amadeo Roldán, born in Paris in 1900 to a Cuban mother, the pianist Albertina Gardes, and a Spanish father, was started in music by her and made his career in Havana as a violinist, concertmaster, conductor and composer. He is one of the two figures of Afrocubanismo, the movement that brought Afro-Cuban rhythm and percussion into concert music: his 'Rítmicas' V and VI of 1930 are among the first Western concert works written for percussion alone. They survive because Henry Cowell printed them in 'New Music Quarterly'. In the same year he set Nicolás Guillén's 'Motivos de son'. He and Alejandro García Caturla wrote to each other constantly and died within a year of one another, Roldán in 1939, leaving Cuban modernism suddenly without either of its founders."},
  "alejandrogarciacaturla":{"wiki":"Alejandro García Caturla","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/G.%20Caturla.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Afrocubanismo's other voice, murdered young.","bio":"Alejandro García Caturla, born in Remedios in 1906, was a Cuban composer who worked creolised Cuban and Afro-Cuban material into art music, and who forms with Amadeo Roldán the pair at the origin of Afrocubanismo. The two corresponded constantly, set the same Afro-Cuban verse by Nicolás Guillén, and died within a year of each other. He studied with Nadia Boulanger in Paris in 1928 and also conducted, but he earned his living in the law: he was serving as a judge when he was murdered in 1940, at thirty-four. What he left is a small body of work fusing European technique with the Cuban material he had grown up among, and a reputation resting as much on how much further it looked as though he would go."},
  "ernestolecuona":{"wiki":"Ernesto Lecuona","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/LecuonaE.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Cuba's melody-maker at the piano.","bio":"Ernesto Lecuona y Casado, born in Havana in 1895, was a Cuban composer and pianist whose music travelled further than that of almost any of his countrymen. He left more than six hundred works, among them songs, zarzuelas, piano pieces and music for symphony orchestra, and a striking number of them settled permanently into the Latin, jazz and classical repertoires at once. He worked as a songwriter and as a film composer as well, moving easily between the concert platform, the lyric theatre and the screen. A formidable pianist, he made the keyboard the natural home of the Cuban idiom, and his playing carried his own music abroad. He belongs to the Afrocubanismo generation that took Afro-Cuban material seriously as concert music, though his instinct was always melodic rather than theoretical. He died in 1963."},
  "gilbertovaldes":{"wiki":"Gilberto Valdés","blurb":"Brought Afro-Cuban rhythm onto the bandstand.","bio":"Gilberto Valdés, born in Havana in 1905, was a Cuban composer, conductor, flautist and clarinettist who worked in the afro strand of Cuban popular music. He was among the first bandleaders to build African melody, rhythm and traditional subject matter directly into his music rather than treat them as decoration, and he did it from the dance band rather than the conservatory. His songs reached a wider public in 1935, when Rita Montaner sang 'Bembé', 'Baró', 'Tambó' and 'Sangre africana' at the Teatro Principal de la Comedia, with Rafael Betancourt at the piano. He later held American as well as Cuban nationality, continuing to direct and conduct. Where the Afrocubanismo composers approached the same sources from the concert tradition, Valdés came at them through popular performance, which gave his music its drive. He died in 1972."},
  "nicolasguillen":{"wiki":"Nicolás Guillén","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nicol%C3%A1sGuill%C3%A9n-1942.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The poet Afro-Cuban composers set to music.","bio":"Nicolás Guillén, born in 1902, was a Cuban writer — poet, journalist, political activist — remembered as his country's national poet. He was not a composer, but he supplied the words, and with them the rhythm, that the Afrocubanismo composers needed: his verse took the speech and the popular music of black Cuba and made it literature, which meant it could also be made into concert music. Amadeo Roldán set his Motivos de son in 1930, and Alejandro García Caturla turned to the same body of Afro-Cuban verse, both men finding in Guillén a writer who had already done the difficult work of treating a vernacular idiom as serious material. The politics of race and nation in his poems came with the music that used them. He remained a central figure in Cuban letters, working in Havana until his death in 1989."},
  "yamadakosaku":{"wiki":"Kōsaku Yamada","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kosaku%20Yamada%2002.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Gave Japan its first symphony and orchestra.","bio":"Yamada Kōsaku, born in Tokyo in 1886, was the composer and conductor who gave Japan its own foothold in the European orchestral tradition. He studied at the Berlin Hochschule between 1910 and 1913 under Max Bruch, absorbing the German tradition at first hand, and came home with an ambition that was institutional as much as artistic. He wrote the first symphony by a Japanese composer and founded Japan's first professional orchestra, so that within a single career the country acquired both new repertoire and the body of players needed to perform it. He worked as a conductor and a musicologist alongside composing. Much of his importance lies in that groundwork: the generations of Japanese composers who followed had a concert culture to write for because he had built one. He died in 1965."},
  "xianxinghai":{"wiki":"Xian Xinghai","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Xianxinghai.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Wrote the Yellow River Cantata.","bio":"Xian Xinghai, who lived from 1905 to 1945, was one of the earliest Chinese composers to take up the Western classical tradition, and his influence on the musicians who came after him was considerable. He trained in Paris, studying with Vincent d'Indy at the Schola Cantorum and with Paul Dukas at the Conservatoire until Dukas's death in 1935, then carried that technique back to a country at war, working in Yan'an. The Yellow River Cantata is the work by which he is remembered; the Yellow River Piano Concerto was later built upon it, and it is through that pair of pieces that most listeners know his name. He composed in the major musical forms, and his example told the next generation what such a career could look like. He died in 1945, at forty."},
  "huangzi":{"wiki":"Huang Tzu","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Huang%20Zi.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Built the Chinese conservatory curriculum.","bio":"Huang Zi — Jinwu by courtesy name — was born in 1904 into the first Chinese generation to take up Western composition, and his lasting work proved to be as much pedagogical as compositional. He studied at Oberlin and at Yale, and returned to Shanghai to build a conservatory curriculum from almost nothing, settling what a Chinese musician's training would consist of and in what order it would come. He worked at the Shanghai Conservatory during the years when the Russian émigré Alexander Tcherepnin taught and published there, and the two of them between them shaped the first generation of Chinese modernists. He died in 1938, at thirty-four, which makes the scale of his influence on later Chinese musical education all the more remarkable: the syllabus outlived the man by decades."},
  "heluting":{"wiki":"He Luting","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/%E5%B9%B4%E8%BD%BB%E6%97%B6%E7%9A%84%E8%B4%BA%E7%BB%BF%E6%B1%80.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Won the competition that made a school.","bio":"He Luting, born in 1903, was a Chinese composer whose career was opened by a single competition. In 1934 Alexander Tcherepnin, the Russian émigré then in Shanghai, offered a prize for a piano piece 'in Chinese style'; He Luting's entry, The Cowherd's Flute, won it, and the prize produced both the piece itself and the career that followed from it. From the 1930s he wrote songs for Chinese films, some of which have stayed popular ever since, reaching audiences far beyond the concert hall. He later held political and institutional office as well, a public figure in Chinese musical life across most of the century. He died in 1999, having lived long enough to see the tradition he helped begin become established."},
  "ahmedadnansaygun":{"wiki":"Ahmet Adnan Saygun","blurb":"Bartók's companion through Anatolia.","bio":"Ahmed Adnan Saygun, born in 1907, was a Turkish composer and musicologist, one of the Turkish Five who built a concert tradition for the young republic. He studied with Vincent d'Indy at the Schola Cantorum in Paris between 1928 and 1931, learning the European forms he would fill with Anatolian material. In 1936 he accompanied Béla Bartók on a folk-song collecting expedition through southern Anatolia, working beside him on the transcriptions — a formative apprenticeship in the discipline of listening to peasant singers accurately rather than picturesquely, and the encounter that connected Turkish ethnomusicology to Bartók's method. He worked as a conductor and choreographer as well as a composer, and was based in Ankara. His music, and his fieldwork, gave the Turkish repertoire its scholarly foundation. He died in 1991."},
  "ulvicemalerkin":{"wiki":"Ulvi Cemal Erkin","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ulvi%20Cemal%20Erkin.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Turkish Five, trained by Nadia Boulanger.","bio":"Ulvi Cemal Erkin, born in 1906, was a Turkish composer, pianist and conductor, one of the group of symphonic composers born between 1904 and 1910 who came to be called the Turkish Five. All of them trained in Europe and returned charged with making a Turkish music that would be universal and local at once. Erkin studied in Paris with Nadia Boulanger, the teacher through whom much of that European training reached his generation. Like his colleagues he worked in the Western forms — symphony, concerto, opera — while drawing melody, mode, harmony and rhythm from Anatolian village music and from the Ottoman court tradition. Based in Ankara, he belonged to the group charged with giving the young republic a concert repertoire of its own. He died in 1972."},
  "komitas":{"wiki":"Komitas","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Komitas.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Founded the Armenian national school.","bio":"Komitas, born Soghomon Soghomonian in 1869, was an Ottoman-Armenian priest, singer, choirmaster and musicologist, and is counted the founder of Armenia's national school of music. He studied in Berlin and worked in Constantinople, collecting and arranging Armenian village song with a rigour that makes him one of the pioneers of ethnomusicology: he treated the peasant repertoire as a body of knowledge, not as raw material. Debussy heard him in Paris in 1906 and said the Antouni alone would have been enough to make him a great composer. The Armenian genocide of 1915 broke him, and he never recovered; he spent his last years unable to work, dying in 1935. The songs he collected and arranged remain the foundation of Armenian art music, and his name is inseparable from what was lost."},
  "halimeldabh":{"wiki":"Halim El-Dabh","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Halim%20El-Dabh2.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Made tape music before musique concrète.","bio":"Halim El-Dabh, born in Cairo in 1921, composed what is now recognised as one of the earliest works of tape music: The Expression of Zaar, made in Cairo in 1944, four years before musique concrète had a name. He came to the United States and studied with Aaron Copland at the Berkshire Music Center at Tanglewood in 1950. From 1959 he worked at the Columbia-Princeton Electronic Music Center alongside Vladimir Ussachevsky, producing some of the most inventive electronic music of the period. His long collaboration with Martha Graham began with Clytemnestra in 1958 and ran to six further scores. He was an ethnomusicologist and a university teacher as well as a composer, and settled in Ohio. He died in 2017, after a career spanning six decades."},
  "felasowande":{"wiki":"Fela Sowande","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fela%20Sowande%20(1961%2C%20other%20scan).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The father of Nigerian art music.","bio":"Fela Sowande, born in Lagos in 1905, is regarded as the father of modern Nigerian art music and remains perhaps the best-known African composer working in the European classical idiom. An organist by training and temperament, he divided his working life between Lagos and London, writing concert works in that idiom and playing the organ repertoire that went with it. He taught, and worked as an academic musician, so that his influence was passed on directly as well as through the scores. What he founded was less a style than the possibility of a Nigerian composer working on that scale at all. He and Ephraim Amu, working in parallel in Lagos and Accra, are the two founders of a West African art-music tradition. Appointed MBE and later a Nigerian chief, he died in 1987."},
  "ephraimamu":{"wiki":"Ephraim Amu","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/20000%20cedis%20(2002).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Built a Ghanaian idiom from Akan speech-tone.","bio":"Ephraim Kɔku Amu, born in 1899, was a Ghanaian composer, musicologist and teacher who built a concert idiom out of the shape of his own language. Working from Akan speech-tone, he insisted that melody set to an African text should follow the pitch contours that give the words their meaning, and from that principle he made a body of concert music that sounds Ghanaian at the level of the phrase rather than the surface. He was a teacher first, and his pupils carried the method on; the musicologist J. H. Kwabena Nketia was among those he mentored. With Fela Sowande in Lagos he is one of the two founders of a West African art-music tradition, the pair of them working in parallel and to the same end. He died in 1995."},
  "alexandertcherepnin":{"wiki":"Alexander Tcherepnin","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/TchAlar3-part.JPG?width=480","blurb":"The émigré who midwifed Chinese modernism.","bio":"Alexander Tcherepnin, born in the Russian Empire in 1899, was a composer, pianist and theorist whose years in East Asia made him the catalyst for a whole national school. Displaced from Russia and based largely in Paris, he arrived in Shanghai in the 1930s, taught at the conservatory there, and set up a publishing house to put the work of young Chinese composers into print. In 1934 he announced a competition for a piano piece 'in Chinese style'; the winner was He Luting's The Cowherd's Flute, and the prize effectively launched its composer's career. Around the conservatory he encountered Huang Zi and the rest of the first generation of Chinese modernists, publishing and promoting them. His own music is substantial, but his largest effect was on other people's. He died in 1977."},
  "aloishaba":{"wiki":"Alois Hába","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/H%C3%A1ba.JPG?width=480","blurb":"Quarter-tones, and a piano to play them.","bio":"Alois Hába, born in 1893, was a Czech composer, theorist and teacher who spent his career in the spaces between the notes of the piano. Working in Prague, he is one of the central figures of microtonal music: the quarter-tone scale above all, but also sixth-tones, fifth-tones and twelfth-tones, and in his theoretical writing a three-quarter-tone system that surfaces in passages of his own works. This was not a sideline. He wrote three operas, an enormous quantity of chamber music including sixteen string quartets, and piano, organ, choral and orchestral pieces, all requiring instruments that did not exist, so he had special keyboards and woodwinds built to play them. He was a theorist and a teacher as much as a composer, and he set the systems out in writing as well as in the scores. He died in 1973."},
  "ivanwyschnegradsky":{"wiki":"Ivan Wyschnegradsky","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/%D0%98.%D0%90.%D0%92%D1%8B%D1%88%D0%BD%D0%B5%D0%B3%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B4%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9%20%D0%B2%20%D0%9F%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%B6%D0%B5.jpg?width=480","blurb":"A lifetime given to microtonal music.","bio":"Ivan Wyschnegradsky, born in 1893, was a Russian composer known almost entirely for his microtonal music. From 1920 until the end of his life he lived in Paris, where he pursued the microtonal idea with a single-mindedness that kept him at the edge of the concert world for decades: the standard instruments could not play what he wrote, and his music waits on players and instruments willing to work with a finer grid of pitch than the twelve-note keyboard allows. He wrote theory alongside the music, working out how a divided octave might be organised into a coherent harmonic system rather than used for colour. He belongs to the handful of composers who took that idea seriously, and took it up early, when it had neither repertoire nor audience. He died in 1979."},
  "harrypartch":{"wiki":"Harry Partch","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Harry%20Partch%20portrait%201952.jpg?width=480","blurb":"A 43-tone scale, built by hand.","bio":"Harry Partch, who lived from 1901 to 1974 and worked mostly in California, rejected the equal-tempered keyboard entirely. He composed in just intonation, using scales of unequal intervals reaching to forty-three tones to the octave, and since no existing instrument could play them he built his own — a whole workshop of them, tuned to his scale and as striking to look at as to hear. He set out the reasoning in his book Genesis of a Music in 1947. With Lou Harrison he was among the first Western composers of the century to work systematically with microtonal scales, and he did it largely outside institutions, dependent on what private support he could find: the patron Betty Freeman sustained him and his instruments through his last years. The music cannot be played without them, which was the point."},
  "juliancarrillo":{"wiki":"Julián Carrillo","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Juli%C3%A1n%20Carrillo%20en%20Par%C3%ADs%2C%204%20sep%201899.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The theorist of Mexico's Thirteenth Sound.","bio":"Julián Carrillo Trujillo, born in Mexico in 1875, was a composer, conductor, violinist and music theorist, and the author of one of the century's most thoroughgoing microtonal systems. He called it Sonido 13, the Thirteenth Sound: everything beyond the twelve notes of the tempered scale, the tone divided into ever finer intervals and organised into a system he worked out in full and set down. It is that theory, rather than any single piece, on which his fame rests — the rare case of a composer known first for what he established about pitch and only then for what he wrote with it. He conducted and played the violin as well, and composed across a long life in Mexico that ran from 1875 to his death in 1965."},
  "conlonnancarrow":{"wiki":"Conlon Nancarrow","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Conlon-nancarrow-1418515548.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Player-piano studies beyond human hands.","bio":"Conlon Nancarrow, born in the United States in 1912, settled in Mexico City and gave most of his working life to the player piano. The idea came from Henry Cowell's book New Musical Resources, which pointed to the auto-playing instrument as a way past the limits of human performance; Nancarrow took the suggestion literally, and the Studies for Player Piano that followed were written for a machine precisely because no performer could play them. He worked in near-isolation for most of his life, with no players to satisfy and no audience waiting, which is part of why the music is so uncompromising. Wider recognition came only in the 1980s, when composers found the studies and could not quite believe their dates. He died in 1997."},
  "oliviermessiaen":{"wiki":"Olivier Messiaen","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Lezing%20Franse%20compoist%20Olivier%20Messiaen%20in%20Koninklijk%20Conservatorium%20in%20Den%20Haa%2C%20Bestanddeelnr%20933-8262.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Taught almost the entire postwar avant-garde.","bio":"Olivier Messiaen, born in 1908, was a French composer, organist and ornithologist, and the most consequential composition teacher of the twentieth century. His own Mode de valeurs et d'intensités, which extended serial ordering to duration and dynamics as well as pitch, gave the postwar generation the idea of total serialism. He had studied with Paul Dukas at the Paris Conservatoire, whose advice — listen to the birds, they are great masters — he followed for the rest of his life. His 1944 harmony class produced Pierre Boulez and much of the French avant-garde; Stockhausen came to him in 1952, Alexander Goehr in 1955, György Kurtág in 1957. He told Xenakis to abandon harmony lessons and use his mathematics and architecture instead. Grisey, Murail and, last of all, George Benjamin followed. He died in 1992."},
  "pierreboulez":{"wiki":"Pierre Boulez","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Pierre%20Boulez%20(1968).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Serialism's polemicist, then its institution-builder.","bio":"Pierre Boulez, born in 1925, was the dominant strategist of postwar European music: composer, conductor, polemicist and founder of institutions, IRCAM among them. He came out of Messiaen's 1944 harmony class at the Paris Conservatoire, learned twelve-tone technique from René Leibowitz the following year and then repudiated him in public. His 1952 essay 'Schoenberg est mort' pushed the teacher aside in favour of Webern, whose music he declared the only usable starting point after 1945. Wolfgang Steinecke gave him the platform at Darmstadt. He corresponded closely with John Cage between 1949 and 1954 until the friendship broke over chance, which Boulez attacked as an abdication of choice; they barely spoke again. Le Marteau sans maître remains his emblematic score. As a conductor he reshaped how the modern repertoire is played. He died in 2016."},
  "karlheinzstockhausen":{"wiki":"Karlheinz Stockhausen","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Stockhausen%20March%202004%20excerpt.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Electronic music's restless father.","bio":"Karlheinz Stockhausen, born in 1928, was the most inventive and most argued-over composer of the postwar German avant-garde. He studied with Messiaen in Paris in 1952 and took the pointillist Webern of the Op. 27 Variations as the model for Kreuzspiel. Wolfgang Steinecke placed him at the centre of the Darmstadt courses; Herbert Eimert founded the electronic studio at WDR in Cologne and handed it to him, and out of it came Gesang der Jünglinge. Gruppen is his, and so is musical spatialization: the placing of players around the hall as part of the composition. György Ligeti worked beside him in the Cologne studio from 1956 before turning away from serialism. Cornelius Cardew was his assistant on Carré for three years, then attacked him in Stockhausen Serves Imperialism. He taught Kevin Volans and Wolfgang Rihm. He died in 2007."},
  "luiginono":{"wiki":"Luigi Nono","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Luigi%20Nono%20(1979).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Serialism with an explicit politics.","bio":"Luigi Nono, born in Venice in 1924, was the Italian avant-gardist who refused to let the new technique be politically neutral. He studied in Venice with Bruno Maderna and with Malipiero, and emerged as one of the leading serial composers of the Darmstadt years, but he used the language for texts of protest, resistance and mourning, insisting that a compositional method carried a politics whether or not its users admitted it. In 1955 he married Nuria Schoenberg, the composer's daughter, with Stravinsky as a witness — a marriage that tied him directly to the origins of the tradition he was extending. He taught Helmut Lachenmann in Venice between 1958 and 1960, passing on both the technique and the conviction that went with it. He lived and worked in Venice until his death in 1990."},
  "lucianoberio":{"wiki":"Luciano Berio","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Luciano%20Berio.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Sequenzas, Sinfonia, and Berberian's voice.","bio":"Luciano Berio, born in 1925, was the most companionable of the Italian avant-garde, an experimenter who never lost his taste for singing lines. His early music absorbed Stravinsky and then serial and electronic technique; the later work opened onto indeterminacy and the use of spoken text as raw material. He studied with Luigi Dallapiccola at Tanglewood in 1952. The Sequenzas, a series of solo pieces, made virtuosity itself the subject, and Sinfonia carried furthest his idea of the spoken text as basic compositional material. He married the singer Cathy Berberian in 1950 and wrote Sequenza III, Circles and Recital I for her voice; they went on working together for a decade after the divorce. He taught Louis Andriessen and, at Mills College, Steve Reich, whom he told that if he wanted to write tonal music he should write tonal music. Paul Sacher championed him in Basel. He died in 2003."},
  "brunomaderna":{"wiki":"Bruno Maderna","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/B%20Maderna%201963.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Venice's teacher, the avant-garde's conductor.","bio":"Bruno Maderna, born in Venice in 1920, was a composer, conductor and teacher who did as much for other people's music as for his own. He was the conductor the postwar avant-garde relied on to make its most difficult scores sound like music rather than arithmetic, working between Venice and Darmstadt through the years when that repertoire was being created. As a teacher he shaped Luigi Nono, who studied with him and with Malipiero in Venice. He composed throughout, and held academic posts as well, but it was the conducting that placed him at the centre of the new repertoire and kept him there. He worked almost to the end, dying in 1973 at fifty-three, in the middle of a conducting career that showed no sign of slowing."},
  "henripousseur":{"wiki":"Henri Pousseur","blurb":"Belgian serialist and restless theorist.","bio":"Henri Pousseur, born in Liège in 1929, composed, taught and theorised in roughly equal measure, and belonged to the group of young Europeans who gathered around the serial project after 1945. He is of the Darmstadt generation both by date and by conviction, and like several of them he wrote as much as he composed, working out in prose what the new technique could and could not carry. Teaching occupied much of his life — he held a university post for many years — so his effect ran through classrooms as much as through scores. He composed steadily across a long career, and his theoretical writing remains part of the record of how that generation argued with itself about what serialism was for. He died in 2009, having spent half a century close to the centre of European new music without ever settling into a single manner."},
  "jeanbarraque":{"wiki":"Jean Barraqué","blurb":"A small, severe body of serial work.","bio":"Jean Barraqué, born in Paris in 1928, was a French composer and writer on music whose reputation rests on a small output. What he did finish is serial, and severe, pursued with a seriousness that left no room for the provisional. He wrote criticism and analysis alongside the composing, and was as much a writer on music as a maker of it. He belongs to the Paris of the postwar years and is grouped with the Darmstadt serialists, sharing the language of that generation, though his own working life ran at a distance from its platforms and its public arguments. There was never a movement to carry the work, only the scores themselves, and there were never many of those. He died in 1973, at forty-five."},
  "miltonbabbitt":{"wiki":"Milton Babbitt","blurb":"American serialism, arrived at independently.","bio":"Milton Babbitt, born in 1916, was an American composer, theorist, mathematician and teacher who extended Schoenberg's twelve-tone method into a full compositional system built on permutations of the total chromatic — reaching total serialism in America at much the same time as, and independently of, the Europeans. He taught at Princeton for decades and was central to the Columbia-Princeton Electronic Music Center, where he worked with the RCA Mark II synthesiser alongside Vladimir Ussachevsky and where Mario Davidovsky made the Synchronisms. His pupils were not all what one might expect: Stephen Sondheim took private lessons in the early 1950s that were spent largely analysing Jerome Kern, and Jonathan Harvey came to Princeton in 1969 on a Harkness fellowship. He received both a Pulitzer Prize and a MacArthur Fellowship. He died in 2011."},
  "wolfgangsteinecke":{"wiki":"Wolfgang Steinecke","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bundesarchiv%20B%20145%20Bild-F004561-0004%2C%20Darmstadt%2C%20Internationaler%20Kurs%20f%C3%BCr%20neue%20Musik%20(cropped-Steinecke).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Convened Darmstadt, and the avant-garde with it.","bio":"Wolfgang Steinecke, born in 1910, was a German musicologist, critic and cultural politician, and one of the most important enablers in twentieth-century music. In Darmstadt after the war he set about reviving the city's cultural life, and in 1946 he initiated the Ferienkurse — the summer courses that reconnected German musicians to an international contemporary scene from which the war and the years before it had cut them off. His decisive choice was to put the young serialists at the centre of the enterprise rather than the established figures: Boulez and Stockhausen were given the platform there, and 'Darmstadt' became shorthand for a whole postwar aesthetic. He ran the courses until his death in 1961, by which time they had become an institution in their own right."},
  "pierreschaeffer":{"wiki":"Pierre Schaeffer","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Psconcer.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Invented musique concrète inside a radio station.","bio":"Pierre Schaeffer, born in 1910, was an engineer, broadcaster, acoustician and writer who invented a way of composing with recorded sound. At the RTF studio in Paris in 1948 he began making music directly from recorded sound rather than from notes, treating the recorded object itself as the compositional material and naming the result musique concrète. He acknowledged Luigi Russolo's intonarumori as its ancestor. With Pierre Henry he made Symphonie pour un homme seul in 1950, and in 1958 he founded the Groupe de Recherches Musicales, with Luc Ferrari among its co-founders, which became the training ground for a generation: Éliane Radigue served her apprenticeship in his studio, and José Maceda worked there too. He wrote extensively on sound, communication and culture. He died in 1995."},
  "pierrehenry":{"wiki":"Pierre Henry","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Pierre%20Henry%20Waiting%20With%20His%20Life%20Asisstance.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Musique concrète's most prolific hand.","bio":"Pierre Henry, born in Paris in 1927, was the composer who turned musique concrète from an experiment into a body of work. He collaborated with Pierre Schaeffer at the Paris studio, and their Symphonie pour un homme seul of 1950 is the founding large-scale piece of the medium; he was part of the Groupe de Recherches Musicales that followed. Working afterwards from his own Studio Apsome, he composed prolifically for the concert room and for film, and Éliane Radigue worked there as his assistant before developing her own music. He was as much a record producer and sound artist as a composer, and his tape pieces found audiences far outside the avant-garde that had produced them. He died in 2017."},
  "herberteimert":{"wiki":"Herbert Eimert","blurb":"Founded Cologne's electronic studio, then handed it on.","bio":"Herbert Eimert, born in 1897, was a German music theorist, musicologist, critic, editor and radio producer whose most consequential act was administrative. At the WDR in Cologne he founded the electronic music studio, the counterweight to musique concrète: where that began from recorded sound, Cologne worked from sound generated electronically. Eimert set it up, argued its case in print and on air, and then handed it to the young Karlheinz Stockhausen. He composed as well, and edited, and broadcast. A critic and radio producer by trade, he understood that a studio, a broadcast slot and a publication together amount to a movement, and he assembled all three. His name is now far less familiar than those of the composers he made room for. He died in 1972."},
  "iannisxenakis":{"wiki":"Iannis Xenakis","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/XenakisMDaniel%20crop.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Mathematics, architecture, and sound as mass.","bio":"Iannis Xenakis, born in 1922, came to composition through engineering and architecture, and never stopped thinking like a builder. He worked in Le Corbusier's Paris office for twelve years, designed the Philips Pavilion himself and had to fight to be credited for it. When he showed his music to Messiaen, the advice was to skip harmony lessons and use his mathematics and architecture instead — which he did, developing stochastic composition, in which the behaviour of a mass of sounds is shaped by probability rather than by note-to-note choice. He taught Gérard Grisey at Darmstadt and in Paris, and Yuji Takahashi, who became the principal interpreter of his piano music. He and José Maceda arrived at mass structures from opposite directions and knew it. He died in 2001."},
  "lucferrari":{"wiki":"Luc Ferrari","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Luc%20Ferrari%20Composer%20of%20France.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Founding member of the GRM.","bio":"Luc Ferrari, born in 1929, was a French composer and one of the pioneers of two overlapping things: musique concrète, which composed directly with recorded sound, and electroacoustic music more broadly. In 1958 he became a founding member, with Pierre Schaeffer, of the Groupe de Recherches Musicales at French radio, the studio where much of that technique was worked out, and he belonged to the Paris circle that included Schaeffer and Pierre Henry through its most inventive years. Composing there meant treating sound as a material to be cut, layered and shaped rather than notated for players, and Ferrari stayed with it long after the founding excitement had passed, working in tape and electroacoustic media for the rest of his career. He died in 2005."},
  "daphneoram":{"wiki":"Daphne Oram","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Daphne%20Oram%20silhouette%202.svg?width=480","blurb":"Co-founded the Radiophonic Workshop; invented Oramics.","bio":"Daphne Oram, born in 1925, was among the first British composers to make electronic sound, and an early practitioner in Britain of composing with recorded material. Working at the BBC, she argued the case for a studio devoted to electronic music and in 1958 co-founded the Radiophonic Workshop — then left it the following year, a departure that shaped both her career and the Workshop's, where Delia Derbyshire would soon arrive. On her own she developed Oramics, a system for making sound by drawing it, which put the composer's hand directly on the shape of the sound instead of on a keyboard. Her uncredited score for the 1961 film The Innocents helped pioneer the electronic soundtrack. She was an engineer as much as a composer. She died in 2003."},
  "deliaderbyshire":{"wiki":"Delia Derbyshire","blurb":"Realised the Doctor Who theme on tape.","bio":"Delia Derbyshire, born in 1937, was an English composer of electronic music who spent the 1960s at the BBC Radiophonic Workshop, the studio Daphne Oram had co-founded in 1958 and left the year after. Her electronic arrangement of the theme to the science-fiction series Doctor Who — built up on tape, sound by sound — became one of the most widely heard pieces of electronic music in Britain, familiar to millions who never thought of it as experimental at all. She has been called the unsung heroine of British electronic music, and the description fits: the Workshop's output reached far more people than her name ever did. Aphex Twin, the Chemical Brothers and Orbital's Paul Hartnoll have all acknowledged her influence. She died in 2001."},
  "elianeradigue":{"wiki":"Éliane Radigue","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/%C3%89liane%20Radigue%20and%20Cat.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Drone as an act of patience.","bio":"Éliane Radigue, born in 1932, is a French composer who learned the craft of recorded sound in the Paris studios and then used it for something entirely her own. She served her apprenticeship with Pierre Schaeffer at the RTF studio and continued with Pierre Henry, working as his assistant at the Studio Apsome. She had begun working in the 1950s, but her own first compositions were presented only in the late 1960s, and from then until around 2000 they were made almost exclusively with the ARP 2500 modular synthesiser and tape: long, slow pieces in which change happens beneath the threshold of attention, so that listening becomes a matter of duration rather than event. From 2001 she has composed mainly for acoustic instruments, and the patience of the synthesiser years carried straight over into them."},
  "bebebarron19252008andlouisbarron":{"wiki":"Teo Macero","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Teo%20Macero.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The first all-electronic film score.","bio":"Bebe Barron, born in 1925, and Louis Barron, who lived from 1920 to 1989, worked together in New York, making music out of electronic sound when almost no one else in America was doing it. Their score for Forbidden Planet was the first written for a feature film entirely from electronic sound — no orchestra, no players, nothing but tape and the sounds they generated for it. That put the new medium in front of an audience of ordinary cinemagoers years before most of them would have heard anything of the kind in a concert hall, and it did so without announcing itself as experimental music. They belong to the postwar generation working with tape and electronics, but they reached their public through the cinema rather than through the studios and festivals where that music was usually heard."},
  "vladimirussachevsky":{"wiki":"Vladimir Ussachevsky","blurb":"Built the Columbia-Princeton studio.","bio":"Vladimir Ussachevsky, born in 1911 in the Russian Empire and later an American citizen, was a composer known above all for his electronic music and for the institution he created to make it possible. With Otto Luening he founded the Columbia-Princeton Electronic Music Center in New York, and made it a place where composers could actually work: the RCA Mark II synthesiser there was central to Milton Babbitt's music, and Halim El-Dabh joined from 1959. Ussachevsky's own pieces were made with tape and electronic means, and he wrote for film as well. His importance is inseparable from the studio: he supplied a generation of composers with the room, the machines and the argument for using them, which is a different kind of authorship. He died in 1990."},
  "johncage":{"wiki":"John Cage","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Opdracht%20GPD%20componist%20John%20Cage%20%2C%20kop%2C%20Bestanddeelnr%20934-2728.jpg?width=480","blurb":"4'33\", and everything it opened.","bio":"John Cage, born in 1912, is the most argued-over composer of the century, and the one whose questions outlasted everyone's answers. He studied with Henry Cowell, who sent him on to Schoenberg; Schoenberg taught him free in Los Angeles from 1935 on condition that he devote his life to music. Chance operations entered his work as a way of getting the composer's own taste out of the way of the sounds, and 4'33\" went further still, proposing that a piece could consist of whatever happened to be audible. His New School class between 1957 and 1959 produced Allan Kaprow's Happenings and much of Fluxus, and sent Toshi Ichiyanagi home to Tokyo with indeterminacy. Nam June Paik dated the end of his life as a composer to meeting him; La Monte Young, Takemitsu and Lutosławski each changed course on hearing him. He died in 1992.","disco":[["1948","In a Landscape","solo piano — a Cage that makes a sound"]]},
  "mortonfeldman":{"wiki":"Morton Feldman","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Persconferentie%20componist%20Morton%20Feldman%20in%20concertgebouw%20Amsterdam%20in%20verband%20m%2C%20Bestanddeelnr%20928-6142.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Very quiet, and very long.","bio":"Morton Feldman, born in New York in 1926, wrote music that asks for a kind of attention nothing else in the repertoire requires. He met John Cage in the lobby of Carnegie Hall in 1950, the two of them having walked out of the same concert, and became a central figure of the New York School alongside Cage, Christian Wolff and Earle Brown. His scores use notational inventions of his own to produce rhythms that float free of pulse, softly unfocused shadings of pitch, and quiet, slowly turning patterns that never quite repeat. After 1977 the pieces began to explore extremes of duration. He spent years in factory and dry-cleaning work before academia; at Buffalo he ran the Creative Associates and brought in Julius Eastman. He died in 1987."},
  "earlebrown":{"wiki":"Earle Brown","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/David%20Arden%20(left)%20with%20Earle%20Brown%20in%20recording%20session%20in%201995.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The inventor of open form.","bio":"Earle Brown, born in 1926, was an American composer, producer and educator, a close associate of John Cage in the New York School and a collaborator on the Project for Music for Magnetic Tape. His contribution was structural: he devised open form, in which the material is fixed but its order is not, so that the performers assemble the piece in real time and no two performances share a shape. Formal and notational systems of his own went with it, and December 1952 remains one of the emblematic scores of the century, a page that belongs as much to the history of notation as to the repertoire. The idea travelled: it fed John Zorn and the New York downtown scene of the 1980s, and generations of composers after them. He died in 2002."},
  "christianwolff":{"wiki":"Christian Wolff (composer)","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Christian%20Wolff%20prepared%20piano%20performance%202007%20Feb.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Gave Cage the I Ching.","bio":"Christian Wolff, born in 1934, is an American composer of experimental music and, in his other life, a classicist and philologist who has taught at university for decades. He was the youngest of the New York School around John Cage, and it was Wolff, aged sixteen, who gave Cage the copy of the I Ching from which the chance operations came — one of the more consequential gifts in twentieth-century music. His own music belongs to the experimental line that came out of that circle, where the older certainties about what a score fixes and what it leaves to the players were being taken apart, and where a piece could be less an object than a set of conditions. He has gone on composing and teaching from New Hampshire, the quietest and longest-lasting presence in that group."},
  "davidtudor":{"wiki":"David Tudor","blurb":"The pianist who made it all playable.","bio":"David Tudor, born in 1926, was the performer without whom much of the postwar repertoire would have stayed on paper. He gave the American premiere of Boulez's Second Piano Sonata in 1950 and went on to premiere the works of the New York School — Feldman, and above all Cage, who wrote for him constantly; it was Tudor who first performed 4'33\". Stockhausen dedicated a piece to him, a measure of how completely he was also part of the Darmstadt world. He then turned to composing in his own right, much of it in projects for the Merce Cunningham Dance Company, the ensemble he was tied to for the rest of his life. When Cage died in 1992, Tudor took over as its music director. He died in 1996."},
  "paulineoliveros":{"wiki":"Pauline Oliveros","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/POliveros2010.JPG?width=480","blurb":"Turned listening itself into a practice.","bio":"Pauline Oliveros, born in 1932, was an American composer and accordionist who spent a career redefining what a composer's material might be. In 1962 she co-founded the San Francisco Tape Music Center with Morton Subotnick and Ramon Sender, one of the crucial early studios of American electronic music, and she went on to teach and work in San Diego. Her lasting contribution is Deep Listening, at once a body of pieces and a discipline: an approach in which attention to sound — ambient, environmental, incidental — is the practice being taught, and performers and listeners are engaged in the same activity. She wrote theory alongside the music and taught widely. The accordion, an unglamorous instrument she never abandoned, ran through all of it. She died in 2016."},
  "alvinlucier":{"wiki":"Alvin Lucier","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Alvin%20Lucier%20(25536346088).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Composer of rooms and their resonances.","bio":"Alvin Lucier, born in 1931, was an American sound artist and experimental composer who made the physics of hearing his subject rather than his medium. He taught for decades at Wesleyan University in Middletown, Connecticut, the town that became his base, and in 1966 he joined Robert Ashley, David Behrman and Gordon Mumma in the Sonic Arts Union, an influential group of composers. His best-known piece, 'I Am Sitting in a Room', records a spoken text, plays it back into the room and re-records it over and over until the room's own resonances swallow the words; it is a demonstration and a piece of music at the same time. Much of his catalogue proceeds this way, framing psychoacoustic phenomena patiently enough that a listener can hear them happen. He died in 2021."},
  "robertashley":{"wiki":"Robert Ashley","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Composer%20Robert%20Ashley.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The composer of operas for television.","bio":"Robert Ashley, born in Michigan in 1930, was an American composer best known for his operas for television. His theatrical works, among them 'Automatic Writing' of 1979 and 'Perfect Lives' of 1983, set intertwining spoken narratives against electronics and extended technique, and they read as surreal Americana as much as music. From 1966 he was part of the Sonic Arts Union with Alvin Lucier, David Behrman and Gordon Mumma. He worked between Michigan and New York, taught, and produced film. His pieces have gone on being staged by new interpreters since his death in 2014, which is unusual for music this closely bound to its maker. Ashley's insistence that speech, storytelling and the screen belonged inside opera left a template later music theatre still borrows."},
  "meredithmonk":{"wiki":"Meredith Monk","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Meredith%20Monk%20Brooklyn%20Academy%20of%20Music%20(15326312734)%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"She made the voice an instrument again.","bio":"Meredith Monk, born in 1942 and based in New York, is an American composer, singer, director, filmmaker and choreographer whose instrument is the human voice pushed past its usual manners. Since the 1960s she has built multi-disciplinary works that fuse music, theatre and dance rather than laying one over another, recording extensively for ECM. In 1991 came 'Atlas', an opera jointly commissioned and produced by the American Music Theater Festival and the Houston Grand Opera. Her music has travelled far beyond the concert hall: the Coen brothers and Jean-Luc Godard have used it in films, and the trip hop musician DJ Shadow sampled her 'Dolmen Music' on 'Midnight in a Perfect World'. She received the National Medal of Arts in 2014. Working largely outside the usual score-and-premiere economy, Monk turned extended vocal technique into a language other singers and composers could learn."},
  "corneliuscardew":{"wiki":"Cornelius Cardew","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Cornelius%20Cardew%20at%20Grunwick%20march.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Wrote Treatise, then renounced the avant-garde.","bio":"Cornelius Cardew, 1936-1981, was an English composer, theorist and improviser whose career is a record of loyalties taken up and then thrown over. For three years he worked as Karlheinz Stockhausen's assistant on 'Carré', absorbing the methods of the European avant-garde from the inside. His own best-known score, 'Treatise', and the Scratch Orchestra, the experimental performing ensemble he founded in London, both belong to a practice that handed decisions outward, away from the composer and towards the people playing. Then politics overtook aesthetics. In 1974 he published 'Stockhausen Serves Imperialism', the book that broke with his former employer and with the entire avant-garde, and in his own programme notes to the 'Piano Album 1973' he set out why he had given up writing in an avant-garde idiom. A committed political activist and university teacher, he died in 1981, his repudiation as influential among younger composers as his scores."},
  "mercecunningham":{"wiki":"Merce Cunningham","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Merce%20Cunningham%20April%2016%2C%201919-%20July%2026%2C%202009.png?width=480","blurb":"Cage's partner; dance freed from its music.","bio":"Merce Cunningham, 1919-2009, was an American dancer and choreographer, not a composer, and yet inseparable from the music of his century. For more than fifty years he stood at the front of American modern dance, and for those same fifty years he was John Cage's partner in life and in work. Their arrangement was the radical part: Cage wrote the music and Cunningham made the choreography separately, the two meeting for the first time in front of an audience, so that sound and movement shared a duration without illustrating one another. He also collaborated with David Tudor and Brian Eno, with the artists Robert Rauschenberg, Jasper Johns, Andy Warhol, Roy Lichtenstein, Bruce Nauman and Frank Stella, and with the fashion designer Rei Kawakubo. The results reached well past dance, and set a working model for collaboration between the arts."},
  "lamonteyoung":{"wiki":"La Monte Young","blurb":"An early minimalist, holding a single tone.","bio":"La Monte Young, born in 1935 and long resident in New York, is an American composer and performance artist counted among the first of the American minimalists. His 'Trio for Strings' of 1958 held tones for lengths nobody had asked of a string trio before, and the text scores of 'Compositions 1960' asked whether the results were music at all. Hearing Cage's music at Darmstadt in 1959 redirected him entirely. With Marian Zazeela, his partner in forty years of joint light-and-sound work, he built the Dream House; with Terry Riley, whom he had known at Berkeley, he ran the Theatre of Eternal Music, the sustained-tone ensemble in whose orbit 'In C' was written. From 1970 he, Zazeela and Riley all became disciples of Pandit Pran Nath. Few of Young's recordings stay in print, and his influence runs through avant-garde, rock and ambient music anyway."},
  "terryriley":{"wiki":"Terry Riley","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Terry%20Riley%2C%20Tokyo%208%20Nov%202017%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Wrote In C and set minimalism going.","bio":"Terry Riley, born in California in 1935, is an American composer and pianist, and one of the founders of minimalism. His 'In C' of 1964, built from short repeating figures that an ensemble of any size moves through at its own speed, was written in the orbit of La Monte Young, whom he had known at Berkeley and alongside whom he played in the Theatre of Eternal Music. Steve Reich took part in the premiere and supplied the steady pulse that holds the piece together. Riley's music draws on jazz and on Indian classical music, which he studied with Pandit Pran Nath on annual trips to India across twenty-six years, and on tape-delay systems and improvisation, the approach behind his 1969 album 'A Rainbow in Curved Air'. Both works became landmarks, and both shaped rock, experimental music and contemporary electronic music well outside the concert hall."},
  "stevereich":{"wiki":"Steve Reich","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Steve%20Reich%2C%20composer%2C%20cropped.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Music as an audible, gradual process.","bio":"Steve Reich, born in New York in 1936, is an American composer and a pioneer of minimal music, whose real subject is process you can hear. He studied at Mills College from 1961 to 1963 with Luciano Berio and Darius Milhaud; Berio's advice, that if he wanted to write tonal music he should write tonal music, gave him permission for the music he actually wanted. In 1964 he played in the premiere of Terry Riley's 'In C', supplying the steady pulse that holds it together. His own early pieces work by phase shifting: identical repeated figures drift out of step with one another, and the new patterns that emerge belong to the process rather than to a decision. He wanted that process audible all the way through. 'Music for 18 Musicians' carried the method into a large ensemble, and repetition, slow harmony and canon have anchored his work ever since."},
  "philipglass":{"wiki":"Philip Glass","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Philip%20Glass%20018%20crop.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Repetition turned into epic theatre.","bio":"Philip Glass, born in New York in 1937, is an American composer and pianist, and one of the most influential figures of the late twentieth century. He studied with Darius Milhaud at Aspen in 1960 and then in Paris with Nadia Boulanger from 1964 to 1966, on a brutal regime of species counterpoint. In the same Paris years he transcribed Ravi Shankar's score for the film 'Chappaqua', a job that forced him to rethink how rhythm works from the ground up. What came out was music built from repetitive phrases and slowly shifting layers, which he preferred to describe as music with repetitive structures rather than as minimalism. In 1976 he and Robert Wilson made 'Einstein on the Beach', an opera on a scale and a logic nobody had attempted, and their collaboration ran on for four decades after it. He has kept evolving the style since."},
  "juliuseastman":{"wiki":"Julius Eastman","blurb":"Minimalism's outsider, lost then reclaimed.","bio":"Julius Eastman, 1940-1990, was an American composer, singer, dancer and professor who worked between Buffalo and New York. Morton Feldman ran the Creative Associates in Buffalo and brought him in, and it was there that Eastman arrived at what he called organic music: minimalist process crossed with his own methods of extending and modifying material as a piece went along. 'Stay On It', from 1973, carries melodic hooks as catchy as the pop of its moment. Other works he gave deliberately confrontational titles, 'Gay Guerrilla' among them, alongside pieces whose titles turned racial slurs back on his audience; he refused to leave his Blackness or his sexuality outside the concert hall. Eastman died homeless in 1990. Rediscovered a decade and more later, he is now performed, recorded and argued over far more than he ever was in his lifetime."},
  "johnadams":{"wiki":"John Adams (composer)","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/JA-portrait-1-LW.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Made recent history into opera.","bio":"John Adams, born in California in 1947, is an American composer and conductor, and among the most regularly performed figures in contemporary classical music. He studied at Harvard with Leon Kirchner. His reputation rests above all on operas built around historical events, 'Nixon in China' first among them, written while the events were still in living memory and making documented politics a legitimate operatic subject. Around the operas sits a large catalogue of orchestral, concertante, choral, vocal, chamber, electroacoustic and piano music. The patron Betty Freeman commissioned 'Grand Pianola Music' from him, one of some forty works she paid for outright, part of a private economy of commissions that kept a generation of American composers writing. Adams took the pulse and repetition he inherited from minimalism and turned them towards narrative, the orchestra and the opera house, which is why his music reaches audiences the movement's founders did not."},
  "louisandriessen":{"wiki":"Louis Andriessen","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Louis%20Andriessen%2C%20Bestanddeelnr%20932-5716.jpg?width=480","blurb":"A harder, more European minimalism.","bio":"Louis Andriessen, 1939-2021, was a Dutch composer, pianist and academic, generally reckoned his generation's most influential Dutch composer and the central proponent of The Hague school. He studied with Luciano Berio in Milan and Berlin between 1962 and 1965, and his early music ran through neoclassicism and serialism before he found the manner he is known for, which fused American minimalism with big band jazz and with Igor Stravinsky's expressionism. 'De Staat' is the emblem of it, a harder and more argumentative minimalism than the American kind, made in a country with its own performing culture of amplified ensembles and committed players. He taught for decades and shaped a generation of younger composers, in the Netherlands and beyond. Andriessen is the reason minimalism stopped being a purely American story and became a language others could bend to their own purposes."},
  "michaelnyman":{"wiki":"Michael Nyman","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Michael%20Nyman%20Sant%20Cugat.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The critic who named minimal music.","bio":"Michael Nyman, born in London in 1944, is an English composer, pianist, musicologist, librettist and filmmaker who named the thing he later helped make. Writing as a critic, he coined the term minimal music, and the label stuck to a whole generation. His own catalogue is large and varied: six concerti, five string quartets and a great deal of chamber music, much of it for his own Michael Nyman Band, alongside the film scores that made him famous. The multi-platinum soundtrack to Jane Campion's 'The Piano' put him on record shelves worldwide. He says he would rather write opera than anything else, and has done so repeatedly, from 'The Man Who Mistook His Wife for a Hat' and 'Letters, Riddles and Writs' to 'Man and Boy: Dada', 'Facing Goya', 'Love Counts' and 'Sparkie: Cage and Beyond'. He was appointed CBE."},
  "tomjohnson":{"wiki":"Tom Johnson (composer)","blurb":"Composer-critic who covered minimalism from inside.","bio":"Tom Johnson, 1939-2024, was an American composer and music critic whose working life ran between New York and Paris. He grew up in Colorado in a religious household, studied at Yale with Allen Forte, and then continued in New York with Morton Feldman. Writing for The Village Voice, he covered the composers working around him and brought several of them to a wider readership than the concert hall could reach on its own, which makes him one of the few figures in this story who both made the music and wrote its first draft of history. His own work is associated with minimalism, and he held French nationality alongside his American one, having settled in Paris. Critic and composer at once, he documented a scene from the inside while adding to it, and the two roles turned out to reinforce rather than compromise each other."},
  "witoldlutosawski":{"wiki":"Witold Lutosławski","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Lutoslawski2.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Turned chance into disciplined counterpoint.","bio":"Witold Lutosławski, 1913-1994, was a Polish composer and conductor, widely held to be the most important Polish composer since Szymanowski, who was in turn the only recent Polish model his generation had to work from. He wrote in most traditional genres except opera: four symphonies, concertos, orchestral song cycles and chamber music, including the Variations on a Theme by Paganini of 1941 and the Concerto for Orchestra of 1954. In 1960 he heard Cage's Concert for Piano and Orchestra on the radio, and within weeks had devised limited aleatory counterpoint, in which players are free in their timing while the composer keeps hold of the harmony. Mstislav Rostropovich drew the Cello Concerto out of him in 1970, and Paul Sacher commissioned the Basel companion pieces to the Third Symphony. With Krzysztof Penderecki he shared the Warsaw Autumn generation and a wary mutual regard."},
  "krzysztofpenderecki":{"wiki":"Krzysztof Penderecki","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Krzysztof%20Penderecki%2020080706.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Sonorism's loudest voice, and its liturgist.","bio":"Krzysztof Penderecki, 1933-2020, was a Polish composer and conductor based in Kraków, and the most notorious new voice of the Polish school. 'Threnody for the Victims of Hiroshima' made his name with orchestral sounds that had no real precedent, deployed as an act of public mourning, and 'Anaklasis' worked the same seam. Alongside them stands a body of sacred music on a grand scale: the St Luke Passion, 'Utrenja' and the Polish Requiem, choral settings of mainly religious texts that he went on producing across a career comprising five operas, eight symphonies, instrumental concertos, chamber and instrumental works. He belonged to the Warsaw Autumn generation with Witold Lutosławski, the two holding each other in wary regard, and with Henryk Górecki, a Polish contemporary who moved in the opposite direction after 1970. Penderecki showed a generation that extreme sound could carry historical grief."},
  "henrykgorecki":{"wiki":"Henryk Górecki","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Henryk%20Miko%C5%82aj%20G%C3%B3recki%20Polish%20composer.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The avant-gardist with a million-selling symphony.","bio":"Henryk Górecki, 1933-2010, was a Polish composer from Katowice who became, improbably, a bestseller. He emerged as one of the leaders of the Polish avant-garde during the cultural thaw after Stalin, writing dissonant, Webern-haunted serial music through the 1950s and 1960s under the influence of Karlheinz Stockhausen, Luigi Nono, Kazimierz Serocki and Krzysztof Penderecki. By the mid-1970s he had turned away from that complexity towards a less complex sacred minimalism, a change audible in the transitional Second Symphony, the 'Copernican', and complete in the Third, the 'Symphony of Sorrowful Songs'. The later style went through further phases in 'Beatus Vir' of 1979, the choral 'Miserere' of 1981 and the 'Kleines Requiem' of 1993. Then in 1992 a recording of the Third Symphony sold over a million copies. He and Penderecki, contemporaries, moved in opposite directions after 1970."},
  "kazimierzserocki":{"wiki":"Kazimierz Serocki","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kazimierz%20Serocki%20w%20latach%2060.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Co-founder of the Warsaw Autumn festival.","bio":"Kazimierz Serocki, 1922-1981, was a Polish composer and pianist who worked in Warsaw, and his largest single act was organisational rather than compositional. In 1956, with Tadeusz Baird, he founded the Warsaw Autumn, the contemporary music festival that gave a whole Polish generation its meeting place and, eventually, its name: composers of the period are still described as the Warsaw Autumn generation. The festival opened a channel between Polish composers and the new music being written elsewhere at a moment when such a channel was rare. Serocki was a pianist as well as a composer, and his own music belongs to the sonorist current of the Polish school, preoccupied with texture and instrumental colour rather than with theme and development. He kept composing until his death in 1981, but the festival is the part of his work the rest of the century inherited."},
  "grazynabacewicz":{"wiki":"Grażyna Bacewicz","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Grazyna%20bacewicz.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Poland's leading woman composer, and a violinist.","bio":"Grażyna Bacewicz, 1909-1969, was a Polish composer and violinist, Lithuanian by origin, who worked in Warsaw, and the second Polish woman composer to win recognition at home and abroad; the first, Maria Szymanowska, had done it more than a century earlier. She studied in Paris with Nadia Boulanger on a Paderewski scholarship, joining the long line of composers Boulanger equipped with a technique, and she remained a performing violinist, which tells in the string writing that carries much of her catalogue. She was a musicologist and music educator as well. Her working life spanned the war years and the decades after, and she belongs squarely inside the postwar Polish school rather than at its margins. Bacewicz is now heard as the leading Polish woman composer of the century, and increasingly simply as one of its leading Polish composers."},
  "tadeuszbaird":{"wiki":"Tadeusz Baird","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Tadeusz%20Baird.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Warsaw Autumn's other founder.","bio":"Tadeusz Baird, 1928-1981, was a Polish composer who spent his career in Warsaw, and his name survives above all for an act of organisation. In 1956, together with Kazimierz Serocki, he founded the Warsaw Autumn, the festival that became the fixed point of Polish new music and gave its generation a label. Baird also taught, as a music educator and university teacher passing the postwar Polish idiom on to younger composers, and he wrote for film alongside his concert work. He died in 1981; the Warsaw Autumn outlived both him and Serocki, and it is that institution rather than any single score of his that carries his name now. Founder, teacher and composer, Baird built the room the music happened in."},
  "gerardgrisey":{"wiki":"Gérard Grisey","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gerardgrisey.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Composed from inside the harmonic spectrum.","bio":"Gérard Grisey, 1946-1998, was a French composer and a major pioneer of spectral music. He trained at the Paris Conservatoire with Olivier Messiaen and worked with Iannis Xenakis at Darmstadt and in Paris, then took a step neither had taken: he built his harmony out of the acoustic spectrum itself, treating the overtones inside a single sounded note as material for a whole orchestra, and letting music unfold on the timescale of sound rather than of phrases. His cycle 'Les Espaces acoustiques' is the fullest demonstration of the method. In 1973, with Tristan Murail, he founded the ensemble L'Itinéraire in Paris to get such music played, while Hugues Dufourt supplied the name the movement has carried since. Grisey died in 1998, and spectral thinking has spread since through composers who never studied with him, which is the mark of a genuinely new idea about sound."},
  "tristanmurail":{"wiki":"Tristan Murail","blurb":"Co-founder of spectralism; composer of Gondwana.","bio":"Tristan Murail, born in 1947, is a French composer and music pedagogue who has divided his working life between Paris and New York, and one of the founders of the spectral technique. He studied with Olivier Messiaen at the Paris Conservatoire, and in 1973 he and Gérard Grisey founded L'Itinéraire in Paris, an ensemble built to perform music no existing group was equipped for: scores whose harmony is derived from the acoustic spectrum of a sound rather than from the tempered scale. His large orchestral work 'Gondwana' is the piece most often named as the demonstration of what that means in practice, harmony heard from inside a sounding note. As a teacher he did much to turn spectral thinking into technique that could be handed on, which is how a French experiment of the 1970s became a working method used by composers internationally."},
  "huguesdufourt":{"wiki":"Hugues Dufourt","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dufourt.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The philosopher who named spectral music.","bio":"Hugues Dufourt, born in Lyon in 1943, is a French composer, conductor, musicologist and philosopher who studied composition and piano at the Geneva Conservatory and has long worked in Paris. He gave the spectral school its name, and the fact that a philosopher named it is not incidental: alongside composing he has written extensively about music, and the term he coined framed how a generation understood what it was doing with sound. Grisey and Murail founded the ensemble L'Itinéraire to get such music performed; Dufourt supplied the word that let it be argued about, taught and grouped. His own catalogue belongs to the same current, concerned with the interior of sound rather than with themes and their development. He is the rare case of a movement's namer and theorist who was also, in his own right, one of its composers."},
  "kaijasaariaho":{"wiki":"Kaija Saariaho","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kaija%20Saariaho%20in%202022.png?width=480","blurb":"Finnish spectralist who composed from inside sound.","bio":"Kaija Saariaho, 1952-2023, was a Finnish composer who lived and worked in Paris, and by the end of her life the most admired living composer in her field: a 2019 poll of composers by BBC Music Magazine ranked her first. She studied at the Sibelius Academy in Helsinki with Paavo Heininen and then with Brian Ferneyhough in Freiburg in 1981 and 1982, and in 1977, with Magnus Lindberg, she founded Korvat auki, Ears Open, the Helsinki group through which a Finnish generation argued its way into modernism. Her music belongs to the spectral current, built from the inside of sounds rather than from themes. The commissions accumulated: the Kronos Quartet by way of Lincoln Center, the Ensemble Intercontemporain by way of IRCAM, and then the New York Philharmonic, the BBC, the Finnish National Opera, the Théâtre du Châtelet and the Salzburg Music Festival."},
  "michaellevinas":{"wiki":"Michaël Lévinas","blurb":"A pianist-composer among the Paris spectralists.","bio":"Michaël Lévinas, born in 1949, is a French composer and pianist who works in Paris and teaches at university level. He belongs to the generation of French composers grouped as the spectral school, the Paris circle that built its harmony out of the acoustic properties of sound itself rather than out of the tempered scale, and in doing so changed what a French composer could take as raw material. Lévinas has kept a double practice throughout, performing as a pianist alongside his composing, which is rarer among his contemporaries than it sounds and keeps his music tied to the physical business of playing. A composer who is also a working performer, he has stayed inside the performing life his music comes from, and he counts among the enduring figures of the Paris spectral generation."},
  "jonathanharvey":{"wiki":"Jonathan Harvey (composer)","blurb":"English spectralist of the IRCAM years.","bio":"Jonathan Harvey, 1939-2012, was a British composer and cellist who held teaching posts at universities and music conservatories across Europe and the United States. On a Harkness fellowship he went to Princeton in 1969 and 1970 to study with Milton Babbitt, an odd waypoint for a composer whose lasting reputation rests on electronics and the inner life of sound. 'Mortuos Plango, Vivos Voco', made at IRCAM, is the piece that fixed his name, computer music in which the interior of a sonority becomes the structure of the whole. Harvey spent most of his life in England while working at the centre of European electronic music, one of the few British composers of his generation equally at home in the concert hall and the studio."},
  "brianferneyhough":{"wiki":"Brian Ferneyhough","blurb":"New Complexity's central figure and teacher.","bio":"Brian Ferneyhough, born in 1943, is an English composer, and the figure usually placed at the centre of the New Complexity: music notated in such extreme detail that the effort of performance becomes part of what a listener hears. He has taught almost continuously, at the Hochschule für Musik Freiburg, at the University of California, San Diego, and at Stanford, where he teaches now, and he has been a regular lecturer at the Darmstadt summer courses, the clearing house through which postwar European ideas passed. Among his Freiburg pupils in 1981 and 1982 was Kaija Saariaho, who took the rigour and went somewhere quite different with it. He has lived in California since 1987. Ferneyhough's importance is double: a catalogue of formidably difficult scores, and several generations of composers who learned from him how to think about notation itself."},
  "michaelfinnissy":{"wiki":"Michael Finnissy","blurb":"Prolific pianist-composer of the New Complexity.","bio":"Michael Finnissy, born in London in 1946, is an English composer, pianist and pedagogue, and one of the composers grouped with the New Complexity. He is immensely prolific, and a pianist who performs his own music, which has been described as remarkable for its dramatic urgency and its immediacy of expression: a characterisation that separates him from the caricature of complexity as dry calculation. A performer as well as a writer of scores, he knows the piano from the inside. As a teacher he has taken generations of composers through their training. His work sits inside the loose British current that pushed notation towards its limits and trusted performers to reach for what could not quite be played cleanly, and it belongs there without being wholly explained by it. The urgency, not the difficulty, is what he seems to have been after."},
  "jamesdillon":{"wiki":"James Dillon (composer)","blurb":"Self-taught Scot of the New Complexity.","bio":"James Dillon, born in Scotland in 1950, is a Scottish composer usually counted a member of the New Complexity, and the unlikeliest one: in composition he is self-taught. What he did study was everything around it, art and design, linguistics, acoustics, piano, mathematics, Indian rhythm and computer music, and that spread tells in music built from densely organised surfaces that come out of thinking about sound rather than out of a conservatory syllabus. Working outside the teacher-pupil chains that structure most of this map, Dillon arrived somewhere close to his British contemporaries by an entirely different road, which is a useful reminder that lineage is not the only way musical ideas travel. Autodidact, polymath and meticulous notator, he has become one of the distinctive British voices of the period."},
  "chrisdench":{"blurb":"London complexity, transplanted to Melbourne.","bio":"Chris Dench, born in 1953, is a composer who came out of London and has spent much of his working life in Melbourne. He is grouped with the New Complexity, the current of the decades after 1945 whose scores are notated in extreme detail, and he represents its expatriate wing."},
  "richardbarrett":{"wiki":"Richard Barrett (composer)","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Richard%20Barrett%20in%20June%202015.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Welsh voice of the New Complexity.","bio":"Richard Barrett, born in 1959, is a Welsh composer who has divided his career between Britain and Berlin, and who teaches at university level. He is placed among the composers of the New Complexity, the British current whose scores are notated in extraordinary detail and which treats the difficulty of performance as part of the music's substance rather than as an obstacle to it. His working life has been made as much on the European continent as at home, divided between Britain and Berlin, which places him in the long line of British composers who found an audience abroad. Composer and university teacher at once, Barrett has carried a British way of writing into a continental scene with its own reasons for wanting it."},
  "arvopart":{"wiki":"Arvo Pärt","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Arvo%20P%C3%A4rt.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Invented tintinnabuli; the world's most-performed living composer.","bio":"Arvo Pärt, born in 1935, is an Estonian composer who trained at the Tallinn Conservatory with Heino Eller and later worked for many years in Berlin. From 1976 he has written in the style built on tintinnabuli, a technique he invented himself, in which a melodic line is shadowed by the notes of a single triad so that harmony rings rather than progresses. Gregorian chant stands behind the stillness of the result. 'Für Alina' of 1976, 'Fratres' of 1977 and 'Spiegel im Spiegel' of 1978 are the pieces through which most listeners meet him. He was the world's most performed living composer between 2011 and 2018, and once more in 2022 and in 2025, a standing without recent precedent for a composer of sacred music. The Arvo Pärt Centre at Laulasmaa opened to the public in 2018."},
  "johntavener":{"wiki":"John Tavener","blurb":"Sacred minimalism, launched by Apple Records.","bio":"Sir John Tavener, 1944-2013, was an English composer whose central work was choral and religious, and who lived and worked in London. He studied with Lennox Berkeley at the Royal Academy of Music, and his career began through an accident of proximity: his brother Roger was renovating Ringo Starr's house, made the introduction, and Apple Records released 'The Whale' in 1970, the Beatles' label putting out a young composer's concert work. What followed was a long turn towards religious devotion and a music of great simplicity, in 'The Lamb' of 1982, 'The Protecting Veil' of 1988 and 'Song for Athene' of 1993. He also wrote poetry and conducted. While she was still at school he taught Judith Weir privately, sending a composer of a very different stamp on her way. He was knighted for his work, and died in 2013."},
  "sofiagubaidulina":{"wiki":"Sofia Gubaidulina","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sofia%20Gubaidulina%20(2016-11-03).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Told to continue on her incorrect path.","bio":"Sofia Gubaidulina, 1931-2025, was a composer of modernist sacred music, Soviet and then Russian, who worked in Moscow and later in Hamburg. At an examination early on, Dmitri Shostakovich told her to continue on her own incorrect path, a sentence she quoted for the rest of her life and effectively her licence to write religious, mystical music in a state that had no use for it. In Moscow she formed, with Alfred Schnittke and Edison Denisov, the troika of the unofficial avant-garde, composers whose work circulated outside official approval. She was highly prolific, producing chamber, orchestral and choral music as well as film scores, and her language turns on microtonality and chromaticism used inventively, on rhythm taking priority over inherited form, and on the tension between Western and Eastern music that she explored rather than resolved."},
  "giyakancheli":{"wiki":"Giya Kancheli","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gia%20Kancheli.jpg?width=480","blurb":"A Georgian voice in the new spirituality.","bio":"Giya Kancheli, 1935-2019, was a Georgian composer, born in Tbilisi and resident in later life in Belgium, at Antwerp. He wrote for the concert hall, composed extensively for film, and taught. His music is grouped with the holy minimalism and new spirituality of the late Soviet decades, a current that answered official modernism with slowness, quiet and religious feeling rather than with further complexity. Kancheli came to it from Tbilisi rather than from any imperial centre, and the distance tells: a composer of the Soviet generation who worked at the periphery of its musical politics, then left, kept writing, and became one of the best-known figures his country's music has produced. His later Belgian decades kept him in contact with Western European performers while his idiom stayed recognisably his own."},
  "alfredschnittke":{"wiki":"Alfred Schnittke","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/%D0%A1%20%D0%90%D0%BB%D1%8C%D1%84%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%BE%D0%BC%20%D0%A8%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%BA%D0%B5%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Polystylism: every style at once.","bio":"Alfred Schnittke, 1934-1998, was a Soviet and Russian composer, one of the most performed and recorded figures of late twentieth-century classical music. He worked in Moscow and, in his final years, in Hamburg. His method was polystylism: incompatible idioms set against one another inside a single work, so that the piece becomes an argument between the musics available to it. With Sofia Gubaidulina and Edison Denisov he made up the Moscow troika of the unofficial avant-garde, working around rather than within the Soviet musical establishment; he taught and performed as a pianist besides. Mstislav Rostropovich drew the First Cello Concerto out of him. The musicologist Ivan Moody saw in him a composer set on depicting, in depth and in detail, the moral and spiritual struggles of the contemporary man, which is as good an account of the polystylistic clatter as any."},
  "georgewalker":{"wiki":"George Walker (composer)","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/George%20Theophilus%20Walker.jpg?width=480","blurb":"First Black composer to win the Pulitzer.","bio":"George Walker, 1922-2018, was an American pianist, organist and composer who worked in Washington and later in New Jersey. He was the first Black graduate of the Curtis Institute, where he studied with Rosario Scalero, and in 1947 he went to Fontainebleau to work with Nadia Boulanger. In 1996 he became the first African American composer to be awarded the Pulitzer Prize for Music, for 'Lilacs', an award that arrived half a century into a career the concert establishment had been slow to notice. He was a formidable pianist as well, and a music educator for most of his working life. The firsts are the facts people cite, and they were real barriers genuinely broken, but the catalogue behind them is what earned them, and Walker's long presence in American conservatories mattered to composers who came after him."},
  "ulysseskay":{"wiki":"Ulysses Kay","blurb":"King Oliver's nephew, Hindemith's neoclassical pupil.","bio":"Ulysses Kay, 1917-1995, was an American composer who spent his career in New York, and one of the more substantial neoclassicists of his generation. Music ran in the family: he was a nephew of the jazz cornetist King Oliver, though his own path led away from jazz and into the conservatory. He studied with Otto Luening at the Eastman School of Music and then with Paul Hindemith at Yale, whose craftsman's neoclassicism became the ground of his own, and his music stayed mostly neoclassical in style. William Grant Still encouraged him from his student years onward and pressed his music on conductors, an act of advocacy passed from the older generation of Black American composers to the younger that helped get Kay heard at all. His career is a case study in what mentorship inside a marginalised tradition could accomplish."},
  "ollywilson":{"wiki":"Olly Wilson","blurb":"Electronic studios and West African fieldwork.","bio":"Olly Wilson, 1937-2018, was an American composer, pianist, double bassist and musicologist who taught at Berkeley, and one of the preeminent Black composers of the twentieth and twenty-first centuries. He held two practices in a single career: he established the TIMARA programme at Oberlin Conservatory, the first electronic music programme at any conservatory, and he did ethnomusicological fieldwork on West African music, letting each inform the other. Out of that came his heterogeneous sound ideals, a set of criteria still widely used to analyse music, and his formulation that the essence of Africanness lies in a way of doing something rather than in the thing done. With T. J. Anderson he belonged to the network of Black composers around the Symposium of Black Composers and the Black Music Repertory Ensemble, built to get this repertory programmed. He was a jazz musician too."},
  "coleridgetaylorperkinson":{"wiki":"Coleridge-Taylor Perkinson","blurb":"Between the concert hall and Max Roach's band.","bio":"Coleridge-Taylor Perkinson, 1932-2004, was an American composer, conductor and pianist whose parents named him after a composer, and who then spent a career declining to choose a single music. Working in New York, his interests spanned jazz, dance, pop, film, television and the concert tradition. He wrote and arranged for Max Roach's groups while running a concert career in parallel, treating neither as a sideline, and he conducted, played piano and scored for the screen. Professionally he was often known simply as Coleridge Perkinson. The division most of his contemporaries accepted, between composed and improvised music, between the hall and the studio, he simply refused; the range is the point, and it made him one of the most useful musicians of his generation to have in the room."},
  "tjanderson":{"wiki":"T. J. Anderson","blurb":"Brought Joplin's Treemonisha to the stage.","bio":"T. J. Anderson, born in 1928, is an American composer, conductor and orchestrator, and an educator, based in Boston. His best-known piece of work was done on someone else's music: in 1972 he orchestrated Scott Joplin's opera 'Treemonisha' from the surviving vocal score for its first full staging, sixty years after Joplin wrote it. That performance turned Joplin from a ragtime name into an opera composer, and it is a reminder that influence can run backwards as well as forwards, a living composer putting an ancestor on stage. With Olly Wilson, Anderson was part of the network of Black composers around the Symposium of Black Composers and the Black Music Repertory Ensemble, the bodies that pushed this repertory into concert programmes. He has conducted and taught widely, and much of his importance lies in the music he made playable for others."},
  "halesmith":{"wiki":"Hale Smith","blurb":"Composer and arranger across jazz and concert music.","bio":"Hale Smith, 1925-2009, was an American composer, pianist and arranger who worked in New York. He moved between the concert tradition and jazz with unusual ease: a jazz pianist and music arranger by practice as well as a composer of concert works, he wrote and arranged for others while building his own catalogue, and he was a musicologist and music pedagogue besides. That double competence, a modernist's ear and a working arranger's hands, placed him among the Black composers of the postwar concert tradition who refused the choice between the two musics. He taught for many years, and the arranging work kept him in the company of musicians on both sides of a divide he never accepted as real. Smith's career is easiest to see whole when the jazz and concert halves are read together rather than separately."},
  "tanialeon":{"wiki":"Tania León","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Tania%20Leon.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Havana to Harlem, with a baton.","bio":"Tania León, born in Havana in 1943 and long resident in New York, is a Cuban-born American composer whose catalogue runs from chamber pieces to large-scale works, and a conductor of standing. Her career turned on an institution: she became the founding music director of the Dance Theatre of Harlem, working with Arthur Mitchell, and composed the ballets of its early years, building the musical side of a company from scratch. She has conducted widely since, taught at university level, and advised arts organisations, a form of influence that never appears in a work list but decides which music gets commissioned and played. León's own scores carry a rhythmic inheritance from Havana into American concert halls, and her institutional work has opened doors for the composers who came after her."},
  "undinesmithmoore":{"wiki":"Undine Smith Moore","blurb":"The Dean of Black Women Composers.","bio":"Undine Smith Moore, 1904-1989, was an American composer and a professor of music who spent her career in Virginia, and who was known as the Dean of Black Women Composers. Originally trained as a classical pianist, she developed an output that was mostly vocal music, her preferred genre, and drew much of its material from Black spirituals and folk music, so that a body of concert music grew directly out of a communal tradition. Teaching, though, was the centre of the life: she once said she experienced teaching itself as an art, and generations of students passed through her classes. The awards came late and largely for her work as a music educator. Her nickname records what colleagues understood about her, that she made a place for Black women in American concert music by occupying it and then handing it on."},
  "dorothyruddmoore":{"wiki":"Dorothy Rudd Moore","blurb":"Co-founder of the Society of Black Composers.","bio":"Dorothy Rudd Moore, 1940-2022, was an American composer, singer, pianist and music educator who worked in New York, and a co-founder of the Society of Black Composers, an organisation that existed because the ordinary channels of American concert music were closed to its members. Commissions came from Opera Ebony, the National Symphony, the Buffalo Philharmonic and individual soloists, and she is reckoned among the leading women composers of colour of her generation. She belonged to the American Composers Alliance, BMI, New York Women Composers and the New York Singing Teachers Association, the kind of membership list that shows a composer assembling her own infrastructure. Her works went unpublished and remain available through the American Composers Alliance, which is its own comment on whose music the industry chose to print."},
  "wendelllogan":{"wiki":"Wendell Logan","blurb":"Founder of Oberlin's jazz department.","bio":"Wendell Morris Logan, born in 1940, was an American composer who worked with equal conviction in jazz and in the concert tradition. A trumpeter and saxophonist as well as a composer, and a musicologist besides, he belonged to the generation of Black American composers who refused to accept that the two musics should be kept in separate rooms. His most consequential act was institutional: at the Oberlin Conservatory of Music he created the jazz department from nothing, giving jazz the same conservatory standing that Western art music had long enjoyed there and, in doing so, shaping the training of a generation of American musicians. Oberlin is the place his name stays attached to, and the department outlived him as the clearest form his argument could take. Logan died in 2010."},
  "torutakemitsu":{"wiki":"Tōru Takemitsu","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Toru%20Takemitsu%20Shinchosha%201961-7.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Where Debussy, Cage and gagaku meet.","bio":"Tōru Takemitsu, born in Tokyo in 1930, was a Japanese composer, largely self-taught, admired above all for the subtlety with which he handled instrumental and orchestral timbre. His music is often placed at the meeting point of Debussy, Cage and gagaku, and the Cage strand has a documented origin: Toshi Ichiyanagi carried Cage's scores back to Tokyo in 1961, which is how Takemitsu first encountered them, and Cage's own visit to Tokyo the following year altered how Takemitsu understood his inheritance. He credited that encounter with letting him stop being ashamed of Japanese tradition, and the instruments and silences of that tradition entered his work openly thereafter. Alongside the concert music he wrote extensively for film, and he wrote about aesthetics with the same care he gave to sound. He died in 1996."},
  "toshiromayuzumi":{"wiki":"Toshiro Mayuzumi","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mayuzumi%20toshirou.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Japan's first composer of electronic music.","bio":"Toshiro Mayuzumi, born in Tokyo in 1929, was a Japanese composer who studied at the Paris Conservatoire and returned home to become the first musician in his country to work in musique concrète and electronic music. He was a conductor, choreographer and musicologist as well, and his curiosity ranged unusually wide: jazz and Balinese music both left marks on his scores, alongside the traditional techniques of his own country. The Nirvana Symphony is the work in which those interests converge most famously, setting avant-garde instrumentation beside traditional Japanese technique rather than choosing between them. That combination — European method used to amplify what was already at hand rather than replace it — became a template that many Japanese composers of the postwar decades worked from, whether or not they acknowledged it. Mayuzumi died in 1997."},
  "toshiichiyanagi":{"wiki":"Toshi Ichiyanagi","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Toshi%20Ichiyanagi.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Brought Cage's indeterminacy home to Tokyo.","bio":"Toshi Ichiyanagi, born in Tokyo in 1933, was a Japanese composer and pianist who studied with John Cage in New York, in the New School class, and then carried indeterminacy back to Japan. In 1961 he brought Cage's scores to Tokyo, which is how Tōru Takemitsu first encountered them, and the Japanese postwar avant-garde dates much of its self-understanding from that moment. From 1956 to 1962 he was married to the artist Yoko Ono; together they ran the Chambers Street loft concerts that gathered the downtown New York scene. His own output was wide rather than doctrinaire — Western-style operas, orchestral and chamber works, pieces for traditional Japanese instruments — and it used chance procedures, extended technique and nontraditional scoring without treating any of them as a creed. He remained a leading figure in Japanese music until his death in 2022."},
  "yujitakahashi":{"wiki":"Yūji Takahashi","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Y%C5%ABji%20Takahashi%20PBS%201970.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Xenakis's pianist and principal interpreter.","bio":"Yuji Takahashi, born in Tokyo in 1938, is a Japanese composer, pianist, conductor, critic and author. He studied with Iannis Xenakis and became the principal interpreter of Xenakis's piano music, and that advocacy did much to establish where the music belonged and how it should sound. The double life — writing and playing, with neither treated as the servant of the other — has shaped his career, as has a habit of writing prose about music with the same seriousness he brings to the notes. He has worked as a conductor as well, and the several trades sit alongside one another rather than in a hierarchy: the interpreter's knowledge feeds the composer's, and the critic's argument is made partly at the keyboard, in what he chooses to play and how he plays it."},
  "toshiohosokawa":{"wiki":"Toshio Hosokawa","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Vioworld%20trifft...%20Toshio%20Hosokawa.jpg?width=480","blurb":"German modernism refracted through Japanese tradition.","bio":"Toshio Hosokawa, born in 1955, is a Japanese composer who trained in Germany and then turned that training back toward the classical music and culture of Japan. From 1976 he studied in Berlin with Isang Yun, and afterwards with Klaus Huber in Freiburg — a lineage that placed him inside the European postwar avant-garde while leaving him free of its orthodoxies. He has written operas and instrumental music, and the oratorio Voiceless Voice in Hiroshima. His mature style is personal rather than school-bound: European technique used to make room for a Japanese sense of sound, silence and gesture, so that the two traditions bear weight at the same time. He teaches as well as composes, and works between Tokyo and Berlin."},
  "isangyun":{"wiki":"Isang Yun","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Berlin-Gatow%20Landschaftsfriedhof%20Ehrengrab%20Isang%20Yun.JPG?width=480","blurb":"Abducted from Berlin, freed by protest.","bio":"Isang Yun, born in Korea in 1917, was a composer whose later career was made in West Germany, where he studied in Berlin with Boris Blacher and then settled. In 1967 he was abducted from West Berlin by the South Korean intelligence service; he was freed after an international campaign, and the episode made him, unwillingly, one of the most visible composers in Europe. He returned to Berlin and to teaching, and from 1976 his pupils included Toshio Hosokawa, who inherited the same question — how a musician raised in East Asian traditions works from inside European modernism — and carried it into the next generation. Yun worked as a conductor and musicologist as well as a composer, and held both German and South Korean nationality. He died in 1995."},
  "younghipaghpaan":{"wiki":"Younghi Pagh-Paan","blurb":"First woman with a German composition chair.","bio":"Younghi Pagh-Paan, born in Korea in 1945, was a composer who built her career in Germany. She studied in Freiburg with Klaus Huber, whom she later married, and the two taught together first in Freiburg and then in Bremen, where she became the first woman in Germany to hold a chair in composition — an appointment whose significance reached well past her own students. Her music holds her Korean upbringing and her European training in the same frame rather than reconciling them, which is close to what her whole generation of Korean composers abroad was reaching for. She taught for many years, at a time when neither a Korean name nor a woman's was a common sight on European new-music programmes, and the Bremen chair mattered as much for what it opened as for what it recognised. She died in 2023."},
  "tandun":{"wiki":"Tan Dun","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Tan%20Dun.JPG?width=480","blurb":"From Hunan to the Oscars.","bio":"Tan Dun, born in Hunan in 1957, is a composer and conductor, Chinese-born and American, whose work draws on Western and Chinese sources in roughly equal measure. He studied with Chou Wen-chung at Columbia University, and it is that pairing of inheritances, rather than either one alone, that has shaped his life and his music. His opera Marco Polo (1996) won the Grawemeyer Award, and his score for Ang Lee's Crouching Tiger, Hidden Dragon (2000) took both an Academy Award and a Grammy, making him one of very few living concert composers widely known outside the concert hall. His catalogue runs from opera and orchestral music to chamber, vocal and solo works, and includes two categories he invented for himself, 'organic music' and 'music ritual'. He works between New York and China."},
  "chenyi":{"wiki":"Chen Yi (composer)","blurb":"China's first woman master of composition.","bio":"Chen Yi, born in Guangzhou in 1953, is a Chinese-American composer and violinist, and was the first woman to take a master's degree in composition in China, at the Central Conservatory of Music in Beijing. She went on to Columbia University to study with Chou Wen-chung, as did her husband Zhou Long, whom she had met as a fellow student in Beijing. Her music integrates Chinese and Western traditions closely enough that neither reads as decoration on the other, and it is played around the world. Si Ji made her a Pulitzer finalist in 2006, and she has held Guggenheim and National Endowment for the Arts fellowships and awards from the Koussevitzky Music Foundation and the American Academy of Arts and Letters, to which she was elected in 2019. She teaches in Kansas City."},
  "zhoulong":{"wiki":"Zhou Long","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Zhou%20Long%20with%20NYO-China%20and%20Ludovic%20Morlot%20at%20Carnegie%20Hall%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"A Pulitzer for the post-Cultural Revolution generation.","bio":"Zhou Long, born in Beijing in 1953, is a Chinese-American composer who won the Pulitzer Prize for Music in 2011. He studied at the Central Conservatory of Music in Beijing alongside Chen Yi, whom he married, and both then went to Columbia University to work with Chou Wen-chung on the programme he had built specifically for the first generation of Chinese composers to emerge after the Cultural Revolution — a case of a teacher shaping a whole cohort rather than a single pupil. Zhou has worked as a pianist and musicologist as well as a composer, and has taught for many years in Kansas City. Like his wife's, his music treats Chinese material as structural rather than picturesque, which is what separates this generation from the earlier fashion for Chinese colour in Western scores."},
  "brightsheng":{"wiki":"Bright Sheng","blurb":"Bernstein's assistant in the last years.","bio":"Bright Sheng, born in Shanghai in 1955, is a Chinese-American composer, conductor and pianist. He studied with Chou Wen-chung at Columbia University, and became Leonard Bernstein's assistant in Bernstein's last years, afterwards writing a memorial piece dedicated to him. Commissions and performances have come from virtually every major American orchestra, and from ensembles running from Paris, Amsterdam and London to St Petersburg, Tokyo, Seoul and Sydney, and conductors including Kurt Masur, Christoph Eschenbach and Charles Dutoit have taken it up. He received a MacArthur Fellowship in 2001 and was twice a Pulitzer Prize finalist. He has taught for many years in Michigan. Like several of his Columbia contemporaries, he writes music in which Chinese material and Western orchestral practice are load-bearing at the same time, rather than one being applied to the surface of the other."},
  "josemaceda":{"wiki":"José Maceda","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jos%C3%A9%20Maceda%202017%20stamp%20of%20the%20Philippines.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Fifty years recording the Philippine villages.","bio":"José Montserrat Maceda, born in Manila in 1917, was a Filipino composer and ethnomusicologist who spent some fifty years recording the village music of the Philippines. He studied in Paris with Nadia Boulanger in the 1950s and worked with Pierre Schaeffer at the Groupe de Recherches Musicales, so the European avant-garde was already in his hands when the fieldwork began — and the fieldwork then reorganised everything. His own compositions think in masses of sound rather than in lines, an approach he shared with Iannis Xenakis, whom he knew; the two had arrived at comparable structures from opposite directions and were aware of it. The recordings he made remain a primary archive of Philippine musical practice. In 1998 his country named him a National Artist for Music. He died in 2004."},
  "akineuba":{"wiki":"Akin Euba","blurb":"The theorist of African pianism.","bio":"Olatunji Akin Euba, born in Nigeria in 1935, was a Nigerian composer, pianist and musicologist, and the theorist of what he called African pianism — a way of writing for the European piano whose idioms come from African instrumental practice rather than being imported wholesale from Europe. The idea was compositional and scholarly at once, and it gave a generation of African composers a framework to argue from as well as a technique to use. He taught for many years, latterly in Pittsburgh, and among his pupils in Nigeria was Joshua Uzoigwe, who became his principal successor and carried African pianism into the next generation. Euba's musicology and his composing were never separate activities; each supplied the other with material, and the writing made the music legible to people who had no other way in. He died in 2020."},
  "joshuauzoigwe":{"wiki":"Joshua Uzoigwe","blurb":"Igbo tradition carried to the piano.","bio":"Joshua Uzoigwe, born in Nigeria in 1946, was a composer and ethnomusicologist and a member of the Igbo people, whose traditional music supplies the material for a great many of his works. He studied with Akin Euba and became the principal successor to Euba's idea of African pianism, carrying into the next generation the practice of writing for the piano out of African instrumental idiom rather than European convention. Like his teacher he worked in two registers at once — as an academic recording and analysing traditional music, and as a composer using what he found — and the scholarship kept the compositions honest. He worked as an academic in Nigeria for most of his career. He belonged to the postwar generation across Africa, Asia and Latin America that took European training and turned it back toward its own inheritance. He died in 2005."},
  "kevinvolans":{"wiki":"Kevin Volans","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kevin%20Volans%20(comp)%202015%20(Annamarie%20Ursula)%20P1200477.JPG?width=480","blurb":"Cologne modernism turned toward southern Africa.","bio":"Kevin Volans, born in South Africa in 1949, is a composer and pianist who studied in Cologne in the 1970s with Karlheinz Stockhausen, becoming his teaching assistant, and with Mauricio Kagel, and was associated there with the Neue Einfachheit, the city's new simplicity. In the late 1970s he turned toward the music indigenous to the country he came from, beginning a series of pieces that tried to hold African and contemporary European practice within one structure; White Man Sleeps is the best known of them. From the early 1990s he moved away from direct engagement with African material, though the interlocking patterns, the repetition and the open forms stayed audible in music that had turned toward the procedures of abstract painting. He settled permanently in Ireland in 1986, taking Irish citizenship in 1994."},
  "mariodavidovsky":{"wiki":"Mario Davidovsky","blurb":"Live instruments answered by tape.","bio":"Mario Davidovsky, born in Buenos Aires in 1934, was an Argentine-American composer best known for the Synchronisms, a long series in which acoustic instruments play against pre-recorded electronic sound on tape. Aaron Copland brought him from Buenos Aires to Tanglewood in 1958; he emigrated to the United States in 1960 and lived there for the rest of his life. In New York he worked at, and later ran, the Columbia-Princeton Electronic Music Center, the studio where Milton Babbitt and others were pursuing an American electronic music and where the Synchronisms were made. What distinguishes them is that the tape is never accompaniment: instrument and loudspeaker exchange gestures closely enough that a listener stops separating the two. He was also a violinist and a long-serving teacher, and he died in 2019."},
  "roquecordero":{"wiki":"Roque Cordero","blurb":"Twelve-tone method with Panamanian rhythm.","bio":"Roque Cordero, born in Panama in 1917, was a Panamanian composer, conductor and teacher who studied in Minneapolis with Ernst Krenek and took the twelve-tone method he learned there and turned it onto Panamanian rhythm. That is a harder trick than it sounds: serial organisation and the dance rhythms of the isthmus do not obviously belong together, and Cordero's achievement was to make the combination sound inevitable rather than programmatic. He conducted, taught at university level for decades, and was for many years the central figure in Panamanian art music, a position that meant conducting and teaching as much as composition. His example mattered to Latin American composers looking for a way to be modernist without ceasing to be local, which was the central argument of his generation across the continent. He died in 2008."},
  "marlosnobre":{"wiki":"Marlos Nobre","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Marlos%20Nobre%20M%C3%A9rito%20Cultural%202013%20cropped.jpg?width=480","blurb":"From Recife to Ginastera's Di Tella.","bio":"Marlos Nobre, born in Recife in 1939, was a Brazilian composer, conductor and pianist who studied with Alberto Ginastera at the Di Tella Institute in Buenos Aires. Commissions came from across Europe and the Americas: Spain's Ministry of Culture, the Maracaibo Music Festival in Venezuela, the Free University of Music in São Paulo, Bremen's Apollon Foundation and the Neuchâtel Chamber Orchestra in Switzerland. He also sat on the juries of major international competitions, including the Arthur Rubinstein Piano Master Competition, the Paloma O'Shea Santander International Piano Competition and the Città di Alessandria Prize — work that shapes careers other than one's own. That commissioning and jury record, spread across two continents, is much of the shape of the career itself. He died in 2024."},
  "camargoguarnieri":{"wiki":"Camargo Guarnieri","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Camargo%20Guarnieri%20(sem%20data).tiff?width=480","blurb":"Brazil's symphonist after Villa-Lobos.","bio":"Mozart Camargo Guarnieri, born in 1907, was Brazil's central symphonist in the generation after Villa-Lobos, and the composer around whom much of São Paulo's musical life turned. He studied in Paris with Charles Koechlin in 1938. Longer-lasting than any lesson, though, was his relationship with the writer Mário de Andrade, author of Macunaíma, who for twenty years directed his reading, his politics and his career — a literary modernist deliberately equipping a composer to carry a national project. Guarnieri was a pianist and a conductor as well as a composer, and worked as a musicologist besides. The symphonies are the centre of the output, and the nationalism in them was a position taken rather than a manner inherited — Andrade's project carried on in sound long after the twenty years were over. He died in 1993."},
  "leobrouwer":{"wiki":"Leo Brouwer","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/FotoBrouwer2011Bianchini-1.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The guitar's most important living composer.","bio":"Juan Leovigildo Brouwer Mezquida, born in Havana in 1939, is a Cuban composer and conductor — and a classical guitarist — who is by common consent the most important living composer for his instrument. He studied at Juilliard with Vincent Persichetti. A guitarist himself, he writes for the instrument from the inside, which is much of why the classical guitar's modern repertory is hard to imagine without him: the writing lies under the hand in a way that composers approaching the instrument from outside rarely manage. He has worked extensively as a conductor as well, and the three trades — composing, conducting, playing — have run in parallel rather than in sequence across a long career. The International Music Council has made him a Member of Honour."},
  "gabrielaortiz":{"wiki":"Gabriela Ortiz","blurb":"Mexico City's voice at Carnegie Hall.","bio":"Gabriela Ortiz Torres, born in Mexico City in 1964, is a Mexican composer and music educator whose music is being taken up by ensembles in Vienna, Berlin, London, New York and Los Angeles. Carnegie Hall took her as its composer in residence for the 2024–25 season. Her 2024 album Revolución diamantina — with María Dueñas, Gustavo Dudamel and the Los Angeles Philharmonic — drew four nominations at the 2025 Grammys and took three of them, including best contemporary classical composition; her works Yanga and Dzonot took three more at the 2026 Grammys. She teaches as well as composes, and belongs to the generation of Latin American composers whose music has travelled north on its own terms rather than as local colour supplied to somebody else's programme."},
  "benjaminbritten":{"wiki":"Benjamin Britten","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fortepan%20137711%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The voice of postwar English opera.","bio":"Benjamin Britten, born in 1913, was the central figure of twentieth-century British music: a composer, conductor and pianist whose operas gave the English stage a repertory it had lacked for generations. From the age of thirteen he studied with Frank Bridge, the only English composer he thought had taught him anything. Serge Koussevitzky commissioned Peter Grimes, first heard in 1945; the War Requiem followed in 1962. From 1937 his partner was the tenor Peter Pears, for whom he wrote almost every tenor role in his output. He worked with W. H. Auden on Paul Bunyan, the film documentaries and the early song cycles before a bitter break, and with Mstislav Rostropovich, whom he met in 1960, on the Cello Symphony and the three suites. Across the Iron Curtain he kept a close friendship with Dmitri Shostakovich; the two dedicated works to each other. He died in 1976."},
  "peterpears":{"wiki":"Peter Pears","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Peter%20Pears%20publicity%20photo%201971%20crop.png?width=480","blurb":"The tenor Britten wrote everything for.","bio":"Sir Peter Neville Luard Pears, born in 1910, was an English tenor whose career is inseparable from Benjamin Britten's; they were professional and romantic partners for nearly forty years, together from 1937. Almost every tenor role Britten wrote was written for Pears, and that fact shapes the music itself: the parts were built for an unmistakable voice with its own colour and its own limits, rather than for a generic ideal, which is why they still sound the way they do. Pears sang the premieres, recorded the repertory, and was central to the musical life the two of them made at Aldeburgh. He also worked as an editor. His importance is not that he sang Britten's music well but that a large part of the century's English vocal writing is the shape it is because of him. He died in 1986."},
  "williamwalton":{"wiki":"William Walton","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/William%20Walton%20(1976).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Belshazzar's Feast and the coronation marches.","bio":"Sir William Turner Walton, born in 1902, was an English composer whose sixty-year career ranged across most of the classical genres and well beyond them, from film scores to opera. He made his name with Façade and consolidated it with the cantata Belshazzar's Feast, a work of choral extravagance that has never left the British repertory. The Viola Concerto and the First Symphony are the other pillars of his reputation. He also wrote Crown Imperial and Orb and Sceptre, marches for coronations, which fixed him in the public mind as a composer of state occasions — a role that sat a little oddly beside the young man who had written Façade, and one he never entirely shook off. He conducted, wrote for the cinema, and spent his later decades on the island of Ischia, where he died in 1983."},
  "michaeltippett":{"wiki":"Michael Tippett","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mittsommerhochzeit%20(Michael%20Kemp%20Tippett)%20-%20LABW%20-%20Generallandesarchiv%20Karlsruhe%2057-3%20Nr.%2010444.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Britten's opposite number in postwar Britain.","bio":"Sir Michael Kemp Tippett, born in 1905, was an English composer who came to prominence in the years during and just after the Second World War, and was thereafter ranked, in his own lifetime, with Benjamin Britten among the leading British composers of the century. The oratorio A Child of Our Time remains his best-known work; the opera The Midsummer Marriage and the Fantasia Concertante on a Theme of Corelli stand close behind it. The music is discursive where Britten's is taut, and it asks to be met on its own terms rather than admired from a distance, which is part of why the two names have always been paired and have always resisted the pairing. He was a conductor and a writer as well, published an autobiography, and worked as a musicologist. He died in 1998."},
  "petermaxwelldavies":{"wiki":"Peter Maxwell Davies","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Peter%20Maxwell%20Davies.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Manchester radical who settled in Orkney.","bio":"Sir Peter Maxwell Davies, born in 1934, was an English composer and conductor and one of the group that from 1953 formed the Manchester School — with Harrison Birtwistle, Alexander Goehr, John Ogdon and Elgar Howarth — a set of students who between them turned British music toward the European postwar avant-garde. He later settled far from the metropolitan centres, in Orkney, which became the setting for the rest of his working life. He wrote prolifically, conducted widely, taught, and composed for film, moving between the concert hall, the theatre and the cinema without treating any of them as a lesser room. In 2004 he was appointed Master of the Queen's Music, a post at the centre of British musical officialdom and an odd destination for a musician who had begun where he did. He died in 2016."},
  "harrisonbirtwistle":{"wiki":"Harrison Birtwistle","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Harrison%20Birtwistle%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Mythology turned into modernist theatre.","bio":"Sir Harrison Birtwistle, born in 1934, was an English composer best known for operas built on mythological subjects: The Mask of Orpheus (1986), Gawain (1991) and, in 2008, The Minotaur — which Guardian critics in 2019 placed third among the pieces of the twenty-first century. From 1953 he belonged to the Manchester School alongside Peter Maxwell Davies, Alexander Goehr, John Ogdon and Elgar Howarth. Even his concert works are theatrical in conception — The Triumph of Time (1972) among the clearest instances — and his music tends to move in blocks and rituals rather than in arguments. A performance of his saxophone concerto Panic at the BBC's Last Night of the Proms brought him national notoriety, a rare distinction for a modernist. Awards and honorary degrees came to him from many countries. He died in 2022."},
  "alexandergoehr":{"wiki":"Alexander Goehr","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Composer%20Alexander%20Goehr.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Cambridge's teacher of a British generation.","bio":"Alexander Goehr, born in 1932, was a German-born English composer and teacher who studied with Olivier Messiaen in Paris in 1955–56 and belonged, from his Manchester student years, to the circle around Peter Maxwell Davies. His father, Walter Goehr, had studied with Schoenberg in Berlin, so the Second Viennese inheritance reached him directly rather than through books. For decades he was the professor of music at Cambridge, where his teaching came to matter at least as much as his composing: Judith Weir studied with him there, and his influence runs through Robin Holloway, George Benjamin, Julian Anderson and Thomas Adès — most of a British generation. He composed across the concert genres over a long career, and remained a formidable arguer about music until his death in 2024."},
  "elisabethlutyens":{"wiki":"Elisabeth Lutyens","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Elisabeth-Lutyens.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Among Britain's first twelve-tone composers.","bio":"Agnes Elisabeth Lutyens, born in London in 1906, was an English composer and among the first in Britain to write with the twelve-tone method — a choice made in a country whose music was then overwhelmingly tonal, and one that put her at an angle to almost everything around her. She was appointed CBE. Alongside the concert works she wrote film scores, so that the same hand arguing for serialism in Britain was also at work in the cinema. Priority is not a small thing: whatever British twelve-tone music later became, she was there before it, working with a method that had barely any foothold in the country, and she kept at it while the fashion caught up with her. She died in 1983."},
  "theamusgrave":{"wiki":"Thea Musgrave","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Thea%20Musgrave%202017%20-%20St%20Brides%2C%20London.jpg?width=480","blurb":"A Scottish opera composer in America.","bio":"Thea Musgrave, born in 1928, is a Scottish composer known above all for her operas. She studied in Paris with Nadia Boulanger from 1950 to 1954, the teacher through whom a striking proportion of the century's composers passed, and has written for the stage as well as the concert hall. The United States has been her home since 1972, and she has worked there as a professor, building a second career and a second audience in mid-life. She was appointed CBE. Her working life has been divided between the two countries, and so, unusually for a British composer of her generation, has her reputation: the operas that made her name have been taken up on both sides of the Atlantic rather than in a single national repertory."},
  "judithweir":{"wiki":"Judith Weir","blurb":"First woman Master of the King's Music.","bio":"Dame Judith Weir, born in 1954, is a British composer who held the post of Master of the King's Music between 2014 and 2024 — appointed by Queen Elizabeth II, and the first woman ever to hold it. Her training was unusually direct: John Tavener taught her privately while she was still at school, and she then studied with Alexander Goehr at Cambridge, so two very different accounts of what British music might be reached her early and at close range. She began as an oboist and has worked as a musicologist and a music educator alongside composing. Her music is known for a clarity and economy that make difficult material sound plain-spoken — an idiom that has never traded on difficulty for its own sake, and never mistaken plainness for simplicity."},
  "oliverknussen":{"wiki":"Oliver Knussen","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Oliver%20knussen.jpg?width=480","blurb":"A small output, immense influence.","bio":"Stuart Oliver Knussen, born in 1952, was a British composer and conductor, and among the most influential British musicians of his generation despite a famously small catalogue. His music grew out of twentieth-century modernism while answering to no school other than the one he made for himself, and he let comparatively little of it out. As a conductor he was indispensable, and a great deal of his influence ran through that work: with George Benjamin he formed the centre of an Aldeburgh and BBC circle whose members programmed and conducted each other's music for thirty years, giving new British scores the kind of expert advocacy they rarely receive. He was closely associated with Aldeburgh, where he lived and worked, and the conducting mattered to his contemporaries' music as much as his own composing did. He died in 2018."},
  "georgebenjamin":{"wiki":"George Benjamin (composer)","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/George%20Benjamin.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Messiaen's last and youngest pupil.","bio":"Sir George Benjamin, born in London in 1960, is an English composer and conductor, a pianist and a teacher, who arrived in Paris at sixteen to study with Olivier Messiaen and became his last pupil. That start gave him a European rather than a narrowly English formation, and an early reputation that he then took decades to fill out. He is also a conductor of real authority in twentieth- and twenty-first-century repertory, and he belonged with Oliver Knussen to the Aldeburgh and BBC circle whose members programmed and conducted each other's music for thirty years. He teaches as well as composing and conducting, three activities he has kept in balance across his career rather than letting any one of them take the others over. He was appointed CBE and knighted."},
  "henridutilleux":{"wiki":"Henri Dutilleux","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/HD%20%C2%A9Guy%20Vivien.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Outside every school, admired by all.","bio":"Henri Dutilleux, born in 1916, ranked among France's leading composers of the later twentieth century and belonged to no school at all — which is much of why composers from every school admired him. His music grew out of the Impressionist inheritance of Debussy and Ravel into something idiosyncratic and entirely his own: the debt audible, the result unmistakable. The early Flute Sonatine and Piano Sonata established him; the symphonies followed, No. 1 in 1951 and No. 2, Le Double, in 1959. He wrote the cello concerto Tout un monde lointain… for Mstislav Rostropovich in 1970, and a violin concerto, L'arbre des songes, and the string quartet Ainsi la nuit is among the most admired of its century. He worked in Paris, played the piano, wrote about music, wrote for film, and died in 2013."},
  "edisondenisov":{"wiki":"Edison Denisov","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Edison%20Denisov%201975%20%C2%A9DmitriSmirnov.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Soviet underground's third voice.","bio":"Edison Vasilievich Denisov, born in 1929, was a Soviet and Russian composer who belonged to the unofficial avant-garde — the underground, alternative or nonconformist wing of Soviet music, which worked in Moscow without official approval. With Sofia Gubaidulina and Alfred Schnittke, both of whom he knew in the same Moscow circle, he formed the trio by which that generation is usually identified: three composers pursuing Western postwar technique inside a system that had declared it decadent. He was also a musicologist, a teacher and a prolific composer of film scores, which was one of the few reliable ways for such a composer to make a living. His name is now inseparable from Schnittke's and Gubaidulina's, which is accurate and a little unfair to music that sounds like neither of theirs. He died in 1996."},
  "yvonneloriod":{"wiki":"Yvonne Loriod","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Loriod%20Caen.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The pianist Messiaen wrote for.","bio":"Yvonne Louise Georgette Loriod-Messiaen, born in 1924, was a French pianist, teacher and composer whose career and Olivier Messiaen's cannot be separated. She became his pupil in 1941, and from then on she was the pianist of very nearly everything he wrote: the piano writing in his major works was conceived for her hands and for what he knew she could be relied on to do with it. After the death of his first wife they married, in 1961. She taught and she composed as well, so that the musician remembered as an interpreter was in fact working in three trades at once. Her sister Jeanne Loriod played the ondes Martenot — one family, two instruments, standing near the centre of French musical life. She died in 2010."},
  "elliottcarter":{"wiki":"Elliott Carter","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Elliott%20Carter%201917.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Metric modulation, and a century of work.","bio":"Elliott Cook Carter Jr., born in New York in 1908, was an American modernist, and few composers of the century's second half were more widely respected. Charles Ives wrote his letter of recommendation to Harvard; he then studied in Paris with Nadia Boulanger from 1932 to 1935. After an early neoclassical phase he built a language of his own that combined European modernism with American ultra-modernism, its rhythmic signature the technique called metric modulation, in which the pulse itself shifts by proportion. He won the Pulitzer Prize twice, both times for string quartets, and wrote the orchestral triptych Symphonia: sum fluxae pretium spei. Paul Sacher's Basel commissions supported his later decades — decades that simply kept going: he composed past his hundredth birthday and died in 2012."},
  "leonardbernstein":{"wiki":"Leonard Bernstein","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Leonard%20Bernstein%20by%20Jack%20Mitchell%20(high%20quality).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The tradition's greatest populariser.","bio":"Leonard Bernstein, born in 1918, was an American conductor, composer, pianist, educator and author, and the first American-born conductor to win international acclaim. Serge Koussevitzky was his conducting teacher and the reason for the Tanglewood career that anchored the rest of his life. Aaron Copland was never formally his teacher but was the one Bernstein always named as decisive; their friendship lasted fifty years, during which Bernstein conducted and recorded almost everything Copland wrote. His gift for explanation made him the tradition's greatest populariser, reaching audiences on television that no concert hall could hold. In his last years Bright Sheng worked as his assistant and afterwards wrote a memorial piece for him. He won sixteen Grammys, seven Emmys and two Tonys, and received the Kennedy Center Honor in 1981. He died in 1990."},
  "georgecrumb":{"wiki":"George Crumb","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/George%20Crumb.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Extended technique as haunted soundscape.","bio":"George Henry Crumb Jr., born in 1929, was an American composer who rejected the serialism that dominated his early years and built instead a highly personal language, one that could be serene in a single bar and terrifying in the next. He wrote mostly for chamber ensembles and soloists, pushing players into extended techniques to produce timbres that give the music its surreal and sometimes haunted atmosphere. Black Angels is the best known of these pieces. Among his few large-scale works, Echoes of Time and the River (1967) won the Pulitzer Prize in 1968 and Star-Child (1977) took a Grammy in 2001. At the University of Pennsylvania he was a colleague of George Rochberg, and the two of them left serialism behind in the same decade by quite different routes. Crumb taught there for many years and died in 2022."},
  "georgerochberg":{"wiki":"George Rochberg","blurb":"The serialist who turned back to tonality.","bio":"George Rochberg, born in 1918, was an American composer who wrote strict serial music for years and then, in 1965, abandoned the technique altogether. His teenage son had died, and Rochberg said serialism had proved inadequate to that grief and empty of expressive power. The tonal passages that entered his music through the 1970s provoked real hostility from critics and fellow composers, for whom the return to tonality looked like desertion; the quarrel that followed is one of the defining arguments of postwar American music. He chaired the music department at the University of Pennsylvania until 1968 and taught there until 1983, becoming in 1978 its first Annenberg Professor of the Humanities. His colleague there, George Crumb, left serialism in the same decade by another road. Rochberg died in 2005."},
  "nedrorem":{"wiki":"Ned Rorem","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ned-Rorem-1968.jpg?width=480","blurb":"America's song composer and frankest diarist.","bio":"Ned Miller Rorem, born in 1923, was an American composer and writer, known above all for his art songs — more than five hundred of them, enough to make him the most prominent American writing in the genre in his lifetime. Frequently described as neoromantic, he took limited interest in the modernist aesthetic that dominated his lifetime, and wrote melody without apology through decades when that was unfashionable. He was equally known as a diarist: the journals he kept and later published spoke with unusual candour about his relationships and exchanges with cultural figures in America and France, and made him famous outside music in a way few composers manage. He taught at university level alongside composing, and he died in 2022."},
  "ellentaaffezwilich":{"wiki":"Ellen Taaffe Zwilich","blurb":"First woman to win the Pulitzer.","bio":"Ellen Taaffe Zwilich, born in 1939, is an American composer and the first woman to win the Pulitzer Prize for Music. Her earliest works explore atonality, but by the late 1980s she had moved toward the postmodernist, neoromantic manner in which most of her mature music is written. That she was the first woman to take the prize is the fact that follows her name everywhere; that the music kept its audience afterwards is the less-quoted half of the story. Among living American composers she is reckoned one of the most often performed, and one of the best liked. Trained as a violinist and a musicologist as well as a composer, she was inducted into the Florida Artists Hall of Fame in 1994 and has held the Francis Eppes Distinguished Professorship at Florida State University."},
  "joantower":{"wiki":"Joan Tower","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Joan%20Tower%20in%20music%20is%20(Speaking%20Portraits)%20(Vol.%20I).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Fanfares for the uncommon woman.","bio":"Joan Tower, born in 1938, is an American composer, concert pianist and conductor whose first orchestral work, Sequoia (1981) — a tone poem that climbs a giant tree, trunk to needles — brought her wide recognition. She was a founding member and the pianist of the Da Capo Chamber Players, winners of the Naumburg Award; the ensemble took up and first performed many of her early pieces, including the much-played Petroushskates: a composer with her own band, able to test everything she wrote. Her catalogue includes five string quartets, the Island Prelude, a run of further tone poems, and Fanfare for the Uncommon Woman, written as something of a response to Copland's Fanfare for the Common Man. She has won a Grammy, and her music is played worldwide."},
  "johncorigliano":{"wiki":"John Corigliano","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/John%20Corigliano%202023.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Concert hall and cinema, both won.","bio":"John Paul Corigliano, born in New York in 1938, is an American composer with more than a hundred works to his name and an unusually broad reach. His shelf holds a Pulitzer Prize, an Academy Award, the Grawemeyer Award for Music Composition and five Grammys — close to a clean sweep of the honours available to a composer who works on both sides of the line between the concert hall and the cinema, and a set of credentials very few of his contemporaries can match. He has taught at university level for decades alongside composing, and worked as a concertmaster earlier in his career. His music is dramatic by instinct and unafraid of direct emotional address, a disposition that reads as natural in a composer equally at home writing for the screen. He continues to work in New York."},
  "gyorgyligeti":{"wiki":"György Ligeti","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gy%C3%B6rgy%20Ligeti%20(1984).jpg?width=480","blurb":"'Atmosphères', and a lifetime of restless invention.","bio":"György Ligeti, born in Budapest in 1923, became one of the most inventive figures of the postwar avant-garde. He trained at the Budapest Academy under Ferenc Farkas, and in 1945 met György Kurtág there as a fellow student, beginning a friendship that lasted sixty years. He reached Cologne in 1956 and worked in the studio alongside Karlheinz Stockhausen before turning away from serialism and finding his own ground: the orchestral world of 'Atmosphères', and the Études. He held Hungarian and Austrian nationality, played the piano, and taught for years in Hamburg, where Unsuk Chin studied with him between 1985 and 1988. Writers have called him one of the most important and most innovative composers of the second half of the twentieth century, and the description has stuck. He died in 2006."},
  "gyorgykurtag":{"wiki":"György Kurtág","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kurt%C3%A1g%20Gy%C3%B6rgy.jpeg?width=480","blurb":"Whole worlds held in a few bars.","bio":"György Kurtág, born in 1926 and long based in Budapest, is a Hungarian composer and pianist who holds both Hungarian and Romanian nationality. He met György Ligeti as a fellow student in Budapest in 1945, and the two stayed friends for sixty years. In 1957–58 he went to Paris and studied with Olivier Messiaen and with Milhaud, a year that redirected him. His music is usually traced back to Bartók and Webern, with Stravinsky further off, and it is defined by extreme concision: small forces, compressed spans, and a directness of expression that lands at once. Alongside composing and playing he has taught for decades. A 2023 assessment placed him among the few surviving links to the postwar composers who defined the European avant-garde — a survivor of the generation he had himself helped to shape."},
  "hanswernerhenze":{"wiki":"Hans Werner Henze","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bundesarchiv%20B%20145%20Bild-F008277-0008%2C%20K%C3%B6ln%2C%20Schloss%20Br%C3%BChl%2C%20Meisterkurse%20Musik.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Prolific, political, and hostile to Darmstadt.","bio":"Hans Werner Henze, born in Germany in 1926, spent much of his working life in Italy and died in 2012. He studied with Wolfgang Fortner at Heidelberg and learned twelve-tone technique from René Leibowitz — a training he then turned against the Darmstadt orthodoxy rather than serving it. Paul Sacher championed him from Basel. The oeuvre that followed is enormous and deliberately varied, drawing on atonality and serialism, on neoclassicism and jazz, and on Italian and Arabic music, as readily as on the German schools he was raised in. Above all he cultivated music for the theatre, returning to the stage throughout his life. He was also a musicologist, a jazz musician and a university teacher, and his politics ran openly through the work — which is precisely what set him against the postwar avant-garde's official centre."},
  "berndaloiszimmermann":{"wiki":"Bernd Alois Zimmermann","blurb":"'Die Soldaten', and music made of quotation.","bio":"Bernd Alois Zimmermann, born in 1918 and based in Cologne, was a German composer, musicologist and university teacher who died in 1970. His name rests above all on the opera 'Die Soldaten', reckoned among the twentieth century's most important German operas, after those of Berg, and that comparison is the measure usually applied to it. His output is deliberately eclectic, gathering dodecaphony and outright musical quotation into the same scores, and it sits across the avant-garde, the serial and the postmodern at once. That refusal to settle in one camp is the point: he worked at the centre of postwar German music without belonging tidily to any of its factions, and the borrowed material in his music keeps the past audible inside the new."},
  "mauriciokagel":{"wiki":"Mauricio Kagel","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mauricio%20Kagel%20(1985).jpg?width=480","blurb":"He turned performance itself into theatre.","bio":"Mauricio Kagel was born in Buenos Aires in 1931 and settled in Cologne, becoming an Argentine-German composer and an academic teacher. He is identified above all with instrumental theatre, in which the visible act of performance — the players, their gestures, the apparatus of the concert itself — becomes part of the piece rather than the neutral means of delivering it. He worked as a conductor and a film director as well, and that appetite for the staged and the seen runs through everything he made. Arriving in Cologne from Argentina, he became one of the outsiders who reshaped the German avant-garde from within, teaching there for years. He died in 2008, leaving a body of work that treats the concert ritual as material to be played with rather than a frame to be respected."},
  "helmutlachenmann":{"wiki":"Helmut Lachenmann","blurb":"The inventor of musique concrète instrumentale.","bio":"Helmut Lachenmann, born in 1935 and long associated with Stuttgart, is a German composer, pianist and teacher. Between 1958 and 1960 he studied in Venice with Luigi Nono, the decisive apprenticeship of his life and the link that ties him back to the first postwar generation. His own contribution is the idea he named musique concrète instrumentale: sound approached through the physical effort that produces it, so that bow pressure, breath and the mechanics of an instrument become the substance of the music rather than incidental noise. It changed what a generation of younger composers thought an instrument was for. He has taught widely — at the Darmstädter Ferienkurse and at the Musikhochschulen of Stuttgart and Hannover — and that teaching has carried the method well beyond his own catalogue."},
  "wolfgangrihm":{"wiki":"Wolfgang Rihm","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Rihm%20Wolfgang%20Philharmonie%20koeln%200806%202007.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Five hundred works, and no fixed style.","bio":"Wolfgang Rihm, born in 1952, spent his career based in Karlsruhe and died in 2024. He studied with Karlheinz Stockhausen in Cologne and with Klaus Huber in Freiburg, then went his own way, becoming a voice of unusual originality and independence in postwar European music precisely by refusing to adopt a house style. His output is vast — more than five hundred works, several of them operas — and it never settled into a manner that could be copied from the outside. He was also a librettist and a musicologist, and an academic teacher, which put him at the centre of German musical life for decades. The scale of the catalogue, and its restlessness, is the argument he made against orthodoxy — a body of work too large and too various to be reduced to a method."},
  "salvatoresciarrino":{"wiki":"Salvatore Sciarrino","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/SalvatoreSciarrino%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Italy's most-performed living composer.","bio":"Salvatore Sciarrino, born in Italy in 1947, is a composer of contemporary classical music who has been described as the most widely performed and best known of living Italian composers. He is his own librettist as often as not, a doubling that gives his vocal and dramatic work an unusual unity of word and sound; he has also worked as a conductor. Among the pieces that carry his reputation are 'Quaderno di strada' of 2003 and 'La porta della legge', written between 2006 and 2008. He belongs to the generation of Italian composers who came up after the postwar avant-garde had settled its arguments, and who found room to work in a different direction without either joining that movement or renouncing it outright."},
  "francodonatoni":{"wiki":"Franco Donatoni","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Franco%20Donatoni.jpg?width=480","blurb":"An Italian modernist, and a teacher.","bio":"Franco Donatoni, born in 1927, was an Italian composer and teacher of the generation that came up after 1945, and he is placed here in Milan. On this map his position rests on the teaching as much as on the music: he stands in the Milanese line through which Italian modernism was passed on to the composers who followed him. The record gathered for him is unusually thin. No works are listed against his name, and no teachers, pupils or colleagues are recorded here, so what survives is an outline rather than a portrait — an Italian modernist of the postwar decades, working and teaching in Milan, and belonging to the same restless European moment as the composers set around him in this part of the map. He died in 2000."},
  "chouwenchung":{"wiki":"Chou Wen-chung","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Chou%20Wen-chung%2C%20Chinese%20American%20composer%20of%20contemporary.%20classical%20music.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Varèse's executor, and Columbia's great teacher.","bio":"Chou Wen-chung, born in 1923, emigrated to the United States in 1946 and trained at the New England Conservatory and at Columbia University, where he spent his career. He became Edgard Varèse's pupil and assistant, and after Varèse's death his editor and executor — the man who kept that music in order and in circulation. Nicolas Slonimsky credited him as among the first Chinese composers to carry genuine East Asian melo-rhythms over into modern Western practice. At Columbia he built the programme that received the first post-Cultural Revolution generation of Chinese composers, and taught Zhou Long, Chen Yi, Bright Sheng and Tan Dun there. That single teaching room is one of the most consequential in this whole map, and the careers that came out of it are still running. He died in 2019."},
  "unsukchin":{"wiki":"Unsuk Chin","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Unsuk%20Chin.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Ligeti's pupil, from Seoul to Berlin.","bio":"Unsuk Chin, born in Seoul in 1961, is a South Korean composer who has been based in Berlin for most of her working life. She taught herself the piano from a young age, studied composition at Seoul National University, and then went to the Hochschule für Musik und Theater in Hamburg to work with György Ligeti between 1985 and 1988 — the apprenticeship that shaped her, and one of the clearest lines of descent from the postwar avant-garde into the present. Alongside composing she works as an artistic director, programming other people's music as well as writing her own. Her career traces a route that this map keeps returning to: a musician trained on one continent, formed by a teacher on another, and belonging finally to neither exclusively."},
  "astorpiazzolla":{"wiki":"Astor Piazzolla","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Astor%20Piazzolla.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The bandoneón that remade the tango.","bio":"Astor Piazzolla, born in 1921 and identified above all with Buenos Aires, was an Argentine composer, arranger and virtuoso bandoneón player. He studied with Alberto Ginastera in Buenos Aires from 1941, and later with Nadia Boulanger, who listened to his symphonic ambitions and told him to stop writing symphonies and go back to the tango — advice that decided his life. What followed was nuevo tango, a rebuilding of the traditional form with elements drawn from jazz and from classical music, played by the composer himself at the head of a succession of ensembles. He also wrote for film. By his death in 1992 the critic Stephen Holden could call him the foremost tango composer in the world, and the label has not been seriously contested since."},
  "albertoginastera":{"wiki":"Alberto Ginastera","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fotograf%C3%ADa%20del%20compositor%20argentino%20Alberto%20Ginastera.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Argentina's leading modernist, and Piazzolla's teacher.","bio":"Alberto Ginastera, born in Buenos Aires in 1916, is considered one of the most important twentieth-century classical composers of the Americas. He was a musicologist and librettist as well as a composer, and wrote for film. In 1946 he worked with Aaron Copland at Tanglewood, a link that ties Argentine modernism directly to the American one. His own teaching mattered as much as his music: from 1941 he taught Astor Piazzolla in Buenos Aires, and at the Di Tella Institute he taught Marlos Nobre, part of a stream of Latin American composers who passed through his hands. He gave the continent's new music an institutional home at a moment when it badly needed one. He died in 1983, having done as much as anyone to make Argentine composition a serious international presence."},
  "eleanorofaquitaine":{"wiki":"Eleanor of Aquitaine","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Queen%20Eleanor%20(Frederick%20Sandys%2C%201858).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The queen who carried troubadour song north.","bio":"Eleanor of Aquitaine, born in 1124, was Duchess of Aquitaine in her own right from 1137 until her death in 1204, Queen of France as the wife of Louis VII, and Queen of England as the wife of Henry II. As reigning duchess she ruled alongside her husbands and later two of her sons, Richard I and John. She had inherited the House of Poitiers, whose lands covered much of south-western France, which left her richer and more powerful than almost anyone in Western Europe. She belongs in this map not as a musician but as a carrier: granddaughter of Guillaume IX, Duke of Aquitaine, she brought troubadour culture north into the French and English courts. Bernart de Ventadorn's vida places him in her service, and several of his songs are most likely addressed to her."},
  "robertiicountofartois":{"blurb":"He took Adam de la Halle to Naples.","bio":"Robert II, Count of Artois, is remembered here as a patron rather than a musician. He took Adam de la Halle into his household, and then to the Angevin court at Naples. That single act of patronage carried a trouvère's career out of the French-speaking north and into Italy, which is why Adam belongs to two musical worlds at once."},
  "johnofluxembourgkingofbohemia":{"wiki":"Bonne of Luxembourg","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bona%20Lucembursk%C3%A1.png?width=480","blurb":"Machaut's king, from chancery to Crécy.","bio":"John of Luxembourg, King of Bohemia, enters this map as a patron rather than a musician. Guillaume de Machaut served him as secretary and travelling companion from 1323 until the king's death at Crécy in 1346 — twenty-three years attached to a single household, an unusually long and unusually close arrangement for a composer of the Ars Nova. The post was a chancery job as much as a musical one: a royal secretary drafted and kept his lord's documents and went where his lord went, and the phrase 'travelling companion' suggests how much of that service was spent on the road. What it gave Machaut was a salary, a place at court, and the standing to write. The poet-composer at the centre of the Ars Nova was formed in a king's service, and Crécy ended it."},
  "jeanducdeberry":{"wiki":"John, Duke of Berry","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Duc%20de%20Berry.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The magnificent duke who kept Machaut late.","bio":"Jean, Duc de Berry, born in 1340, held Berry and Auvergne as duke and Poitiers and Montpensier as count, and was one of the great collectors of his age — known to posterity as John the Magnificent. He was a brother of King Charles V of France, of Louis I of Anjou and of Philip the Bold of Burgundy, and he acted as Regent of France between 1380 and 1388, while his nephew Charles VI was still a minor. He appears in this map as a patron of Guillaume de Machaut in the composer's later years, one of several royal supporters alongside Charles II of Navarre. That layer of competing royal patronage is what allowed a poet-composer to work at the highest level without a single fixed post. He died in 1416."},
  "cantigasdesantamaria":{"blurb":"Medieval song's single largest surviving collection.","bio":"The Cantigas de Santa María are not a person but a book: the largest single collection of medieval monophony to survive, commissioned, compiled and in part composed by Alfonso X, 'el Sabio'. Songs in honour of the Virgin, gathered at a royal court, they show how much of a repertory a monarch's patronage could preserve."},
  "mastinoiidellascala":{"blurb":"Verona's lord, where two composers competed.","bio":"Mastino II della Scala was the lord of Verona, and his court is where Jacopo da Bologna and Giovanni da Cascia competed by setting the same texts. That contest, held under a patron's roof and at his invitation, is one of the founding episodes of secular song in the Italian Trecento."},
  "luchinovisconti":{"blurb":"Milan's lord, Jacopo's patron in the 1340s.","bio":"Luchino Visconti was the Milanese lord whose court employed Jacopo da Bologna in the 1340s. In a period when composers moved between the northern Italian courts at a patron's word, households like his are the fixed points that make a Trecento career traceable at all."},
  "francescozabarella":{"wiki":"Francesco Zabarella","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Portret%20van%20Francesco%20Zabarella%2C%20RP-P-1909-4547.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The cardinal in whose house Ciconia wrote.","bio":"Francesco Zabarella, born in 1360, was an Italian jurist, theologian and canon lawyer who became a cardinal. He belongs in this map through Padua: Johannes Ciconia worked in his household there, and wrote motets for him. That relationship is a good example of how music reached the top of the church hierarchy in the years of the Ars Subtilior — not through a chapel appointment alone but through the personal service of a composer to a powerful churchman, whose learning and public standing the motets were written to honour. Padua under Zabarella was one of the places where a composer working in Italy could find both a salary and an audience of the highest rank, and Ciconia's motets are the surviving record of the arrangement. Zabarella died in 1417."},
  "jacopodelcasentino":{"blurb":"A Giotto-circle painter, and Landini's father.","bio":"Jacopo del Casentino was a Florentine painter working in Giotto's circle, and the father of Francesco Landini, the leading composer of the Italian Trecento. The line from one to the other is a reminder that Landini came out of a working artistic household, not from nowhere."},
  "heloisedargenteuil":{"wiki":"Heloise","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Heloise%20World%20Noted%20Women.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The abbess Abelard wrote hymns for.","bio":"Héloïse d'Argenteuil, born in 1101, was a French nun, philosopher, writer and scholar who became an abbess — by any measure one of the formidable intellects of the twelfth century. She enters this map through the music written for the house she led. Peter Abelard, her partner before the catastrophe that separated them, wrote a hymnal and a planctus for the Paraclete, the convent she governed afterwards. The music exists because she was there to need it and to use it, which is the ordinary way religious repertories came into being: an institution with a particular liturgy, and someone determined enough to have it supplied. Her name stands here beside a body of song she did not compose but caused to be written. She died in 1164."},
  "richardisvonstade":{"blurb":"Hildegard's closest companion, and her hardest loss.","bio":"Richardis von Stade was the nun who worked most closely with Hildegard von Bingen on the Scivias. When Richardis was transferred away, Hildegard fought the move bitterly and never fully recovered from it — a rare glimpse of the human cost inside a monastic collaboration, and of how much one woman's work depended on the presence of another."},
  "volmarofdisibodenberg":{"wiki":"Hildegard of Bingen","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Hildegard%20von%20Bingen.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The hand that wrote Hildegard down.","bio":"Volmar of Disibodenberg was Hildegard von Bingen's secretary and provost for some forty years. He took down her visions in writing and, with them, the music — the patient practical hand behind a body of work that would otherwise have stayed inside one woman's head. Hildegard (1098–1179) was a Benedictine abbess and polymath: writer, composer, philosopher, mystic and visionary, and a medical writer and practitioner too, credited by some scholars with founding the scientific study of nature in Germany. She is among the best-known composers of sacred monophony and the most recorded of them in modern times. None of that reaches us except through parchment, and the parchment was Volmar's work. Collaborations of this kind — a visionary and a scribe, forty years at the same table — are why medieval music has come down to us at all."},
  "thomastomkins":{"wiki":"Thomas Tomkins","blurb":"The last of the English virginalists.","bio":"Thomas Tomkins, born in Wales in 1572, was a composer and organist working through the late Tudor and early Stuart years, and he lived on until 1656 — long enough to outlast the world that formed him. He studied with William Byrd, and later dedicated a madrigal to his \"ancient and much reverenced master\", one of the plainest statements of discipleship in English music. He was a prominent member of the English Madrigal School, but his reach went well beyond it: he wrote skilfully for keyboard and for consort, and he counts as the last of the English virginalists, the keyboard tradition he had been trained in. Living on into the 1650s, he came to represent the end of a line rather than the start of one, and the line did not reopen after him."},
  "ercoleidestedukeofferrara":{"wiki":"Ercole I d'Este","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ercole%20I%20d'Este.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The duke who chose Josquin over Isaac.","bio":"Ercole I d'Este, born in 1431, ruled Ferrara, Modena and Reggio as duke from 1471 until he died in 1505. An Este, he strengthened the duchy's economy and its political standing, and made the court one of the great patronage machines of the Italian Renaissance. He is famous in music history for a hiring decision: in 1503 he engaged Josquin des Prez, after an agent's celebrated letter had weighed him against Isaac and set out the case for each man. He also drew Jacob Obrecht to Ferrara twice, and Obrecht died there of plague in 1505, the same year as the duke himself. Few patrons have shaped the Franco-Flemish generations so directly, and Ercole did it simply by deciding whom to pay."},
  "alfonsoidestedukeofferrara":{"wiki":"Alfonso I d'Este","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bemberg%20Fondation%20Toulouse%20-%20Portrait%20d'Alphonse%20dEste%20-%20Titien%20Inv.1053.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The soldier duke who engaged Brumel.","bio":"Alfonso I d'Este, born in 1476, succeeded his father as Duke of Ferrara, Modena and Reggio in 1505 and ruled until 1534. He is best remembered as a soldier — a prominent military leader of the Italian Wars, serving on various sides through the long conflict — but the court's musical life did not lapse when the duchy changed hands. After his father's death he engaged Antoine Brumel, who took up the Ferrara post in 1506. That continuity matters: the Franco-Flemish singers and composers who criss-crossed Europe depended on courts where one ruler's musical commitments survived into the next reign, and Ferrara under the Este was one of those courts. A duke remembered chiefly for his campaigns went on paying for composers. He died in 1534."},
  "lorenzodemedici":{"wiki":"Lorenzo de' Medici","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bottega%20di%20Agnolo%20di%20Cosimo%2C%20detto%20il%20Bronzino%20(Firenze%201503-1572)%20-%20Ritratto%20di%20Lorenzo%20il%20Magnifico%20-%20865%20-%20Uffizi%20Gallery%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Florence's ruler; Isaac set his verses.","bio":"Lorenzo de' Medici, born in 1449 and known as Lorenzo the Magnificent, ruled the Florentine Republic in fact if not in title, and was the most powerful patron of culture in Renaissance Italy. A banker, politician, writer and poet, he kept the Italic League in equilibrium and sponsored artists including Botticelli and Michelangelo. His resistance to Pope Sixtus IV's territorial ambitions made him the target of the Pazzi conspiracy of 1478, which cost his brother Giuliano his life. In music his key relationship was with Heinrich Isaac, who taught the Medici children and set Lorenzo's own verses — patronage in its most intimate form, with the ruler supplying the words and the household supplying the composer. Lorenzo died in 1492, at the close of Florence's golden age."},
  "maximilianiholyromanemperor":{"wiki":"Maximilian I, Holy Roman Emperor","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Albrecht%20D%C3%BCrer%20-%20Portrait%20of%20Maximilian%20I%20-%20Google%20Art%20Project.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The emperor who kept Isaac from 1497.","bio":"Maximilian I, born in 1459, held the title King of the Romans from 1486; in 1508 he became Holy Roman Emperor, and ruled until his death in 1519. No pope ever crowned him — the Venetians blocked the road to Rome — so at Trento in 1508 he proclaimed himself elected emperor, a break with tradition that Julius II later recognised. Frederick III's only surviving son by Eleanor of Portugal, he ran a joint government with his father from 1486 until Frederick's death in 1493. For this map his significance is a single appointment: from 1497 Heinrich Isaac served him as court composer, which carried the Franco-Flemish style into the imperial lands and made the emperor's chapel one of the conduits by which that international manner spread across German-speaking Europe."},
  "albrechtvdukeofbavaria":{"wiki":"Albert V, Duke of Bavaria","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Albrecht%20V%20Bayern%20Jugendbild.jpg?width=480","blurb":"He paid for Europe's costliest chapel.","bio":"Albrecht V, born in Munich in 1528, the son of William IV and of Maria Jacobäa of Baden, became Duke of Bavaria in 1550 and ruled until his death in 1579. His importance to music is straightforward and expensive: he maintained what was reckoned the most costly chapel in Europe, and he did it in order to keep Orlande de Lassus in Munich. The investment worked. With Lassus in his service the Bavarian court chapel became one of the great musical establishments of the later sixteenth century, and Munich became a centre of the international style rather than a provincial capital. Few patrons have bought so much musical history with a payroll, and the Bavarian court's standing as a musical power long outlasted the duke who paid for it."},
  "popejuliusiii":{"blurb":"The pope who promoted Palestrina twice.","bio":"Pope Julius III is the patron who set Giovanni Pierluigi da Palestrina on his path, appointing him first to the Cappella Giulia and then to the Sistine Chapel. Those two posts placed the young composer at the centre of papal music-making, and the Roman School as we know it follows from them."},
  "philipiiofspain":{"wiki":"Philip II of Spain","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Portrait%20of%20Philip%20II%20of%20Spain%20by%20Sofonisba%20Anguissola%20-%20002b.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The king who took his organist everywhere.","bio":"Philip II, born in 1527, ruled as King of Spain from 1556, of Naples and Sicily from 1554, and of Portugal from 1580; he had been Duke of Milan since 1540, and from 1555 held the lordship of the Netherlands' Seventeen Provinces. Through his marriage to Mary I he was also King of England and Ireland, from 1554 until her death in 1558. In Spain he was called Philip the Prudent. In this map he matters as the employer of Antonio de Cabezón, his court organist, whom he took with him on his journeys through Italy, the Netherlands and England. That travelling appointment carried Spanish keyboard music north with the court, and set a Spanish player in front of the musicians of three countries. Philip died in 1598."},
  "elizabethi":{"wiki":"Elizabeth I","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Darnley%20stage%203.jpg?width=480","blurb":"She gave Tallis and Byrd their monopoly.","bio":"Elizabeth I, born in 1533, came to the throne of England and Ireland on 17 November 1558 and held it until she died in 1603 — the last of the Tudors and the longest-reigning of them, and the ruler whose reign gave the Elizabethan era its name. Her importance to English music is concrete rather than decorative. In 1575 she granted Thomas Tallis and William Byrd a twenty-one-year monopoly on printed music, effectively handing the country's music publishing to two composers and shaping what got into print for a generation. She also protected Byrd, a recusant Catholic, at the very heart of her Chapel Royal, letting him keep his place and his faith at a time when that combination was dangerous. Without those two decisions the English repertory would look markedly thinner."},
  "williamshakespeare":{"wiki":"William Shakespeare","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Shakespeare.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The neighbour whose lyric Morley set.","bio":"William Shakespeare, born in 1564 and dead in 1616, was an English actor, poet and playwright, commonly called the greatest of all writers in English: 154 sonnets, some thirty-nine plays, three long narrative poems and a handful of other verses, translated into every major living tongue and performed more often than any other dramatist's. He appears in this map not as a musician but as a source and a neighbour. He and the composer Thomas Morley were parish neighbours in Bishopsgate, and Morley set 'It was a lover and his lass' for 'As You Like It' — one of the closest documented meetings between the Elizabethan stage and the Elizabethan madrigal. The songs written into the plays gave English composers texts that have never stopped being set."},
  "nicolavicentino":{"wiki":"Nicola Vicentino","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nicola%20Vicentino.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Microtonal inventor, loser of the Roman debate.","bio":"Nicola Vicentino, born in 1511, was an Italian composer and music theorist, and among the most progressive musicians of his age. He argued that modern music could recover the ancient genera, and built a microtonal keyboard capable of playing the intervals his theory required — an instrument well ahead of anything the ordinary practice of his time needed. In June 1551 he took the argument into public, debating Vicente Lusitano in Rome over whether contemporary music did in fact use the ancient genera. The judges found for Lusitano, and Vicentino lost. He kept working regardless, as composer, theorist and inventor, and died in 1576. The defeat has not stopped later musicians from returning to his tuning ideas, which read now less like an eccentricity than like an experiment made too early."},
  "bernardopasquini":{"wiki":"Bernardo Pasquini","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bernardo%20Pasquini.png?width=480","blurb":"Rome's keyboard master between Frescobaldi and Scarlatti.","bio":"Bernardo Pasquini, born in 1637, wrote operas, oratorios and cantatas as well as keyboard music, and was a renowned virtuoso organist and harpsichordist. He is usually placed as the most important Italian composer for the harpsichord in the years between Girolamo Frescobaldi and Domenico Scarlatti — the link that keeps that line unbroken across two generations — while also contributing substantially to opera and oratorio. His teaching carried further than his own playing did: in Rome he taught Domenico Zipoli, who left in 1717 for the Jesuit missions of Paraguay, taking a Roman keyboard training across the Atlantic and into South America. Through that single pupil, Pasquini's Rome reaches into the mission churches of the New World, and a Roman teaching room becomes part of the story of music in South America. He died in 1710."},
  "saintecolombe":{"wiki":"Monsieur de Sainte-Colombe","blurb":"The viol master who taught Marais.","bio":"Sainte-Colombe, born about 1640 and dead by about 1700, was a French violist and composer celebrated in his own time as a master of the viola da gamba. In his 'Traité de la viole' of 1687 Jean Rousseau credited him with adding the seventh string to the bass viol, tuned to a low A — an alteration that extended the instrument's range downward and changed what French composers could write for it. His pupil was Marin Marais, and the story attached to that lesson, given by Titon du Tillet and repeated ever since, is that the master stopped teaching once the pupil had overtaken him. Whether or not it happened that way, the line from Sainte-Colombe to Marais is the spine of the French viol tradition."},
  "heinrichscheidemann":{"wiki":"Heinrich Scheidemann","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Heinrich%20Scheidemann.jpg?width=480","blurb":"North Germany's organ voice before Buxtehude.","bio":"Heinrich Scheidemann, born in 1596, was northern Germany's leading organ composer through the early and middle decades of the seventeenth century. He was one of the Hamburg organists trained by Jan Pieterszoon Sweelinck, and he brought that training home with him — the mechanism by which the Sweelinck manner became the north German organ style. He worked at the Katharinenkirche in Hamburg and taught Johann Adam Reincken, who succeeded him at that church and kept the succession running. Scheidemann stands as a forerunner of Dieterich Buxtehude and, further along the same line, of J. S. Bach. Much of what the north German organ school later became had passed through his hands first, carried out of a teacher's studio into a city church and out again through his own pupil. He died in 1663."},
  "johannchristophbach":{"wiki":"Johann Christoph Bach","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Johann%20Christoph%20Bach.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Bach that J. S. called profound.","bio":"Johann Christoph Bach, born in 1642, was a German organist and composer working in the Baroque, and the elder cousin of Johann Sebastian Bach, who in his family genealogy called him \"the profound composer\" — a judgement that implies a reputation well beyond the household. He should not be confused with Johann Sebastian's son Johann Christoph Friedrich Bach. The name in fact recurs across the family, and the connections drawn around it here reach a second bearer of it: Johann Sebastian's elder brother, also Johann Christoph, a pupil of Johann Pachelbel who took the ten-year-old in and gave him his first keyboard training, passing on the Pachelbel line at first hand. Between the two of them, the name marks both the family's admired elder composer and the brother who taught its greatest member. He died in 1703."},
  "juanhidalgo":{"wiki":"Juan Hidalgo de Polanco","blurb":"The father of Spanish opera and zarzuela.","bio":"Juan Hidalgo de Polanco, born in 1614, was a Spanish composer and harpist, and became the Hispanic world's most influential musician of his generation. He supplied the music for the earliest two operas ever written in Spanish, and is widely regarded as the father of both Spanish opera and the zarzuela — the vernacular form that grew out of the same Madrid stages. His reach extended across the Atlantic. Tomás de Torrejón y Velasco later set the same Calderón text that Hidalgo had set in Madrid, and the result was the first opera composed in the Americas: a Madrid model taken up on the far side of the ocean. Hidalgo died in 1685, having given the Spanish-speaking world an operatic repertory in its own language."},
  "louiscouperin":{"wiki":"Louis Couperin","blurb":"The Paris keyboard master dead at thirty-five.","bio":"Louis Couperin, born at Chaumes-en-Brie in 1626, moved to Paris in 1650–51, helped there by Jacques Champion de Chambonnières, and was quickly reckoned among the city's most prominent musicians — harpsichordist, organist and violist at once. He served as organist of St Gervais and as a musician at court. The decisive outside influence on him came from Johann Jacob Froberger, whose visits to Paris lie behind the emergence of the French unmeasured prelude, the free, barless keyboard writing that became a signature of the tradition. Couperin's career was cut short by his death in 1661 at the age of thirty-five; what survives of his work is enough to show why Paris had rated him so highly, and so quickly, in the decade he was given."},
  "ferdinandoidemedici":{"wiki":"Ferdinando I de' Medici","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Pulzone%2C%20Scipione%20-%20Ferdinando%20I%20de'%20Medici%2C%20granduca%20di%20Toscana%20-%201590.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The grand duke behind Peri's 'Euridice'.","bio":"Ferdinando I de' Medici, born in 1549, held the grand duchy of Tuscany from 1587 until his death in February 1609, coming to the throne from the Church after the death of his elder brother Francesco I. He enlarged Tuscany's culture and its economy alike, extending the Naviglio canal and setting an irrigation scheme going in the Val di Chiana, and he backed Henry IV of France with money after the assassination of Henry III. His place in this map rests on the Medici wedding festivities of 1600, the occasion for which Jacopo Peri's 'Euridice' was written and presented — opera arriving not through a theatre but through a dynastic celebration. Court spectacle, in other words, is where the new form found its first great occasion."},
  "vincenzoigonzagadukeofmantua":{"wiki":"Vincenzo I Gonzaga","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Frans%20Pourbus%20the%20younger%20-%20Portrait%20of%20Vincenzo%20Gonzaga%2C%20Duke%20of%20Mantua%2C%20wearing%20armour%20and%20the%20Order%20of%20the%20Golden%20Fleece.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Monteverdi's duke for twenty-two years.","bio":"Vincenzo I Gonzaga, born in 1562, ruled Mantua and Montferrat from 1587 until his death in 1612, and was an avid collector and patron of the arts. His household employed Claudio Monteverdi for twenty-two years, a long service that ended in dismissal. It was for this court that 'L'Orfeo' was written. The relationship shows patronage at its most double-edged: the duke's resources and appetite for spectacle made 'L'Orfeo' possible, and the same court ended by dismissing the composer who had made it. Mantua under Vincenzo is where the seconda pratica found the means to be staged, and where the new manner was given singers, players and a stage on which to be proved. Vincenzo's court is the reason 'L'Orfeo' exists at all."},
  "cardinalpietroottoboni":{"wiki":"Arcangelo Corelli","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Arcangelo%20Corelli%2C%20portrait%20by%20Hugh%20Howard%20(1697)%20-%202.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The cardinal whose palace housed Corelli.","bio":"Cardinal Pietro Ottoboni was one of the central musical patrons of Baroque Rome, and his household is where several careers converge. Arcangelo Corelli lived in the cardinal's palace, the Cancelleria, and led the Monday concerts there for two decades — an arrangement that gave a violinist a standing platform for twenty years. Corelli's music proved decisive: it was key to the development of the sonata and the concerto as modern genres, it established the pre-eminence of the violin, and it is where modern tonality and functional harmony first cohere. Alessandro Scarlatti worked in the same Roman orbit, and the patronage network Ottoboni anchored carried the young George Frideric Handel through his Italian years, from 1706 to 1710. One cardinal's payroll, in other words, sits underneath a great deal of what Roman Baroque music became."},
  "mariabarbaraofportugal":{"wiki":"Barbara of Portugal","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Barbaradebragan%C3%A7a.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The queen Scarlatti wrote the sonatas for.","bio":"Maria Barbara of Portugal, born in 1711, was an Infanta of Portugal who became Queen consort of Spain in 1746 through her marriage to Ferdinand VI, and held that place until she died in 1758. Her position on this map begins earlier, in the Lisbon of her childhood, where Domenico Scarlatti taught her the keyboard. She kept him: when she went to Spain he followed, and the pupil ended as the employer of her old teacher. The sonatas were written for her — a body of keyboard music shaped to one player's hands, which makes her one of the very few patrons whose taste can still be heard in the notes themselves. She was known for her close relationship with Ferdinand and for her influence over affairs of state, and she was a patron of the arts as well as a musician."},
  "alexandrelerichedelapoupliniere":{"wiki":"Alexandre Le Riche de La Poupelinière","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/La%20Poupelini%C3%A8re%20par%20Balechou.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The tax farmer who kept Rameau's orchestra.","bio":"Alexandre Le Riche de La Pouplinière (1693-1762) was a fermier général — a tax farmer of enormous wealth, the only son of a fermier général father — and one of the greatest private patrons of music of the eighteenth century. Enlightenment patronage in its fullest form: he kept a household open to musicians, writers and artists, lodging the finest Italian violinists and singers under his roof and feeding them at his own table. At the centre of it stood a private orchestra that Jean-François Marmontel reckoned the best of its day. Jean-Philippe Rameau directed it for twenty-two years and lived in the house for more than twenty of them; Johann Stamitz took the band over afterwards, and then François-Joseph Gossec. He was a banker, a writer and an art collector besides, but it is the orchestra he is remembered for."},
  "dukeofchandos":{"blurb":"The patron behind Handel's Cannons years.","bio":"The Duke of Chandos appears here as an employer. In 1717 and 1718 he kept George Frideric Handel at Cannons, and out of that residency came the Chandos Anthems and Acis and Galatea. Two years of one man's patronage, and a body of music that still carries the patron's name rather than the place's."},
  "queenanne":{"wiki":"Anne of Denmark","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Anne%20of%20Denmark%20in%20mourning.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The queen whose pension steadied Handel.","bio":"Anne of Denmark (1574-1619) married James VI in August 1589 and was Queen of Scotland from that day; when the Scottish and English crowns were joined in March 1603 she became Queen of England and Ireland as well, and remained so until she died in 1619. She belongs on this map as a patron rather than a musician — a consort and aristocrat whose philanthropy and support of the arts were part of the ordinary business of a royal household. Her connection here is financial. The record credits her with granting George Frideric Handel an annual pension, one that survived a change of dynasty, so that a composer kept a guaranteed royal income across a shift of ruling house. Arrangements of that kind decide whether a career continues, and they rarely appear in the story of the music itself."},
  "frederickthegreat":{"wiki":"Frederick the Great","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Friedrich%20der%20Gro%C3%9Fe%20(1781%20or%201786)%20-%20Google%20Art%20Project.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The flute-playing king who set Bach's theme.","bio":"Frederick II of Prussia (1712-1786), known as Frederick the Great and nicknamed Old Fritz, ruled from 1740 until his death — the last Hohenzollern to be titled King in Prussia, and King of Prussia after he annexed Royal Prussia in 1772. The victories of the Silesian wars, the rebuilding of the Prussian army and the First Partition of Poland sit alongside his backing of the arts and of Enlightenment thought. He played the flute seriously: Johann Joachim Quantz was his teacher and the best-paid musician at Potsdam, while Carl Philipp Emanuel Bach spent nearly thirty underpaid years there as court harpsichordist. In 1747, at Potsdam, Frederick gave Johann Sebastian Bach the theme that became the Musical Offering. Few rulers have left so direct a mark on a single piece of music."},
  "charlesii":{"wiki":"Charles II of England","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sir%20Peter%20Lely%20(1618-80)%20-%20Charles%20II%20(1630-1685)%20-%20RCIN%20405672%20-%20Royal%20Collection.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The king who sent Humfrey abroad.","bio":"Charles II (1630-1685) held Scotland from 1649 to 1651, and then, from the Restoration of the monarchy in 1660 until his death, ruled England, Scotland and Ireland. He gives his name to the musical culture that resumed with him — Restoration England — and he spent on it. He sent Pelham Humfrey to France and Italy at royal expense, so that an English musician learned continental manners abroad on the Crown's money and brought them home. It is a small piece of state spending with consequences out of all proportion to its size: a court that wanted its music up to date, and was willing to pay for the education that made it so. A politician and a serious collector of art, Charles understood display as an instrument of rule."},
  "galileogalilei":{"blurb":"The son of Vincenzo Galilei.","bio":"Galileo Galilei belongs on this map through his family. He was the son of Vincenzo Galilei, whose experiments on the tension of strings count among the first quantitative work in acoustics — measurement applied to sound, and the reason a name from outside music has a place on this map at all."},
  "giuliostrozzi":{"wiki":"Giulio Strozzi","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/T%20Tinelli%20Retrato%20del%20poeta%20Giulio%20Strozzi%20Galer%C3%ADa%20de%20los%20Uffizi.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The librettist who launched his daughter.","bio":"Giulio Strozzi (1583-1652) was a Venetian poet, playwright and librettist, one of the writers who supplied words to the composers of Venice. His libretti were set by Claudio Monteverdi, Francesco Cavalli, Francesco Manelli and Francesco Sacrati, which places him at the elbow of nearly every important composer working in the city in his lifetime. He sometimes published under the pseudonym Luigi Zorzisto. His most consequential act, though, was on behalf of Barbara Strozzi: she was his daughter, probably natural rather than adoptive, and he wrote texts for her, then founded an academy expressly to show her off. Without that deliberate machinery — words written to order, an audience assembled on purpose — her work would very likely never have left the house. He was a writer who used his standing to build a career that was not his own."},
  "annamagdalenabach":{"wiki":"Anna Magdalena Bach","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Anna%20Magdalena%20Bach%202.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The singer whose hand preserved Bach.","bio":"Anna Magdalena Bach (1701-1760) sang professionally in Germany — a court musician engaged for her voice, not a gifted amateur — and married Johann Sebastian Bach as his second wife. Her importance to the music runs in two directions at once. She was a working performer in her own right, and she was the copyist of a great deal of the music that survives. Much of what we now have of her husband's output reaches us in her handwriting, made in a household where copying was the only way a piece could be performed, taught or kept. That is an unglamorous kind of authorship, and an absolute one: what she wrote out survived, and manuscripts nobody troubled to copy generally did not. She stands in the Bach story as a musician and as the reason so much of it is still legible."},
  "moliere":{"wiki":"Molière","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Pierre%20Mignard%20-%20Portrait%20de%20Jean-Baptiste%20Poquelin%20dit%20Moli%C3%A8re%20(1622-1673)%20-%20Google%20Art%20Project%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Lully's collaborator, until the break over Psyché.","bio":"Jean-Baptiste Poquelin (1622-1673), who acted under the name Molière, ranks among the great writers of the French language — so completely that French is sometimes called the language of Molière. He was a playwright, actor, poet and satirist, and the work that survives runs from farce and comedy through tragicomedy to the comédie-ballet; the Comédie-Française still stages him more often than any other author. The comédie-ballet is where he enters this map. He made ten of them with Jean-Baptiste Lully, a form that stitched spoken comedy to sung and danced episodes and belonged to nobody else in Europe. The partnership ended badly, in a bitter break over Psyché. Between them, though, the two of them decided what French theatre with music sounded like."},
  "philippequinault":{"wiki":"Philippe Quinault","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Philippe%20Quinault%20-%20Versailles%20MV%202922.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Lully's librettist for eleven tragédies en musique.","bio":"Philippe Quinault (1635-1688) was a French dramatist and librettist, and the writer who gave French opera its first native shape. He worked as a playwright, poet and choreographer, but his standing rests on a single collaboration: he was the librettist of eleven tragédies en musique for Jean-Baptiste Lully. That is not a footnote to Lully's career but half of it. The tragédie en musique was France's answer to Italian opera — sung declamation fitted to the rhythm of French verse — and its verbal architecture is Quinault's. Composers and librettists elsewhere drifted apart into hierarchy, the words serving the music; here two men worked as a unit across eleven full-length works, and the results set the pattern that French opera would argue with for a century afterwards."},
  "voltaire":{"wiki":"Voltaire","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nicolas%20de%20Largilli%C3%A8re%20-%20Portrait%20de%20Voltaire%20(1694-1778)%20en%201718%20-%20P208%20-%20Mus%C3%A9e%20Carnavalet%20-%202.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Rameau's brilliant, quarrelsome stage collaborator.","bio":"François-Marie Arouet (1694-1778) took the pen name Voltaire and became the most quotable philosopher, poet, historian and essayist of the French Enlightenment — witty, relentless against Christianity and against slavery, and an early campaigner for free expression, religious liberty and a state kept separate from the church. He is on this map because he wrote for the stage with music in mind. He and Jean-Philippe Rameau made several stage works together and sustained a long, quarrelsome working friendship — two difficult, formidably intelligent men who needed each other and said so rarely. The pairing matters because it puts a leading composer inside the Enlightenment's central argument rather than beside it: opera as a place where ideas about nature, reason and the state could be staged. Voltaire's music, such as it was, was written by other people."},
  "jeanjacquesrousseau":{"wiki":"Jean-Jacques Rousseau","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jean-Jacques%20Rousseau%20(painted%20portrait).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The philosopher who declared French unsingable.","bio":"Jean-Jacques Rousseau (1712-1778), born in Geneva, was a philosopher, novelist and composer whose political thought shaped the Enlightenment across Europe, fed into the French Revolution, and altered modern ideas about politics, economics and education. He was also a practising musician and musicologist, and he fought in public about music with real ferocity. In the Querelle des Bouffons he argued that the French language was fundamentally unsingable and that Jean-Philippe Rameau's theoretical system was a fraud — an attack on a composer and on the theory underwriting French music at the same time. The spark was the 1752 Paris revival of Giovanni Battista Pergolesi's La serva padrona, whose lightness and speed gave the Italian party its evidence. The quarrel was about taste, and also about what kind of country France meant to be."},
  "florianleopoldgassmann":{"wiki":"Florian Leopold Gassmann","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Florian%20Leopold%20Gassmann%20by%20Balzer.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Salieri's teacher, Vienna's comic-opera craftsman.","bio":"Florian Leopold Gassmann (1729-1774) was a German-speaking Bohemian composer who worked across the join between the baroque and classical styles, and among the leading writers of dramma giocoso in the years just before Mozart. He was a conductor and an organist as well. His comic operas were the immediate model against which the next generation measured itself, the working repertory audiences knew before they knew Mozart. His most lasting act, though, was an act of recognition: in 1766 he brought a sixteen-year-old Venetian named Antonio Salieri to Vienna and took charge of his training. Everything Salieri became, and everything he in turn handed on to his own pupils, begins with an established composer deciding that a boy from Venice was worth the journey."},
  "philippedorleansducdechartres":{"wiki":"Élisabeth Marguerite d'Orléans","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gascar%20-%20Isabelle%20d'Orl%C3%A9ans%20-%20Museum%20of%20John%20Paul%20II%20Collection.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The duke who gave Saint-Georges a theatre.","bio":"Philippe d'Orléans, Duc de Chartres (1646-1696), was a French aristocrat, and he appears on this map for a single appointment. He made Joseph Bologne, Chevalier de Saint-Georges, the director of his private theatre at the Palais-Royal. What that meant in practice was a company, a stage and a payroll placed under the direction of a musician, inside one of the grandest households in the kingdom — the resources of a princely establishment handed to a Black composer at a moment when very few doors of that kind opened at all. Private theatres were where reputations could be made before the public houses would take a risk, and a patron's choice of director worked as a public verdict on a musician's standing. That verdict is what the duke contributed, and it is why his name sits here."},
  "georgeiv":{"wiki":"George IV","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/George%20IVcoronation.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The prince who paid for Bridgetower's schooling.","bio":"George IV (1762-1830) was King of the United Kingdom and of Hanover from January 1820 until his death, having already governed as Prince Regent from 1811 through his father George III's final illness. He was a serious art collector and a conspicuous patron of the arts, and that appetite made him useful to musicians. As Prince of Wales he took up the young George Bridgetower, paid for the boy's education and placed him in the orchestra at Brighton — a training and a salaried seat in a working band, given by the heir to the throne to a boy with no other obvious route to either. Patronage of that kind was capricious, and the prince's motives were probably closer to collecting than to conscience. The effect, all the same, was a musician made and a career begun."},
  "domjoaoviofportugal":{"wiki":"John VI of Portugal","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Retrato%20D.%20Joao%20(Henri-Fran%C3%A7ois%20Riesener).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The king who raised, then sidelined, Nunes Garcia.","bio":"Dom João VI (1767-1826), the king they called the Clement, ruled the United Kingdom of Portugal, Brazil and the Algarves between 1816 and 1825; once Brazil's independence was recognised he held the imperial title in Brazil and the Portuguese crown alike until he died in 1826. His court crossed the Atlantic, and its chapel went with it. In Rio de Janeiro he kept José Maurício Nunes Garcia as mestre de capela to the court in exile, which for a few years put the music of a royal household newly arrived from Europe into one musician's hands. Then he gradually displaced him. The same patron who raised Nunes Garcia to that position is the one who eased him out of it, and the record of the reign holds both halves of the story."},
  "dukeofmontagu":{"blurb":"The household that educated Ignatius Sancho.","bio":"The Duke of Montagu stands here for a household rather than a career in music. It was the Montagu household that had Ignatius Sancho educated, and the Montagus who later set him up as a shopkeeper in Westminster — first the schooling, then the premises, which between them gave a musician a footing of his own."},
  "marieantoinette":{"wiki":"Marie Antoinette","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Louise%20Elisabeth%20Vig%C3%A9e-Lebrun%20-%20Marie-Antoinette%20dit%20%C2%AB%20%C3%A0%20la%20Rose%20%C2%BB%20-%20Google%20Art%20Project.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The queen who imposed Gluck on Paris.","bio":"Marie Antoinette (1755-1793) married Louis XVI and was Queen of France from May 1774 until the monarchy fell in 1792 — the last queen before the Revolution, and one of the few consorts whose musical taste changed a repertory. Before Paris she had grown up in the Habsburg court at Vienna, where Christoph Willibald Gluck was among her teachers. When she arrived in France she used her position on his behalf, pressing and effectively forcing his operas onto the Paris stage against the resistance of an establishment with its own composers and its own habits. Gluck's reform of opera therefore reached its most influential audience because a former pupil had become queen. It is the clearest case on this map of a court connection deciding what the public got to hear."},
  "princenikolausesterhazy":{"wiki":"Nikolaus I, Prince Esterházy","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nikolaus%20Esterhazy.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The prince who kept Haydn thirty years.","bio":"Nikolaus I, Prince Esterházy (1714-1790), was a Hungarian prince of the famous Esterházy family, called the Magnificent for his palace-building, his extravagant dress and his appetite for opera and grand musical productions. What secures his place here is that he employed Joseph Haydn. For some thirty years Haydn ran the music at Eszterháza, contracted to supply whatever the prince wanted to hear and given the resources of the household to perform it. The isolation was the point, as Haydn himself explained: cut off from the world, he was forced to become original. Modern ears hear a servant's contract; what it actually bought was a laboratory, permanently staffed, in which one composer could try an idea and hear it the same week. Few patrons have got such value from a payroll."},
  "barongottfriedvanswieten":{"wiki":"Gottfried van Swieten","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gottried%20van%20Swieten%20(c%20clavereau).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The baron who gave Vienna Bach.","bio":"Gottfried Freiherr van Swieten (1733-1803) was a Dutch-born diplomat, librarian and imperial official in Habsburg service, a keen amateur musician and a librettist — and the most consequential private patron in Classical Vienna. His Sunday mornings, given over to the music of Bach and Handel, permanently changed the way Mozart wrote counterpoint. For Joseph Haydn he did something more direct still, commissioning The Creation and The Seasons and preparing their translated texts himself. Beethoven dedicated his First Symphony to him. Mozart, Haydn and Beethoven were all altered by the enthusiasms of one well-placed amateur, which is a remarkable return on a taste for old music and a habit of asking people round. His patronage was less about money than about repertory: he decided what a generation heard."},
  "emperorjosephii":{"wiki":"Joseph II, Holy Roman Emperor","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Anton%20von%20Maron%20006.png?width=480","blurb":"The emperor who commissioned Die Entführung.","bio":"Joseph II (1741-1790) became Holy Roman Emperor in August 1765 and took sole charge of the Habsburg monarchy in 1780, ruling until he died. He was the eldest of Empress Maria Theresa and Emperor Francis I's sons, and a brother of Marie Antoinette and Leopold II, and the first ruler of the joined houses of Habsburg and Lorraine. On this map he is Mozart's commissioner and, eventually, his employer: he ordered Die Entführung, and in time gave the composer a modest court salary as well — a retainer that steadied a career without securing it. The relationship has the standard shape of patronage in this period: a monarch with money and a theatre, a composer who needed the work, and an opera that has outlived the reign which paid for it."},
  "princekarllichnowsky":{"wiki":"Karl Alois, Prince Lichnowsky","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Prince%20Carl%20Lichnowsky.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The prince Beethoven walked out on.","bio":"Karl Alois, Prince Lichnowsky (1761-1814), was the second of his line to hold the title, and he served the imperial Austrian court as a chamberlain; what he is remembered for now is his patronage of music, and his dealings with Mozart and with Beethoven. With Beethoven the arrangement was unusually generous: he housed the young composer and paid him an annuity — money that arrived whether or not anything was written, which is precisely what freed a musician from having to hold a court or church post. It also freed him to be rude to the man paying. In 1806 Beethoven walked out of Lichnowsky's house, and the break has been retold ever since as the moment a composer stopped being a servant. What is easy to miss is that the independence Beethoven asserted had been paid for by the person he asserted it against."},
  "archdukerudolphofaustria":{"wiki":"Archduke Rudolf of Austria","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Rudolf-habsburg-olmuetz.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Beethoven's pupil, patron and greatest dedicatee.","bio":"Rudolph Johann Joseph Rainier, Archduke of Austria and Prince Royal of Hungary and Bohemia (1788-1831), was a Habsburg-Lorraine prince who entered the church, taking the archbishopric of Olomouc in 1819 and a cardinal's hat in the same year. He was also Ludwig van Beethoven's pupil and, across many years, his most important patron. The two roles together produced the densest run of dedications in the repertory: the Archduke Trio, the Emperor Concerto and the Missa solemnis all carry his name. A patron who is also a student changes the usual arrangement — the money runs one way and the instruction the other — and what emerges looks less like service than like a working friendship between very unequal men. Rudolph composed himself, but his lasting contribution to music was the room he made for somebody else's."},
  "countrazumovsky":{"wiki":"Ignaz Schuppanzigh","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Schuppanzigh-ignaz-in-1926-08-06-radio-wien-jg02-nr45-s1991.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The count who commissioned Beethoven's Op. 59.","bio":"Count Razumovsky belongs to this map as a patron with a standing ensemble. He kept a private string quartet led by the violinist Ignaz Schuppanzigh, founded late in 1808 and generally reckoned the first professional string quartet there had ever been; until then quartet music had been the business of amateurs, or of professionals who came together for an occasion and afterwards dispersed. From Ludwig van Beethoven he commissioned the quartets published as Op. 59, which have carried his name ever since. The two facts belong together. Schuppanzigh's players premiered many of Beethoven's quartets, the late ones included, and having paid musicians who rehearsed as a permanent group is what made writing at that level of difficulty a practical proposition rather than a private gamble."},
  "johannwolfgangvongoethe":{"wiki":"Johann Wolfgang von Goethe","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Goethe%20(Stieler%201828).jpg?width=480","blurb":"German literature's giant, and Mendelssohn's old friend.","bio":"Johann Wolfgang von Goethe (1749-1832) was a German polymath — poet, playwright, novelist, critic, theatre director, statesman and natural scientist, with treatises on botany, anatomy and colour alongside the plays and the verse — and the most influential writer the German language has produced. Music surrounds him at both ends of a long life. Anna Amalia of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel set his Erwin und Elmire and brought him to Weimar, the move that made the rest of his career possible. Much later he formed a friendship with Felix Mendelssohn across sixty years of age difference, the boy spending a fortnight in 1821 playing Bach to him. For the composers of the Lied his poems were the raw material of an entire genre; for this map he is the writer whose words German music kept setting."},
  "thomasgainsborough":{"blurb":"The painter who fixed Sancho's face.","bio":"Thomas Gainsborough enters this map as a painter rather than a musician. At Bath in 1768 he painted Ignatius Sancho, in a sitting reported to have lasted an hour and forty minutes — under two hours' work, and the reason a musician who might have left no likeness at all still has a face in the record."},
  "laurencesterne":{"blurb":"The writer whose Sancho letters fed abolition.","bio":"Laurence Sterne appears here through his correspondence with Ignatius Sancho. Their exchange of letters on slavery was published and became an abolitionist text — a private argument between two writers turned into public evidence, and one of the rare places on this map where a musician is heard in his own words rather than through his music."},
  "lorenzodaponte":{"wiki":"Lorenzo Da Ponte","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Lorenzo%20da%20Ponte.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Mozart's librettist for the three great operas.","bio":"Lorenzo Da Ponte (1749-1838) was a Venetian-born poet and Roman Catholic priest who made his living writing libretti, and who ended his long life as an American. Twenty-eight operas by eleven composers were set to his words, but three of them account for his fame: The Marriage of Figaro in 1786, Don Giovanni in 1787 and Così fan tutte in 1790, all with Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart. What those three share is a quality of characterisation that gives a composer something to set — people who change their minds mid-scene, ensembles built so that several intentions can sound at once. That is a librettist's contribution rather than a composer's, and it is why the three are always named together. The partnership remains the standard case for the proposition that an opera's words decide how far its music can go."},
  "emanuelschikaneder":{"wiki":"Emanuel Schikaneder","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Emanuel%20Schikaneder.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The impresario who wrote The Magic Flute's libretto.","bio":"Emanuel Schikaneder (1751-1812) worked as an impresario, dramatist, actor, singer and composer in the German theatre — a man of the popular stage rather than the court, and, in Peter Branscombe's judgement, among the most gifted men of the theatre in his generation. He wrote the libretto of Die Zauberflöte, produced it and sang in it himself: author, manager and performer of the same piece, which is rarer than it sounds. He also built the Theater an der Wien. Salieri, Haydn and Beethoven were all people he knew and worked with, besides Mozart, which places him at the practical centre of Viennese theatrical life for a generation. The Magic Flute is often discussed as though it descended from philosophy; its words were made by a working showman who knew exactly what an audience would sit still for."},
  "marcosportugal":{"wiki":"Marcos Portugal","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Marcos%20Portugal.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The court composer who displaced Nunes Garcia.","bio":"Marcos António da Fonseca Portugal (1762-1830), who was known in Italy as Marco Portogallo, worked as a composer, organist and music teacher in Portugal and later in Brazil. His comic operas reached the stages of Italy and of Europe more widely across the 1790s and the early nineteenth century, though at home, in Portugal and Brazil, it was his sacred music that audiences actually knew. He arrived in Brazil in 1811 as the Portuguese court's own composer, and his arrival pushed José Maurício Nunes Garcia out of the principal musical posts. The rivalry was less a contest of talent than a demonstration of how colonial hierarchy worked: a European reputation outranked a local one on local ground. His Brazilian years are inseparable from the man he displaced, which is a hard thing for a composer's name to carry."},
  "archbishopcolloredo":{"wiki":"Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Wolfgang-amadeus-mozart%201.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The employer Mozart could not bear.","bio":"Archbishop Colloredo was Mozart's employer in Salzburg, and he survives in the record as the antagonist of the most famous departure in music. In 1781 his steward dismissed the composer with a kick, and the Salzburg service ended there for good. Nothing about the arrangement was unusual for its time: a churchman ran an establishment, a musician was engaged to serve it, and the terms of that service were the employer's to set. What made this particular parting a landmark was the temperament on the other side of it, and the fact that the man dismissed had somewhere else to go. Colloredo has been written about ever since almost entirely through the eyes of the musician he lost, which is the fate of any patron remembered for a quarrel. He appears here as the household a genius could not stay in."},
  "jeandelphinalard":{"wiki":"Jean-Delphin Alard","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Delphin%20Alard.png?width=480","blurb":"The Conservatoire violinist who trained José White.","bio":"Jean-Delphin Alard (1815-1888) taught, composed and played the violin in France, and stood as one of the pillars of the Paris Conservatoire's violin school in the middle of the century. Jean-Baptiste Vuillaume was his father-in-law, and Pablo de Sarasate came through his class. So did José White Lafitte, who enrolled at the Conservatoire and took the premier prix in 1856, a single year after arriving — about as fast as that institution ever moved. Alard's importance on this map is exactly that: a teacher whose standards were high enough, and whose classroom was open enough, that a young violinist from the Americas could walk in and be measured on his playing alone. Teaching of that kind leaves no works behind, only players, which is why his name survives attached to other people's careers."},
  "charlesvalentinalkan":{"wiki":"Charles-Valentin Alkan","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Charles-Henri-Valentin%20Morhange%20dit%20Alkan.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Chopin and Liszt's formidable Parisian peer.","bio":"Charles-Valentin Alkan (1813-1888) composed and played the piano in Paris, the city where he spent virtually his whole life. His fame was at its height in the 1830s and 1840s, when he was counted among the leading pianists of the city beside Frédéric Chopin and Franz Liszt, both friends and colleagues of his — the same rooms, the same audiences, the same standard of playing. He was a linguist and a translator besides, a virtuoso with scholarly habits. He taught at the Paris Conservatoire, where Ignacio Cervantes studied with him alongside Marmontel and carried a strand of the Parisian keyboard tradition back into the Americas. The Conservatoire's classrooms fed the Americas as steadily as they fed Europe, and Alkan is one of the reasons why: a pianist of the first rank whose name reaches this part of the map through a pupil."},
  "fromentalhalevy":{"wiki":"Fromental Halévy","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fromental%20Halevy%20by%20Etienne%20Carjat-crop.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Bizet's teacher, and then his father-in-law.","bio":"Jacques-François-Fromental-Élie Halévy (1799-1862) wrote operas in France, and his contemporaries counted him among the central figures of nineteenth-century French music. He was also an academic musician who took teaching as seriously as composition, and his classroom at the Paris Conservatoire is why he matters here. Edmond Dédé studied with him after leaving Louisiana for good — an emigrant finding in Paris the formal training he had crossed an ocean to get. So did Georges Bizet, who later married Halévy's daughter Geneviève, making his teacher his father-in-law as well. Between the exile from Louisiana and the young Bizet, Halévy's teaching joins two parts of the century that rarely touch, and he is remembered now at least as much for the composers who came out of his class as for the operas he wrote."},
  "louismoreaugottschalk":{"wiki":"Louis Moreau Gottschalk","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Louis%20Moreau%20Gottschalk%20-%20Brady-Handy.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Creole virtuoso who toured the hemisphere.","bio":"Louis Moreau Gottschalk (1829-1869) was a composer and pianist whose background was Louisiana Creole and Jewish-American, and who built a career as a virtuoso playing romantic piano music of his own. Most of his working life was spent outside the United States, which is the key fact about him: the Louisiana he came from travelled with him, and his reputation was made in front of audiences a long way from the country he was born in. In New York he gave the eight-year-old Teresa Carreño her first lessons, putting a pupil on her way before she was old enough to know who he was. He wrote for himself to play, which is why the music sounds like a performer's rather than a composer's — built out of what his own hands could do, and carried wherever he went."},
  "edwardmacdowell":{"wiki":"Edward MacDowell","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Edward%20A.%20MacDowell.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The American Romantic of Woodland Sketches.","bio":"Edward Alexander MacDowell (1860-1908) was an American composer and pianist working at the close of the Romantic period. The works that carried his name were his second piano concerto and three suites for the instrument: Woodland Sketches, Sea Pieces, New England Idylls. The first of those holds 'To a Wild Rose', the short piece by which most people know him. In 1904 he was among the first seven Americans honoured with membership of the American Academy of Arts and Letters, a mark of how completely he had come to stand for serious composition in the United States. Teresa Carreño taught him as a boy and, twenty years later, gave his concerto its premiere: a teacher who followed a pupil's whole arc and then put the result in front of the public herself. He taught in his turn, holding a professorship in music."},
  "harrytburleigh":{"wiki":"Harry Burleigh","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Maud%20Cuney%20Hare-Harry%20T%20Burleigh%20328.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The baritone who sang Dvořák the spirituals.","bio":"Harry T. Burleigh (1866-1949) was an American composer, arranger and baritone, and the first Black composer central to the making of a characteristically American music. He put the spirituals within reach of classically trained singers twice over: by introducing musicians to them at all, and by arranging them in a form concert artists could perform. At the National Conservatory in New York he was Antonín Dvořák's student, and also his source — he sang spirituals to Dvořák for hours, and the New World Symphony followed, along with Dvořák's declaration that Black music would be the foundation of an American classical music. The transmission continued across the Atlantic: in London Burleigh brought the same repertory to Samuel Coleridge-Taylor, out of which came the Twenty-Four Negro Melodies. Few musicians have moved so much material between traditions."},
  "friedrichwieck":{"wiki":"Friedrich Wieck","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Friedrich%20Wieck%20age%2045.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The teacher who fought his daughter's marriage.","bio":"Friedrich Wieck (1785-1873) was a German piano and voice teacher, the owner of a piano shop, and the author of essays and reviews — a formidable pedagogue whose results nobody could argue with. He trained his daughter Clara, who was giving international tours at eleven and grew into one of the century's most celebrated pianists, and he trained Robert Schumann, who lodged in his house as a pupil. Then the two halves of his life collided. When Schumann and Clara wanted to marry, Wieck opposed it with extraordinary bitterness and fought them through the courts, and lost. His other daughter Marie Wieck also made a musical career, and Hans von Bülow was among his pupils. He shaped two of the central figures of German Romanticism and then spent years trying to keep them apart."},
  "simonmayr":{"wiki":"Simon Mayr","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Simon%20Mayr%20by%20Winter.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Donizetti's teacher and Bergamo's founding maestro.","bio":"Johann Simon Mayr (1763-1845), known in Italy as Giovanni Simone Mayr, was a German composer whose music sits on the hinge between the Classical and the Romantic era, and who made his career in Italy. The Bergamo Conservatory was his own foundation, opened in 1805. Rossini and Meyerbeer both took early inspiration from him, but his real monument is Gaetano Donizetti, whom he taught at Bergamo and then advocated for — finding the young man his first commissions, which is the harder half of teaching and the part most teachers never do. A German who wrote Italian opera and then handed the tradition on to a pupil who carried it further than he had, Mayr is one of the crucial relay points on this map: a composer whose influence is measured in other people's careers."},
  "nicolazingarelli":{"blurb":"Bellini's teacher at the Naples Conservatory.","bio":"Nicola Zingarelli belongs to this map as a teacher. At the Naples Conservatory he trained Vincenzo Bellini, which makes his classroom one of the places where an entire style can be said to begin. The record here gives the fact and no more: an institution, a master, a pupil, and everything that followed from the three."},
  "vincenzolavigna":{"wiki":"Fedele Fenaroli","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fedele%20Fenaroli.JPG?width=480","blurb":"The private teacher who took on Verdi.","bio":"Vincenzo Lavigna was an Italian composer and music theorist who came out of the Neapolitan schools as a pupil of Fedele Fenaroli — the Naples maestro whose other students included Domenico Cimarosa, Niccolò Antonio Zingarelli and Saverio Mercadante, and whose treatises were in use across the nineteenth century. That lineage matters because of what Lavigna did with it. When the Conservatory turned Giuseppe Verdi down, Lavigna took him on privately in Milan, and the grounding the young man could not get from an institution he got instead across a table, one pupil at a time. It makes Verdi a second-generation heir of Fenaroli's teaching, and it runs a direct line from the Naples conservatories to Milan by way of a rejected applicant. Lavigna is remembered for a refusal he quietly repaired."},
  "ambroisethomas":{"wiki":"Ambroise Thomas","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ambroise%20Thomas%202.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Massenet's teacher, and Mignon's composer.","bio":"Charles Louis Ambroise Thomas (1811-1896) made his name in France as a composer of opera and as a teacher; the two works still attached to that name are Mignon, of 1866, and Hamlet, of 1868. He was a musicologist and a university teacher as well, and it is the teaching that carries his influence forward. At the Paris Conservatoire he taught Jules Massenet, whose own career in French opera followed directly from that training. It is a common shape for a nineteenth-century French conservatoire man: a life divided between the stage and the classroom, with the classroom quietly outlasting the stage. Thomas stands on this map at the head of a line rather than at the centre of a repertory — two famous operas to his name, and a pupil who carried the training onward."},
  "henriduparc":{"wiki":"Henri Duparc (composer)","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Henri%20Duparc.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Franck's pupil, master of the mélodie.","bio":"Eugène Marie Henri Fouques Duparc (1848-1933) composed in France at the end of the Romantic period, and his reputation rests almost entirely on his mélodies. He studied with César Franck, the form's other great practitioner, so that the French art song passes here straight from teacher to pupil — an unusually tidy transmission for a genre that normally spreads by imitation. He belongs to the world of the French salon and of the Société Nationale, where composers of his generation set about building a serious French repertory of their own. He lived until 1933, decades past the era his music comes from, long enough for the songs to settle into the repertory while their author was still alive to hear them sung. The mélodie has two names at its summit, and his is the second."},
  "alexanderglazunov":{"wiki":"Alexander Glazunov","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/AlexanderGlazounov.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The director who fed the young Shostakovich.","bio":"Alexander Konstantinovich Glazunov (1865-1936) composed, taught and conducted in Russia at the close of the Romantic period. He had studied privately with Nikolai Rimsky-Korsakov between 1879 and 1881, and later joined the faculty of the institution that would define his life: he ran the Saint Petersburg Conservatory from 1905 to 1928, steering it through its reorganisation as the Petrograd and then the Leningrad Conservatory after the Bolshevik Revolution. He stayed its head until 1930, having quit the Soviet Union in 1928 without ever returning. The best-known student of those early Soviet years was Dmitri Shostakovich, for whom Glazunov found extra rations and a stipend when the boy needed both. Keeping a conservatory running through revolution and scarcity was the great work of his later life."},
  "bookertwashington":{"blurb":"The champion who arranged Coleridge-Taylor's American tours.","bio":"Booker T. Washington appears here as an advocate rather than a musician. He wrote the preface to Samuel Coleridge-Taylor's Twenty-Four Negro Melodies and arranged the composer's American tours, and that sponsorship, offered from outside the profession, is what carried the work and its maker to audiences across the United States."},
  "dompedroiiofbrazil":{"wiki":"Pedro II of Brazil","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Pedro%20II%20circa%201887b%20transparent.png?width=480","blurb":"The emperor who paid for Il Guarany.","bio":"Dom Pedro II, born in 1825, was the second and last emperor of Brazil, known as the Magnanimous. He came to the throne as a child in 1831 and was removed by the military coup of 1889, and no reign in Brazilian history has run longer or more steadily. His place on this map is not political but practical. He paid for the young Antônio Carlos Gomes to study in Milan, and that Italian training produced Il Guarany, the opera that gave Brazil a work of its own to claim and gave its composer a name beyond it. Patronage of that kind, direct from a head of state, was how an ambitious musician far from the European capitals got a hearing at all. He died in 1891, two years after losing his throne."},
  "fritzsimrock":{"wiki":"Fritz Simrock","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/FritzSimrock.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Brahms's publisher, and the making of Dvořák.","bio":"Friedrich August Simrock, known as Fritz, was a German music publisher, born in 1837, who inherited the family firm from his grandfather Nikolaus. He is remembered above all as the publisher of most of Johannes Brahms's music, and it was Brahms who pointed him towards a second composer worth having. That composer was Antonín Dvořák. Simrock took him on, and the Slavonic Dances that appeared under the Simrock imprint made the fortunes of publisher and composer alike, carrying a Czech name into every German house with a piano in it. Most of Dvořák's music went to the same firm thereafter. Between Brahms and Dvořák, a family business inherited rather than built held two of the strongest catalogues in Europe, which is why a publisher belongs on a map of composers. Simrock died in 1901."},
  "ottowesendonck":{"wiki":"Mathilde Wesendonck","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mathilde%20Wesendonck%20by%20Karl%20Ferdinand%20Sohn%2C%201850.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The patron who housed Wagner in Zurich.","bio":"Otto Wesendonck belongs on this map as a patron rather than a musician. He housed Richard Wagner in Zurich, and the shelter he provided became the setting for one of the most awkward episodes in nineteenth-century music. Wesendonck's wife was Agnes Mathilde Wesendonck, a German poet and author; Wagner grew infatuated with her, and set five of her verses as the songs that carry the family name, the Wesendonck Lieder. Wagner's own wife, Minna, held Mathilde responsible for the ruin of their marriage, and the affair brought the Zurich arrangement to an end. What survives of it is a set of songs written under a patron's roof and addressed to the patron's wife, which is a reminder that the music of the period is rarely separable from the households that paid for it."},
  "bartolomeomerelli":{"wiki":"Bartolomeo Merelli","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bartolomeo%20Merelli.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The impresario who handed Verdi Nabucco.","bio":"Bartolomeo Merelli, born in 1794, made his living as an Italian impresario and wrote libretti himself, and from 1829 to 1850 he managed La Scala in Milan. The job put a great deal of power in his hands: he chose the seasons, engaged the singers and decided which composers were given a stage in Milan. He managed careers as well as houses. His place here rests on a single decision. Merelli backed the young Giuseppe Verdi and pressed the libretto of Nabucco into his hands, and the whole of Verdi's career followed from it. Impresarios are usually remembered as businessmen rather than makers, but the choice of who gets a commission is as consequential as anything a composer does at the desk. He died in 1879."},
  "jeanettethurber":{"wiki":"Antonín Dvořák","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dvorak.jpg?width=480","blurb":"She brought Dvořák to New York.","bio":"Jeanette Thurber was the patron behind the National Conservatory in New York, and her instrument was money. She offered Antonín Dvořák twenty-five times his Prague salary to come and direct the school, an inducement calculated to be impossible to refuse, and he accepted. What she was buying was a method as much as a name. Dvořák had built a national idiom out of the folk music of his native Bohemia and of Moravia, following where Bedřich Smetana had led, and he arrived with the habit of listening to whatever a country already sang. It was that ear the conservatory acquired. Hers is the reason a Czech composer, reckoned among the most versatile musicians of his time, ran a conservatory in New York at all, and the reason the question of an American music was asked there so plainly."},
  "augustjaeger":{"wiki":"August Jaeger","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/A-J-Jaeger.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Elgar's editor, and his Nimrod.","bio":"August Johannes Jaeger, born in Germany in 1860, settled in Britain and worked as an editor for the music publisher Novello, where Edward Elgar's manuscripts crossed his desk. What began as professional contact turned into a close friendship. Jaeger read the scores carefully, argued for what he thought right, offered advice and practical help, and in the composer's worst periods talked him out of abandoning music altogether. Elgar then wrote him into the Enigma Variations as Nimrod, which is how an editor at a publishing house came to be immortalised by the composer he served. Jaeger died in 1909. His case is a useful corrective to the idea that music is made by composers alone: somebody has to read the pages first, and say so plainly."},
  "mariedagoult":{"blurb":"Liszt's partner, and Cosima's mother.","bio":"Marie d'Agoult was Franz Liszt's partner from 1835 to 1844, and the mother of their three children. The second of them was Cosima, who became Cosima Wagner. That single family fact is why d'Agoult appears on a musical map at all: the line of descent from Liszt runs through her daughter into the next generation."},
  "cosimawagner":{"wiki":"Cosima Wagner","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Cosimawagner1877london.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Wagner's widow, and Bayreuth's iron director.","bio":"Francesca Gaetana Cosima Wagner, born in 1837, was the daughter of Franz Liszt and the Franco-German author Marie d'Agoult. She married the conductor Hans von Bülow, then left him for Richard Wagner while Bülow was still conducting Wagner's premieres; she and Wagner married in 1870. Together they built the Bayreuth Festival into a showcase for his stage works, and commentators have long recognised her as the principal inspiration behind his later music, Parsifal above all. Wagner died in 1883, and what followed makes her a figure in her own right: she directed the Festival until 1906 and gave the remainder of her life to promoting his music and his philosophy, running Bayreuth on her own terms. She died in 1930, forty-seven years after her husband."},
  "aristidefarrenc":{"wiki":"Aristide Farrenc","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Aristide%20Farrenc.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Louise Farrenc's husband and her publisher.","bio":"Jacques Hippolyte Aristide Farrenc, born in 1794, was a French flautist who became a music publisher, a bibliophile and a musicologist. He was the husband of the composer Louise Farrenc, and also her publisher, which in a century that offered women composers few routes into print mattered a great deal more than the word husband suggests. The couple's largest joint undertaking was Le Trésor des pianistes, the anthology of keyboard music they compiled and issued together, a work of scholarship rather than of commerce. His playing, his collecting of old editions and his research all fed the same instinct: that the music of the past was worth having in usable form, and that somebody had to do the editing. He died in 1865."},
  "ninahagerup":{"wiki":"Nina Grieg","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nina%20Grieg%20c1934.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The soprano who sang Grieg's songs first.","bio":"Nina Hagerup, born in 1845, sang as a lyric soprano and was Danish as well as Norwegian; after her marriage she was known as Nina Grieg. She and Edvard Grieg were first cousins as well as husband and wife. She was the singer of his songs, and in that sense the sound he was composing for: the voice in the room while the music was being written, and the voice his listeners heard it in first. A song repertory conceived for one particular singer is shaped by her whether or not she is credited, and Grieg wrote his own songs with his wife in his ear. She was a trained pianist besides, and is credited with composing as well. She died in 1935."},
  "wilhelmhensel":{"blurb":"The painter who married Fanny Mendelssohn.","bio":"Wilhelm Hensel was the painter who married Fanny Mendelssohn in 1829, which is how she came to be known as Fanny Mendelssohn Hensel. He sketched the company that gathered at the Sunday concerts she ran for some twenty years, leaving a visual record of one of the most remarkable private musical institutions of the century."},
  "giuseppinastrepponi":{"wiki":"Giuseppina Strepponi","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Karoly%20Gyurkovich%20-%20Giuseppina%20Strepponi.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The soprano of Nabucco, and Verdi's wife.","bio":"Clelia Maria Giuseppa Strepponi, known as Giuseppina, was born in 1815 and became one of the most renowned Italian sopranos of the century. She sang in Nabucco, and a soprano of her standing on the bill was a considerable asset to any composer. The two later lived together for about a decade before they married, an arrangement that caused real local scandal and which neither of them troubled to justify. As his second wife she stood at the centre of Giuseppe Verdi's household, a professional singer who knew from the inside what he was asking of voices and who could read his work with an experienced ear. She is the rare consort on this map with a stage career of her own to point to. She died in 1897."},
  "emmabardac":{"wiki":"Emma Bardac","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Emma%20Debussy%20Bardac%20juillet1931%20T.Bonney.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Fauré's love, then Debussy's wife.","bio":"Emma Bardac, born in 1862, was a French singer, remembered as the mutual love interest of two French composers. With Gabriel Fauré she is linked through the Dolly Suite, which he wrote for her daughter. In 1904 Claude Debussy left his first wife for her, and the two later married. She appears on this map not as a composer but as the point where the private lives of Fauré and Debussy intersect, in exactly the years when French song and French piano writing were being remade. The music of a period is rarely separable from the households it was written in, and hers was one of the most consequential of them. What these composers heard in her voice is the part the record does not preserve. She died in 1934."},
  "aliceelgar":{"wiki":"Alice Elgar","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Caroline%20Alice%20Elgar.jpg?width=480","blurb":"She made a career out of Elgar.","bio":"Caroline Alice, Lady Elgar, born in 1848, wrote verse and prose fiction, and was the decisive figure in Edward Elgar's career. She came to him as a pupil and became his wife, and it was she who organised a career out of a provincial violin teacher. That phrase diminishes neither party: Elgar was teaching the violin in the provinces when they met, and the position he later occupied was built by two people, only one of whom signed the scores. Her own writing is remembered now chiefly because of whose wife she was, which is the usual bargain in such partnerships and no less real for being usual. She was a poet, a novelist and an autobiographer in her own right. She died in 1920."},
  "paullaurencedunbar":{"wiki":"Paul Laurence Dunbar","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Paul%20Laurence%20Dunbar%20circa%201890.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The poet Coleridge-Taylor set to music.","bio":"Paul Laurence Dunbar was born in Dayton, Ohio in 1872 to parents who had been held in slavery in Kentucky before the Civil War, and became an American poet, novelist and writer of short stories. He was making verse and stories as a child, printed his first poems in a Dayton newspaper at sixteen, and led the literary society at his high school as its president. He belongs on a map of music as a partner and a supplier of words: he gave joint recitals with Samuel Coleridge-Taylor in London and in Washington, and the composer set his verse. Dunbar wrote as a librettist too. A poet whose parents had been enslaved, working with a composer on both sides of the Atlantic, was making a claim about whom the concert platform belonged to. He died in 1906."},
  "johannmichaelvogl":{"wiki":"Johann Michael Vogl","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Johann%20Michael%20Vogl.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The baritone who launched Schubert's songs.","bio":"Johann Michael Vogl, born in 1768, was an Austrian baritone who also composed, and was famous in his own lifetime as an opera singer. He is remembered now for what he did for Franz Schubert. Vogl was the established man of the two, with the reputation the composer did not yet possess. He took up the songs, premiered a great deal of the repertory, and toured it with Schubert himself at the keyboard. That pairing of a celebrated singer with an unknown composer at the piano is how the Lieder first reached listeners beyond the private room, and something like it has been the model of the song recital ever since. The friendship was as close as the professional partnership, and it is why Vogl's name survives. He died in 1840."},
  "mathildewesendonck":{"wiki":"Mathilde Wesendonck","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mathilde%20Wesendonck%20by%20Karl%20Ferdinand%20Sohn%2C%201850.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The poet behind the Wesendonck Lieder.","bio":"Agnes Mathilde Wesendonck, born in 1828, was a German poet and author who also wrote for children and worked as a librettist. Her name survives attached to five of her verses: Richard Wagner set them as the Wesendonck Lieder, the songs known as the studies for Tristan. That is no small thing, because they are where the harmonic world of Tristan is first tried out, and the words are hers. Wagner was infatuated with her, and his own wife, Minna, held Mathilde responsible for the collapse of their marriage — which is how the episode is usually told, with the poet cast as the cause rather than as the collaborator. Poet, author, children's writer, librettist: the occupations recorded beside her name are those of a working writer, not a muse. She died in 1902."},
  "giuseppegiacosa":{"wiki":"Giuseppe Giacosa","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Giuseppe%20Giacosa%2002.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Half of Puccini's librettist team.","bio":"Giuseppe Giacosa, born in 1847, wrote poetry, plays and libretti, and was reckoned at the turn of the twentieth century among the leading dramatists in Italy in his own right. He is remembered chiefly for the work he did in double harness with Luigi Illica for Giacomo Puccini: between them the two men wrote the libretti of La bohème, Tosca and Madama Butterfly. Sharing the task suited both, and it gave Puccini unusually polished texts to set. That his characters speak so naturally, and that the words survive the size of the orchestra beneath them, is in no small part Giacosa's doing. Librettists are the invisible half of opera, credited on the title page and forgotten by the audience. He died in 1906, and the partnership ended with him."},
  "maxbruch":{"wiki":"Max Bruch","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Max%20bruch.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Romantic to the last, and Yamada's teacher.","bio":"Max Bruch, born in 1838, was a German Romantic composer who also played the violin, conducted and taught. He wrote more than two hundred works and is known to most listeners by one of them, the first of his three violin concertos, which has never left the repertoire. He remained a Romantic to the end and lived until 1920, long enough to watch the language he wrote in become the property of the past. He appears in this constellation as a teacher rather than as a composer. At the Berlin Hochschule between 1910 and 1913 his composition class included Yamada Kōsaku, and the German symphonic training handed over in those three years travelled a great deal further from Berlin than Bruch can have expected it to."},
  "ernestguiraud":{"wiki":"Ernest Guiraud","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ernest%20Guiraud.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Debussy's teacher, and Carmen's recitatives.","bio":"Ernest Guiraud, born in 1837, was French, born in America, and a composer and teacher whose own music is now less remembered than two pieces of practical craftsmanship: he wrote the orchestral recitatives traditionally used in Bizet's Carmen, and did the same service for Offenbach's Les contes d'Hoffmann. Both operas are still heard in his versions. He taught at the Paris Conservatoire, where his most consequential pupil was Claude Debussy. Guiraud let the young man argue with him, and the record kept of those conversations, with Debussy defending parallel fifths, is the best evidence we have of how he was thinking before he had written the music that proved him right. A good teacher is sometimes simply the one who writes the heresy down. He died in 1892."},
  "walterpiston":{"wiki":"Walter Piston","blurb":"Harvard's composer-theorist, trained by Boulanger.","bio":"Walter Hamor Piston Jr., born in 1894, was an American composer, a theorist of music, and the holder of a professorship in music at Harvard. Like much of his generation he went to Paris to study with Nadia Boulanger, and the training stayed with him. His music is built with a craftsman's care, and his theory is that same care set out for students to follow. His influence ran wider than his own catalogue, because a great many American musicians met the discipline of composition through his teaching rather than through his scores. He was a pianist as well. Piston died in 1976, having spent a working life on the faculty side of American music, which is a kind of authorship too."},
  "louisetalma":{"wiki":"Louise Talma","blurb":"Boulanger's American, first in many things.","bio":"Louise Juliette Talma, born in 1906, was an American composer, pianist and academic. She trained in New York and in France, taking the piano from Isidor Philipp and composition from Nadia Boulanger, and returned to Fontainebleau over many summers before teaching at the American Conservatory there herself, the pupil turned teacher on the ground where she had studied. From 1935 she gave her attention chiefly to composition, and she also taught for years at Hunter College. The Alcestiad, her opera, was the first full-scale work of its kind by an American woman to reach a European stage, and other firsts followed: she was the first woman to be given the Sibelius Medal for Composition, and the National Institute of Arts and Letters had no woman member before her. The Boulanger discipline is audible throughout. She died in 1996."},
  "erichwolfgangkorngold":{"wiki":"Erich Wolfgang Korngold","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Erich%20Wolfgang%20Korngold%2001.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Vienna prodigy who scored Hollywood.","bio":"Erich Wolfgang Korngold, born in 1897, was an Austrian composer and conductor, and a child prodigy of a kind that startled even Vienna, where he studied with Alexander Zemlinsky. He was a noted pianist, and he had a large late-Romantic technique in place before he was fully grown. In the mid-1930s he left Europe, and he later took American citizenship. In Hollywood he became one of the most influential figures in the history of film music: the first composer of international standing to write for the screen, which settled the question of whether such work was beneath a serious musician. Later studio composers worked either from his example or against it. He went on writing concert music alongside the films. He died in 1957."},
  "hanskoessler":{"wiki":"Hans von Koessler","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Koessler.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Taught both Bartók and Kodály.","bio":"Hans von Koessler, born in 1853, was a German composer and conductor who taught, and who spent twenty-six years of his working life in Hungary, where he went by the Hungarian form of his name, János Koessler. He belongs here for the classroom rather than the concert hall. At the Budapest Academy his composition pupils included both Béla Bartók and Zoltán Kodály, the two men who would go on to define Hungarian music in the century that followed. That a German trained the founders of Hungarian musical modernism is a useful reminder of how the conservatory system actually worked: solid technique inherited from one tradition, then turned to the service of another. Twenty-six years is long enough to shape a national school, and he shaped one that would sound nothing like him. He died in 1926."},
  "lukasfoss":{"wiki":"Lukas Foss","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Lukas%20Foss%20-%20Southern%20Campus%201960.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Hindemith's pupil, restless all his life.","bio":"Lukas Foss, born in 1922, was a German-American composer who was also a pianist and a conductor. He was born in Europe and made his career in the United States, where he studied composition with Paul Hindemith at Yale and acquired a rigorous, craft-centred grounding that stayed with him. Foss kept three careers going at once: he played, he conducted, and he composed, and he taught at university level besides. A musician who works in that many directions is hard to place in a single school, which is part of why his name turns up at the edges of so many stories in American music rather than at the centre of one. The Hindemith training is the constant underneath all of it: whatever else Foss was doing, he was doing it with a technique he could rely on. He died in 2009."},
  "maximiliansteinberg":{"wiki":"Maximilian Steinberg","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Steinberg%20Maximilian%20(1915).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Rimsky's heir and Shostakovich's cautious teacher.","bio":"Maximilian Osseyevich Steinberg, born in 1883, was a Russian composer trained at the St Petersburg Conservatory under Nikolai Rimsky-Korsakov, whose daughter Nadezhda he married. He was the heir of that school in every sense, a conservative craftsman who carried his teacher's methods intact into the Soviet period. At the same conservatory, by then in Petrograd, he taught the young Dmitri Shostakovich. The teaching was conservative and the pupil was not, and the friction between them is part of how the younger man's style formed: a technique inherited straight from the Rimsky-Korsakov line, applied to material his teacher would never have chosen. Steinberg conducted and taught as well as composing, and what he passed on has outlasted what he wrote. He died in 1946."},
  "horatioparker":{"wiki":"Horatio Parker","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Horatio%20William%20Parker%201916.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Yale professor who trained Ives.","bio":"Horatio William Parker, born in 1863, was an American composer, organist, conductor and teacher, and a central figure in the musical life of New Haven, Connecticut in the late nineteenth century. He directed choirs and he conducted, and the teaching ran alongside the composing all his working life. He is remembered above all as the man who taught Charles Ives during his undergraduate years at Yale. The relationship is easily caricatured as the stuffy professor and the American original, but it was more useful than that: Ives absorbed the technique Parker drilled into him and kept it for life, while quietly hiding the experiments he knew his teacher would not tolerate. Discipline learned and then disobeyed is still discipline learned. He died in 1919."},
  "charlesseeger":{"wiki":"Charles Seeger","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Charles%20Seeger.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Musicology's radical, and Ruth Crawford's partner.","bio":"Charles Louis Seeger Jr., born in 1886, was an American musicologist who also composed, taught and worked as a folklorist, and one of the people who turned the study of music in the United States towards ethnomusicology. He taught the composer Ruth Crawford and married her in 1932; afterwards the two worked together on transcribing American folk song, work that occupied both of them for years. The household became an institution in its own right. He was the father of the folk singers Pete, Peggy and Mike Seeger, and the brother of the First World War poet Alan Seeger and of Elizabeth Seeger, who wrote for children and taught. Few individuals connect the modernist avant-garde and the folk revival so directly. He died in 1979."},
  "georgewhitefieldchadwick":{"wiki":"George Whitefield Chadwick","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/George%20w%20chadwick.jpg?width=480","blurb":"New England's realist, and Still's teacher.","bio":"George Whitefield Chadwick, born in 1854, was an American composer counted among the Second New England School, alongside Amy Beach, Arthur Foote, John Knowles Paine, Horatio Parker and Edward MacDowell: the generation that gave the United States a concert tradition of its own. His music was touched by Realism in the other arts, dealing in the plain business of ordinary lives rather than in myth. He taught in both the conservatory and the university, and the teaching mattered as much as the music. As a teacher at the New England Conservatory he took on William Grant Still, so the line from that first professional American generation runs directly into the next one, and it runs there by way of a classroom rather than a concert hall. He died in 1931."},
  "mariodeandrade":{"wiki":"Mário de Andrade","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mario%20de%20andrade%201928b.png?width=480","blurb":"The writer who directed Guarnieri's life.","bio":"Mário Raul de Morais Andrade, born in 1893, was a Brazilian poet, novelist, critic, photographer and musicologist, and one of the architects of Brazilian modernism. Paulicéia Desvairada, published in 1922, was among the earliest gatherings of modern Brazilian poetry and among the most influential; he was also the author of Macunaíma; and as a scholar he pioneered ethnomusicology, with an influence that carried well beyond Brazil. In music his importance lies in what he did with one composer. For twenty years he directed the reading, the politics and the career of Camargo Guarnieri. Few composers have had a mind of that reach at their elbow, and few writers have taken so practical an interest in what music a country ought to be making. He died in 1945."},
  "arturrubinstein":{"wiki":"Arthur Rubinstein","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Arthur%20Rubinstein.jpg?width=480","blurb":"He played Villa-Lobos to the world.","bio":"Artur Rubinstein, born in Poland in 1887 and later an American citizen, was one of the outstanding pianists of his day, admired above all for his Chopin. He played in public across eight decades, with a repertoire running from Beethoven, Mozart and Schubert to Brahms, Schumann, Liszt, Tchaikovsky and Saint-Saëns, and his many recordings carried it to listeners who would never hear him in a hall. He belongs on this map for something else. Hearing Heitor Villa-Lobos in Rio in 1918, he recognised what he was listening to, and spent the next thirty years playing that music to the rest of the world. A pianist of his standing putting an unfamiliar name on international programmes was worth more than any prize. He died in 1982."},
  "arturotoscanini":{"wiki":"Arturo Toscanini","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Arturo%20Toscanini%20c1930.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The broadcast that made the Adagio famous.","bio":"Arturo Toscanini, born in Italy in 1867, ranked among the most influential musicians of his time, and among the most acclaimed. What was written about him always came down to the same things: the fierce intensity, the perfectionism, the ear that caught every detail of orchestral sound, and a memory that held whole scores. He was at various times music director at La Scala in Milan and at the New York Philharmonic, and from 1937 to 1954 the first to hold that post with the NBC Symphony Orchestra, whose radio and television broadcasts made him a household name in the United States. That reach is why he matters here as well as in the concert hall. When he broadcast Samuel Barber's Adagio for Strings in 1938 the piece became famous instantly, and it has stayed so. He died in 1957."},
  "sergekoussevitzky":{"wiki":"Serge Koussevitzky","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Serge%20Koussevitzky.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The conductor who commissioned the century.","bio":"Serge Koussevitzky, born in 1874, was a Russian and American conductor who also composed and played the double bass, and who led the Boston Symphony Orchestra for twenty-five years, from 1924 to 1949. No conductor of the century did more for living composers. He commissioned Béla Bartók's Concerto for Orchestra in 1943, when Bartók was ill and destitute, and he commissioned Peter Grimes from Benjamin Britten. He premiered nearly everything Aaron Copland wrote, and built Tanglewood partly around him. He taught Leonard Bernstein to conduct, and the Tanglewood career that followed was his doing. Much of what is now the standard twentieth-century repertoire exists because Koussevitzky asked for it, paid for it and then played it. He died in 1951."},
  "georgeives":{"wiki":"Charles Ives","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Charles%20Edward%20Ives%20-%20National%20Portrait%20Gallery%2C%20Smithsonian%20Institution%20(NPG.82.185).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The bandmaster who taught two keys at once.","bio":"George Ives was the American bandmaster who fathered Charles Ives and gave him his first training, and the source of the strangest lesson in American music. He set the boy exercises few teachers would attempt, having him sing a tune in one key while the accompaniment ran in another, so that the ear learned to hold two things at once instead of reconciling them. It is the lesson his son's music is usually traced back to. Charles Ives became a modernist composer who spent his working life as an actuary and a businessman, and one of the first Americans in music to be recognised worldwide, though his work was largely ignored early on and much of it went unplayed for years, until Henry Cowell and Lou Harrison pressed his case and he was received as an American original."},
  "dittapasztorybartok":{"wiki":"Ditta Pásztory-Bartók","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ditta%20P%C3%A1sztory.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Bartók's pupil, wife and second piano.","bio":"Ditta Pásztory-Bartók, born in 1903, was a Hungarian pianist. She was Béla Bartók's piano pupil before she became his second wife, and she remained a working musician within the marriage rather than beside it. A number of his works are dedicated to her, among them the Third Piano Concerto and Out of Doors, and she was the pianist of the Sonata for Two Pianos and Percussion, a piece written for two players who know each other's touch exactly. That is an unusual kind of influence: not a muse in the abstract but a specific pair of hands the composer could write for and count on in performance. The dedications are the record of it, and the Sonata put her on the platform. She died in 1982."},
  "wassilykandinsky":{"blurb":"The painter who wrote to Schoenberg.","bio":"The painter Wassily Kandinsky wrote to Arnold Schoenberg in 1911, having heard the Second Quartet and recognised in it what he was reaching for in paint. The friendship ran both ways: Schoenberg's own paintings hung in the Blaue Reiter exhibition, and the two men's parallel departures from tonality and from representation are still discussed together."},
  "cecilsharp":{"wiki":"Cecil Sharp","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Cecil%20James%20Sharp%20(1916%2C%20full).jpg?width=480","blurb":"He collected the songs England forgot.","bio":"Cecil James Sharp, born in 1859, spent his life gathering English folk song, folk dance and instrumental music, and worked besides as a teacher, a lecturer, a composer and a musician. He was the key figure in the Edwardian revival of folk song, and scholarship still ranks him first among those who studied folk music in England. From 1903 he was collecting for the Folk-Song Society, often over the same Somerset and Essex ground as Ralph Vaughan Williams, and the two harvests fed each other. What Sharp gathered, published and taught put a body of tunes and modal habits in front of a generation of English composers who had been looking elsewhere for a language. A good deal of what sounds most English in the music that followed came out of those notebooks. He died in 1924."},
  "marthagraham":{"wiki":"Martha Graham","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Martha%20Graham%2C%20seated%2C%20c.%201940%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The choreographer who commissioned Halim El-Dabh.","bio":"Martha Graham, born in 1894, was an American dancer, teacher and choreographer, a central figure of modern dance and the creator of the Graham technique. She had new scores written for her dances, which makes her a commissioner of music as much as a maker of dances: composers who worked for her had to write to a dramatic architecture that did not originate in music. Halim El-Dabh was one of them. His Clytemnestra of 1958 was the first of seven scores he wrote for her, and that run of commissions carried a composer from outside the European line onto the American stage. Seven scores is not a one-off experiment; it is a working relationship, and it made her a patron of new music as well as its choreographer. She died in 1991."},
  "stephanemallarme":{"wiki":"Stéphane Mallarmé","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mallarm%C3%A9%20Nadar.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The poem behind Debussy's Prélude.","bio":"Étienne Mallarmé, who published as Stéphane, born in 1842, was a French poet and critic and the major figure of Symbolism. The movements that came after him — Cubism, Futurism, Dadaism, Surrealism — all took something from his example. His importance for music is more direct than that. Claude Debussy composed the Prélude for a staged reading of Mallarmé's poem, and the poet, who had every reason to be wary of a composer attaching notes to lines already made of sound, told him the piece went far beyond the poem itself. That verdict matters, because it marks the moment when Symbolist poetics — suggestion rather than statement, atmosphere rather than argument — passed into music and gave Debussy his direction. He died in 1898."},
  "vaslavnijinsky":{"wiki":"Vaslav Nijinsky","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nijinsky%20(1890-1950)%20photographed%20at%20Krasnoe%20Selo%2C%20summer%201907.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Rite's choreographer, and its riot.","bio":"Vaslav Nijinsky, born in 1889 and Polish by descent, made his career in Russia as a dancer and choreographer, and is held to be the finest male dancer of the early twentieth century. He was celebrated for his virtuosity, for how deeply and intensely he characterised a role, for dancing en pointe when almost no man did, and for leaps that appeared to defy gravity. With Sergei Diaghilev's Ballets Russes he turned choreographer, making L'après-midi d'un faune in 1912, Jeux and Le Sacre du Printemps in 1913 and Till Eulenspiegel in 1916. It was his choreography for Igor Stravinsky's Rite of Spring that brought on the most famous riot in music, and the steps were as much the provocation as the score. He died in 1950."},
  "pablopicasso":{"blurb":"Parade's cubist costumes, and Stravinsky's portraitist.","bio":"Pablo Picasso's dealings with music ran through the theatre. In 1917 he made the cubist costumes for Parade, with Erik Satie's score, Cocteau's scenario and Massine's choreography, and in 1920 he worked with Igor Stravinsky on Pulcinella. His pencil portraits of Stravinsky date from the same years, which is why the composer's face is as familiar as his music."},
  "robertcraft":{"wiki":"Robert Craft","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Stravinski-1961-Helsinki.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The assistant who turned Stravinsky serial.","bio":"Robert Lawson Craft, born in 1923, was an American conductor and writer, known above all for the professional intimacy of his relationship with Igor Stravinsky. He came to the composer as assistant and amanuensis, and drew on the association for a long series of recordings and books, through which Stravinsky's opinions reached the public in Craft's shaping. He is also the reason for one of the strangest turns in twentieth-century music. Craft talked Stravinsky into serial composition at seventy, decades after neoclassicism had made him famous, and the late works came out of that persuasion. It is an unusual thing for an assistant to have caused, and it leaves a question the books cannot settle: how much of the late Stravinsky is Stravinsky, and how much is the man at his elbow. He died in 2015."},
  "langstonhughes":{"wiki":"Langston Hughes","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/LangstonHughes%20crop.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The poet Margaret Bonds kept setting.","bio":"James Mercer Langston Hughes, from Joplin, Missouri, was an American poet, novelist, playwright, columnist and social activist, an early innovator of jazz poetry and one of the leaders of the Harlem Renaissance. His verse took the rhythms and forms of blues and jazz into print, which is part of why composers reached for it so readily: the music is already in the line. The most sustained of those partnerships was with Margaret Bonds, thirty years of song settings and stage works made together, an unusually long run for a poet and a composer to keep working together. Hughes wrote novels, plays and newspaper columns alongside the poetry, and the poems have gone on drawing composers to them. He died in 1967."},
  "gertrudestein":{"blurb":"Virgil Thomson's librettist, twice over.","bio":"Gertrude Stein was the writer whose words Virgil Thomson set in two stage works, Four Saints in Three Acts and The Mother of Us All. Twice is the telling number: whatever Thomson found in her language, he went back for a second helping of it."},
  "mauricemaeterlinck":{"wiki":"Maurice Maeterlinck","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Picture%20of%20Maurice%20Maeterlinck.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Symbolist playwright behind Pelléas.","bio":"Maurice Maeterlinck, born in Belgium in 1862, was a playwright, poet and essayist, Flemish by birth but writing entirely in French. A leading figure of La Jeune Belgique, he made his plays central to Symbolism, circling death and the question of what a life means in a half-lit, fairy-tale register that worked on a reader's own feelings rather than telling them what to think. The Nobel Prize in Literature came to him in 1911. His importance to music is Pelléas: Claude Debussy set the play, and Maeterlinck, having given his permission, turned on the opera in print when his mistress was not cast. He was styled Count Maeterlinck from 1932, and was credibly accused of plagiarism in his later years. He died in 1949."},
  "rosarioscalero":{"wiki":"Rosario Scalero","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Rosario%20Scalero%2C%20foto%20pubblicata%20sul%20Musical%20Courier%2C%206%20aprile%201929.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Curtis teacher who trained George Walker.","bio":"Natale Rosario Scalero, born in Italy in 1870, was a violinist, composer and music teacher, and it is the last of those that fixes his place here. He is remembered above all through the pupils who came to him at the Curtis Institute, chief among them George Walker, the first Black graduate of that school, who studied composition in Scalero's classroom. Teachers of this kind rarely appear on concert programmes, yet they set the terms of what their students are able to do, and an Italian grounding in craft passed through him into American music of the following generation. Scalero went on writing and playing himself, but the record of his life is largely a record of what he handed on. He died in 1954."},
  "ottoluening":{"wiki":"Otto Luening","blurb":"Tape music's American pioneer.","bio":"Otto Clarence Luening, born in 1900, was a German-American composer, conductor and flutist who reached tape and electronic music early, at a time when both were curiosities rather than disciplines. With Vladimir Ussachevsky he founded the Columbia-Princeton Electronic Music Center, the studio where a generation first heard what tape could do to a musical idea. Luening was also a university teacher, and at the Eastman School of Music his students included Ulysses Kay. Between the tape pieces and the teaching, his influence ran through both the avant-garde and the conservatory, two worlds that in those years rarely shared a room, and he was equally at home conducting in one or the other. He died in 1996, having seen electronic music become ordinary."},
  "klaushuber":{"wiki":"Klaus Huber","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Klaus%20Huber%20(1981).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Freiburg's teacher of a whole generation.","bio":"Klaus Huber, born in Switzerland in 1924, was a composer and academic who worked in Basel and in Freiburg, and one of the most consequential composition teachers of the postwar era. The list of those who studied with him reads like a map of late twentieth-century music: Brian Ferneyhough, Michael Jarrell, Wolfgang Rihm, Kaija Saariaho, and, arriving in Freiburg after his Berlin years with Isang Yun, Toshio Hosokawa. Younghi Pagh-Paan also came to him in Freiburg; the two married, and taught together first in Freiburg and later in Bremen, a partnership of teaching as much as of life. Huber's own music ran alongside all of this rather than behind it, and the Ernst von Siemens Music Prize came to him in 2009. He died in 2017."},
  "vincentpersichetti":{"wiki":"Vincent Persichetti","blurb":"Trained a generation at Juilliard.","bio":"Vincent Ludwig Persichetti, born in 1915, was an American composer, pianist and conductor who also worked as a musicologist and wrote about music, but his largest legacy is as a teacher. He absorbed new compositional ideas rather than resisting them, folding whatever seemed useful into both his own scores and his classes, and at the Juilliard School he trained a long line of noted composers, among them Leo Brouwer. That openness to any idiom made him an unusually undogmatic figure in a period of hardening schools, when many teachers were asking students to choose a side. His writing on music carried the same practical cast of mind, and his students tended to leave with technique rather than a doctrine. He died in 1987, remembered less for a single signature work than for the number of composers who learned their craft from him."},
  "reneleibowitz":{"wiki":"René Leibowitz","blurb":"Brought the twelve-tone method to Paris.","bio":"René Leibowitz, born in Poland in 1913 and later French, worked as composer, conductor and teacher, and wrote as a theorist; he is the figure who carried the Second Viennese School into postwar Paris. He promoted its music from the podium and on the page, and passed the twelve-tone technique to the serialists of the next generation. In 1945 Pierre Boulez learned that technique from him, and then repudiated him publicly, in the manner of the young. Hans Werner Henze also took the method from Leibowitz, and used it against the Darmstadt orthodoxy rather than in its service. Few teachers have been so thoroughly absorbed and so thoroughly disowned by their own pupils, and postwar Parisian serialism is unimaginable without him. He died in 1972."},
  "stephensondheim":{"wiki":"Stephen Sondheim","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Stephen%20Sondheim%20-%20smoking.JPG?width=480","blurb":"Reinvented the American musical.","bio":"Stephen Joshua Sondheim, born in 1930, wrote both the music and the words, and is held to be one of the twentieth century's most important theatre figures, credited with the reinvention of the American musical. The accolades followed accordingly: eight Tonys, eight Grammys, five Oliviers; the American Theater Hall of Fame took him in 1982; the Kennedy Center honoured him in 1993; and the Presidential Medal of Freedom arrived in 2015. His presence in a map of concert music comes from an unlikely place. In the early 1950s he took private lessons with Milton Babbitt, and the two spent most of that time analysing the songs of Jerome Kern rather than anything more forbidding, a detail that says something about both men. Sondheim died in 2021."},
  "allankaprow":{"blurb":"Happenings came out of Cage's classroom.","bio":"Allan Kaprow reaches this map through John Cage. He sat in Cage's class at the New School between 1957 and 1959, the class out of which Happenings and Fluxus came, and out of which a generation of artists began making work from action and duration rather than from objects."},
  "panditprannath":{"wiki":"Pran Nath (musician)","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Chitarapathnan%202.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The raga master the minimalists followed.","bio":"Pandit Pran Nath, born in 1918, sang Indian classical music as a master of the Kirana gharana, devoted to traditional raga principles and to passing them on intact. American performances began in the 1970s, and his Kirana Center for Indian Classical Music dates from 1972; he taught at universities across the United States and Europe. His effect on American music was out of all proportion to his public fame. La Monte Young, Marian Zazeela and Terry Riley all became his disciples from 1970, and Riley kept travelling to India annually for twenty-six years to go on studying with him; Don Cherry was drawn in from the jazz side. Through those pupils, the discipline of the raga entered minimalism at its source. He died in 1996."},
  "davebrubeck":{"wiki":"Dave Brubeck","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dave%20Brubeck%20(1964).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Cool jazz's master of odd metres.","bio":"David Warren Brubeck, born in 1920, was an American pianist and composer working in jazz, and is often counted a foremost exponent of the cool style. His music is marked by unusual time signatures and by contrasting rhythms, metres and tonalities superimposed on one another, mixing classical procedure with jazz and blues until the seams stopped showing. That classical side has a clear source: Brubeck studied with Darius Milhaud at Mills College, and thought enough of his teacher to name a son after him. What he took away was a composer's ear working inside a live jazz group, which is why his records sound both worked-out and improvised. He died in 2012, one of the few musicians to have carried a modernist composer's training into a jazz band and made it swing."},
  "leonkirchner":{"wiki":"Leon Kirchner","blurb":"Taught John Adams at Harvard.","bio":"Leon Kirchner, born in 1919, was an American composer and pianist who spent much of his working life inside the university, and whose music earned the standing that went with it: he belonged to the American Academy of Arts and Letters and to the American Academy of Arts and Sciences, and his String Quartet No. 3 took the Pulitzer Prize. He taught for years at Harvard, and it was there that John Adams studied composition with him, a lineage worth noticing precisely because Adams's later music went somewhere his teacher's did not. Kirchner belonged to the postwar American generation for whom serious composition and serious teaching were the same activity, and he pressed both to the end. He died in 2009."},
  "paavoheininen":{"wiki":"Paavo Heininen","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Paavo-Heininen-1982.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The Sibelius Academy's modernist teacher.","bio":"Paavo Johannes Heininen, born in 1938, was a Finnish composer, pianist and pedagogue whose influence is felt most clearly through the students he taught at the Sibelius Academy in Helsinki. Two of them changed the direction of Finnish music: Kaija Saariaho and Magnus Lindberg both studied composition with him, and together went on to found the Ears Open society, the group through which their Helsinki generation argued the case for contemporary music. He was himself a pianist and a university teacher for most of his career, and those two names are only the most visible of the composers who passed through his classes. It is that teaching line that makes him load-bearing here, since a small country's whole modernist turn can be traced back through his classroom. He died in 2022."},
  "magnuslindberg":{"wiki":"Magnus Lindberg","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Magnus%20Lindberg%203811.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Composer-in-residence to New York and London.","bio":"Magnus Gustaf Adolf Lindberg, born in Finland in 1958, is a composer and pianist, and one of the central figures of the generation that came out of the Sibelius Academy in the late 1970s. He studied composition there with Paavo Heininen, and in 1977, with Kaija Saariaho and others of their circle, founded Korvat auki, Ears Open, the Helsinki society through which that generation pressed the case for contemporary music. His later career has been thoroughly international: composer-in-residence with the New York Philharmonic from 2009 to 2012 and with the London Philharmonic Orchestra from 2014 to 2017, two posts that placed a Finnish modernist inside major orchestral institutions on either side of the Atlantic. He continues to compose and to play the piano."},
  "heinoeller":{"wiki":"Heino Eller","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Heino%20Eller%201930s.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The founder of Estonian symphonic music.","bio":"Heino Eller, born in 1887, was an Estonian composer, violinist and pedagogue, generally credited with founding his country's modern symphonic tradition: the man who gave a small nation an orchestral music of its own. His importance is doubled by his teaching. At the Tallinn Conservatory his composition students included Arvo Pärt, who took his formal grounding there. A violinist by training, Eller spent his working life as a teacher as much as a composer, and the conservatory in Tallinn was where the two roles met, one feeding the other. He belongs to the class of figures in this map whose names are known chiefly to their own countrymen, but whose classrooms turned out to be the place where a national music started. He died in 1970."},
  "waltergoehr":{"wiki":"Walter Goehr","blurb":"Schoenberg's Berlin pupil, conductor in Britain.","bio":"Walter Goehr, born in Germany in 1903, was a composer and conductor trained at the sharp end of musical modernism: he studied with Arnold Schoenberg in Berlin between 1925 and 1928. From 1937 his life and work were based in the United Kingdom, conducting and writing for film, a career of practical musicianship rather than manifestos, and one shaped by the emigration that scattered so many musicians of his generation. Film scores and the podium kept him working in his adopted country for the rest of his life. He was also the father of the composer Alexander Goehr, and that transmission matters to this map: the son grew up with a direct family line back to Schoenberg's classroom, one of the shortest routes by which Viennese modernism reached postwar British music. He died in 1960."},
  "lennoxberkeley":{"wiki":"Lennox Berkeley","blurb":"English composer who taught John Tavener.","bio":"Sir Lennox Randal Francis Berkeley, born in 1903, was an English composer who moved easily between composing, scholarship and teaching, and who was appointed CBE and knighted. Alongside his own catalogue he worked as a musicologist and a music educator, and it is the teaching that gives him his place here: at the Royal Academy of Music his composition students included John Tavener. The Academy was one of the main routes into British musical life, and Berkeley's classroom sat astride it. The pairing is a good example of how this map works, since a young composer's technique can be formed by a teacher whose own music goes in quite another direction; a catalogue and a set of pupils are two different kinds of legacy, and he left both. Berkeley died in 1989."},
  "ferencfarkas":{"wiki":"Ferenc Farkas","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ferenc%20Farkas%2C1971.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Ligeti's teacher in Budapest.","bio":"Ferenc Farkas, born in Hungary in 1905, was a composer, conductor and music educator whose life spanned nearly the whole of the twentieth century. He taught at the Budapest Academy, and among the composition students who passed through his classroom was György Ligeti, whose formal grounding was laid there before anything that made him famous had been written. Teaching posts of that kind are unusually consequential for a national tradition: a single classroom can settle what a country's next generation of composers knows. Farkas built a substantial career at home as composer, conductor and university teacher, but like several other figures in this constellation he is now best known outside Hungary for the pupil he set on his way. He died in 2000."},
  "wolfgangfortner":{"wiki":"Wolfgang Fortner","blurb":"Henze's teacher at Heidelberg.","bio":"Wolfgang Fortner, born in Germany in 1907, was a composer, conductor and academic teacher of composition, one of the men through whose classrooms the postwar German generation passed. At Heidelberg his students included Hans Werner Henze, who left with the craft that carried him through a long career. What a student takes from a teacher is usually technique rather than aesthetics, and a foundation of that kind outlasts whatever a composer later decides to do with it. Fortner conducted and composed throughout, but his position in this map is the one he held for decades in German musical education, where the argument about what music should be after the war was largely conducted in front of students. He died in 1987."},
  "borisblacher":{"wiki":"Boris Blacher","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Boris%20Blacher.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Isang Yun's teacher in Berlin.","bio":"Boris Blacher, born in 1903, was a German composer, librettist and university teacher who wrote both his own music and words for other people to set. His place here is a teaching one: in Berlin his composition students included Isang Yun, who made from that training a music of his own. Blacher was a musicologist as well, and his work as a librettist put him at the word end of the operatic trade rather than only the note end — a useful thing in a teacher of composition, who has to explain why a setting works as well as how it is built. He taught in Berlin, and his studio was one of the doors through which composers from outside Germany came. He died in 1975."},
  "namjunepaik":{"blurb":"Cage turned him from composer to artist.","bio":"Nam June Paik's turn away from music is one of the sharpest breaks in this map. He said that meeting John Cage in 1958 was the moment he stopped being a composer and became something else, and the work that followed left the concert hall behind entirely for a different kind of medium."},
  "bettyfreeman":{"wiki":"Betty Freeman","blurb":"The patron who paid for American experiment.","bio":"Betty Freeman, born in 1921, was an American photographer and philanthropist, and one of the very few private patrons the American experimental tradition ever had. She had trained as a concert pianist, twenty years of six-to-eight-hour practice days, before giving up that ambition by the mid-1960s and turning first to concert managing and then to paying for new music directly. Some forty works were commissioned outright with her money, among them John Adams's Grand Pianola Music. She supported John Cage, and she carried Harry Partch and his instruments through his last years. Composers with no institution behind them had in her a patron who simply wrote the cheque, and a good deal of American music exists because of it. She died in 2009."},
  "thebeatles":{"blurb":"Apple Records released The Whale, 1970.","bio":"The Beatles enter this map as patrons rather than as composers. John Tavener's brother Roger was renovating Ringo Starr's house and made the introduction, and the group's Apple Records label released Tavener's The Whale in 1970, an improbable piece of pop-world patronage that put a young composer on record."},
  "paulsacher":{"wiki":"Paul Sacher","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Beck%20Stravinsky%20Sacher%20(cropped-Sacher).jpeg?width=480","blurb":"The conductor who commissioned a century.","bio":"Paul Sacher, born in Switzerland in 1906, was a conductor, patron and businessman: the majority shareholder of Hoffmann-La Roche and, at his death, reckoned the world's third richest person, worth some thirteen billion dollars. He spent that fortune on music. Across five decades he commissioned from Igor Stravinsky and collected his manuscripts; he commissioned Béla Bartók's Music for Strings, Percussion and Celesta and the Divertimento, and Bohuslav Martinů's Double Concerto among much else. Elliott Carter received Basel commissions through his later decades, and Witold Lutosławski, Luciano Berio and Hans Werner Henze were drawn into the same orbit. Few individuals have shaped the twentieth-century repertoire so directly: a striking number of its canonical scores exist because Sacher asked for them and paid. He died in 1999."},
  "nuriaschoenbergnono":{"wiki":"Luigi Nono","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Luigi%20Nono%20(1979).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Schoenberg's daughter, who married Luigi Nono.","bio":"Nuria Schoenberg Nono belongs to this map by family and by marriage rather than by a catalogue of her own. She was Arnold Schoenberg's daughter, raised inside the household of the Second Viennese School, and in 1955 she married Luigi Nono, an Italian composer working at the avant-garde edge of concert music. Igor Stravinsky stood as a witness at the wedding. It is a scene worth pausing on, because a single afternoon joined the Viennese line she came from to the postwar Italian avant-garde her husband was making, with a third name standing beside them in the register. Family ties are not usually load-bearing in a history of music, but this one is: the connection runs through her, and she is the reason the two traditions can be drawn on a single line."},
  "yokoono":{"blurb":"Co-ran the Chambers Street loft concerts.","bio":"Yoko Ono enters this map through the loft rather than the concert hall. She was married to Toshi Ichiyanagi from 1956 to 1962, and together they ran the Chambers Street loft concerts, one of the earliest gathering points for the American experimental avant-garde and a room where new work could simply be tried out."},
  "cathyberberian":{"wiki":"Cathy Berberian","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Cathy%20Berberian%20(1972).jpg?width=480","blurb":"The voice the avant-garde wrote for.","bio":"Catherine Anahid Berberian, born in 1925, was an American mezzo-soprano who also composed. She spent her working life in Italy, and her voice became an instrument composers wrote for directly. She married Luciano Berio in 1950; Sequenza III, Circles and Recital I were all made for her, and the two went on working together for a decade after the divorce. Bruno Maderna, Igor Stravinsky, John Cage, Darius Milhaud, Henri Pousseur, Sylvano Bussotti and Roman Haubenstock-Ramati worked with her too, and she sang Monteverdi, Villa-Lobos and Weill with equal seriousness. As a curator of her own recitals she brought folk songs and Louis Andriessen's arrangements of Beatles songs into classical programmes. She also composed: Stripsody, from 1966, built out of comic-book onomatopoeia, and Morsicat(h)y, from 1969, a keyboard piece on Morse code. She died in 1983."},
  "marianzazeela":{"wiki":"Marian Zazeela","blurb":"Light artist of the Dream House.","bio":"Marian Zazeela, born in 1940, was an American light artist, painter, calligrapher, designer and musician who lived and worked in New York. She belonged to the Theatre of Eternal Music, the 1960s experimental collective, and her work is inseparable from that of La Monte Young, the minimalist composer she married. Together they made the Dream House, in which her light and calligraphic forms are not decoration for his sound but the other half of the work, so that a listener is also a viewer standing inside a single continuous environment. Her painting and calligraphy fed straight into that light work, and the partnership ran for some forty years of joint light-and-sound making, a body of work with no obvious precedent. She died in 2024."},
  "mortonsubotnick":{"wiki":"Morton Subotnick","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Simultaneous%20MIDI%20control%20and%20Buchla%20touch%20keyboard.jpg?width=480","blurb":"Silver Apples of the Moon's composer.","bio":"Morton Subotnick, born in 1933, is an American composer who helped build the conditions in which West Coast electronic music could exist. In 1962 he co-founded the San Francisco Tape Music Center with Ramon Sender, the workshop where Pauline Oliveros and a whole California experimental circle did their early work. His Silver Apples of the Moon, from 1967, was the first piece of electronic music a record company had commissioned — the label was Nonesuch — which turned electronic composition into something you bought and played at home rather than something you attended. He helped found the California Institute of the Arts and taught there for many years, passing that studio culture on to composers who arrived after the equipment had become ordinary."},
  "scottjoplin":{"wiki":"Scott Joplin","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Scott%20Joplin.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The King of Ragtime.","bio":"Scott Joplin, born in 1868, was an American pianist and composer dubbed the King of Ragtime: more than forty rags, two operas and a ballet in ragtime. Among his earliest and best-loved pieces, the 'Maple Leaf Rag' became the genre's first great hit and is still the rag by which the others are measured. Joplin himself regarded ragtime as a form of classical music, written for the concert hall, and largely disdained the honky-tonk saloon manner in which it was usually played, an argument he lost in his lifetime and won afterwards. He died in 1917 with his opera Treemonisha never fully staged. T. J. Anderson orchestrated it from the vocal score for its first complete production in 1972, sixty years after Joplin wrote it."},
  "maxroach":{"wiki":"Max Roach","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Max%20Roach%2C%20Three%20Deuces%2C%20ca.%201947.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The bebop drummer who hired composers.","bio":"Maxwell Lemuel Roach, born in 1924, drummed and composed, a pioneer of bebop who is generally counted among the most important drummers the music has had. The list of those he worked with is close to a history of modern jazz in itself: Charlie Parker, Dizzy Gillespie, Clifford Brown, Miles Davis, Thelonious Monk, Duke Ellington, Charles Mingus, Sonny Rollins, Abbey Lincoln, Eric Dolphy and Booker Little, and later his own daughter, the violist Maxine Roach. Two halls of fame took him in: DownBeat's in 1980, Modern Drummer's in 1992. His groups were also a workplace for composers: Coleridge-Taylor Perkinson wrote and arranged for them while running a concert career in parallel, one of the clearest bridges in this map between jazz and the concert tradition. He died in 2007."},
  "arthurmitchell":{"wiki":"Joni Mitchell","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Joni%20Mitchell%202021%20Kennedy%20Center%20Honors%20(cropped).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Where Tania León's ballets were danced.","bio":"Arthur Mitchell belongs to this map through the Dance Theatre of Harlem, and through what that company made possible for a composer. Tania León was its founding music director, and she wrote the ballets it danced in its early years. A post of that kind is worth more to a young composer than a commission: it means a company, a repertory to build and a reason to write the next piece before the last one has finished being danced. Institutions are usually invisible in a history told through names, and yet they are where careers of this sort begin. The company is where León's first body of work came from, and the line from a dance company in Harlem into the concert tradition runs directly through it."},
  "lecorbusier":{"wiki":"Le Corbusier","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Le%20Corbusier%20(1964).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Modern architecture's pioneer, Xenakis's employer.","bio":"Charles-Édouard Jeanneret took the working name Le Corbusier. Born in French-speaking Switzerland in 1887, he became an architectural designer who also painted, planned cities and wrote, one of the founding figures of modern architecture. He took French nationality in 1930 and built over five decades in Europe, in Japan and India, and in the Americas. Music enters his story through the Philips Pavilion at the 1958 Brussels Expo, the building made to house Edgard Varèse's Poème électronique, sound and architecture conceived as one work. The pavilion was in fact designed by Iannis Xenakis, who worked in Le Corbusier's office for twelve years and had to fight to be credited for it: a composer built the room in which another composer's music was heard. Le Corbusier died in 1965."},
  "robertrauschenberg":{"blurb":"The White Paintings that prompted 4'33\".","bio":"Robert Rauschenberg reaches this map through John Cage. His White Paintings prompted Cage's silent 4'33\", and the two took part together in Theatre Piece No. 1 at Black Mountain in 1952, a meeting of painting, music and performance from which a great deal of the American avant-garde followed."},
  "ravishankar":{"blurb":"His Chappaqua score reset Glass's rhythm.","bio":"Ravi Shankar enters this map through Philip Glass. In Paris, Glass took on the job of transcribing Shankar's score for the film Chappaqua, and found that to do it at all he had to rethink how rhythm works, a lesson that stayed in his own music long afterwards."},
  "robertwilson":{"wiki":"Robert Wilson (director)","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/2014-01-19%20Luebeck%20Wachter%20Wilson%20(cropped%20-%20Robert%20Wilson).jpg?width=480","blurb":"Director of Einstein on the Beach.","bio":"Robert Wilson, born in 1941, worked as a director and playwright in experimental theatre, and also as a choreographer, a performer, a painter and sculptor, a video artist, and the designer of his own sound and lighting. His importance to music rests on Einstein on the Beach, made in 1976 with Philip Glass and with Lucinda Childs, the work that put minimal music on the operatic stage and began four decades of collaboration with Glass, a partnership longer than most in the arts; he also worked frequently with Tom Waits. In 1991 he founded The Watermill Center on Long Island's East End, calling it a laboratory for performance and running it as a workshop for opera and theatre companies and for festivals. He died in 2025."},
  "mstislavrostropovich":{"wiki":"Mstislav Rostropovich","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/MRostropovich.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The cellist who commissioned a repertoire.","bio":"Mstislav Leopoldovich Rostropovich, born in 1927, was a cellist and conductor of the Soviet Union and then of Russia, and no cellist before or since has added so much to what the instrument can play. Over a hundred pieces were written at his prompting or first played by him, and it happened through friendship as much as through commission. Dmitri Shostakovich wrote both cello concertos for him; Benjamin Britten, whom he met in 1960 and worked with until Britten's death, wrote the Cello Symphony and the three suites. Witold Lutosławski's Cello Concerto and Henri Dutilleux's Tout un monde lointain came to him in 1970, and Alfred Schnittke's First Cello Concerto later. Prokofiev, Myaskovsky, Messiaen, Berio, Penderecki, Khachaturian and Bernstein were among the many others who wrote for him. He died in 2007."},
  "whauden":{"wiki":"W. H. Auden","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/AudenVanVechten1939.jpg?width=480","blurb":"The poet Britten set, then lost.","bio":"Wystan Hugh Auden, born in 1907, was a British-American poet whose work ranged across love, politics, morals and religion with a technical command few of his contemporaries could match: 'Funeral Blues', 'September 1, 1939', 'The Shield of Achilles', The Age of Anxiety, 'For the Time Being', 'Horae Canonicae'. To composers he mattered as a collaborator, and above all to Benjamin Britten. The two worked together on Paul Bunyan, on the film documentaries and on Britten's early song cycles, in the years when Britten was finding out what setting English words could be, before the friendship broke down bitterly and for good. Auden died in 1973, having done as much as any poet of the century to shape what composers thought a text could carry."},
};
